1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:07,780
Welcome to the complete course for the AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner Exam exam code CLF-C02.

2
00:00:08,654 --> 00:00:15,972
Over the next several hours, we will cover every domain the exam guide names and a few things it does not name but does test.

3
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:19,396
Let us start with what you are actually walking into.

4
00:00:21,102 --> 00:00:24,772
Here is the single most useful thing to understand before you begin.

5
00:00:25,134 --> 00:00:29,060
Almost nobody fails this exam because they have never heard of a service.

6
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,348
They fail because four options all sound plausible and the clock is running.

7
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:36,388
Every module in this course is built around that.

8
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:43,940
We do not just say what a service does, we say what it is confused with and what word in the question separates them.

9
00:00:45,646 --> 00:00:46,852
The format first.

10
00:00:47,930 --> 00:00:49,850
65 questions in 90 minutes.

11
00:00:50,574 --> 00:00:53,732
Only 50 of those count towards your score.

12
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:58,308
The other 15 are trial questions.

13
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:05,924
AWS is testing for future exams and they are not identified, so treat every question as if it counts.

14
00:01:06,542 --> 00:01:08,560
You need 700 out of 1000.

15
00:01:09,742 --> 00:01:12,996
That is a scaled score, not a raw percentage.

16
00:01:13,294 --> 00:01:16,580
So do not try to compute how many you can afford to miss.

17
00:01:18,094 --> 00:01:19,972
Two things matter enormously here.

18
00:01:20,334 --> 00:01:23,876
There is no penalty for guessing, and the scoring is compensatory.

19
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:28,708
Compensatory means a weak domain cannot fail you on its own.

20
00:01:29,902 --> 00:01:30,980
Four domains.

21
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:36,000
Cloud concepts at 24%, security and compliance at 30%.

22
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:39,200
Cloud technology and services at 34%.

23
00:01:40,110 --> 00:01:42,560
Billing, pricing, and support at 12%.

24
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:46,052
Notice that security is nearly a third of the exam.

25
00:01:46,894 --> 00:01:51,040
People coming from a technical background often skim it because it feels like paperwork.

26
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,640
That is the most common preventable mistake in this exam.

27
00:01:56,174 --> 00:01:59,844
A word on how this was built because it changes how you should use it.

28
00:02:00,558 --> 00:02:06,212
918 practice questions were analyzed to work out which decisions this exam keeps coming back to.

29
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:08,804
Those questions are not reproduced here.

30
00:02:09,742 --> 00:02:17,828
Reproducing a bank trains you to recognize those exact questions, which is worth very little when the real paper phrases things differently.

31
00:02:18,254 --> 00:02:24,708
Instead, every scenario in this course is original, written around the decision the analysis found.

32
00:02:25,102 --> 00:02:29,316
Every explanation traces back to current AWS documentation.

33
00:02:31,054 --> 00:02:35,396
Now for something you need to hear at the start rather than discover in module 15.

34
00:02:36,078 --> 00:02:40,772
Certification content lags the AWS catalog, sometimes by more than a year.

35
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:47,524
There are services on this exam that are correct answers and are also close to new customers, renamed or scheduled for shutdown.

36
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:53,444
If you study from current AWS documentation alone, you will get some of these wrong.

37
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:59,236
If you study only from an old question bank, you will believe things that are no longer true.

38
00:03:00,430 --> 00:03:10,372
Here is the short version and a whole module later covers it properly. The Snow family is close to new customers and snowball support ends at the end of this year.

39
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,092
QuickSight has been renamed twice.

40
00:03:13,646 --> 00:03:17,124
The entire AWS support plan lineup is being replaced.

41
00:03:17,934 --> 00:03:22,052
Code Catalyst and Code Commit are both close to new customers.

42
00:03:22,542 --> 00:03:25,250
SageMaker is now called SageMaker.

43
00:03:25,646 --> 00:03:27,480
AI in the exam guide.

44
00:03:28,494 --> 00:03:30,660
In every case, the rule is the same.

45
00:03:31,374 --> 00:03:37,860
Answer from the documented exam catalog and know the reality so you are not confused when you open the console.

46
00:03:39,502 --> 00:03:41,348
Here is the shape of what is ahead.

47
00:03:41,614 --> 00:03:43,620
Four modules on cloud concepts.

48
00:03:43,950 --> 00:03:46,980
Five on security and compliance, which is the largest domain.

49
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:51,908
Eight on cloud technology and services, because that section covers the most ground.

50
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:54,436
3 on billing, pricing, and support.

51
00:03:55,182 --> 00:04:03,204
Then 3 cross-cutting modules on confusion pairs, on what has changed under the exam, and on exam technique itself.

52
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:07,520
Finally, 3 practice exams of 20 questions each.

53
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:14,240
That is roughly 7 and a quarter hours in total. The modules are deliberately not equal lengths.

54
00:04:14,830 --> 00:04:18,244
Each one is sized by how much the exam actually tests it.

55
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:22,320
A study route rather than a contents list.

56
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:26,820
Watch in order because later modules assume vocabulary from earlier ones.

57
00:04:27,374 --> 00:04:31,012
When a scenario slide pauses, actually pause and commit to an answer.

58
00:04:31,470 --> 00:04:35,428
Watching a walkthrough without answering first teaches you almost nothing.

59
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:39,844
It feels like learning because you agree with every step.

60
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:46,320
Take practice exam 1 after the teaching modules and score it by domain rather than by total.

61
00:04:46,574 --> 00:04:48,676
Rewatch only your weakest domain.

62
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:50,308
Then exam 2.

63
00:04:50,798 --> 00:04:52,900
Then leave several days before exam.

64
00:04:53,582 --> 00:04:54,000
3.

65
00:04:54,926 --> 00:04:56,420
That gap is the point.

66
00:04:57,358 --> 00:05:01,476
Revising what you already know is comfortable and moves nothing.

67
00:05:03,310 --> 00:05:04,560
Some conventions.

68
00:05:05,422 --> 00:05:11,492
The amber bar at the bottom of a slide is the exam takeaway. If you take one note per slide, take that one.

69
00:05:12,590 --> 00:05:15,972
The marker running down the left edge is split by domain weight.

70
00:05:16,654 --> 00:05:21,668
The lid segment tells you where you are, so you absorb the weighting without memorizing it.

71
00:05:22,318 --> 00:05:24,292
Green points are true or advisable.

72
00:05:24,686 --> 00:05:26,052
Red points are traps.

73
00:05:26,542 --> 00:05:31,268
And in every work scenario, the wrong answers get explained as carefully as the right one.

74
00:05:31,982 --> 00:05:37,700
That is deliberate because understanding why a distractor fails transfers to questions you have never seen.

75
00:05:39,310 --> 00:05:40,644
One last calibration.

76
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:47,012
AWS describes the target candidate as someone with up to six months of exposure to the AWS cloud.

77
00:05:48,078 --> 00:05:57,636
The exam guide explicitly lists coding, designing cloud architecture, troubleshooting, implementation, and load testing as out of scope.

78
00:05:58,542 --> 00:06:05,252
If you are worrying about writing policy documents by hand or sizing instance families, you are studying above the level.

79
00:06:05,614 --> 00:06:08,324
Breadth beats depth here every time.

80
00:06:10,158 --> 00:06:11,588
So what does ready look like?

81
00:06:12,942 --> 00:06:17,280
You can say in one sentence what each in-scope service is for.

82
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:25,680
You can separate two services that sound alike and you notice the qualifier words because most cost-effective and least operational overhead point

83
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:27,760
at genuinely different answers.

84
00:06:28,238 --> 00:06:33,252
What ready does not look like is memorized instance families or pricing tables.

85
00:06:33,614 --> 00:06:35,300
None of that is on this exam.

86
00:06:35,886 --> 00:06:36,676
Let us begin.

87
00:06:39,630 --> 00:06:42,560
This module covers the first half of domain one.

88
00:06:43,022 --> 00:06:47,492
Why organizations move to the cloud and what happens to their costs when they do.

89
00:06:48,078 --> 00:06:50,148
It looks like the soft part of the exam.

90
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:51,300
It is not.

91
00:06:52,142 --> 00:06:58,756
Questions here are decided by one vocabulary word, and if you pick the wrong benefit name, you lose the mark,

92
00:06:59,598 --> 00:07:01,348
even though your reasoning was fine.

93
00:07:02,894 --> 00:07:04,420
Here is the shape of this domain.

94
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:10,756
A scenario describes something good happening to a company, and you have to attach the correct AWS benefit name to it.

95
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:12,868
Faster launches is agility.

96
00:07:13,294 --> 00:07:15,204
Surviving of failure is resilience.

97
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:20,356
Lower unit prices, because AWS is enormous, is economies of scale.

98
00:07:20,878 --> 00:07:22,756
The scenarios vary endlessly.

99
00:07:23,502 --> 00:07:30,500
The list of names does not learn the names precisely, and this domain becomes almost mechanical.

100
00:07:32,174 --> 00:07:36,580
This is the canonical list of six advantages, and it is worth memorizing as a set.

101
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:39,428
Trade capital expense for variable expense.

102
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:41,956
Benefit from massive economies of scale.

103
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:43,680
Stop guessing capacity.

104
00:07:43,886 --> 00:07:45,572
Increase speed and agility.

105
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:48,868
Stop spending money running and maintaining data centers.

106
00:07:49,838 --> 00:07:51,492
And go global in minutes.

107
00:07:51,886 --> 00:08:00,644
Notice how similar some of them sound. Stop guessing capacity and increase speed and agility. Both describe good outcomes from flexibility.

108
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:02,980
The exam will make you separate them.

109
00:08:04,270 --> 00:08:06,864
Capital expense against variable expense.

110
00:08:07,104 --> 00:08:16,356
On premises, you pay a large sum before anything runs. For capacity, you guessed at, and that hardware depreciates whether you use it or not.

111
00:08:16,974 --> 00:08:19,556
On AWS, you pay for what you consume.

112
00:08:19,984 --> 00:08:22,788
And when the resource stops, the charge stops.

113
00:08:23,630 --> 00:08:25,796
Watch for a specific distractor here.

114
00:08:26,304 --> 00:08:34,340
Options that say the cloud eliminates expense or eliminates both fixed and variable costs are always wrong.

115
00:08:35,278 --> 00:08:39,140
You are trading one shape of cost for another, not removing it.

116
00:08:40,814 --> 00:08:44,324
Economies of scale deserves its own slide because it appears constantly.

117
00:08:44,846 --> 00:08:46,372
The mechanism is aggregation.

118
00:08:47,104 --> 00:08:57,732
Enormous numbers of customers combine into purchasing volume no single organization could reach. That lowers what AWS pays per unit and AWS passes it on as lower prices.

119
00:08:58,704 --> 00:09:00,612
The distractors are consistent.

120
00:09:00,864 --> 00:09:04,036
Price matching a competitor is not economies of scale.

121
00:09:04,686 --> 00:09:06,884
The free tier is not economies of scale.

122
00:09:07,438 --> 00:09:09,732
Unlimited resources is not a real thing at all.

123
00:09:10,350 --> 00:09:16,164
If the STEM mentions aggregating usage across customers, the answer is economies of scale.

124
00:09:17,870 --> 00:09:23,076
Agility and elasticity are the pair that cost people the most marks in this domain.

125
00:09:23,504 --> 00:09:26,116
Agility is about speed of doing something new.

126
00:09:27,502 --> 00:09:31,424
A marketing campaign that used to take three weeks now takes three days.

127
00:09:31,630 --> 00:09:32,868
That is agility.

128
00:09:33,646 --> 00:09:36,388
Elasticity is about capacity following demand.

129
00:09:37,358 --> 00:09:41,824
Traffic triples on Friday and the fleet grows, then shrinks again on Sunday.

130
00:09:41,984 --> 00:09:46,276
That is elasticity. The question usually hands you the clue.

131
00:09:47,022 --> 00:09:49,796
If it mentions time to launch, choose agility.

132
00:09:50,862 --> 00:09:54,820
If it mentions demand rising and falling, choose elasticity.

133
00:09:56,544 --> 00:09:59,984
This table is the reverse lookup you actually use in the exam.

134
00:10:00,224 --> 00:10:04,228
Rather than starting from the benefit, start from the phrase in the scenario.

135
00:10:04,544 --> 00:10:07,876
Faster launches and cheap experiments means agility.

136
00:10:10,062 --> 00:10:12,612
Capacity tracking load means elasticity.

137
00:10:13,774 --> 00:10:18,276
Surviving failure and improving security posture means operational resilience.

138
00:10:18,862 --> 00:10:22,788
Staff no longer doing undifferentiated heavy lifting means staff productivity.

139
00:10:23,662 --> 00:10:25,988
Aggregated demand means economies of scale.

140
00:10:26,734 --> 00:10:29,284
Deploying into a new region means global reach.

141
00:10:30,190 --> 00:10:36,932
And no big upfront purchase means trading capital expense for variable expense.

142
00:10:38,638 --> 00:10:42,788
Two benefit names get confused with cost savings, so let us pin them down.

143
00:10:43,504 --> 00:10:52,388
Operational resilience bundles, availability, fault tolerance, disaster recovery, and improved security controls into one name.

144
00:10:52,814 --> 00:10:59,236
If a company migrates for higher uptime and stronger security, that is operational resilience.

145
00:10:59,694 --> 00:11:04,704
Staff productivity means your people stop doing undifferentiated heavy lifting.

146
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:14,628
Racking hardware, swapping failed disks, patching hypervisors, both of these do usually save money, which is exactly why cost savings appears as a distractor.

147
00:11:15,246 --> 00:11:19,972
Choose cost savings only when the scenario is explicitly about the bill.

148
00:11:21,422 --> 00:11:25,316
Understanding where on premises money goes makes several questions easy.

149
00:11:25,744 --> 00:11:27,908
There is hardware with its refresh cycle.

150
00:11:28,224 --> 00:11:32,100
Facilities meaning space, power, cooling, and physical security.

151
00:11:32,464 --> 00:11:35,396
Licensing, often charged per socket or per core.

152
00:11:36,544 --> 00:11:38,468
Staffing to rack and patch it all.

153
00:11:39,406 --> 00:11:44,644
And idle capacity, the headroom bought for a peak that rarely arrives.

154
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:51,216
Now, the exam question.

155
00:11:51,456 --> 00:11:53,856
Which costs does migration eliminate?

156
00:11:54,158 --> 00:11:56,612
Data center costs and hardware, yes.

157
00:11:56,896 --> 00:12:03,652
But you still pay for security work, for monitoring, and for training your people. Those move, they do not vanish.

158
00:12:05,006 --> 00:12:09,616
Licensing appears in the exam guide explicitly, so know both strategies.

159
00:12:09,966 --> 00:12:13,540
Bring your own license means you reuse an agreement you already hold.

160
00:12:14,030 --> 00:12:21,252
That often requires dedicated hosts because socket-based and core-based license terms need visibility of the physical server.

161
00:12:21,934 --> 00:12:33,056
License included means AWS builds the cost into the hourly rate. That is simpler and usually dearer per hour, but it stops charging when you stop the instance.

162
00:12:33,376 --> 00:12:41,732
The exam link worth remembering is that license terms tied to physical hardware are the classic justification for dedicated hosts.

163
00:12:43,118 --> 00:12:44,656
Two more named concepts.

164
00:12:44,976 --> 00:12:51,268
Right sizing means matching instance size to measured usage and doing it repeatedly rather than once at launch.

165
00:12:51,616 --> 00:12:58,628
The tools named for this are AWS Compute Optimizer and the write sizing recommendations inside Cost Explorer.

166
00:12:59,406 --> 00:13:05,092
Automation reduces cost by removing manual effort and by turning things off when nobody needs them.

167
00:13:06,414 --> 00:13:08,736
Here is the distinction that gets tested.

168
00:13:09,056 --> 00:13:14,336
Right sizing changes the size of an instance. Autoscaling changes the number of instances.

169
00:13:14,656 --> 00:13:16,336
Size versus count.

170
00:13:17,774 --> 00:13:18,916
Worked scenario.

171
00:13:19,536 --> 00:13:19,972
1.

172
00:13:21,422 --> 00:13:23,556
A retailer runs its own data center.

173
00:13:24,430 --> 00:13:32,336
Every November it buys extra servers for the Christmas peak, and for the other 11 months, about 60% of that hardware sits idle.

174
00:13:32,910 --> 00:13:39,536
After migrating to AWS, the finance director notices there is no longer a large hardware purchase each autumn.

175
00:13:39,696 --> 00:13:43,972
Instead, the monthly bill rises through November and falls again in January.

176
00:13:44,496 --> 00:13:48,420
Which advantage of cloud computing does this most directly describe?

177
00:13:48,976 --> 00:13:52,656
a benefit from massive economies of scale.

178
00:13:53,166 --> 00:13:53,520
B.

179
00:13:53,902 --> 00:13:56,484
Trade capital expense for variable expense.

180
00:13:56,816 --> 00:13:57,056
C.

181
00:13:57,582 --> 00:13:59,296
Increase speed and agility.

182
00:13:59,616 --> 00:13:59,856
D.

183
00:14:00,256 --> 00:14:01,732
Go global in minutes.

184
00:14:02,094 --> 00:14:03,108
Pause and decide.

185
00:14:09,166 --> 00:14:10,308
The answer is B.

186
00:14:10,798 --> 00:14:13,508
Trade capital expense for variable expense.

187
00:14:14,830 --> 00:14:23,044
The deciding sentence is that the autumn hardware purchase disappeared and was replaced by a bill that follows demand. That is the definition.

188
00:14:24,398 --> 00:14:26,948
Now look at why C is genuinely tempting.

189
00:14:27,886 --> 00:14:32,900
Elasticity is clearly happening in the background, and elasticity feels like agility.

190
00:14:33,296 --> 00:14:35,396
But nothing in this scenario got faster.

191
00:14:35,854 --> 00:14:40,676
The Christmas peak arrives at the same time it always did. Only the shape of the payment changed.

192
00:14:40,976 --> 00:14:46,564
A is true of AWS but unsupported here, and D has no geography in it at all.

193
00:14:47,854 --> 00:14:48,836
Work scenario.

194
00:14:49,774 --> 00:14:50,116
2.

195
00:14:50,990 --> 00:14:58,692
A media company used to need 6 weeks to get servers approved, ordered, delivered, and racked before it could test a new video format.

196
00:14:59,310 --> 00:15:02,180
Two of the last three formats were abandoned after testing.

197
00:15:02,896 --> 00:15:10,820
On AWS, the same test now takes two days to set up and the team runs four experiments a quarter instead of one.

198
00:15:11,278 --> 00:15:15,556
Which benefit of the AWS cloud does this scenario demonstrate?

199
00:15:16,016 --> 00:15:24,900
a Elasticity, B Operational Resilience, C Increase Business Agility, D. High Availability.

200
00:15:25,518 --> 00:15:26,724
Pause and decide.

201
00:15:33,006 --> 00:15:34,304
The answer is C.

202
00:15:34,926 --> 00:15:36,772
Increase business agility.

203
00:15:38,638 --> 00:15:48,544
Compare this deliberately with scenario 1. There, the payment shape changed and the timeline did not. Here, the timeline changed and nothing was said about money.

204
00:15:48,784 --> 00:15:53,316
Same domain, different name benefit, and the difference is one sentence in the stem.

205
00:15:54,254 --> 00:15:58,884
That is why reading for the deciding detail matters more than knowing the definitions.

206
00:15:59,504 --> 00:16:02,564
A is wrong because no capacity moves with demand.

207
00:16:03,182 --> 00:16:07,940
B and D both need something to have failed or gone down, and nothing did.

208
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:14,372
Worked scenario 3. And this one is a multiple response.

209
00:16:14,704 --> 00:16:21,220
A logistics firm is building its migration business case and wants to know which cost lines will actually disappear.

210
00:16:21,742 --> 00:16:31,876
The list includes the data center lease, server hardware refresh, its security operations team, its monitoring tooling subscription, and the upfront purchase of storage arrays.

211
00:16:33,230 --> 00:16:36,228
Which two costs will migrating to AWS eliminate?

212
00:16:37,774 --> 00:16:39,588
A data center costs.

213
00:16:40,224 --> 00:16:40,464
B

214
00:16:41,518 --> 00:16:43,044
Data security costs.

215
00:16:44,110 --> 00:16:46,340
C Fixed hardware costs.

216
00:16:46,864 --> 00:16:47,104
D

217
00:16:48,494 --> 00:16:49,796
monitoring costs.

218
00:16:50,144 --> 00:16:50,304
E.

219
00:16:50,704 --> 00:16:52,064
Staff training costs.

220
00:16:52,462 --> 00:16:54,660
Choose to pause and decide.

221
00:17:00,814 --> 00:17:02,276
The answers are A and C.

222
00:17:02,544 --> 00:17:08,324
The word doing the work in this question is eliminate, not reduce, not shift, eliminate.

223
00:17:08,624 --> 00:17:12,624
Data center costs go entirely because AWS owns the buildings.

224
00:17:12,864 --> 00:17:16,420
Fixed hardware costs go because purchase becomes consumption.

225
00:17:16,814 --> 00:17:18,948
Everything else changes shape and stays.

226
00:17:19,854 --> 00:17:29,540
Security is explicitly still yours under the shared responsibility module. Monitoring is still a build service and training usually goes up during a migration, not down.

227
00:17:30,094 --> 00:17:34,180
When a question users eliminate, test each option against gone entirely.

228
00:17:35,790 --> 00:17:37,124
Work scenario 4.

229
00:17:37,582 --> 00:17:43,940
A finance team reviews its AWS bill and finds 12 development instances running continuously for 8 months.

230
00:17:45,166 --> 00:17:50,180
Peak CPU utilization is 9% and nobody uses them outside working hours.

231
00:17:51,054 --> 00:17:56,544
Development says the instances must keep their current configuration and data during working hours.

232
00:17:56,784 --> 00:17:59,396
Which two actions address this most directly?

233
00:18:00,064 --> 00:18:03,108
a Right size the instances to a smaller type.

234
00:18:03,664 --> 00:18:07,236
B put the instances behind an auto-scaling group.

235
00:18:08,110 --> 00:18:08,384
C.

236
00:18:08,910 --> 00:18:12,384
Schedule the instances to stop outside working hours.

237
00:18:12,942 --> 00:18:13,184
D.

238
00:18:13,664 --> 00:18:16,304
Buy three year reserved instances for them.

239
00:18:16,784 --> 00:18:17,104
E.

240
00:18:17,614 --> 00:18:19,876
Move the workload to spot instances.

241
00:18:20,526 --> 00:18:22,756
Choose to pause and decide.

242
00:18:28,974 --> 00:18:30,916
The answers are A and C.

243
00:18:31,344 --> 00:18:35,332
This scenario plants two constraints, one for each mark.

244
00:18:36,064 --> 00:18:39,812
9% peak utilization is the right sizing signal.

245
00:18:40,494 --> 00:18:44,164
Nobody using them outside working hours is the scheduling signal.

246
00:18:44,544 --> 00:18:46,852
Now look at the two best distractors.

247
00:18:47,726 --> 00:18:54,052
D is the trap that catches cost conscious candidates because reserved instances do save money.

248
00:18:54,544 --> 00:19:01,188
But committing three years to instances that are the wrong size and mostly idle just buys the waste at a discount.

249
00:19:01,984 --> 00:19:04,228
Fix the waste first, then commit.

250
00:19:06,304 --> 00:19:09,264
And E fails on a stated constraint.

251
00:19:10,606 --> 00:19:15,588
The developers need their working state to persist, and spot interruption destroys it.

252
00:19:17,262 --> 00:19:24,548
To recap, the six advantages are vocabulary and the exam grades the name rather than your reasoning, so learn them precisely.

253
00:19:25,584 --> 00:19:29,412
Agility measures time, elasticity measures capacity.

254
00:19:30,064 --> 00:19:33,348
Economies of scale means aggregation across customers.

255
00:19:34,158 --> 00:19:42,788
Migration eliminates facilities and upfront hardware while security, monitoring, and planning change shape rather than disappearing.

256
00:19:43,534 --> 00:19:47,780
And right sizing changes size while auto-scaling changes count.

257
00:19:49,198 --> 00:19:51,844
Next module, the well-architected framework.

258
00:19:53,454 --> 00:20:00,836
The well-architected framework is named directly in the exam guide and questions about it are unusually predictable.

259
00:20:01,646 --> 00:20:04,804
A scenario describes a design goal and you name the pillar.

260
00:20:05,390 --> 00:20:08,740
Get the pillar definitions crisp and these become fee marks.

261
00:20:10,446 --> 00:20:13,424
One framing that makes the pillars easier to remember.

262
00:20:13,664 --> 00:20:15,664
They are not a checklist you take off.

263
00:20:16,078 --> 00:20:19,588
They are six priorities that genuinely pull against each other.

264
00:20:20,142 --> 00:20:22,144
Tighter security costs performance.

265
00:20:22,384 --> 00:20:24,452
Higher reliability costs money.

266
00:20:25,264 --> 00:20:34,596
The framework exists because those trade-offs need naming before a team can argue about them. That is also why a scenario can touch three pillars and still have one correct answer.

267
00:20:35,024 --> 00:20:37,156
One goal is always the stated goal.

268
00:20:38,670 --> 00:20:39,652
Six pillars.

269
00:20:40,144 --> 00:20:44,516
Operational excellence asks whether you can run and improve the system day to day.

270
00:20:45,024 --> 00:20:48,612
Security asks whether you can protect data and prove who did what.

271
00:20:49,614 --> 00:20:53,668
Reliability asks whether it does its job correctly and recovers from failure.

272
00:20:54,574 --> 00:20:59,940
Performance efficiency asks whether you are using resources well as demand changes.

273
00:21:00,302 --> 00:21:04,804
Cost optimization asks whether you are getting value at the lowest price.

274
00:21:05,344 --> 00:21:09,860
And sustainability asks whether you are minimizing environmental impact.

275
00:21:11,278 --> 00:21:13,604
Older study material lists five pillars.

276
00:21:14,606 --> 00:21:18,980
Sustainability was added in 2021 and it is examinable.

277
00:21:20,398 --> 00:21:27,460
Reliability and performance efficiency are the pair most often confused because both sound like the system working properly.

278
00:21:27,982 --> 00:21:30,596
The separator is what the scenario worries about.

279
00:21:31,118 --> 00:21:33,028
Reliability worries about failure.

280
00:21:33,742 --> 00:21:39,364
Recovering automatically, multi-availability zone deployment, backups, health checks.

281
00:21:40,464 --> 00:21:43,492
Performance efficiency worries about efficiency.

282
00:21:43,904 --> 00:21:50,692
Choosing the light resource type, adopting caching or serverless, adapting as demand changes.

283
00:21:51,264 --> 00:21:55,844
If the words recover or fail appear, choose reliability.

284
00:21:57,582 --> 00:22:01,892
The other pair worth separating is operational excellence and reliability.

285
00:22:02,830 --> 00:22:05,892
Operational excellence is about how the team runs the system.

286
00:22:06,478 --> 00:22:13,412
Deployment processes, run books, observability, making small reversible changes, learning from failures.

287
00:22:14,318 --> 00:22:17,444
Reliability is about how the system behaves without you.

288
00:22:18,478 --> 00:22:19,824
It recovers on its own.

289
00:22:20,174 --> 00:22:25,508
A useful test is to ask whether the sentence is about people and process or about architecture.

290
00:22:27,662 --> 00:22:29,860
Process points at operational excellence.

291
00:22:31,598 --> 00:22:36,260
The framework also has general design principles that get quoted in questions.

292
00:22:36,942 --> 00:22:38,436
Stop guessing capacity.

293
00:22:38,894 --> 00:22:45,184
Test systems at production scale, which is affordable in the cloud because you delete the environment afterwards.

294
00:22:45,424 --> 00:22:48,612
Automate to make architectural experimentation easier.

295
00:22:49,550 --> 00:22:51,492
Allow for evolutionary architectures.

296
00:22:52,250 --> 00:22:53,770
Drive architectures using data.

297
00:22:55,662 --> 00:23:01,412
And improve through game days, meaning rehearsing failure deliberately rather than waiting to meet it.

298
00:23:02,384 --> 00:23:07,044
Game days and testing at production scale are the two that turn up most as correct answers.

299
00:23:08,750 --> 00:23:13,124
There is a framework and there is a tool, and the exam distinguishes them.

300
00:23:17,454 --> 00:23:26,660
The architected tool is a free console service where you answer a structured questionnaire about a workload and receive an improvement plan.

301
00:23:27,040 --> 00:23:28,708
It is a review you run.

302
00:23:29,680 --> 00:23:34,916
Trusted Advisor, by contrast, inspects your live account continuously and automatically.

303
00:23:35,566 --> 00:23:37,764
That is the separator the exam uses.

304
00:23:38,480 --> 00:23:41,476
Continuous and automatic points at Trusted Advisor.

305
00:23:42,574 --> 00:23:47,172
A structured review, you sit down and complete points at the well-architected tool.

306
00:23:48,462 --> 00:23:52,484
This reverse lookup mirrors the phrasing AWS actually uses.

307
00:23:52,800 --> 00:23:56,740
Recover quickly and perform consistently means reliability.

308
00:23:57,040 --> 00:24:00,196
Protect data and trace actions means security.

309
00:24:00,686 --> 00:24:04,100
Run and monitor day-to-day means operational excellence.

310
00:24:04,750 --> 00:24:09,252
Use resources efficiently as demand changes means performance efficiency.

311
00:24:10,286 --> 00:24:13,988
Deliver value at the lowest price means cost optimization.

312
00:24:14,798 --> 00:24:18,180
And reduce environmental impact means sustainability.

313
00:24:19,086 --> 00:24:25,444
Learning the phrasings is more useful than learning the names because the phrasings are what appears on screen.

314
00:24:26,880 --> 00:24:28,324
Worked scenario.

315
00:24:28,880 --> 00:24:33,764
1. A payments company is redesigning its transaction service.

316
00:24:34,160 --> 00:24:43,652
It must continue processing correctly if an entire availability zone becomes unavailable and must return to full capacity without an engineer intervening.

317
00:24:44,494 --> 00:24:51,524
The team is deploying across three availability zones with health checks and automatic replacement of failed instances.

318
00:24:52,334 --> 00:24:54,532
Which pillar does this design represent?

319
00:24:55,630 --> 00:24:57,295
A security.

320
00:24:57,902 --> 00:24:58,310
B.

321
00:24:59,010 --> 00:25:00,110
Performance efficiency

322
00:25:00,110 --> 00:25:01,570
C.

323
00:25:02,250 --> 00:25:03,330
Operational excellence

324
00:25:03,330 --> 00:25:04,750
D.

325
00:25:05,290 --> 00:25:07,780
Reliability Pause and decide.

326
00:25:13,998 --> 00:25:16,580
The answer is D reliability.

327
00:25:17,040 --> 00:25:18,660
Two phrases decide it.

328
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:24,068
Continue processing correctly and return to full capacity without an engineer intervening.

329
00:25:24,782 --> 00:25:26,560
Those are almost word for word.

330
00:25:27,790 --> 00:25:29,284
The reliability definition.

331
00:25:29,614 --> 00:25:33,860
Watch option. A carefully because it is the best distractor in the set.

332
00:25:34,638 --> 00:25:39,428
The word payments makes security feel relevant and it would be in real life.

333
00:25:39,950 --> 00:25:46,660
But the exam grades the stated requirement and nothing here asks about access, encryption, or audit.

334
00:25:48,270 --> 00:25:52,960
Domain knowledge you bring to the question is not evidence in the question.

335
00:25:54,158 --> 00:25:55,716
Worked scenario 2.

336
00:25:56,302 --> 00:26:02,660
A research group runs Nike simulations on a fixed fleet of large general purpose EC2 instances.

337
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:05,284
The jobs are compute bound.

338
00:26:05,920 --> 00:26:11,044
Memory sits at 11% while CPU pins at 100% for hours.

339
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:20,000
The team wants the jobs to finish sooner without increasing spend and is evaluating compute optimized instance types and a cache-in

340
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:21,860
layer for shared input data.

341
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:24,900
Which pillar is the team primarily working in?

342
00:26:25,360 --> 00:26:27,108
A reliability.

343
00:26:27,694 --> 00:26:29,796
B performance efficiency.

344
00:26:30,254 --> 00:26:32,196
C Cost optimization.

345
00:26:32,560 --> 00:26:34,276
D. Sustainability.

346
00:26:36,366 --> 00:26:37,412
Pause and decide.

347
00:26:43,280 --> 00:26:44,560
The answer is B.

348
00:26:44,800 --> 00:26:45,956
Performance efficiency.

349
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:50,052
This scenario is built to test one specific reading skill.

350
00:26:50,510 --> 00:26:54,500
It mentions spend, which pulls hard towards cost optimization.

351
00:26:54,958 --> 00:26:56,356
But read the sentence again.

352
00:26:56,910 --> 00:27:00,004
Without increasing spend is a constraint on the solution.

353
00:27:00,366 --> 00:27:01,796
Finish sooner is the goal.

354
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:05,508
The goal picks the pillar, not the constraint.

355
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:10,660
That distinction appears throughout this exam well beyond well-architected questions.

356
00:27:10,960 --> 00:27:14,820
Learn to separate what the scenario wants from what it will not allow.

357
00:27:16,400 --> 00:27:17,988
Warped Scenario 3.

358
00:27:18,320 --> 00:27:22,880
An operations manager wants to reduce production incidents caused by deployments.

359
00:27:23,120 --> 00:27:31,300
The team ships one large release a month, has no rollback procedure, and diagnoses problems by reading logs on individual servers.

360
00:27:32,110 --> 00:27:40,964
The proposed changes are to deploy small changes frequently, skip the rollback, centralize logs and metrics, and hold a review after every incident.

361
00:27:41,600 --> 00:27:43,940
Which pillar do these changes belong to?

362
00:27:44,560 --> 00:27:46,372
a Operational Excellence,

363
00:27:47,406 --> 00:27:48,900
B reliability,

364
00:27:49,902 --> 00:27:51,460
C security,

365
00:27:52,160 --> 00:27:52,480
D.

366
00:27:52,880 --> 00:27:54,212
Performance efficiency.

367
00:27:55,182 --> 00:27:56,388
Pause and decide.

368
00:28:02,670 --> 00:28:05,796
The answer is A, operational excellence.

369
00:28:08,270 --> 00:28:11,152
Compare this with scenario one deliberately.

370
00:28:11,598 --> 00:28:14,244
Both scenarios are about failures in production.

371
00:28:15,918 --> 00:28:24,992
In scenario one, the fix was architectural, deploying across availability zones with automatic replacement, and the answer was reliability.

372
00:28:25,518 --> 00:28:29,060
Here, every single proposed change is a process change.

373
00:28:29,454 --> 00:28:35,952
Release cadence, a rollback script, centralized observability, and incident reviews.

374
00:28:36,112 --> 00:28:38,308
Nothing about the architecture moves.

375
00:28:38,512 --> 00:28:40,804
That is operational excellence.

376
00:28:41,072 --> 00:28:45,632
Same problem, different pillar, because the fix lives in a different place.

377
00:28:47,022 --> 00:28:48,324
Worked scenario 4.

378
00:28:49,518 --> 00:28:55,044
A company wants a structured review of its main workload against AWS best practices.

379
00:28:56,206 --> 00:29:04,192
It wants to answer questions about the workload, receive a prioritized list of improvements, and repeat the review quarterly.

380
00:29:04,814 --> 00:29:13,348
It also wants automatic ongoing checks of the live account for unused resources and open security groups without anyone completing a questionnaire.

381
00:29:13,712 --> 00:29:16,352
Which two services meet these requirements?

382
00:29:16,672 --> 00:29:24,672
A AWS Well Architected Tool, B AWS Trusted Advisor, C AWS Config, D.

383
00:29:25,102 --> 00:29:27,300
AWS Compute Optimizer.

384
00:29:28,272 --> 00:29:28,512
E

385
00:29:29,454 --> 00:29:30,692
Amazon Inspector.

386
00:29:31,406 --> 00:29:32,132
Choose to

387
00:29:32,782 --> 00:29:34,052
pause and decide.

388
00:29:40,206 --> 00:29:42,436
The answers are A and B.

389
00:29:43,054 --> 00:29:45,924
Multiple response questions frequently do this.

390
00:29:46,352 --> 00:29:51,428
Two distinct requirements, one service each, and your job is to notice there are two.

391
00:29:52,014 --> 00:29:56,068
The questionnaire-based repeatable review is the well-architected tool.

392
00:29:56,352 --> 00:30:00,420
The continuous automatic account checks are trusted advisor.

393
00:30:01,312 --> 00:30:08,548
C, D, and E are all real services that do adjacent things, which is what makes them good distractors.

394
00:30:09,152 --> 00:30:11,172
Config records configuration.

395
00:30:11,758 --> 00:30:14,084
Compute optimizer right sizes.

396
00:30:14,352 --> 00:30:16,352
Inspector finds vulnerabilities.

397
00:30:16,512 --> 00:30:18,916
None of them runs a best practice questionnaire.

398
00:30:20,622 --> 00:30:25,156
To recap, six pillars and sustainability is examinable.

399
00:30:25,678 --> 00:30:31,044
Reliability worries about failure while performance efficiency worries about efficiency.

400
00:30:31,918 --> 00:30:39,236
Operational excellence lives in process while reliability lives in architecture. So ask where the proposed fix sits.

401
00:30:40,142 --> 00:30:43,076
The goal picks the pillar, not the constraint.

402
00:30:45,006 --> 00:30:51,844
And the well-architected tool is a review you run while trusted advisor watches continuously.

403
00:30:52,814 --> 00:31:01,188
One small note AWS spells the pillar cost optimization with a Z because it is a product name.

404
00:31:01,678 --> 00:31:06,468
Next module, migration strategies and the cloud adoption framework.

405
00:31:08,142 --> 00:31:12,516
This module covers how organizations actually get onto AWS.

406
00:31:13,518 --> 00:31:14,308
Two halves.

407
00:31:14,926 --> 00:31:24,192
First, the seven migration strategies with the exam test as vocabulary. Then the tools AWS provides to plan and execute a move.

408
00:31:24,718 --> 00:31:31,268
The tooling half is where several older question banks have gone out of date, so we will flag that as we go.

409
00:31:32,752 --> 00:31:37,392
The shape here is the same as the benefits questions in module 1.

410
00:31:37,806 --> 00:31:43,428
A scenario describes what a company did to an application and you attach the correct strategy name.

411
00:31:43,950 --> 00:31:46,432
Moved it unchanged means re-host.

412
00:31:51,232 --> 00:31:51,552
Throw

413
00:31:51,712 --> 00:31:55,076
it away and bought software instead means repurchase.

414
00:31:55,822 --> 00:31:57,636
Learn the 7 names precisely,

415
00:31:58,382 --> 00:32:00,676
and most of this becomes mechanical.

416
00:32:02,382 --> 00:32:04,100
Here is the canonical list.

417
00:32:04,494 --> 00:32:07,568
Re-host is lift and shift, moving it unchanged.

418
00:32:07,918 --> 00:32:14,756
Replatform is lift, tinker and shift, making small optimizations without touching the core application.

419
00:32:15,310 --> 00:32:21,088
Repurchase is drop and shop, abandoning what you have and buying software instead.

420
00:32:21,646 --> 00:32:24,932
Refactor means rewriting it to use cloud native services.

421
00:32:25,614 --> 00:32:28,484
Retire means switching it off because nobody used it.

422
00:32:29,006 --> 00:32:30,980
Retain means leaving it alone for now.

423
00:32:31,566 --> 00:32:36,356
And relocate means moving infrastructure wholesale without changing how you operate it.

424
00:32:37,934 --> 00:32:41,412
This reverse lookup is what you actually use under time pressure.

425
00:32:41,728 --> 00:32:43,556
Ask one question of the scenario.

426
00:32:43,888 --> 00:32:46,020
What happened to the application itself?

427
00:32:46,510 --> 00:32:48,128
Unchanged is re-host.

428
00:32:48,718 --> 00:32:50,276
Tweaked is replatform.

429
00:32:50,990 --> 00:32:53,860
Replaced with the purchase product is repurchase.

430
00:32:54,702 --> 00:32:56,356
Re-written is refactor.

431
00:32:56,782 --> 00:32:57,988
Deleted is retire.

432
00:32:58,702 --> 00:32:59,720
Left alone is retain.

433
00:33:00,878 --> 00:33:04,196
And moved wholesale with operations intact is relocate.

434
00:33:04,686 --> 00:33:06,916
Everything else in the scenario is context.

435
00:33:08,334 --> 00:33:15,076
Re-host and replatform are the pair that cost the most marks because both keep the application working the same way.

436
00:33:15,630 --> 00:33:17,764
In a re-host, nothing changes at all.

437
00:33:18,126 --> 00:33:22,596
Same operating system, same code, just running on AWS instead.

438
00:33:23,088 --> 00:33:32,516
In a replatform, one component is swapped for a managed AWS equivalent. Most commonly, a self-managed database, becoming Amazon RDS.

439
00:33:33,408 --> 00:33:36,100
The application code itself is untouched.

440
00:33:36,558 --> 00:33:41,732
So the separator is simple. Did a managed AWS service replace a component?

441
00:33:42,254 --> 00:33:43,716
If yes, replatform.

442
00:33:45,486 --> 00:33:50,852
Refactor and repurchase both abandon the system you had so they get swapped.

443
00:33:51,534 --> 00:33:54,404
The difference is who ends up owning the software.

444
00:33:55,150 --> 00:34:02,084
In a refactor, you rewrite it yourself, turning a monolith into microservices or serverless functions.

445
00:34:02,542 --> 00:34:06,340
It is the most expensive of the seven and usually the most rewarding.

446
00:34:07,168 --> 00:34:12,580
In a repurchase, you stop owning software altogether and subscribe to a product instead.

447
00:34:13,088 --> 00:34:15,780
Refactor changes the shape of your software.

448
00:34:17,166 --> 00:34:18,660
Repurchase throws it away.

449
00:34:20,334 --> 00:34:22,288
Three strategies get overlooked.

450
00:34:22,478 --> 00:34:29,476
Retire is a real one, and discovery exercises routinely find that a tenth of an estate is running with nobody using it.

451
00:34:29,710 --> 00:34:32,448
Switching those off is the cheapest possible outcome.

452
00:34:32,654 --> 00:34:34,884
So retire is often correct answer.

453
00:34:35,342 --> 00:34:43,300
Retain means deliberately not moving something because of latency, a compliance rule, or a license that will not travel.

454
00:34:43,648 --> 00:34:46,788
It is a decision with a reason, not indecision.

455
00:34:47,438 --> 00:34:52,036
And relocate moves infrastructure wholesale without changing how you operate it.

456
00:34:53,614 --> 00:34:56,548
One framing that helps with the scenario questions.

457
00:34:57,006 --> 00:35:00,368
No real organization picks a single strategy for everything.

458
00:35:00,608 --> 00:35:06,788
A portfolio gets sorted, the unused applications get retired, the straightforward ones get re-hosted.

459
00:35:07,088 --> 00:35:10,448
A handful with expensive licenses get replatformed.

460
00:35:10,958 --> 00:35:17,188
One or two strategically important ones get refactored, and a few get retained because they cannot move yet.

461
00:35:18,926 --> 00:35:25,124
When a question describes a whole estate, expect the answer to involve sorting rather than one strategy.

462
00:35:26,862 --> 00:35:32,004
AWS describes a journey in three phases and the tooling maps onto them.

463
00:35:32,846 --> 00:35:37,156
Assess is where you understand what you have and build a business case.

464
00:35:37,808 --> 00:35:44,036
Mobilize is where you close skill gaps, build a landing zone, and pilot a few applications.

465
00:35:44,974 --> 00:35:50,084
Migrate and modernize is where you move at scale and then improve what you moved.

466
00:35:50,528 --> 00:35:56,848
This matters for the exam because the discovery and business case tools belong to the assess phase.

467
00:35:57,088 --> 00:36:02,404
That is the clue when a question is about early planning rather than execution.

468
00:36:04,238 --> 00:36:06,980
Six services and each has one job.

469
00:36:09,422 --> 00:36:15,172
Application Discovery Service Inventories, your on-premises servers, and how they are used.

470
00:36:16,110 --> 00:36:24,360
Migration Evaluator builds the cost business case for moving. Migration Hub gives you one place to track progress across tools and regions.

471
00:36:25,328 --> 00:36:29,700
Application Migration Service is the lift and shift tool for servers.

472
00:36:29,968 --> 00:36:35,328
Database migration service moves the data while the source database keeps running.

473
00:36:35,488 --> 00:36:41,156
And the Schema conversion tool converts schema and code between different database engines.

474
00:36:42,606 --> 00:36:48,388
DMS and SCCT are constantly swapped and they do different halves of the same job.

475
00:36:48,768 --> 00:36:50,340
DMS moves the data.

476
00:36:52,430 --> 00:37:00,292
The source database stays online throughout. Continuous replication keeps the target in sync and downtime at cut over is minimal.

477
00:37:01,262 --> 00:37:03,140
SCT converts the structure,

478
00:37:04,046 --> 00:37:07,536
tables, views, stored procedures and functions.

479
00:37:07,918 --> 00:37:10,692
Here is the separator that decides most questions.

480
00:37:11,758 --> 00:37:14,532
SCT is only needed when the engines differ.

481
00:37:15,374 --> 00:37:17,508
Oracle to Postgres needs both.

482
00:37:18,286 --> 00:37:21,252
Oracle to Oracle needs DMS alone.

483
00:37:22,670 --> 00:37:25,892
One vocabulary word decides a whole family of questions.

484
00:37:26,256 --> 00:37:33,124
Homogeneous means the same engine on both sides, Oracle to Oracle or MySQL to MySQL.

485
00:37:33,376 --> 00:37:36,388
The schema already fits, so DMS alone is enough.

486
00:37:36,656 --> 00:37:42,016
Heterogeneous means different engines such as Oracle to Amazon, Aurora Postgres.

487
00:37:42,830 --> 00:37:48,420
The schema will not fit, so SCT converts it first and then DMS moves the data.

488
00:37:49,646 --> 00:37:57,252
Worth knowing that DMS C schema conversion is a managed version of SCCP that runs inside the DMS console.

489
00:37:58,926 --> 00:38:02,884
Both of these sit in the assess phase, so they appear together as options.

490
00:38:03,376 --> 00:38:07,076
Application Discovery Service answers, what have we got?

491
00:38:07,694 --> 00:38:12,868
It inventories servers and their dependencies and collects configuration and usage data.

492
00:38:14,990 --> 00:38:18,084
Migration evaluator answers what would it cost?

493
00:38:18,926 --> 00:38:24,324
It builds a cost comparison and a business case aimed at finance rather than engineers.

494
00:38:24,750 --> 00:38:26,948
So the separator is the audience.

495
00:38:27,616 --> 00:38:30,564
A technical inventory means discovery service.

496
00:38:31,056 --> 00:38:34,916
A cost projection to get approval means migration evaluator.

497
00:38:36,590 --> 00:38:37,828
Two more to separate.

498
00:38:38,256 --> 00:38:42,628
Application migration service is the recommended way to re-host servers.

499
00:38:42,816 --> 00:38:48,036
It replicates them continuously while they keep running, then cuts over with minimal downtime.

500
00:38:48,750 --> 00:38:53,732
If a question says lift and shift with no application changes, that is the service.

501
00:38:54,670 --> 00:38:59,376
Migration Hub tracks progress across tools and regions in one place.

502
00:38:59,936 --> 00:39:03,620
The important thing about migration hub is what it does not do.

503
00:39:03,950 --> 00:39:05,316
It never moves anything.

504
00:39:05,696 --> 00:39:10,020
It is a dashboard, and that is exactly why it works as a distractor.

505
00:39:11,726 --> 00:39:13,828
One availability warning belongs here.

506
00:39:14,190 --> 00:39:17,092
The Snow family used to be the centerpiece of this module.

507
00:39:17,326 --> 00:39:20,388
It is now closed to new customers and off the in-scope list.

508
00:39:21,102 --> 00:39:25,316
Snowball support ends in commercial regions on the 31st of December this year.

509
00:39:26,222 --> 00:39:33,796
Older question banks still use Snowball as the offline bulk transfer answer, and on the exam that answer still stands.

510
00:39:34,030 --> 00:39:39,616
In practice, the successors are datasync, the data transfer terminal, and direct connect.

511
00:39:40,078 --> 00:39:42,212
Module 22 covers this properly.

512
00:39:43,982 --> 00:39:46,212
Worked scenario 1.

513
00:39:50,478 --> 00:40:01,776
A company runs a Java application on Linux servers in its own data center with a self-managed Postgres database on separate hardware. The lease expires in 5 months.

514
00:40:02,094 --> 00:40:08,452
The team moves the Java application onto EC2 instances with no code changes at all.

515
00:40:09,296 --> 00:40:16,964
The database moves on to Amazon RDS for Postgres, which migration strategy describes what happened to the database.

516
00:40:17,296 --> 00:40:23,536
A rehost, B Replatform, C Refactor, D. Repurchase.

517
00:40:23,886 --> 00:40:24,932
Pause and decide.

518
00:40:30,912 --> 00:40:32,228
The answer is B.

519
00:40:32,512 --> 00:40:33,316
Replatform.

520
00:40:33,710 --> 00:40:40,004
Notice what this scenario does. It describes two different strategies happening at once and then asks about one of them.

521
00:40:42,190 --> 00:40:43,844
The application was re-hosted.

522
00:40:44,192 --> 00:40:46,052
The database was replatformed.

523
00:40:46,670 --> 00:40:52,516
Option A is not a wrong description of the migration, it is a wrong answer to the question that was asked.

524
00:40:52,910 --> 00:40:58,180
That pattern is common in this domain because real migrations always make strategies.

525
00:40:58,702 --> 00:41:02,116
Read the last line carefully and check which component it names.

526
00:41:03,886 --> 00:41:05,060
Work scenario 2.

527
00:41:05,678 --> 00:41:14,660
An insurer must migrate an Oracle database to Amazon Aurora Postgres. It contains several hundred stored procedures written in Oracle PLSQL.

528
00:41:15,278 --> 00:41:22,592
The business requires the existing Oracle database to stay available to users throughout with only a brief cutover window at the end.

529
00:41:23,182 --> 00:41:25,764
Which two services should the insurer use?

530
00:41:26,126 --> 00:41:30,532
A Database Migration Service B schema conversion tool.

531
00:41:30,992 --> 00:41:35,300
C application migration service D Migration Hub.

532
00:41:35,726 --> 00:41:35,952
E.

533
00:41:36,366 --> 00:41:37,860
AWS Backup.

534
00:41:38,574 --> 00:41:39,232
Choose two.

535
00:41:39,694 --> 00:41:40,932
Pause and decide.

536
00:41:47,150 --> 00:41:48,772
The answers are A and B.

537
00:41:48,992 --> 00:41:57,668
Two details in the STEM do the work. Oracle to Aurora Postgres means different engines, which makes this heterogeneous and therefore a 2-tool job.

538
00:41:58,030 --> 00:42:05,572
And the several hundred stored procedures make SCT unavoidable because PLSQL will not run on Postgres unconverted.

539
00:42:06,192 --> 00:42:11,716
The stay available throughout requirement points straight at DMS continuous replication.

540
00:42:12,352 --> 00:42:20,672
Option D is the interesting distractor because migration hub genuinely would be used here. It just does not migrate anything.

541
00:42:21,646 --> 00:42:23,236
Work scenario 3.

542
00:42:23,982 --> 00:42:26,916
A manufacturer is planning to leave its data center.

543
00:42:27,950 --> 00:42:30,756
Before committing, the board wants a cost projection.

544
00:42:31,552 --> 00:42:34,148
It can compare against the current estate.

545
00:42:35,246 --> 00:42:38,244
It must be in a form the finance director can review.

546
00:42:39,182 --> 00:42:44,036
Nobody has yet produced a list of what is actually running on the 400 servers.

547
00:42:44,494 --> 00:42:46,500
Which two services should they start with?

548
00:42:47,022 --> 00:42:49,348
A Application Discovery Service,

549
00:42:49,998 --> 00:42:52,292
B Migration Evaluator,

550
00:42:53,102 --> 00:42:55,012
C Pricing Calculator,

551
00:42:55,726 --> 00:42:58,372
D application migration service,

552
00:42:59,152 --> 00:42:59,392
E.

553
00:42:59,792 --> 00:43:00,868
Cost Explorer.

554
00:43:02,318 --> 00:43:04,836
Choose to pause and decide.

555
00:43:11,022 --> 00:43:12,420
The answers are A and B.

556
00:43:12,750 --> 00:43:15,076
Two phrases place this in the assess phase.

557
00:43:15,438 --> 00:43:18,404
Before committing, and nobody has yet produced a list.

558
00:43:20,238 --> 00:43:26,884
Option C is the strongest distractor by some distance because the pricing calculator does estimate AWS costs.

559
00:43:27,726 --> 00:43:33,348
But it only prices a configuration you already know and it cannot compare against your current state.

560
00:43:34,926 --> 00:43:42,340
Migration evaluator exists specifically to make that comparison, and option E fails on a simple point of logic.

561
00:43:43,694 --> 00:43:48,324
Cost Explorer analyses AWS spend, and this company has none yet.

562
00:43:50,010 --> 00:43:51,230
Worked Scenario 4.

563
00:43:52,610 --> 00:43:56,650
During a discovery exercise, a retailer finds three forgotten systems.

564
00:43:57,646 --> 00:44:00,530
A reporting tool has had no logins for 14 months.

565
00:44:01,230 --> 00:44:07,460
A payroll system must stay on premises until a regulator approves the move, which will take at least a year.

566
00:44:08,366 --> 00:44:15,940
And a customer support picketing system is being replaced with a commercial SaaS subscription the team has already chosen.

567
00:44:16,814 --> 00:44:20,676
Which strategies apply in the order the systems are described.

568
00:44:21,390 --> 00:44:22,660
Pause and decide.

569
00:44:28,846 --> 00:44:30,384
The answer is A.

570
00:44:30,958 --> 00:44:35,044
This is the portfolio pattern from earlier in the module made explicit.

571
00:44:35,424 --> 00:44:41,184
One estate, three systems, three different strategies, and each one is signaled by a single clause.

572
00:44:41,424 --> 00:44:43,744
No logins in 14 months is retired.

573
00:44:44,046 --> 00:44:45,988
Blocked by a regulator is retained.

574
00:44:46,926 --> 00:44:50,244
Replace by a SaaS subscription is repurchase.

575
00:44:51,246 --> 00:44:54,980
Option D is the closest miss and the separator is ownership.

576
00:44:56,014 --> 00:45:00,484
Replatform keeps your software and swaps a component underneath it.

577
00:45:01,294 --> 00:45:05,668
Here the software is being abandoned altogether, which is repurchase.

578
00:45:07,342 --> 00:45:12,676
To recap, ask what happened to the application itself and match it against the seven names.

579
00:45:13,326 --> 00:45:17,828
Rehost changes nothing while replatform swaps a component for a managed service.

580
00:45:18,638 --> 00:45:24,964
Different database engines means SCT, then DMS, while the same engine means DMS alone.

581
00:45:25,902 --> 00:45:34,692
Discovery Service Inventories, Migration Evaluator, Builds the Business Case and Migration Hub tracks without moving anything.

582
00:45:35,886 --> 00:45:39,332
Next module, the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework.

583
00:45:40,814 --> 00:45:49,540
The AWS Cloud Adoption Framework is named directly in the exam guide and it turned up in 29 questions in the bank analysis.

584
00:45:50,606 --> 00:45:54,372
That is a lot for a topic most courses give 4 minutes to.

585
00:45:56,110 --> 00:46:00,710
It is also almost entirely vocabulary which makes it some of the cheapest marks available.

586
00:46:02,610 --> 00:46:09,770
Start with why AWS bothered to write this. Migrations do not usually fail because the technology did not work.

587
00:46:10,126 --> 00:46:17,444
They failed because nobody owned the decision or the operations team was never trained or finance could not approve a variable bill.

588
00:46:18,126 --> 00:46:24,772
The cloud adoption framework exists to name those organizational gaps before they sync a program.

589
00:46:26,830 --> 00:46:31,504
Once you see that, the structure of the framework stops feeling arbitrary.

590
00:46:32,814 --> 00:46:36,996
These two are the frameworks people swap, so separate them now.

591
00:46:37,806 --> 00:46:40,868
The cloud adoption framework is about the organization.

592
00:46:41,486 --> 00:46:42,304
Is it ready?

593
00:46:42,542 --> 00:46:43,504
Who owns what?

594
00:46:43,822 --> 00:46:45,188
Where are the skill gaps?

595
00:46:45,870 --> 00:46:48,324
It is used before and during a transformation.

596
00:46:48,878 --> 00:46:51,908
And its audience includes executives and HR.

597
00:46:52,704 --> 00:47:00,100
The well-architected framework is about a specific workloads design used when architects build or review a system.

598
00:47:00,544 --> 00:47:07,104
If the scenario mentions readiness, skills, stakeholders, or governance, it is CAF.

599
00:47:08,878 --> 00:47:13,572
Six perspectives and each one names a group of people who need to be in the room.

600
00:47:15,598 --> 00:47:19,460
Business makes sure cloud investment matches business objectives.

601
00:47:20,078 --> 00:47:23,876
People covers culture, skills, training, and organizational change.

602
00:47:24,526 --> 00:47:28,228
Governance manages risk, cost, compliance, and oversight.

603
00:47:29,166 --> 00:47:33,124
Platform builds the cloud environment and modernizes workloads.

604
00:47:33,424 --> 00:47:37,604
Security covers confidentiality, integrity, and availability.

605
00:47:38,094 --> 00:47:40,868
And operations runs and supports the services.

606
00:47:41,454 --> 00:47:45,956
Version 3 of the framework splits these into 47 capabilities.

607
00:47:47,214 --> 00:47:49,796
You need the six names, not the 47.

608
00:47:51,502 --> 00:47:54,788
A split that makes the six much easier to hold.

609
00:47:55,310 --> 00:48:01,860
Business, people, and governance are the business capabilities owned by executives, HR, and compliance.

610
00:48:02,144 --> 00:48:05,924
They answer should we, can we, and who decides?

611
00:48:06,704 --> 00:48:11,748
Platform, security, and operations are the technical capabilities owned by engineering.

612
00:48:12,558 --> 00:48:15,012
They answer how do we build and run it?

613
00:48:15,406 --> 00:48:24,676
Most study material covers the technical three and skims the others, which is precisely backwards, because the business three are where transformations actually stall.

614
00:48:26,126 --> 00:48:28,612
Here is the reverse lookup you use in the exam.

615
00:48:28,846 --> 00:48:32,272
Return on investment and business cases means business.

616
00:48:32,512 --> 00:48:36,672
Training, cloud fluency, and resistance to change means people.

617
00:48:38,112 --> 00:48:41,828
Cost control, risk, and oversight means governance.

618
00:48:42,592 --> 00:48:45,252
Building the landing zone means platform.

619
00:48:46,352 --> 00:48:49,072
Identity and data protection means security.

620
00:48:49,358 --> 00:48:52,032
Monitoring and service levels means operations.

621
00:48:52,192 --> 00:48:54,432
Two of these carry most of the questions.

622
00:48:54,672 --> 00:48:57,124
Anything about training or culture is people.

623
00:48:57,312 --> 00:49:00,324
Anything about cost control or risk is governance.

624
00:49:01,710 --> 00:49:07,072
The people perspective deserves its own slide because it is the one that appears most.

625
00:49:07,232 --> 00:49:09,508
It covers culture, not just training.

626
00:49:10,190 --> 00:49:13,796
Moving a team from waterfall to agile is a people matter.

627
00:49:14,766 --> 00:49:23,428
Closing skill gaps, certification paths, and cloud fluency programs all sit here, as do organizational change management and staff resistance.

628
00:49:24,110 --> 00:49:25,348
One thing worth noticing

629
00:49:25,998 --> 00:49:28,708
hiring a partner to do the work does not close.

630
00:49:29,166 --> 00:49:33,252
A people gap because your own staff still cannot run the result afterwards.

631
00:49:34,958 --> 00:49:38,500
Governance and operations sound adjacent and get swapped.

632
00:49:39,118 --> 00:49:42,052
Governance is controlled before and around the work.

633
00:49:42,574 --> 00:49:46,372
Cost management, risk, compliance, portfolio decisions.

634
00:49:47,214 --> 00:49:49,956
Operations is running the result day to day.

635
00:49:50,606 --> 00:49:54,592
Monitoring incidents, problems, service levels.

636
00:49:55,118 --> 00:49:58,032
The clean test is the question each one asks.

637
00:49:58,352 --> 00:50:01,156
Governance asks whether we should be spending this.

638
00:50:01,632 --> 00:50:05,028
Operations asks whether it is working right now.

639
00:50:05,550 --> 00:50:07,108
And be careful with one thing.

640
00:50:07,854 --> 00:50:11,620
Monitoring for threats is the security perspective, not operations.

641
00:50:13,294 --> 00:50:17,476
Four transformation domains and these describe what is actually changing.

642
00:50:18,062 --> 00:50:23,588
Technology means migrating and modernizing infrastructure, applications, and data.

643
00:50:24,270 --> 00:50:28,516
Process means digitizing and automating how the business operates.

644
00:50:30,222 --> 00:50:34,916
Organization means reorganizing teams around products rather than functions.

645
00:50:35,854 --> 00:50:40,004
And product means reimagining what the business sells.

646
00:50:41,870 --> 00:50:45,636
A single initiative usually touches all four at once.

647
00:50:46,350 --> 00:50:47,268
Note the spelling.

648
00:50:47,950 --> 00:50:55,716
AWS writes the third domain as organization with a Z because it is a proper noun in the framework.

649
00:50:57,294 --> 00:51:00,484
Four phases and they describe when things happen.

650
00:51:01,072 --> 00:51:06,852
Envision shows how cloud will accelerate business outcomes and prioritizes opportunities.

651
00:51:07,312 --> 00:51:13,028
Align finds capability gaps across the six perspectives and surfaces stakeholder concerns.

652
00:51:13,966 --> 00:51:18,788
Launch delivers pilot initiatives in production and proves incremental value.

653
00:51:20,014 --> 00:51:24,484
Scale expands those pilots and makes sure the benefits are actually realized.

654
00:51:25,870 --> 00:51:31,460
The sequence loops rather than ending because each new workload starts the cycle again.

655
00:51:32,750 --> 00:51:36,804
Three layers and three different questions, and they are not alternatives to each other.

656
00:51:37,710 --> 00:51:40,420
Six perspectives tell you who is responsible.

657
00:51:41,358 --> 00:51:43,492
Four domains tell you what is changing.

658
00:51:44,238 --> 00:51:46,980
Four phases tell you where you are in the journey.

659
00:51:47,918 --> 00:51:49,988
All three operate at once.

660
00:51:50,894 --> 00:51:54,468
The useful exam habit is to identify the layer first.

661
00:51:54,862 --> 00:51:57,712
A named group of people means a perspective.

662
00:51:58,030 --> 00:52:00,100
A type of change means a domain.

663
00:52:01,774 --> 00:52:04,292
A point in the timeline means a phase.

664
00:52:05,358 --> 00:52:08,868
Answer options rarely mix the three, which makes this quick.

665
00:52:10,574 --> 00:52:16,112
The exam guide names four business outcomes. The framework aims at so learn them as a set.

666
00:52:16,366 --> 00:52:20,612
Reduce business risk, meaning better resilience, security, and compliance.

667
00:52:21,646 --> 00:52:26,788
Improved environmental, social and governance performance, usually shortened to ESG.

668
00:52:27,790 --> 00:52:38,352
Increased revenue through new products, new markets, and faster time to market. And increased operational efficiency, meaning the same work with less effort and cost.

669
00:52:39,374 --> 00:52:45,552
ESG is the one candidates do not expect and it is named explicitly in the guide.

670
00:52:46,862 --> 00:52:48,868
Worked scenario 1.

671
00:52:49,326 --> 00:52:52,228
A logistics company has approved a cloud migration.

672
00:52:52,526 --> 00:53:04,192
Six weeks in, progress has stalled. The infrastructure team is comfortable with AWS, but the operations team has never run a cloud environment and is openly resistant. No training program exists.

673
00:53:04,814 --> 00:53:07,716
Which CAF perspective addresses this?

674
00:53:08,238 --> 00:53:09,508
A. Operations.

675
00:53:09,952 --> 00:53:10,912
B people.

676
00:53:11,598 --> 00:53:12,772
C governance.

677
00:53:13,072 --> 00:53:14,272
D platform.

678
00:53:14,606 --> 00:53:15,588
Pause and decide.

679
00:53:21,774 --> 00:53:23,792
The answer is B people.

680
00:53:24,352 --> 00:53:33,092
Option A is one of the better distractors in this course because the STEM literally says operations team and there is an operations perspective.

681
00:53:33,472 --> 00:53:35,712
But read what the gap actually is.

682
00:53:36,046 --> 00:53:38,992
Never run a cloud environment is a skills gap.

683
00:53:39,438 --> 00:53:42,052
Openly resistant is a change management gap.

684
00:53:42,990 --> 00:53:45,124
No training program is a training gap.

685
00:53:45,646 --> 00:53:46,948
All three are people.

686
00:53:47,662 --> 00:53:54,212
The operations perspective would apply if the question were about monitoring, incidents, or service levels.

687
00:53:54,766 --> 00:53:59,108
A named team in the STEM is not the same as a named perspective.

688
00:54:00,846 --> 00:54:01,892
Worked scenario.

689
00:54:02,446 --> 00:54:06,628
2. A retailer is at the very start of its cloud program.

690
00:54:07,022 --> 00:54:14,468
It has run a facilitated workshop to identify which transformation opportunities would most accelerate its business objectives.

691
00:54:15,022 --> 00:54:19,556
Each one is linked to a senior stakeholder and a measurable outcome.

692
00:54:20,014 --> 00:54:23,396
It has not yet assessed anyone's skills or built anything.

693
00:54:24,110 --> 00:54:27,076
Which CAF phase is the retailer in?

694
00:54:28,110 --> 00:54:29,092
A Envision.

695
00:54:29,392 --> 00:54:30,340
B align.

696
00:54:30,592 --> 00:54:31,748
C launch.

697
00:54:32,112 --> 00:54:33,152
D scale.

698
00:54:33,632 --> 00:54:34,788
Pause and decide.

699
00:54:40,654 --> 00:54:41,872
The answer is A.

700
00:54:42,352 --> 00:54:43,044
Envision.

701
00:54:43,758 --> 00:54:50,404
Envision and Align are the pair worth separating carefully because both happen before anything is built.

702
00:54:51,054 --> 00:54:56,516
Envision prioritizes opportunities against business objectives and ties them to stakeholders.

703
00:54:57,070 --> 00:55:00,832
Align finds the capability gaps across the six perspectives.

704
00:55:01,262 --> 00:55:04,996
The scenario plans the separator deliberately in its last sentence.

705
00:55:05,232 --> 00:55:07,492
It has not yet assessed anyone's skills.

706
00:55:07,792 --> 00:55:13,188
Skills assessment is the align activity, so if it has not happened, the retailer is still in envision.

707
00:55:14,478 --> 00:55:15,588
Worked scenario.

708
00:55:17,614 --> 00:55:20,592
3. A bank's cloud program is underway.

709
00:55:21,006 --> 00:55:26,340
Finance is concerned that teams can now provision resources without a purchase order.

710
00:55:26,894 --> 00:55:32,900
There is no agreed way to attribute spend to business units or to stop a team exceeding its allocation.

711
00:55:33,712 --> 00:55:39,748
Separately, the chief risk officer wants assurance that regulatory obligations are being tracked.

712
00:55:40,272 --> 00:55:42,692
Which perspective covers both concerns?

713
00:55:43,118 --> 00:55:47,940
A business, B operations, C governance.

714
00:55:48,366 --> 00:55:49,636
D. Security.

715
00:55:50,254 --> 00:55:51,780
Pause and decide.

716
00:55:58,126 --> 00:56:01,124
The answer is C governance.

717
00:56:01,632 --> 00:56:08,484
This scenario deliberately gives two concerns from two different executives and both land in the same perspective.

718
00:56:08,832 --> 00:56:12,932
Uncontrolled spend and no cost attribution is cost management.

719
00:56:14,432 --> 00:56:18,308
Tracking regulatory obligations is risk and compliance management.

720
00:56:19,214 --> 00:56:21,152
Both are governance capabilities.

721
00:56:21,392 --> 00:56:33,312
Option D is the trap for pattern matches. The words bank and chief risk officer make security feel right, but nothing about threats, controls, or data protection is actually described.

722
00:56:33,552 --> 00:56:37,124
Read what is being asked for, not what industry it happens in.

723
00:56:38,766 --> 00:56:40,164
Worked scenario 4.

724
00:56:40,878 --> 00:56:44,196
A publisher's board asks why it should fund a cloud program.

725
00:56:44,782 --> 00:56:48,352
The chief executive lists four expected benefits.

726
00:56:48,686 --> 00:56:53,572
Fewer outages affecting customers, lower energy consumption in its reporting.

727
00:56:54,414 --> 00:56:57,444
A new subscription product it could not build before.

728
00:56:58,222 --> 00:57:00,752
And fewer staff hours spent on manual releases.

729
00:57:00,912 --> 00:57:09,152
How many of these four are named? CAF business outcomes A1, B, 2, C, 3, B, all four.

730
00:57:09,392 --> 00:57:10,468
Pause and decide.

731
00:57:16,302 --> 00:57:17,444
The answer is D.

732
00:57:17,648 --> 00:57:18,372
All four.

733
00:57:18,688 --> 00:57:21,956
Each benefit maps onto exactly one named outcome.

734
00:57:22,288 --> 00:57:28,100
Fewer outages is reduced business risk. Lower energy consumption is improved. ESG performance.

735
00:57:28,528 --> 00:57:31,248
A new subscription product is increased revenue.

736
00:57:31,328 --> 00:57:36,036
And fewer staff hours on manual releases is increased operational efficiency.

737
00:57:36,654 --> 00:57:40,836
Option C is the near miss and it is near for a predictable reason.

738
00:57:41,168 --> 00:57:48,420
Most candidates drop the energy item because they do not expect a sustainability outcome to be named in a cloud framework.

739
00:57:48,974 --> 00:57:51,556
It is named explicitly in the exam guide.

740
00:57:53,230 --> 00:57:59,492
To recap, CAF is about the organization while well architected is about a workload.

741
00:57:59,854 --> 00:58:05,956
Six perspectives, four domains, four phases, and identifying the layer first makes the answer come quickly.

742
00:58:07,054 --> 00:58:12,132
Training and culture is always people, cost control and risk is always governance.

743
00:58:13,102 --> 00:58:17,476
Envision prioritizes opportunities while Align finds capability gaps.

744
00:58:17,902 --> 00:58:21,604
And remember that a name beam in the stem is not a name perspective.

745
00:58:21,870 --> 00:58:23,940
Read the gap, not the job title.

746
00:58:24,846 --> 00:58:29,892
Next module, the shared responsibility model and the start of the largest domain on the exam.

747
00:58:31,438 --> 00:58:36,928
This module opens domain 2, the largest domain on the exam at 30%.

748
00:58:40,366 --> 00:58:49,988
The shared responsibility model appeared in 39 questions in the bank analysis, which makes it the single most tested concept in the whole certification.

749
00:58:51,118 --> 00:58:54,448
It is also the one most people think they already know.

750
00:58:55,822 --> 00:58:59,300
Here is the sentence everything else hangs off.

751
00:59:00,014 --> 00:59:03,268
AWS is responsible for security of the cloud.

752
00:59:03,854 --> 00:59:06,244
You are responsible for security in the cloud.

753
00:59:07,008 --> 00:59:14,724
Of means the infrastructure that runs everything: buildings, hardware, networking, the virtualization layer.

754
00:59:15,566 --> 00:59:19,588
It means whatever you put on top of it and how you configure it.

755
00:59:19,808 --> 00:59:27,812
Most people can decide this. Far fewer can apply it when the question names a specific service which is where the marks actually are.

756
00:59:29,262 --> 00:59:30,468
The two halves in detail.

757
00:59:31,278 --> 00:59:33,764
AWS owns the infrastructure itself.

758
00:59:44,558 --> 00:59:46,928
You own everything you put on it.

759
00:59:47,470 --> 00:59:56,448
Your data and how it is classified, identity and access policies, operating system and firewall configuration where those exist,

760
00:59:56,718 --> 00:59:58,148
and your application code.

761
00:59:59,854 --> 01:00:01,028
A useful check.

762
01:00:03,534 --> 01:00:09,444
You can never inspect an AWS data center, and AWS can never write your bucket policy for you.

763
01:00:10,894 --> 01:00:14,596
Some things are always AWS, whatever service you use.

764
01:00:15,534 --> 01:00:22,084
Physical security of data centers, including guards, badges, cameras, and the destruction of failed drives.

765
01:00:22,894 --> 01:00:26,692
Hardware maintenance and replacement because you never touch a server.

766
01:00:27,598 --> 01:00:31,012
The global network connecting regions and availability zones.

767
01:00:31,918 --> 01:00:37,316
The hypervisor and virtualization layer that isolates customers from one another.

768
01:00:37,678 --> 01:00:41,540
And the availability of regions and availability zones themselves.

769
01:00:42,126 --> 01:00:43,620
Here is a quick test.

770
01:00:44,078 --> 01:00:48,260
If you physically could not do it from a console, it is AWS.

771
01:00:49,902 --> 01:00:51,844
And some things are always yours.

772
01:00:52,142 --> 01:00:57,604
Your data, meaning what it is, how sensitive it is, and where you allow it to live.

773
01:00:58,254 --> 01:00:59,428
Who can access it?

774
01:00:59,758 --> 01:01:05,796
Because I, AM users, groups, roles, and policies are always yours.

775
01:01:06,670 --> 01:01:10,528
Whether encryption is switched on and which keys are used.

776
01:01:11,054 --> 01:01:14,084
Client side encryption and data integrity checks.

777
01:01:14,368 --> 01:01:20,580
And your application code, including any vulnerability in it. The most important of those is the first.

778
01:01:21,742 --> 01:01:28,528
The customer is always responsible for their data, and no service, however, managed moves that line.

779
01:01:29,728 --> 01:01:34,436
Now the part that separates people who pass domain to from people who do not.

780
01:01:34,768 --> 01:01:40,548
The line between AWS and you is not in a fixed place. It moves depending on the service.

781
01:01:40,928 --> 01:01:46,084
The more managed the service, the more AWS takes on, and the less you are left holding.

782
01:01:46,768 --> 01:01:50,116
Reciting of and in will not answer these questions.

783
01:01:50,368 --> 01:01:54,628
You have to know which side a specific task falls on for a specific service.

784
01:01:56,174 --> 01:01:58,750
This table is the module in one slide.

785
01:01:59,566 --> 01:02:02,116
Look at the right hand column and notice two things.

786
01:02:02,510 --> 01:02:07,908
Your data and who can access it appear in every single row without exception.

787
01:02:08,622 --> 01:02:12,452
But guest operating system patching appears only once on EC2.

788
01:02:13,038 --> 01:02:14,052
That is the pattern.

789
01:02:15,190 --> 01:02:18,596
Managed services take the operating system away from you.

790
01:02:19,246 --> 01:02:23,236
They never take your data or your access control away from you.

791
01:02:23,886 --> 01:02:28,068
If you remember nothing else from this module, remember reading down that column.

792
01:02:29,806 --> 01:02:40,612
This is the comparison the exam returns to most often. On EC2, AWS patches the hypervisor beneath your instance and you patch the operating system on top of it.

793
01:02:40,800 --> 01:02:44,132
If you installed a database yourself, you patch that too.

794
01:02:44,494 --> 01:02:49,924
On RDS, AWS patches both the operating system and the database engine.

795
01:02:50,638 --> 01:02:56,356
You keep control of database users, network access, and whether the data is encrypted.

796
01:02:57,358 --> 01:03:01,700
Guest operating system patching is the single clearest indicator on this exam.

797
01:03:02,222 --> 01:03:04,228
On EC2, it is yours.

798
01:03:04,782 --> 01:03:06,628
On RDS, it is not.

799
01:03:08,110 --> 01:03:13,828
EC2 and Lambda are the two ends of the range, so comparing them shows how far the line can travel.

800
01:03:14,862 --> 01:03:22,404
On EC2, you hold the operating system patching, hardening, the runtime, scaling configuration, and network rules.

801
01:03:23,278 --> 01:03:26,320
On Lambda, there is no operating system for you to touch.

802
01:03:26,670 --> 01:03:31,460
AWS patches the language runtime and AWS handles scaling entirely.

803
01:03:32,814 --> 01:03:37,988
What is left to you is your function code, the permissions you granted and your data.

804
01:03:38,862 --> 01:03:41,764
Serverless does not make you responsible for nothing.

805
01:03:43,600 --> 01:03:48,708
AWS names three controls as shared and questions ask about them directly.

806
01:03:49,200 --> 01:03:56,196
Patch management, where AWS patches the infrastructure and you patch your guest operating system and applications.

807
01:03:57,070 --> 01:04:04,612
Configuration management where AWS configures its infrastructure devices and you configure your own resources.

808
01:04:05,262 --> 01:04:10,180
And awareness and training where AWS trains its staff and you train yours.

809
01:04:10,894 --> 01:04:12,772
One clarification that matters.

810
01:04:14,606 --> 01:04:17,348
Shared does not mean split down the middle.

811
01:04:18,318 --> 01:04:23,300
It means both parties do the same kind of work, each for the layer they own.

812
01:04:24,718 --> 01:04:28,356
Encryption is the row people argue about, so let us be precise.

813
01:04:29,294 --> 01:04:31,364
AWS provides the capability.

814
01:04:31,822 --> 01:04:37,508
KMS, encryption options on services, and TLS endpoints for data in transit.

815
01:04:38,574 --> 01:04:43,300
You decide to use it because enabling encryption at rest is a customer action.

816
01:04:43,822 --> 01:04:48,388
That is exactly why encryption at rest is usually described as a shared responsibility.

817
01:04:48,720 --> 01:04:55,204
Client side encryption is entirely yours because, by definition, AWS cannot do it for you.

818
01:04:55,758 --> 01:04:59,524
And key management is yours when you use customer managed keys.

819
01:05:01,134 --> 01:05:04,400
Use this slide as a drill rather than as reading.

820
01:05:04,782 --> 01:05:07,172
Cover the right-hand column and work down.

821
01:05:07,822 --> 01:05:11,040
Destroying a failed disk drive is AWS.

822
01:05:11,982 --> 01:05:14,590
Patching an operating system on EC2 is you.

823
01:05:15,086 --> 01:05:18,400
Patching the operating system on RDS is AWS.

824
01:05:19,278 --> 01:05:21,732
Configuring a bucket policy is you.

825
01:05:22,670 --> 01:05:25,840
Keeping an availability zone available is AWS.

826
01:05:26,222 --> 01:05:29,610
Enabling multifactor authentication on the root user is you.

827
01:05:30,126 --> 01:05:32,772
Classifying which data is sensitive is U.

828
01:05:33,326 --> 01:05:37,280
And isolating one customer's instance from another's is AWS.

829
01:05:38,862 --> 01:05:42,160
This domain builds distractors in four predictable shapes.

830
01:05:42,478 --> 01:05:44,420
A true statement about the RON service.

831
01:05:45,102 --> 01:05:49,412
AWS patches the operating system is true for RDS and false for EC2.

832
01:05:50,510 --> 01:05:56,612
Something AWS does, offered as a customer duty, and physical security appears constantly here.

833
01:05:57,518 --> 01:06:02,788
An absolute such as AWS secures all customer data automatically.

834
01:06:03,438 --> 01:06:08,644
And confusing capability with action because offering encryption is not the same as applying it.

835
01:06:09,198 --> 01:06:13,892
The habit that beats all four is to name the service type before reading the options.

836
01:06:15,502 --> 01:06:16,516
Work scenario.

837
01:06:18,574 --> 01:06:19,044
1.

838
01:06:19,950 --> 01:06:26,628
A company runs a web application on Amazon EC2 instances behind an application load balancer.

839
01:06:28,398 --> 01:06:33,892
A security audit finds the operating systems are missing three months of security updates.

840
01:06:34,800 --> 01:06:42,148
The infrastructure manager tells the auditor that patching is handled by AWS as part of the shared responsibility model.

841
01:06:42,640 --> 01:06:44,560
Is the infrastructure manager correct?

842
01:06:44,846 --> 01:06:45,860
Pause and decide.

843
01:06:51,950 --> 01:06:53,060
The answer is C.

844
01:06:53,486 --> 01:07:03,908
Look at option B carefully because it is the best distractor here and it uses a real technique. It sounds specific and technical, which makes it feel authoritative.

845
01:07:04,814 --> 01:07:06,212
But it is an invention.

846
01:07:07,246 --> 01:07:10,948
An Amazon machine image is patched at the point it is published.

847
01:07:11,470 --> 01:07:15,920
From the moment you launch an instance from it, the operating system is entirely yours.

848
01:07:16,238 --> 01:07:20,388
Beware options that add a plausible sounding qualifier to a wrong answer.

849
01:07:20,654 --> 01:07:24,452
The qualifier is often there to make the wrong answer sound better informed.

850
01:07:26,062 --> 01:07:27,076
Work scenario.

851
01:07:36,560 --> 01:07:42,920
A company is moving a self-managed Postgres database from EC2 to Amazon RDS.

852
01:07:43,982 --> 01:07:47,972
The administrator asks which of her current duties she keeps.

853
01:07:48,240 --> 01:07:55,940
Her duties are applying operating system patches, applying database engine patches, and creating database users.

854
01:07:56,654 --> 01:08:00,676
Also deciding whether the storage is encrypted and replacing failed disks.

855
01:08:01,486 --> 01:08:03,556
Which two remain hers after the move?

856
01:08:05,358 --> 01:08:06,660
Pause and decide.

857
01:08:12,686 --> 01:08:14,180
The answers are C and D.

858
01:08:14,400 --> 01:08:18,212
This is the right hand column of the service table, applied directly.

859
01:08:18,720 --> 01:08:29,284
Moving to a managed service hands over the operating system and the engine, which is the whole point of RDS. It never hands over who can access the data or the decision to encrypt it.

860
01:08:29,774 --> 01:08:31,908
Option E is worth the moment.

861
01:08:32,160 --> 01:08:38,340
It is not something she loses in the move because it was never hers once the workload was on AWS at all.

862
01:08:38,800 --> 01:08:45,892
Even on EC2, AWS replaces the disks. Read the question as which remain hers, and E was never in the set.

863
01:08:47,278 --> 01:08:48,356
Worked scenario.

864
01:08:48,942 --> 01:08:57,092
3. An auditor asks a company to demonstrate the physical access controls protecting the servers running its AWS workloads.

865
01:08:57,550 --> 01:09:04,452
The auditor wants to schedule a data center visit. The company cannot arrange such a visit and must satisfy the auditor another way.

866
01:09:05,038 --> 01:09:06,020
What should it do?

867
01:09:07,886 --> 01:09:10,180
Request a visit through AWS support.

868
01:09:10,990 --> 01:09:15,200
b provide AWS compliance reports from AWS artifact.

869
01:09:15,440 --> 01:09:15,680
C.

870
01:09:16,046 --> 01:09:18,660
Run Amazon inspector against the instances.

871
01:09:19,182 --> 01:09:19,440
D.

872
01:09:19,854 --> 01:09:22,160
Enable CloudRail and supply the logs.

873
01:09:22,542 --> 01:09:23,620
Pause and decide.

874
01:09:29,840 --> 01:09:31,360
The answer is B.

875
01:09:31,920 --> 01:09:34,468
This scenario teaches something you will reuse.

876
01:09:35,200 --> 01:09:40,400
When an auditor asks about AWS's half of the model, you cannot inspect it yourself.

877
01:09:40,590 --> 01:09:43,680
So you evidence it with AWS's own audit reports.

878
01:09:43,886 --> 01:09:46,160
Those live in AWS artifact.

879
01:09:46,766 --> 01:09:53,124
Options C and D are both real security services doing real work, but both look at your half of the model.

880
01:09:53,774 --> 01:09:57,988
Inspector examines your workloads and CloudRail examines your API activity.

881
01:09:58,702 --> 01:10:02,340
Neither can see inside a data center because nothing you run can.

882
01:10:04,078 --> 01:10:06,052
Worked scenario 4.

883
01:10:06,606 --> 01:10:14,480
A company runs three workloads a legacy application on EC2, an image processing function on Lambda,

884
01:10:14,720 --> 01:10:17,380
and its customer records in Amazon S3.

885
01:10:18,286 --> 01:10:26,800
A new policy requires that all software vulnerabilities in the operating systems supporting these workloads are patched within 7 days.

886
01:10:27,120 --> 01:10:31,012
For which workload does this policy create work for the company?

887
01:10:31,758 --> 01:10:32,868
Pause and decide.

888
01:10:39,086 --> 01:10:40,260
The answer is A.

889
01:10:40,704 --> 01:10:47,172
This scenario puts three services side by side deliberately because the same policy lands differently on each.

890
01:10:47,584 --> 01:10:52,420
EC2 exposes a guest operating system, so the policy creates real work.

891
01:10:52,784 --> 01:10:57,348
Lambda has no operating system you can reach, so AWS already does this.

892
01:10:57,966 --> 01:11:03,492
S3 has no operating system exposed at all. Note what the question is really testing.

893
01:11:03,744 --> 01:11:10,596
Not whether patching matters, but whether you know that the same requirement produces different obligations depending on the service.

894
01:11:11,886 --> 01:11:13,252
That is the whole module.

895
01:11:14,926 --> 01:11:23,620
To recap, AWS secures the cloud and you secure what you put in it, but that framing is the starting point rather than the answer.

896
01:11:23,984 --> 01:11:28,356
The line moves by service, so name the service type before you read the options.

897
01:11:29,966 --> 01:11:34,624
Your data and who can access it are yours on every service without exception.

898
01:11:35,150 --> 01:11:44,004
Guest operating system patching is yours on EC2 and AWS's on the managed services, and that single row decides a great many questions.

899
01:11:45,038 --> 01:11:49,284
Next module Identity and Access Management.

900
01:11:50,702 --> 01:11:54,864
Identity and Access Management is the largest single topic in the largest domain.

901
01:11:55,054 --> 01:12:00,612
It appeared in 92 questions in the bank analysis more than any other service by a wide margin.

902
01:12:02,158 --> 01:12:04,164
The good news is that the concepts are few.

903
01:12:05,038 --> 01:12:09,892
The difficulty is that the exam separates them with precision.

904
01:12:10,222 --> 01:12:13,188
So vague understanding does not survive on tact.

905
01:12:14,638 --> 01:12:18,084
Every question in this module reduces to one sentence.

906
01:12:23,182 --> 01:12:24,388
Who is an identity?

907
01:12:24,704 --> 01:12:25,892
What is an action?

908
01:12:26,286 --> 01:12:28,292
Which resource is the thing acted upon?

909
01:12:29,070 --> 01:12:32,804
Conditions are optional constraints such as source address or time.

910
01:12:33,806 --> 01:12:39,332
When a scenario feels tangled, sort it into those four boxes and the answer usually falls out.

911
01:12:40,944 --> 01:12:43,396
Four objects and each has one job.

912
01:12:43,918 --> 01:12:49,892
A user is a single identity with long-term credentials and it can be a person or an application.

913
01:12:50,670 --> 01:12:52,772
A group is a collection of users.

914
01:12:53,486 --> 01:12:56,740
You attach policies to it, but you never sign in as a group.

915
01:12:57,646 --> 01:13:02,788
A role is an identity that is assumed temporarily with no password and no permanent keys.

916
01:13:03,502 --> 01:13:08,228
And a policy is a document that says which actions are allowed or denied on which resources.

917
01:13:09,104 --> 01:13:11,236
Users and roles are identities.

918
01:13:11,744 --> 01:13:14,180
Groups are just a way to manage users.

919
01:13:15,918 --> 01:13:18,212
This is the distinction the exam tests most.

920
01:13:18,544 --> 01:13:24,644
A user has long-term credentials, a password or access keys that persist until you change them.

921
01:13:25,390 --> 01:13:33,188
A role issues temporary credentials on demand, which then expire and it can be assumed by users, applications, or AWS services.

922
01:13:34,062 --> 01:13:36,740
Here is the rule that answers a whole family of questions.

923
01:13:37,104 --> 01:13:46,116
If an application or an AWS service needs permissions, the answer is a role. Storing access keys on an EC2 instance is always the wrong answer.

924
01:13:47,424 --> 01:13:52,996
The root user is created with the account and signs in with the email address rather than a username.

925
01:13:53,504 --> 01:13:58,628
It has complete unrestricted access and IAM policies cannot restrict it.

926
01:13:59,024 --> 01:14:04,964
That is precisely why the guidance is to create an administrative user for daily work and log the root user away.

927
01:14:05,870 --> 01:14:11,108
Delete any root access keys because there is no legitimate reason for the root user to have them.

928
01:14:12,110 --> 01:14:15,204
A small set of tasks still require the root user.

929
01:14:15,630 --> 01:14:17,956
And those are what the exam asks about.

930
01:14:19,504 --> 01:14:21,348
Here are the ones worth knowing.

931
01:14:22,030 --> 01:14:25,636
Changing the account name, email address or root password.

932
01:14:26,224 --> 01:14:28,516
Changing or cancelling the support plan.

933
01:14:28,944 --> 01:14:30,276
Closing the account.

934
01:14:30,944 --> 01:14:36,036
Restoring IAM user permissions when an administrator has locked everyone out.

935
01:14:36,864 --> 01:14:39,812
Enabling MFA delete on an S3 bucket.

936
01:14:40,718 --> 01:14:44,452
And registering as a seller in the reserved instance marketplace.

937
01:14:44,910 --> 01:14:47,844
Rather than memorizing the list, learn the shape.

938
01:14:48,590 --> 01:14:52,516
These are all account level actions, not resource level ones.

939
01:14:52,782 --> 01:14:56,004
Anything that changes the account itself needs root.

940
01:14:57,710 --> 01:15:01,348
Multifactor authentication adds a second factor to the password.

941
01:15:01,838 --> 01:15:05,796
A virtual authenticator app, a hardware token, or a pass key.

942
01:15:06,542 --> 01:15:13,700
AWS supports FIDO2 passkeys and security keys which resist phishing better than one time codes.

943
01:15:14,990 --> 01:15:16,228
Now an honest note

944
01:15:17,070 --> 01:15:22,500
Almost all study material describes root MFA as a best practice you should follow.

945
01:15:23,118 --> 01:15:28,548
Since June 2025, it is enforced for root users on every account type.

946
01:15:30,254 --> 01:15:35,236
The exam answer has not changed because enabling it is still exactly what you should do.

947
01:15:36,878 --> 01:15:39,364
A policy can live in one of two places.

948
01:15:39,920 --> 01:15:46,500
An identity-based policy is attached to a user, group, or role and says what that identity may do.

949
01:15:47,150 --> 01:15:48,452
That is the common case.

950
01:15:48,942 --> 01:15:56,580
A resource-based policy is attached to the resource itself, such as an S3 bucket, and it names the principal explicitly.

951
01:15:57,518 --> 01:16:04,068
Because it names the principal, it can grant access to another account without that account assuming a role.

952
01:16:04,974 --> 01:16:08,068
The separator is simply what the policy is attached to.

953
01:16:09,806 --> 01:16:11,940
Three types of identity-based policy.

954
01:16:12,480 --> 01:16:19,748
AWS managed policies are created and maintained by AWS, such as read-only access and administrator access.

955
01:16:20,880 --> 01:16:28,292
Customer managed policies are ones you write, reusable across many identities and versions so you can roll back.

956
01:16:29,200 --> 01:16:37,028
Inline policies are embedded in a single identity, strictly one-to-one, and they are deleted when that identity is deleted.

957
01:16:39,630 --> 01:16:48,068
The exam tests inline specifically and the giveaway phrase is a policy that applies to one particular user and nothing else.

958
01:16:49,326 --> 01:16:53,316
Policy evaluation follows a fixed order and questions depend on it.

959
01:16:53,582 --> 01:16:57,188
Every request starts denied because nothing is permitted implicitly.

960
01:16:57,360 --> 01:17:01,604
An applicable allow in any attached policy changes the result to allowed.

961
01:17:01,920 --> 01:17:04,964
Then any explicit deny anywhere overrides every allow.

962
01:17:05,870 --> 01:17:09,840
So a request succeeds only if something allowed it and nothing denied it.

963
01:17:10,080 --> 01:17:14,084
The rule to carry away is that explicit deny always wins.

964
01:17:15,054 --> 01:17:17,636
Adding another allow can never override a deny.

965
01:17:19,054 --> 01:17:24,772
Least privilege means granting only the permissions required to perform a task and nothing more.

966
01:17:25,120 --> 01:17:35,812
Start from the minimum and add rather than starting from administrator access and trimming. Use groups so permissions follow a job function rather than an individual.

967
01:17:36,782 --> 01:17:41,796
And review permissions periodically because people change roles and keep their old access.

968
01:17:42,510 --> 01:17:45,060
For the exam, this gives you a reliable tiebreaker.

969
01:17:45,520 --> 01:17:50,628
When two options both work and one grants less, the one granting less is correct.

970
01:17:52,142 --> 01:17:57,380
Plain IAM manages identity inside one AWS account.

971
01:17:57,920 --> 01:18:06,852
IAM Identity Center gives people one sign-in across many accounts and connects an existing corporate directory or identity provider.

972
01:18:07,120 --> 01:18:13,956
It used to be called AWS single sign on and older material still uses that name. So recognize both.

973
01:18:14,830 --> 01:18:17,156
The separator is the number of accounts.

974
01:18:17,934 --> 01:18:20,480
One account means IAM.

975
01:18:21,070 --> 01:18:24,960
Many accounts or an existing corporate directory means I

976
01:18:25,806 --> 01:18:27,652
AM identity center.

977
01:18:29,390 --> 01:18:32,132
This pair catches people who know both services well.

978
01:18:32,560 --> 01:18:35,760
IAM is for people who use AWS.

979
01:18:36,320 --> 01:18:42,000
Employees, administrators, and engineers signing into the console or calling the API.

980
01:18:43,566 --> 01:18:46,372
Cognito is for people who use your application.

981
01:18:47,246 --> 01:18:53,636
Customers signing up and signing in to your app or website, possibly through a social or enterprise identity provider.

982
01:18:54,000 --> 01:18:58,480
The separator is whose users they are. Your customers means Cognito.

983
01:18:59,054 --> 01:19:01,732
Your staff working inside AWS means IA.

984
01:19:02,606 --> 01:19:03,083
AM.

985
01:19:05,038 --> 01:19:13,440
Federation lets people use an identity they already have to access AWS rather than being given a new IAAM

986
01:19:13,680 --> 01:19:13,956
user.

987
01:19:15,118 --> 01:19:21,040
The corporate directory stays the source of truth, so joiners and leavers are handled in one place.

988
01:19:21,998 --> 01:19:27,780
AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory inside AWS.

989
01:19:28,686 --> 01:19:34,884
Federated users assume a role, which is why they receive temporary credentials rather than a password.

990
01:19:36,014 --> 01:19:41,860
The exam signal is a company with an existing on-premises directory that it does not want to duplicate.

991
01:19:47,790 --> 01:19:53,444
External access findings tell you which resources are shared with an outside account or with the public.

992
01:19:54,190 --> 01:20:00,868
Unused access findings tell you which roles, access keys, passwords, and permissions nobody has actually used.

993
01:20:01,774 --> 01:20:09,444
That second capability is how organizations enforce least privilege over time rather than just at the moment of granting.

994
01:20:10,062 --> 01:20:13,220
It can also generate a policy from observed cloud trail activity.

995
01:20:13,760 --> 01:20:16,804
It analyzes permissions, it does not detect threats.

996
01:20:18,382 --> 01:20:19,684
Some practical rules.

997
01:20:20,142 --> 01:20:26,244
Access keys are for programmatic access and a password is for the console and they are not interchangeable.

998
01:20:26,958 --> 01:20:30,564
Rotate access keys regularly and delete ones nobody uses.

999
01:20:30,862 --> 01:20:35,780
Never embed access keys in code in a machine image or in a public repository.

1000
01:20:36,270 --> 01:20:43,108
A password policy sets minimum length, complexity, expiry, and reuse rules for IAM users.

1001
01:20:43,408 --> 01:20:49,476
And prefer rows wherever a role is possible because then there is nothing to rotate and nothing to leak.

1002
01:20:50,734 --> 01:20:51,972
Work scenario 1.

1003
01:20:52,590 --> 01:20:57,444
An application on an Amazon EC2 instance needs to read objects from an S3 bucket.

1004
01:20:58,158 --> 01:21:06,084
A developer created an IAM user for the application, generated access keys, and wrote them into a configuration file on the instance.

1005
01:21:06,958 --> 01:21:11,888
A security review flags this as an anti-pattern. What should the company do instead?

1006
01:21:12,128 --> 01:21:14,528
a rotate the keys every 30 days.

1007
01:21:14,768 --> 01:21:23,808
B. Attach an IAM role to the instance. C. Encrypt the configuration file. D. Move the keys into an environment variable.

1008
01:21:23,982 --> 01:21:25,092
Pause and decide.

1009
01:21:31,086 --> 01:21:32,368
The answer is B.

1010
01:21:32,942 --> 01:21:35,908
Notice the structure of this question because it clickers.

1011
01:21:36,590 --> 01:21:40,292
Three of the four options accept the flaw design and try to manage it better.

1012
01:21:40,814 --> 01:21:44,548
Rotate the keys, encrypt them, hide them in an environment variable.

1013
01:21:44,910 --> 01:21:47,236
Only one option removes the flaw entirely.

1014
01:21:47,950 --> 01:21:55,012
When you see three options that mitigate and one that eliminates, the exam almost always wants the one that eliminates.

1015
01:21:55,694 --> 01:21:57,988
And the underlying rule is the one from earlier.

1016
01:21:58,990 --> 01:22:01,403
An AWS service needing permissions is a role.

1017
01:22:03,118 --> 01:22:04,260
Work scenario.

1018
01:22:04,622 --> 01:22:08,740
2. A company needs a permission set that applies to exactly one.

1019
01:22:09,518 --> 01:22:11,044
IAM user.

1020
01:22:12,622 --> 01:22:23,888
The user is a contractor with unusual access requirements. Nobody else has or will have. The security team wants the permissions removed automatically if that user is ever deleted.

1021
01:22:24,494 --> 01:22:26,372
Which policy type meets this?

1022
01:22:26,928 --> 01:22:29,668
A an AWS managed policy.

1023
01:22:30,350 --> 01:22:33,060
B a customer managed policy.

1024
01:22:33,934 --> 01:22:35,812
C. An inline policy.

1025
01:22:36,688 --> 01:22:37,008
D.

1026
01:22:37,486 --> 01:22:38,980
A resource-based policy.

1027
01:22:39,534 --> 01:22:40,676
Pause and decide.

1028
01:22:46,862 --> 01:22:48,208
The answer is C.

1029
01:22:48,782 --> 01:22:54,048
This question is carefully built because option B would work for the first requirement.

1030
01:22:54,382 --> 01:23:00,516
A customer managed policy can express any permissions you like and you could attach it to just one user.

1031
01:23:01,390 --> 01:23:03,652
What kills it is the second sentence.

1032
01:23:04,462 --> 01:23:11,556
A customer managed policy survives the deletion of the user it was attached to, leaving an orphaned policy behind.

1033
01:23:12,048 --> 01:23:14,852
Inline policies are deleted with their identity.

1034
01:23:15,534 --> 01:23:20,324
Both requirements have to be satisfied, and only one option satisfies both.

1035
01:23:26,382 --> 01:23:31,620
A user belongs to a group whose policy allows all Amazon S3 actions.

1036
01:23:31,888 --> 01:23:38,596
The user also has an inline policy explicitly denying object deletion in one particular bucket.

1037
01:23:39,310 --> 01:23:46,308
An administrator then attaches administrator access directly to the user, hoping to resolve a ticket quickly.

1038
01:23:47,406 --> 01:23:50,180
Can the user now delete objects in that bucket?

1039
01:23:51,118 --> 01:23:52,356
Pause and decide.

1040
01:23:58,510 --> 01:23:59,684
The answer is C.

1041
01:24:00,878 --> 01:24:09,092
This scenario exists to kill an intuition people bring from other systems where a broader permission or a more recent setting wins.

1042
01:24:12,462 --> 01:24:15,236
In IAM, neither is true.

1043
01:24:15,664 --> 01:24:20,304
Explicit deny always wins from any policy regardless of breadth.

1044
01:24:20,544 --> 01:24:23,876
Attaching administrator access is still only an allow.

1045
01:24:24,686 --> 01:24:28,452
And option D is worth noticing because it sounds sophisticated.

1046
01:24:29,198 --> 01:24:31,108
Evaluation is not chronological.

1047
01:24:31,344 --> 01:24:34,436
There is no last one wins rule in AAM.

1048
01:24:36,142 --> 01:24:37,508
Worked scenario 4.

1049
01:24:37,824 --> 01:24:44,388
An engineering form of 400 staff already runs Microsoft Active Directory on premises.

1050
01:24:44,704 --> 01:24:48,644
It is opening 12 AWS accounts, one per project team.

1051
01:24:48,944 --> 01:24:54,852
Staff should sign in to whichever accounts they are entitled to using their existing corporate credentials.

1052
01:24:55,758 --> 01:25:00,388
The identity team does not want to create or maintain separate credentials in each account.

1053
01:25:00,974 --> 01:25:03,184
Which service meets these requirements?

1054
01:25:03,534 --> 01:25:04,836
Pause and decide.

1055
01:25:10,734 --> 01:25:11,904
The answer is B.

1056
01:25:12,494 --> 01:25:18,308
Three separate signals in the STEM all point the same way, 12 accounts rather than one.

1057
01:25:19,214 --> 01:25:21,284
An existing corporate directory.

1058
01:25:21,966 --> 01:25:29,344
And an explicit refusal to duplicate credentials. Any one of those would suggest IAM identity center.

1059
01:25:29,504 --> 01:25:31,652
Together they make it unambiguous.

1060
01:25:32,622 --> 01:25:36,388
Option C is the population trap from earlier in the module.

1061
01:25:37,806 --> 01:25:40,868
Cognito handles your applications end users.

1062
01:25:41,742 --> 01:25:48,676
These 400 people are staff signing into AWS itself, which is a different population entirely.

1063
01:25:50,446 --> 01:25:51,844
Worked scenario 5.

1064
01:25:52,494 --> 01:25:55,492
A security team is asked two questions by an auditor.

1065
01:25:55,744 --> 01:26:01,700
First, are any S3 buckets or I AM roles accessible from outside the organization?

1066
01:26:02,224 --> 01:26:07,584
Second, which I AM roles and access keys have not been used in the last 90 days.

1067
01:26:07,744 --> 01:26:10,532
The team wants a single service that answers both.

1068
01:26:11,118 --> 01:26:13,024
A Amazon Card Duty.

1069
01:26:13,264 --> 01:26:15,184
B AWS Cloud Plane.

1070
01:26:15,424 --> 01:26:17,824
C IAM Access Analyzer.

1071
01:26:18,144 --> 01:26:18,304
D.

1072
01:26:18,798 --> 01:26:20,464
AWS Trusted Advisor.

1073
01:26:20,624 --> 01:26:21,860
Pause and decide.

1074
01:26:27,982 --> 01:26:29,264
The answer is C.

1075
01:26:29,584 --> 01:26:37,188
The auditor's two questions map exactly onto Access Analyzer's two capabilities, which is why one service can cover both.

1076
01:26:39,342 --> 01:26:43,300
External access findings answer whether anything is reachable from outside.

1077
01:26:43,918 --> 01:26:47,524
Unused access findings answer which roles and keys nobody has used.

1078
01:26:48,238 --> 01:26:49,744
Option B deserves a note.

1079
01:26:49,984 --> 01:26:56,068
Cloud Trail does hold the raw evidence for the second question, but you would have to analyze 90 days of logs yourself.

1080
01:26:56,590 --> 01:26:59,424
Access analyzer already does that analysis.

1081
01:26:59,664 --> 01:27:02,436
Prefer the service designed for the question.

1082
01:27:03,694 --> 01:27:08,356
To recap, sort every question into who, what and which resource.

1083
01:27:09,070 --> 01:27:13,584
An application or AWS service needing permissions is always a road.

1084
01:27:13,774 --> 01:27:15,780
Never access keys on an instance.

1085
01:27:16,304 --> 01:27:21,092
Explicit DINI always wins and no amount of additional allow over exit.

1086
01:27:21,966 --> 01:27:28,644
Inline means exactly one identity and deleted with it while customer managed is reusable and persists.

1087
01:27:29,262 --> 01:27:37,796
Many accounts or a corporate directory means IAM identity center while your application's customers means cognito.

1088
01:27:38,926 --> 01:27:41,860
Next module, the detection and threat services.

1089
01:27:43,182 --> 01:27:49,060
These five services appear together as options more often than any other group on the exam.

1090
01:27:49,678 --> 01:27:56,548
In the bank analysis, Shield and Guard Duty co occurred 24 times Guard Duty and Inspector 19.

1091
01:27:57,344 --> 01:28:01,508
That is not because they do similar things, it is because they sound as though they might.

1092
01:28:03,150 --> 01:28:08,036
Start with the thing that resolves more questions in this module than any service detail.

1093
01:28:08,398 --> 01:28:11,108
Everything here detects, nothing here protects.

1094
01:28:11,470 --> 01:28:21,060
Guard duty will tell you an instance is mining cryptocurrency, it will not stop the instance. Inspector will tell you software is vulnerable, it will not patch it.

1095
01:28:21,744 --> 01:28:27,652
When a question asks how to block, filter or prevent something, none of these five is the answer.

1096
01:28:28,144 --> 01:28:30,180
Protection lives in the next module.

1097
01:28:31,758 --> 01:28:36,356
Five services and five nouns. Guard duty watches behavior for active threats.

1098
01:28:36,544 --> 01:28:42,704
Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities. Macy finds sensitive data inside Amazon S3.

1099
01:28:43,104 --> 01:28:45,828
Detective investigates a finding after it appears.

1100
01:28:46,544 --> 01:28:57,604
And Security Hub aggregates findings and checks your account against security standards. Behavior, vulnerabilities, data, investigation, aggregation.

1101
01:28:57,984 --> 01:29:04,100
If you can attach the right norm to the right service, most questions in this module become straightforward.

1102
01:29:05,582 --> 01:29:08,704
Guard Duty and Inspector are the pair most often swapped.

1103
01:29:09,070 --> 01:29:18,372
Guard duty reads logs and network activity continuously, looking for unusual API calls, crypto mining, or credentials being used from somewhere improbable.

1104
01:29:19,470 --> 01:29:21,156
It finds an attack in progress.

1105
01:29:22,062 --> 01:29:31,844
Inspector scans EC2 instances, container images, and lambda functions for known software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure.

1106
01:29:32,144 --> 01:29:38,464
It finds weaknesses before anyone has used them. Attack in progress means guard duty.

1107
01:29:38,784 --> 01:29:41,220
Latent weakness means inspector.

1108
01:29:42,894 --> 01:29:45,124
Guard duty reads LOBs you already have.

1109
01:29:45,614 --> 01:29:52,356
Cloud plane, VPC, flow logs, and DNS logs without needing you to enable them separately.

1110
01:29:52,814 --> 01:29:59,844
There is nothing to install, no agent and no change to your workloads, which is why questions often pair it with a requirement for minimal effort.

1111
01:30:00,558 --> 01:30:08,804
Typical findings are crypto mining, credential use from an unusual location, reconnaissance and communication with known bad addresses.

1112
01:30:09,184 --> 01:30:14,308
The phrase unusual API calls is the single most common giveaway for guard duty.

1113
01:30:15,886 --> 01:30:23,364
Inspector scans three things Amazon EC2 instances for unpatched operating system and application vulnerabilities.

1114
01:30:25,998 --> 01:30:32,324
Container images in Amazon EC are scanned when pushed and rescanned as new vulnerabilities are published.

1115
01:30:33,454 --> 01:30:36,612
And Lambda functions including their code and dependencies.

1116
01:30:37,454 --> 01:30:42,052
It discovers eligible workloads automatically rather than waiting to be pointed at them.

1117
01:30:43,726 --> 01:30:46,116
It also reports unintended network exposure.

1118
01:30:46,574 --> 01:30:52,676
If a question mentions vulnerabilities, patching or CVEs, the answer is inspector.

1119
01:30:54,478 --> 01:30:59,556
Macy is the odd one out in this group, and knowing why makes it easy.

1120
01:31:00,046 --> 01:31:03,584
Macy is the only service here that opens your data.

1121
01:31:03,744 --> 01:31:12,144
It uses machine learning and pattern matching over Amazon S3 to find personally identifiable information and other sensitive content,

1122
01:31:12,304 --> 01:31:13,668
then classifies it.

1123
01:31:14,382 --> 01:31:18,784
Guard duty never looks inside your objects, only at behavior around them.

1124
01:31:18,944 --> 01:31:27,053
So if the words sensitive data, personally identifiable information, or S3 contents appear, the answer is Mackie.

1125
01:31:28,590 --> 01:31:34,244
Detective is the one people struggle to place because it sounds like a detection service and is not.

1126
01:31:34,704 --> 01:31:39,844
Guard duty produces findings on its own and tells you something suspicious happened.

1127
01:31:40,384 --> 01:31:46,980
Detective consumes findings and builds a linked view of related activity so you can work out root cause and scope.

1128
01:31:47,184 --> 01:31:49,956
You open detective because something has already fired.

1129
01:31:50,702 --> 01:31:52,324
The clean separator is this.

1130
01:31:52,910 --> 01:31:55,364
Detective never tells you that something is wrong.

1131
01:31:56,206 --> 01:32:00,036
It tells you why after something else already did.

1132
01:32:01,358 --> 01:32:03,780
Security Hub is the aggregation layer.

1133
01:32:03,984 --> 01:32:12,304
It collects findings from Guard Duty, Inspector, Messi, IAM, Access Analyzer, Firewall Manager, and Partner Tools

1134
01:32:12,464 --> 01:32:13,412
into one place.

1135
01:32:13,824 --> 01:32:17,860
It also runs automated best practice checks against named standards.

1136
01:32:18,624 --> 01:32:26,692
CIS, PCID, SS, and the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices.

1137
01:32:29,326 --> 01:32:34,084
It works across many accounts and regions, which is usually why a question reaches for it.

1138
01:32:35,054 --> 01:32:37,764
But aggregation is not automatically the right answer.

1139
01:32:38,144 --> 01:32:41,732
It is correct only when the requirement is a single center view.

1140
01:32:43,086 --> 01:32:47,332
An availability warning that will confuse you the moment you open the console.

1141
01:32:47,664 --> 01:32:59,140
At reinforce in June 2025, AWS renamed the original Security Hub. It is now AWS Security Hub CSPM for Cloud Security Posture Management.

1142
01:32:59,904 --> 01:33:13,344
A different and genuinely new service then took the name AWS Security Hub, reaching general availability on the 2nd of December 2025. The exam means the old one. On the exam answer security hub.

1143
01:33:15,086 --> 01:33:17,508
In the console, expect to see CSPM.

1144
01:33:18,958 --> 01:33:24,228
Here is the reverse lookup and the exam reuses this vocabulary almost word for word.

1145
01:33:24,864 --> 01:33:26,532
Unusual AP.

1146
01:33:27,104 --> 01:33:31,108
I calls or compromised credentials means guard duty.

1147
01:33:32,078 --> 01:33:37,284
Unpatched software, vulnerabilities or container images means inspector.

1148
01:33:38,224 --> 01:33:43,780
Personally identifiable information or sensitive data in S3 means messy.

1149
01:33:44,718 --> 01:33:48,544
Investigating the root cause of an existing finding means detective.

1150
01:33:49,326 --> 01:34:00,164
One view across many accounts means security hub, and resources shared externally or unused permissions means IAM access analyzer from the previous module.

1151
01:34:01,518 --> 01:34:05,316
It helps to see how these fit together rather than as five separate boxes.

1152
01:34:05,742 --> 01:34:10,052
Something detects, so guard duty, inspector or messy produces a finding.

1153
01:34:10,574 --> 01:34:14,756
Security Hub collects findings from every source into one place and one format.

1154
01:34:15,886 --> 01:34:19,556
Event bridge turns a finding into an event that can trigger something.

1155
01:34:20,398 --> 01:34:26,724
And then you respond by notifying a team, running a lambda function, or opening detective to investigate.

1156
01:34:27,310 --> 01:34:29,572
Detection on its own changes nothing.

1157
01:34:30,062 --> 01:34:32,708
The value is in routing a finding to a response.

1158
01:34:34,350 --> 01:34:37,988
This slide is what more marks than any other in the module.

1159
01:34:38,510 --> 01:34:40,004
Detection services tell you.

1160
01:34:53,262 --> 01:34:59,204
Answering guard duty to a question about blocking an attack is a common and expensive mistake.

1161
01:35:00,078 --> 01:35:02,212
The reliable habit is to read the verb.

1162
01:35:03,413 --> 01:35:07,173
Identify, find, discover, and alert mean detection;

1163
01:35:07,173 --> 01:35:11,940
Block, filter, prevent, and restrict mean protection.

1164
01:35:13,742 --> 01:35:23,780
Worked Scenario 1: A company's monitoring shows an EC2 instance making outbound connections to an address associated with cryptocurrency mining.

1165
01:35:24,750 --> 01:35:30,404
An IAM access key is also being used from a country where the company has no staff.

1166
01:35:31,344 --> 01:35:37,984
The team wants a service that would have identified both automatically with nothing to install on the instances.

1167
01:35:38,510 --> 01:35:45,988
A inspector B Mac key C Guard Duty D AWS config pause and decide.

1168
01:35:52,014 --> 01:35:59,613
The answer is C. Both symptoms are behaviors rather than weaknesses, which rules out inspector immediately.

1169
01:36:00,224 --> 01:36:04,068
Neither involves data content, which rules out macchi.

1170
01:36:06,190 --> 01:36:12,292
And the phrase nothing to install is doing deliberate work here because guard duty is agentless by design.

1171
01:36:13,294 --> 01:36:17,892
That phrase appears often enough to treat as a hint in its own right.

1172
01:36:18,224 --> 01:36:26,244
If a question emphasizes no agents and no changes to workloads alongside threat detection, guard duty is almost always an intended answer.

1173
01:36:27,822 --> 01:36:29,252
Worked scenario.

1174
01:36:29,934 --> 01:36:34,864
2. A healthcare company stores several million objects in Amazon S3.

1175
01:36:35,344 --> 01:36:44,164
It must identify which buckets contain patient records with personally identifiable information and classify them to satisfy a regulator.

1176
01:36:44,814 --> 01:36:48,304
It does not currently know which buckets hold regulated data.

1177
01:36:48,784 --> 01:36:50,704
Which service should it use?

1178
01:36:51,024 --> 01:36:52,388
Pause and decide.

1179
01:36:58,320 --> 01:37:06,404
The answer is B personally identifiable information plus S3 is the makey signature and it is worth learning as a pair.

1180
01:37:09,070 --> 01:37:11,556
Option C is the interesting one.

1181
01:37:11,840 --> 01:37:20,240
Security Hub is a genuinely useful service, and this company should probably run it, but it aggregates findings that other services produce.

1182
01:37:20,654 --> 01:37:26,244
If Messi is not switched on, Security Hub has nothing to aggregate about sensitive data.

1183
01:37:26,510 --> 01:37:32,036
Aggregation layers depend on the layers beneath them, so they are rarely the answer to a discovery question.

1184
01:37:33,614 --> 01:37:34,724
Work scenario 3.

1185
01:37:35,150 --> 01:37:45,380
A company runs a public web application. Its security team receives an alert that the application is being probed for SQL injection and wants to stop those requests reaching the application.

1186
01:37:46,030 --> 01:37:53,060
Separately, the team wants to be alerted in future when any AWS credential is used from an unexpected location.

1187
01:37:53,840 --> 01:37:56,164
Which two services meet these requirements?

1188
01:37:56,960 --> 01:37:58,244
Pause and decide.

1189
01:38:04,080 --> 01:38:06,340
The answers are A and B.

1190
01:38:06,800 --> 01:38:13,636
This scenario is built to test the detection and protection split directly because it puts one of each in the same question.

1191
01:38:14,160 --> 01:38:19,360
Stop those requests reaching the application is a protection verb. So WAF.

1192
01:38:20,142 --> 01:38:26,160
Be alerted when a credential is used from an unexpected location is a detection verb.

1193
01:38:26,320 --> 01:38:27,620
So guard duty.

1194
01:38:28,942 --> 01:38:34,724
Three of the five options are detection services and only one of those matches the second requirement.

1195
01:38:35,598 --> 01:38:38,916
Reading the verbs first would have narrowed this in seconds.

1196
01:38:40,686 --> 01:38:41,796
Worked scenario.

1197
01:38:42,414 --> 01:38:42,916
4.

1198
01:38:43,694 --> 01:38:51,172
A guard duty finding reports that an EC2 instance has been communicating with a known command and control address.

1199
01:38:51,758 --> 01:39:00,548
The team needs to establish how the instance was reached, which credentials were used, whether other resources were touched, and over what period.

1200
01:39:01,390 --> 01:39:06,212
They want a service that assembles this picture from existing log data.

1201
01:39:08,334 --> 01:39:09,508
Pause and decide.

1202
01:39:15,726 --> 01:39:16,900
The answer is B.

1203
01:39:17,360 --> 01:39:19,920
The structure of this question is the important part.

1204
01:39:20,718 --> 01:39:24,356
The finding already exists, so the detection job is finished.

1205
01:39:24,720 --> 01:39:27,588
Everything asked for after that is about understanding.

1206
01:39:28,206 --> 01:39:29,360
How was it reached?

1207
01:39:29,710 --> 01:39:30,800
Which credentials?

1208
01:39:31,214 --> 01:39:32,484
What else was touched?

1209
01:39:32,880 --> 01:39:33,956
Over what period?

1210
01:39:34,926 --> 01:39:39,360
That is scope and root cause, which is detect's entire purpose.

1211
01:39:39,662 --> 01:39:45,444
Once a question moves from what happened to why it happened, you have crossed from detection into investigation.

1212
01:39:46,894 --> 01:39:48,004
Worked scenario

1213
01:39:48,910 --> 01:39:56,708
5. An organization runs 40 AWS accounts, each with guard duty, inspector, and Macy enabled.

1214
01:39:58,160 --> 01:40:02,788
The security team reviews findings by signing in to each account in turn.

1215
01:40:03,680 --> 01:40:10,468
They want one view of all findings and want to measure the estate against the CIS benchmark.

1216
01:40:11,310 --> 01:40:13,668
Which service meets both requirements?

1217
01:40:13,998 --> 01:40:15,332
Pause and decide.

1218
01:40:21,134 --> 01:40:27,780
The answer is C. Compare this deliberately with scenario 2, where Security Hub was the wrong answer.

1219
01:40:28,270 --> 01:40:32,932
There, nothing was producing findings yet, so aggregation had nothing to aggregate.

1220
01:40:33,806 --> 01:40:41,156
Here, three detection services are already running across 40 accounts, and the problem is purely that nobody can see them together.

1221
01:40:41,646 --> 01:40:43,844
That is exactly when aggregation is right.

1222
01:40:44,590 --> 01:40:53,508
The second requirement confirms it because measuring against a named benchmark like CIS is a security hub standards check.

1223
01:40:55,246 --> 01:40:59,716
To recap, everything in this module detects and nothing protects.

1224
01:40:59,920 --> 01:41:01,988
So read the verb in the question.

1225
01:41:02,734 --> 01:41:08,164
Behavior is guard duty, vulnerabilities are inspector, and data content is messy.

1226
01:41:08,878 --> 01:41:13,732
Detective explains a finding that already exists and never raises one itself.

1227
01:41:14,606 --> 01:41:23,876
Security Hub is right when the requirement is one view across accounts or a named standard, and wrong when nothing is producing findings yet.

1228
01:41:26,446 --> 01:41:31,044
Next module: the protection services plus encryption and secrets.

1229
01:41:32,654 --> 01:41:36,064
The previous module covered services that tell you something is wrong.

1230
01:41:36,334 --> 01:41:43,024
This one covers services that stop it happening and services that make data useless to anyone who does get hold of it.

1231
01:41:43,374 --> 01:41:47,684
Two halves and the exam draws distractors freely across both.

1232
01:41:49,326 --> 01:41:52,100
The organizing idea for the first half is layers.

1233
01:41:52,384 --> 01:41:55,812
A volumetric flood arrives at the network and transport layers.

1234
01:41:56,144 --> 01:41:59,108
A malicious request arrives at the application layer.

1235
01:41:59,630 --> 01:42:03,204
A connection to a port is controlled at the instance or subnet.

1236
01:42:04,910 --> 01:42:08,004
Each of those has exactly one AWS answer.

1237
01:42:08,864 --> 01:42:13,156
If you can name the layer the problem lives at, you have almost named the service.

1238
01:42:14,606 --> 01:42:18,948
Five things that stop traffic, and one of them is not what it appears.

1239
01:42:19,310 --> 01:42:24,932
Shield stops volumetric denial of service attacks at the network and transport layers.

1240
01:42:25,824 --> 01:42:31,268
WAF stops malicious, HTTP requests at the application layer.

1241
01:42:32,142 --> 01:42:36,384
Security groups control traffic reaching an instance and network.

1242
01:42:36,704 --> 01:42:39,460
ACLs control traffic entering a subnet.

1243
01:42:39,984 --> 01:42:44,544
Firewall manager is the odd one out because it stops nothing itself.

1244
01:42:44,784 --> 01:42:49,476
It applies WAF and Shield rules consistently across many accounts.

1245
01:42:49,664 --> 01:42:50,980
It is a management layer.

1246
01:42:52,654 --> 01:42:54,436
Shield and WA.

1247
01:42:54,894 --> 01:43:01,892
If both protect internet-facing applications and both attach in similar places, which is why they compete as options.

1248
01:43:02,734 --> 01:43:12,644
Shield cares about how much traffic arrives. It absorbs floods at layers 3 and 4 and never reads a request. WAF cares about what the traffic says.

1249
01:43:13,344 --> 01:43:21,796
It reads every HTTP request and applies rules about SQL injection, cross-site scripting, source address or country.

1250
01:43:22,384 --> 01:43:23,876
Flooding means Shield.

1251
01:43:24,544 --> 01:43:27,424
Inspecting the request means WAF.

1252
01:43:29,166 --> 01:43:31,024
Shield comes in two tiers.

1253
01:43:31,502 --> 01:43:38,756
Standard is automatic for every AWS customer, costs nothing, needs no configuration, and cannot be switched off.

1254
01:43:39,104 --> 01:43:47,684
If a question asks what protects you from common denial of service attacks today, the answer is Shield standard and you already have it.

1255
01:43:48,622 --> 01:43:53,572
Advanced is a paid subscription at $3,000 a month with a one-year commitment.

1256
01:43:54,094 --> 01:44:02,692
It adds the Shield Response Team cost protection for scaling during an attack and covers standard WAF costs on protected resources.

1257
01:44:03,598 --> 01:44:05,604
One detail that catches people out.

1258
01:44:06,638 --> 01:44:11,844
Contacting the Shield Response Team also requires business or enterprise support.

1259
01:44:13,070 --> 01:44:16,356
In practice, WAF works through a web.

1260
01:44:16,846 --> 01:44:22,276
ACL holding rules that allow block or count requests before they reach your application.

1261
01:44:23,214 --> 01:44:28,420
AWS managed rule groups cover common threads without you writing anything yourself.

1262
01:44:29,294 --> 01:44:36,164
Rate-based rules block an address sending more than a chosen number of requests, which handles group flooding at the application layer.

1263
01:44:36,782 --> 01:44:43,504
It attaches to CloudFront, an application load balancer, API gateway, and app sync.

1264
01:44:45,326 --> 01:44:47,300
One limitation worth knowing.

1265
01:44:48,302 --> 01:44:53,092
It cannot protect an EC2 instance directly because something must sit in front.

1266
01:44:54,464 --> 01:45:05,700
Firewall manager applies protection policies centrally across every account in an organization and new accounts and resources inherit those rules automatically as they appear.

1267
01:45:06,286 --> 01:45:08,224
It manages WAF,

1268
01:45:09,038 --> 01:45:14,628
Web ACLs, Shield Advanced Protections and Security Group Policies.

1269
01:45:15,502 --> 01:45:20,356
It requires AWS organizations, so it is never the answer for a single account.

1270
01:45:20,846 --> 01:45:26,436
Note also that AWS Network Firewall is a different service and it is not on the in scope list.

1271
01:45:26,784 --> 01:45:29,572
Older question banks still use it as a distractor.

1272
01:45:31,214 --> 01:45:37,764
Now, the second half of the module it helps to be precise about what encryption is for.

1273
01:45:38,670 --> 01:45:41,188
It does not prevent anyone getting to your data.

1274
01:45:41,710 --> 01:45:44,164
It makes what they get useless without the key.

1275
01:45:44,942 --> 01:45:53,636
That is why encryption questions are almost never about stopping access and almost always about who holds the key and whether it was switched on.

1276
01:45:55,344 --> 01:46:00,196
Data is either sitting still or moving, and the two need different mechanisms.

1277
01:46:00,624 --> 01:46:09,508
Encryption at rest covers S3 objects, EBS volumes, RDS storage and snapshots with keys usually managed through KMS.

1278
01:46:10,094 --> 01:46:14,308
You choose to enable it and it protects against someone obtaining the storage.

1279
01:46:15,086 --> 01:46:23,044
Encryption in transit means TLS between a client and a service using certificates from AWS Certificate Manager

1280
01:46:23,534 --> 01:46:25,572
and it protects against interception.

1281
01:46:26,318 --> 01:46:28,228
At least means KMS.

1282
01:46:31,694 --> 01:46:34,064
In transit means certificates.

1283
01:46:38,990 --> 01:46:44,784
KMS creates, stores, and controls the keys that encrypt data across most AWS services.

1284
01:46:45,024 --> 01:46:52,196
Keys never leave KMS unencrypted, so you call KMS to encrypt and decrypt rather than handling key material yourself.

1285
01:46:52,944 --> 01:46:59,396
Customer managed keys are ones you create and control with your own key policy and rotation settings.

1286
01:46:59,824 --> 01:47:04,548
AWS managed keys are created for you per service and need no administration.

1287
01:47:05,024 --> 01:47:10,500
And every use of a key is recorded in CloudRail, which is how you audit who decrypted what.

1288
01:47:21,664 --> 01:47:26,500
AWS operates the hardware and it integrates with most AWS services.

1289
01:47:27,054 --> 01:47:29,924
It is the right answer unless the question tells you otherwise.

1290
01:47:30,766 --> 01:47:39,904
CloudHSM gives you single tenant hardware security modules that only you can access, validated to FIPS 140-2

1291
01:47:40,064 --> 01:47:40,644
level 3.

1292
01:47:41,104 --> 01:47:47,904
Choose it only when the scenario names a regulatory requirement for dedicated hardware that you alone control.

1293
01:47:49,582 --> 01:47:57,504
AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages TLS certificates for AWS services.

1294
01:47:57,902 --> 01:48:05,572
Public certificates are free when used with integrated services such as CloudFront and Elastic Load Balancing.

1295
01:48:06,062 --> 01:48:13,156
Critically, it renews them automatically, which removes the classic outage caused by a certificate quietly expiring.

1296
01:48:14,990 --> 01:48:20,484
AWS Private CA issues private certificates for internal use.

1297
01:48:21,024 --> 01:48:24,452
And remember, it secures data in transit only.

1298
01:48:25,264 --> 01:48:28,452
It has nothing to do with encrypting data at rest.

1299
01:48:30,126 --> 01:48:34,820
This pair turns on one word, which makes it a gift when you spot it.

1300
01:48:36,718 --> 01:48:44,944
Secrets Manager has built in automatic rotation, including native rotation for RDS, Redshift, and Document

1301
01:48:45,184 --> 01:48:48,784
DB, and it charges per secret per month.

1302
01:48:49,326 --> 01:48:58,564
Parameter Store, part of Systems Manager, stores hierarchical configuration values and can encrypt them with KMS using the secure string type.

1303
01:48:59,310 --> 01:49:03,556
Its standard tier is free and it has no built-in automatic rotation.

1304
01:49:04,302 --> 01:49:06,916
Automatic rotation means secrets manager.

1305
01:49:07,822 --> 01:49:12,036
Free configuration storage means parameter store.

1306
01:49:13,454 --> 01:49:15,744
Here is the reverse lookup for the whole module.

1307
01:49:16,014 --> 01:49:18,144
A flood of traffic means shield.

1308
01:49:19,214 --> 01:49:24,868
SQL injection, cross-site scripting, or blocking a country means WAF.

1309
01:49:25,422 --> 01:49:29,348
The same rules across every account means firewall manager.

1310
01:49:30,030 --> 01:49:33,892
Encrypting stored data and auditing keys means KMS.

1311
01:49:34,830 --> 01:49:39,780
A regulator demanding dedicated single tenant hardware means CloudHSM.

1312
01:49:40,334 --> 01:49:43,236
Certificates expiring means certificate manager.

1313
01:49:43,824 --> 01:49:49,380
And database credentials that must rotate automatically means secrets manager.

1314
01:49:51,054 --> 01:49:53,156
Worked scenario 1.

1315
01:49:54,062 --> 01:49:58,372
A retailer's public website sits behind an application load balancer.

1316
01:49:59,246 --> 01:50:01,700
During a sale, it becomes unreachable.

1317
01:50:02,318 --> 01:50:04,068
Two things are happening at once.

1318
01:50:04,974 --> 01:50:14,308
A very large volume of meaningless packets saturating the connection and a separate set of requests attempting SQL injection against the checkout form.

1319
01:50:15,184 --> 01:50:18,724
The company asks what it needs and what it already has.

1320
01:50:20,014 --> 01:50:21,092
Pause and decide.

1321
01:50:27,278 --> 01:50:29,024
The answer is B.

1322
01:50:29,344 --> 01:50:33,412
Two problems at two layers, so neither service alone covers it.

1323
01:50:33,710 --> 01:50:36,804
But notice the extra clause and what it already has.

1324
01:50:37,166 --> 01:50:43,812
That is there to test whether you know Shi standard is automatic for every AWS customer at no charge.

1325
01:50:44,334 --> 01:50:50,944
Options mentioning Shield Advanced are wrong here because nothing in the scenario justifies a $3,000 subscription.

1326
01:50:51,104 --> 01:50:57,380
And option D is the detection and protection trap from the previous module sitting in this one deliberately.

1327
01:50:58,958 --> 01:51:00,196
Work scenario 2.

1328
01:51:00,464 --> 01:51:04,740
A bank must store encryption keys in hardware no other organization shares.

1329
01:51:05,134 --> 01:51:11,524
Its regulator requires proof that AWS staff cannot access the key material under any circumstances.

1330
01:51:12,046 --> 01:51:17,508
A second team in the same bank simply needs to encrypt EBS volumes with keys it can audit.

1331
01:51:18,350 --> 01:51:20,868
Which two services meet these requirements?

1332
01:51:21,422 --> 01:51:22,724
Pause and decide.

1333
01:51:28,782 --> 01:51:31,076
The answers are A and B.

1334
01:51:31,374 --> 01:51:38,500
This scenario deliberately puts both key services in the same question because the exam wants you to choose rather than default.

1335
01:51:39,726 --> 01:51:45,700
The regulator's wording that AWS staff cannot access the key material forces Cloud HSM.

1336
01:51:46,670 --> 01:51:51,812
Single tenant hardware under your exclusive control is the only thing that satisfies it.

1337
01:51:52,064 --> 01:51:59,332
The second team has an ordinary need with no such constraint and KMS handles it with full CloudRail auditing.

1338
01:51:59,904 --> 01:52:04,704
Without a stated regulatory reason, KMS is always the better answer.

1339
01:52:06,094 --> 01:52:07,588
Worked scenario 3.

1340
01:52:08,206 --> 01:52:12,452
An application reads a database password from a configuration file.

1341
01:52:13,744 --> 01:52:19,652
Policy now requires the password to change every 30 days without a human editing anything.

1342
01:52:20,750 --> 01:52:23,824
The application must always retrieve the current value.

1343
01:52:24,206 --> 01:52:27,780
The database is Amazon RDS for MySQL.

1344
01:52:28,782 --> 01:52:30,944
Which service should the company use?

1345
01:52:31,264 --> 01:52:32,836
Pause and decide.

1346
01:52:38,766 --> 01:52:40,784
The answer is B.

1347
01:52:41,422 --> 01:52:45,184
Option A is the strongest distractor in this module.

1348
01:52:45,614 --> 01:52:53,476
Parameter store genuinely stores secrets securely, encrypts them with KMS, and costs nothing in the standard tier.

1349
01:52:54,030 --> 01:52:59,140
If the requirement were only to stop keeping a password in a file, it would be a fine answer.

1350
01:52:59,886 --> 01:53:06,212
What kills it is the phrase without a human editing anything, which is the automatic rotation requirement in disguise.

1351
01:53:06,464 --> 01:53:12,996
And secrets manager rotates RDS credentials natively, updating both the database and the stored secret.

1352
01:53:16,544 --> 01:53:17,156
4.

1353
01:53:18,542 --> 01:53:27,652
A company's public website went offline for two hours because a TLS certificate expired and nobody noticed the renewal reminder.

1354
01:53:28,558 --> 01:53:33,060
The certificate was manually purchased and installed on an application load balancer.

1355
01:53:33,838 --> 01:53:36,644
The company wants to make sure this cannot happen again.

1356
01:53:37,934 --> 01:53:38,144
A

1357
01:53:39,344 --> 01:53:41,956
set a CloudWatch alarm on the expiry date.

1358
01:53:42,224 --> 01:53:42,464
B.

1359
01:53:42,926 --> 01:53:45,024
Use AWS Certificate Manager.

1360
01:53:45,344 --> 01:53:47,584
C. Store it in Secrets Manager.

1361
01:53:47,904 --> 01:53:50,084
D. Encrypt it with KMS.

1362
01:53:50,574 --> 01:53:51,684
Pause and decide.

1363
01:53:57,582 --> 01:53:58,864
The answer is B.

1364
01:53:59,662 --> 01:54:05,892
This has the same structure as the Access Keys question in module 6 and it is worth recognizing the shape.

1365
01:54:07,438 --> 01:54:10,980
Three options manage the problem better and one removes it.

1366
01:54:11,758 --> 01:54:14,308
An alarm makes the ignored reminder louder.

1367
01:54:15,214 --> 01:54:20,292
Storing the certificate elsewhere or encrypting it changes nothing about the expiry date.

1368
01:54:20,814 --> 01:54:31,172
Only certificate manager makes renewal automatic, so nobody has to act at all. When a question says, make sure this cannot happen again, look for the option that eliminates rather than monitors.

1369
01:54:32,654 --> 01:54:33,636
What scenario?

1370
01:54:37,454 --> 01:54:41,956
A company runs 60 AWS accounts under AWS organizations.

1371
01:54:43,118 --> 01:54:47,172
The security team has written a set of WAF rules.

1372
01:54:48,014 --> 01:54:54,404
Every existing and future internet facing load balancer in every account must have them applied.

1373
01:54:55,342 --> 01:55:03,172
Teams create new accounts and load balancers regularly without telling the security team which service meets this requirement.

1374
01:55:03,694 --> 01:55:04,708
Pause and decide.

1375
01:55:10,830 --> 01:55:12,416
The answer is B.

1376
01:55:12,974 --> 01:55:18,788
Three clauses in the STEM all point the same way, every existing and future resource

1377
01:55:20,718 --> 01:55:22,276
across 60 accounts

1378
01:55:22,926 --> 01:55:25,444
and created without the security team being told.

1379
01:55:25,966 --> 01:55:28,736
Any one of those weakens the manual option.

1380
01:55:28,896 --> 01:55:30,788
Together they eliminate it.

1381
01:55:31,630 --> 01:55:38,256
Option D is a sharper distractor because config genuinely would find load balancers missing a web, ACL.

1382
01:55:38,576 --> 01:55:43,588
But detecting a gap and closing it are different jobs, and the requirement here is that the rules are applied.

1383
01:55:45,038 --> 01:55:54,576
To recap, name the layer first, volume is shield, request content is WAF, and an instance or subnet is security groups and network

1384
01:55:54,736 --> 01:55:55,652
ACLs.

1385
01:55:57,614 --> 01:56:03,940
Shield standard is free and already on, while advanced costs $3,000 a month and needs a stated reason.

1386
01:56:04,896 --> 01:56:11,940
KMS is the default for keys with CloudHSM only when a regulator demands dedicated hardware.

1387
01:56:12,256 --> 01:56:16,260
At rest means KMS, while in transit means certificates.

1388
01:56:16,656 --> 01:56:19,844
And automatic rotation means secrets manager.

1389
01:56:21,390 --> 01:56:26,500
Next module, compliance and governance, which closes domain 2.

1390
01:56:28,270 --> 01:56:30,084
This module poses domain 2.

1391
01:56:30,416 --> 01:56:38,372
It covers how an organization sets boundaries across many AWS accounts and how it produces evidence when an auditor asks.

1392
01:56:38,798 --> 01:56:42,788
Those are two different jobs and the exam keeps them separate, so we will two.

1393
01:56:44,430 --> 01:56:52,996
Two words that sound interchangeable and are not. Governance is about setting boundaries in advance so that certain things cannot happen.

1394
01:56:53,456 --> 01:56:58,660
Compliance is about producing evidence afterwards that the right things did happen.

1395
01:56:58,958 --> 01:57:02,692
Organizations and Control Tower are Governance.

1396
01:57:03,502 --> 01:57:06,532
Artifact and config reports are compliance.

1397
01:57:07,406 --> 01:57:11,620
When a question asks how to prevent something, you are in governance.

1398
01:57:12,366 --> 01:57:15,684
When it asks how to prove something, you are in compliance.

1399
01:57:17,376 --> 01:57:25,924
AWS organizations lets you centrally manage many AWS accounts as one organization and the service itself costs nothing.

1400
01:57:26,256 --> 01:57:35,492
Consolidated billing gives you a single bill and importantly aggregates usage across accounts so volume discounts apply to the combined total.

1401
01:57:36,723 --> 01:57:42,916
Organizational Units Group Accounts So a policy can be applied to a whole group at once.

1402
01:57:44,942 --> 01:57:49,988
One management account pays the bill and everything else is a member account.

1403
01:57:50,990 --> 01:57:55,556
Several services require organizations to exist before you can use them.

1404
01:57:57,294 --> 01:58:02,468
Service control policies are the most misunderstood object in AWS. So be precise.

1405
01:58:03,056 --> 01:58:06,816
An SCP sets the maximum permissions available in an account.

1406
01:58:07,056 --> 01:58:08,176
It is a ceiling.

1407
01:58:08,336 --> 01:58:12,996
It never grants anything, so a user still needs an IAM policy allowing the action.

1408
01:58:13,550 --> 01:58:19,588
An action succeeds only if the SCP allows it and an IAAM policy allows it.

1409
01:58:20,014 --> 01:58:21,348
Both, not either.

1410
01:58:21,966 --> 01:58:27,940
SCPs bind even the root user of a member account, which ordinary AAAM policies cannot do.

1411
01:58:28,718 --> 01:58:31,076
And they never apply to the management account.

1412
01:58:32,686 --> 01:58:38,276
SCPs and IAM policies are constantly swapped, so hold the separator firmly.

1413
01:58:38,656 --> 01:58:42,340
An SCP is attached to an organizational unit or an account.

1414
01:58:42,576 --> 01:58:48,260
It restricts without ever granting and applies to every identity there, including the root user.

1415
01:58:49,056 --> 01:58:56,772
An IAM policy is attached to a user, group or role, both grants and denies, and applies only to that identity.

1416
01:58:57,646 --> 01:59:01,412
Restricting what an entire account can do means SCP.

1417
01:59:01,806 --> 01:59:04,743
Giving one person access means high AAM.

1418
01:59:06,414 --> 01:59:13,376
Open the organization's console today and you will see more than service control policies. So here is what the rest are.

1419
01:59:13,678 --> 01:59:16,896
Tag policies enforce consistent tagging standards.

1420
01:59:17,198 --> 01:59:20,452
Backup policies centrally manage backup plans.

1421
01:59:21,198 --> 01:59:29,476
Resource control policies added in late 2024 set a ceiling on what can be done to a resource rather than by an identity.

1422
01:59:30,414 --> 01:59:36,644
And declarative policies enforce a baseline configuration for EC2, EBS, and VPC.

1423
01:59:37,742 --> 01:59:40,708
The exam guide names service control policies only.

1424
01:59:41,614 --> 01:59:43,908
Recognizing the others is enough.

1425
01:59:45,294 --> 01:59:53,296
Control Tower sets up a multi account landing zone for you using organizations, AAM Identity Center, CloudRail,

1426
01:59:53,456 --> 01:59:54,816
and config underneath.

1427
01:59:55,216 --> 01:59:59,876
Its controls, which older material calls guardrails, come in three kinds.

1428
02:00:02,496 --> 02:00:05,604
Preventive, Detective, and Proactive.

1429
02:00:06,542 --> 02:00:08,016
Account factory provisions.

1430
02:00:08,256 --> 02:00:12,996
New accounts that already conform to your standard. Worth knowing how the pieces connect.

1431
02:00:13,376 --> 02:00:19,172
Preventive controls are implemented with SCPs and detective controls are implemented with config rules.

1432
02:00:19,536 --> 02:00:22,820
Control tower based on organizations rather than replacing it.

1433
02:00:24,334 --> 02:00:26,852
Organizations give you building blocks.

1434
02:00:27,502 --> 02:00:35,776
Accounts, organizational units, service control policies, and consolidated billing with you designing the structure.

1435
02:00:36,302 --> 02:00:46,116
Control tower gives you a landing zone already built to AWS recommendations with controls switched on and account factory ready to provision more accounts.

1436
02:00:46,670 --> 02:00:48,772
It is less flexible and far quicker.

1437
02:00:49,838 --> 02:00:51,684
The giveaway phrase is speed.

1438
02:00:52,878 --> 02:00:56,484
Quickly set up a secure multi-account environment means control tower.

1439
02:00:57,390 --> 02:01:01,252
Manage accounts and apply policies means organizations.

1440
02:01:02,830 --> 02:01:08,336
Service catalog is a curated list of approved products that users can launch for themselves.

1441
02:01:08,590 --> 02:01:15,136
A center team defines what is approved and users deploy from that list without needing broad permissions.

1442
02:01:15,376 --> 02:01:20,804
The products are cloud formation templates, so what gets deployed is consistent every time.

1443
02:01:21,856 --> 02:01:30,884
It exists to resolve the tension between letting teams move fast and keeping control. Do not confuse it with AWS Marketplace, which sells third-party software.

1444
02:01:31,376 --> 02:01:34,180
Service catalog lists your own approved items.

1445
02:01:35,790 --> 02:01:41,412
For services, all report that something is wrong and the exam makes you choose between them.

1446
02:01:42,414 --> 02:01:45,124
The difference is whose standard is being applied.

1447
02:01:45,934 --> 02:01:48,996
Config checks against rules you enable or write.

1448
02:01:49,390 --> 02:01:51,204
So your definition of compliant.

1449
02:01:52,110 --> 02:02:00,656
Trusted advisor checks against AWS best practices across cost, performance, security, fault tolerance,

1450
02:02:00,816 --> 02:02:01,924
and service quotas.

1451
02:02:04,270 --> 02:02:13,188
Security Hub checks against named security standards such as CIR as NP, CIDSS, and aggregates other services findings.

1452
02:02:13,774 --> 02:02:18,148
Control tower checks against its own controls across the landing zone.

1453
02:02:19,758 --> 02:02:25,124
AWS artifact is a self-service download of AWS compliance reports.

1454
02:02:25,646 --> 02:02:30,176
SOC, ISO, PC, I, and many others.

1455
02:02:30,496 --> 02:02:38,084
This is how you evidence AWS's half of the shared responsibility model because you cannot inspect a data center yourself.

1456
02:02:38,958 --> 02:02:45,508
It also holds agreements you can review and accept, such as a business associate addendum for healthcare data.

1457
02:02:46,446 --> 02:02:47,908
Artifact audits nothing.

1458
02:02:48,206 --> 02:02:51,588
It is a document repository, and that is the entire service.

1459
02:02:52,398 --> 02:02:57,924
Note that AWS Audit Manager is a different service and is not in scope for this exam.

1460
02:02:59,182 --> 02:03:06,980
Log questions are common and the answers are distinct. Cloud Trail records API calls and account activity. So who did what?

1461
02:03:08,174 --> 02:03:12,836
CloudWatch logs holds application and operating system logs that you send to it.

1462
02:03:13,296 --> 02:03:18,852
VPC flow logs record IP traffic going to and from network interfaces.

1463
02:03:19,598 --> 02:03:24,196
S3 server access logs record requests made to objects in a bucket.

1464
02:03:24,496 --> 02:03:31,204
And config holds configuration state over time, which is not strictly a log but often what a question is actually after.

1465
02:03:32,846 --> 02:03:38,976
What scenario? 1. A company has 90 AWS accounts under organizations.

1466
02:03:39,216 --> 02:03:48,900
Policy states that no account outside the research organizational unit may launch resources in regions outside the European Union, regardless of who is asking.

1467
02:03:49,856 --> 02:03:54,404
Several accounts have their own administrators with full IAM permissions.

1468
02:03:54,656 --> 02:03:56,576
How should the company enforce this?

1469
02:03:56,942 --> 02:03:58,052
Pause and decide.

1470
02:04:04,302 --> 02:04:05,424
The answer is B.

1471
02:04:05,584 --> 02:04:09,424
Two clauses in the STEM work together to eliminate option.

1472
02:04:09,744 --> 02:04:10,064
A.

1473
02:04:10,606 --> 02:04:16,384
Regardless of who is asking, and several accounts have administrators with full IAM permissions.

1474
02:04:16,750 --> 02:04:23,904
AAAM based control can be removed by anyone with IAM permissions, so it cannot bind an administrator.

1475
02:04:24,590 --> 02:04:28,804
An SCP sits above IAM entirely.

1476
02:04:29,422 --> 02:04:33,540
Option C is the governance and compliance pid from the opening slide.

1477
02:04:34,318 --> 02:04:38,212
Config detects afterwards, and the requirement here is prevention.

1478
02:04:39,854 --> 02:04:41,344
Worked scenario 2.

1479
02:04:41,584 --> 02:04:51,744
A healthcare provider's auditor requests evidence that physical and environmental controls at the data centers hosting its workloads meet SOC2

1480
02:04:51,904 --> 02:04:52,676
requirements.

1481
02:04:53,024 --> 02:05:01,504
The provider also needs to sign a business associate addendum with AWS and cannot visit any AWS facility.

1482
02:05:02,510 --> 02:05:04,304
Which service should it use?

1483
02:05:04,590 --> 02:05:05,892
Pause and decide.

1484
02:05:11,982 --> 02:05:13,264
The answer is B.

1485
02:05:14,144 --> 02:05:20,516
This repeats the auditor question from Module 5 deliberately and adds a second requirement to make it sharper.

1486
02:05:21,550 --> 02:05:29,668
Physical and environmental controls belong to AWS's half of the shared responsibility model, so you cannot produce that evidence yourself.

1487
02:05:30,158 --> 02:05:31,460
Artifact supplies it.

1488
02:05:32,366 --> 02:05:38,020
The business associate addendum is the second signal and agreements live in artifact 2.

1489
02:05:39,374 --> 02:05:46,052
Options A, C and D all look at your half of the model, which is why none of them can help here.

1490
02:05:47,758 --> 02:05:48,900
Worked scenario.

1491
02:05:49,550 --> 02:05:54,948
3. A company is about to expand from one AWS account to roughly 30.

1492
02:05:55,726 --> 02:06:01,156
It wants a secure multi-account environment quickly with a standard set of controls from the start.

1493
02:06:02,062 --> 02:06:06,276
It also wants centralized logging and a repeatable way to request new accounts.

1494
02:06:07,214 --> 02:06:10,436
It has no existing multi-account design and limited time.

1495
02:06:11,086 --> 02:06:12,740
Which service should it use?

1496
02:06:13,262 --> 02:06:14,596
Pause and decide.

1497
02:06:20,654 --> 02:06:22,864
The answer is B.

1498
02:06:24,174 --> 02:06:27,876
Option A is not wrong in the sense of being impossible.

1499
02:06:28,750 --> 02:06:32,036
Organizations plus handwritten policies would get there.

1500
02:06:33,070 --> 02:06:38,276
What rules it out is the combination of quickly and no existing design and limited time.

1501
02:06:39,246 --> 02:06:43,844
The exam uses those words to separate the raw capability from the opinionated setup.

1502
02:06:44,224 --> 02:06:48,676
And notice the fourth requirement, a repeatable way for teams to request new accounts.

1503
02:06:49,166 --> 02:06:52,868
That is account factory, and it belongs to control tower alone.

1504
02:06:54,414 --> 02:06:55,364
Worked scenario.

1505
02:06:55,854 --> 02:06:56,304
4.

1506
02:06:57,646 --> 02:07:06,500
A platform team wants development squads to deploy pre-approved, correctly configured environments on demand without giving them permission to create arbitrary resources.

1507
02:07:07,342 --> 02:07:14,564
Separately, the security team wants an alert whenever any deployed resource drifts from the required encryption settings.

1508
02:07:14,944 --> 02:07:17,344
Which two services meet these requirements?

1509
02:07:17,710 --> 02:07:18,852
Pause and decide.

1510
02:07:24,942 --> 02:07:26,820
The answers are A and C.

1511
02:07:27,342 --> 02:07:29,572
Two requirements, one service each.

1512
02:07:30,478 --> 02:07:34,724
Pre-approved environments deployed without broad permissions is service catalog.

1513
02:07:35,024 --> 02:07:37,904
Drift from a required setting is config.

1514
02:07:38,670 --> 02:07:45,860
Option E is the sharpest distractor here, and the separator is the one from the who checks what table.

1515
02:07:46,830 --> 02:07:50,384
Trusted advisor measures you against AWS's best practices.

1516
02:07:50,624 --> 02:07:52,868
Config measures you against your own rules.

1517
02:07:53,184 --> 02:07:59,364
The scenario says the required encryption settings, meaning the company's own standard, which is config every time.

1518
02:08:00,974 --> 02:08:07,972
To recap, and this closes domain 2, governance prevents while compliance proves. So read which one the question wants.

1519
02:08:09,966 --> 02:08:12,080
An SCP is a ceiling, never a grant.

1520
02:08:12,400 --> 02:08:15,684
The action needs both an SCP allow and an IAM allow.

1521
02:08:17,166 --> 02:08:21,956
SCPs bind the root user of a member account, but never the management account.

1522
02:08:22,862 --> 02:08:28,996
Control tower is the quick opinionated landing zone while organizations is the raw capability underneath.

1523
02:08:29,360 --> 02:08:33,604
An artifact is a repository of AWS's own audit reports.

1524
02:08:34,318 --> 02:08:37,732
Next module opens domain 3 with global infrastructure.

1525
02:08:39,470 --> 02:08:43,920
This module opens domain 3, the largest domain at 34%.

1526
02:08:44,846 --> 02:08:49,700
Global infrastructure is the vocabulary the rest of the domain assumes, so it comes first.

1527
02:08:50,478 --> 02:08:59,108
Region and availability zone co-occurred 24 times in the bank analysis, which makes it one of the most tested pairs on the exam.

1528
02:09:00,846 --> 02:09:09,380
AWS infrastructure is a hierarchy, and almost every question here is really asking which level of that hierarchy solves the stated problem.

1529
02:09:10,926 --> 02:09:12,676
A region is a geographic area.

1530
02:09:13,614 --> 02:09:15,780
Inside it are availability zones.

1531
02:09:16,398 --> 02:09:19,108
Scattered far more widely are edge locations.

1532
02:09:20,046 --> 02:09:26,660
Identify which level the requirement belongs to and the answer is usually already decided.

1533
02:09:27,918 --> 02:09:30,148
5 levels, largest to smallest.

1534
02:09:31,182 --> 02:09:35,396
A region is a geographic area containing multiple availability zones.

1535
02:09:36,590 --> 02:09:41,572
An availability zone is one or more discrete data centers isolated from the others.

1536
02:09:42,254 --> 02:09:46,948
A local zone is an extension of a region placed nearer a population center.

1537
02:09:47,886 --> 02:09:52,868
An edge location is a caching site for CloudFront, and there are far more of these than regions.

1538
02:09:53,280 --> 02:09:58,852
An AWS outposts is AWS managed hardware installed in your own data center.

1539
02:10:00,110 --> 02:10:03,236
Regions and availability zones are where you run things.

1540
02:10:03,822 --> 02:10:06,340
Edge locations are where content is cached.

1541
02:10:08,046 --> 02:10:09,380
The foundational pair.

1542
02:10:10,094 --> 02:10:15,492
A region is a geographic area named for a place containing multiple availability zones.

1543
02:10:15,950 --> 02:10:20,900
You pick one when you create most resources and your data does not leave it unless you move it.

1544
02:10:21,774 --> 02:10:28,132
An availability zone is isolated infrastructure inside that region with its own power and cooling.

1545
02:10:28,400 --> 02:10:33,924
It is physically separated from the others, but close enough for low latency private networking.

1546
02:10:34,798 --> 02:10:40,260
The separator is what the question worries about. Geography and data residency means region.

1547
02:10:41,038 --> 02:10:43,972
Surviving a failure means availability zone.

1548
02:10:45,360 --> 02:10:51,492
It is worth understanding why availability zones are designed the way they are because it explains their use.

1549
02:10:51,840 --> 02:10:57,124
Each is one or more data centers with independent power, cooling, and physical security.

1550
02:10:57,440 --> 02:11:02,180
They are separated by enough distance that one disaster is unlikely to affect two.

1551
02:11:02,990 --> 02:11:07,280
But they stay close enough for replication between them to be synchronous.

1552
02:11:07,520 --> 02:11:09,604
That trade-off is deliberate.

1553
02:11:10,000 --> 02:11:11,460
And note the last point.

1554
02:11:11,918 --> 02:11:17,988
Two instances in the same availability zone give you redundancy but not high availability.

1555
02:11:19,406 --> 02:11:24,880
This pair co-occurred 14 times in the analysis and the separator is clean.

1556
02:11:25,326 --> 02:11:31,876
Availability zones are where your workload runs and you use several of them to survive infrastructure failure.

1557
02:11:32,430 --> 02:11:39,684
Edge locations are where content is cached and you use them to reduce latency for users far from your region.

1558
02:11:40,622 --> 02:11:46,884
There are far more edge locations in many more cities, and you never deploy a workload into one.

1559
02:11:47,694 --> 02:11:50,948
High availability means availability zones.

1560
02:11:51,982 --> 02:11:55,300
Fast delivery to distant users means edge locations.

1561
02:11:56,782 --> 02:12:05,040
In practice, CloudFront caches content at edge locations so users fetch it from somewhere nearby rather than from the origin.

1562
02:12:05,742 --> 02:12:13,796
Regional edge caches sit between the edge locations and the origin, holding content too large or too infrequently requested to stay at the edge.

1563
02:12:14,766 --> 02:12:23,108
Route 53 and Global Accelerator also use the edge network, and Shivi Standard and WAF operate there when used with CloudFront.

1564
02:12:23,406 --> 02:12:24,164
One caution

1565
02:12:25,550 --> 02:12:28,388
Caching at the edge does not make your origin more available.

1566
02:12:28,640 --> 02:12:31,972
If the origin is down, anything uncached still fails.

1567
02:12:33,518 --> 02:12:36,452
Three services exist for when a region is not close enough.

1568
02:12:36,800 --> 02:12:44,804
Local zones place compute and storage nearer a large population center, giving single-digit millisecond latency to users there.

1569
02:12:45,440 --> 02:12:51,204
Outpost installs AWS managed racks in your own data center, running AWS services locally.

1570
02:12:52,110 --> 02:13:00,228
Outposts is the answer when data must physically remain on your premises or when latency to the nearest region is unacceptable.

1571
02:13:00,878 --> 02:13:02,884
Both connect back to a parent region.

1572
02:13:03,438 --> 02:13:09,636
AWS Wavelength serves fifth generation mobile networks and it is not on the in-scope list for this exam.

1573
02:13:11,360 --> 02:13:16,356
Four factors decide a region, and the exam uses them in roughly this order.

1574
02:13:16,800 --> 02:13:22,756
Compliance first, meaning data governance and legal requirements, and this one is non-negotiable.

1575
02:13:23,758 --> 02:13:28,036
Then latency, because proximity to your users costs milliseconds.

1576
02:13:28,782 --> 02:13:37,860
Then service availability, because not every service exists in every region, and finally pricing, since the same resource costs different amounts in different places.

1577
02:13:38,510 --> 02:13:44,068
If a scenario mentions a legal requirement about where data may live, compliance override as everything else.

1578
02:13:45,710 --> 02:13:49,040
How far you spread depends on what you are protecting against.

1579
02:13:49,280 --> 02:13:58,756
Multiple availability zones within one region survives the loss of a data center, and it is the standard answer for high availability with little added complexity.

1580
02:13:59,438 --> 02:14:09,668
Multiple regions survive the loss of an entire region and is used for disaster recovery for serving global users and where data residency rules demand it.

1581
02:14:10,222 --> 02:14:12,740
It costs more and adds complexity.

1582
02:14:13,422 --> 02:14:16,900
High availability means multi-availability zone.

1583
02:14:18,126 --> 02:14:21,732
Disaster recovery or global reach means multi-region.

1584
02:14:23,360 --> 02:14:28,240
Services have a scope and questions about launching in a second region depend on it.

1585
02:14:28,400 --> 02:14:33,828
Global services include IAM, Route 53, CloudFront, and organizations.

1586
02:14:34,080 --> 02:14:43,364
Regional services include EC2, VPC, RDS, DynamoDB, and Lambda. Those must be created again in each region you want them in.

1587
02:14:44,302 --> 02:14:46,212
Two details that catch people.

1588
02:14:48,206 --> 02:14:53,220
S3 bucket names are globally unique, but the bucket itself lives in one region.

1589
02:14:54,798 --> 02:15:00,580
And an Amazon machine image is regional, so copying it elsewhere is a deliberate step.

1590
02:15:01,966 --> 02:15:03,920
Six phrasings and six answers.

1591
02:15:04,160 --> 02:15:09,412
And almost every global infrastructure question is one of these dressed differently.

1592
02:15:10,094 --> 02:15:14,948
Staying available if a data center fails means multiple availability zones.

1593
02:15:17,070 --> 02:15:21,124
Surviving the loss of a whole region means multiple regions.

1594
02:15:22,062 --> 02:15:32,772
Data remaining within a country's borders means choosing the region on compliance grounds. Users worldwide complaining of slow page loads means cloud front and edge locations.

1595
02:15:33,120 --> 02:15:41,316
Data staying in your own building means outposts. And single-digit millisecond latency in one city means local zones.

1596
02:15:42,958 --> 02:15:44,324
Verb scenario 1.

1597
02:15:44,910 --> 02:15:53,124
A company runs its web application on two EC2 instances behind a load balancer, both in the same availability zone.

1598
02:15:53,998 --> 02:15:58,820
During a power event affecting that zone, the application became completely unavailable.

1599
02:15:59,694 --> 02:16:06,560
The company wants it to stay available if a single availability zone fails at the lowest reasonable cost and complexity.

1600
02:16:06,734 --> 02:16:07,652
What should it do?

1601
02:16:08,078 --> 02:16:09,348
Pause and decide.

1602
02:16:15,406 --> 02:16:16,548
The answer is B.

1603
02:16:16,976 --> 02:16:22,308
Option C is worth dwelling on because it is not wrong in the sense of failing to work.

1604
02:16:22,736 --> 02:16:26,820
A second region would certainly survive an availability zone failure.

1605
02:16:27,278 --> 02:16:29,636
It is wrong because it is disproportionate.

1606
02:16:30,510 --> 02:16:37,156
The requirement names a single availability zone failure and asks for the lowest reasonable cost and complexity.

1607
02:16:37,616 --> 02:16:41,348
Multi-region solves a bigger problem at a much higher price.

1608
02:16:42,096 --> 02:16:48,580
On this exam, the option that exactly matches the stated requirement beats the option that exceeds it.

1609
02:16:50,190 --> 02:16:51,616
Work scenario 2.

1610
02:16:51,854 --> 02:16:57,572
A German insurer must keep all customer data physically within Germany to satisfy its regulator.

1611
02:16:57,856 --> 02:17:03,492
It also wants low latency for staff in Frankfurt and Munich and would prefer the cheapest option.

1612
02:17:03,982 --> 02:17:06,308
One region satisfies the residency rule.

1613
02:17:06,576 --> 02:17:08,420
A different region is cheaper.

1614
02:17:09,230 --> 02:17:11,652
Which factor should decide the region?

1615
02:17:12,206 --> 02:17:13,284
Pause and decide.

1616
02:17:19,150 --> 02:17:20,416
The answer is C.

1617
02:17:22,510 --> 02:17:25,188
This scenario is built around two verbs.

1618
02:17:25,870 --> 02:17:30,020
Must keep data within Germany and would prefer the cheapest option.

1619
02:17:30,446 --> 02:17:33,508
One of those is a constraint and the other is a wish.

1620
02:17:33,870 --> 02:17:37,892
And constraints eliminate options before references are read at all.

1621
02:17:38,736 --> 02:17:41,764
That reading habit transfers well beyond this module.

1622
02:17:42,016 --> 02:17:47,300
Watch for must required and mandated on one hand and prefer want and would like on the other.

1623
02:17:47,616 --> 02:17:49,764
They carry very different weight in a question.

1624
02:17:51,423 --> 02:17:52,083
What Scenario?

1625
02:17:55,342 --> 02:18:00,036
A media company hosts video and images in a single United States region.

1626
02:18:00,814 --> 02:18:09,056
Users in Australia and Japan report pages taking several seconds to load, although the application performs normally and is never unavailable.

1627
02:18:09,456 --> 02:18:15,716
The company wants faster page loads for those users without moving or duplicating the application.

1628
02:18:16,270 --> 02:18:17,444
What should it use?

1629
02:18:18,382 --> 02:18:19,652
Pause and decide.

1630
02:18:25,582 --> 02:18:26,816
The answer is C.

1631
02:18:26,976 --> 02:18:31,076
The STEM plans to disqualifying details and both are easy to skim past.

1632
02:18:31,376 --> 02:18:36,676
The application is never unavailable, which rules out every availability answer including option.

1633
02:18:37,456 --> 02:18:41,444
B and without moving or duplicating the application rules out option.

1634
02:18:42,976 --> 02:18:43,296
A.

1635
02:18:44,270 --> 02:18:51,396
What remains is a pure latency problem with static content and distant users, which is the CloudFront signature.

1636
02:18:52,206 --> 02:18:55,396
Notice how much work the phrase never unavailable does.

1637
02:18:56,174 --> 02:19:00,388
Latency and availability are different problems with different answers.

1638
02:19:02,158 --> 02:19:03,332
Worked scenario.

1639
02:19:07,854 --> 02:19:13,476
A manufacturer runs machinery in a factory that must be controlled with very low latency.

1640
02:19:13,998 --> 02:19:20,036
A national regulation requires the processed data to remain physically inside the factory site.

1641
02:19:20,590 --> 02:19:28,004
The nearest AWS region is several hundred kilometers away and the company wants to use AWS services and tooling.

1642
02:19:29,774 --> 02:19:32,096
Which service meets these requirements?

1643
02:19:32,494 --> 02:19:33,828
Pause and decide.

1644
02:19:40,096 --> 02:19:41,412
The answer is B.

1645
02:19:41,856 --> 02:19:51,268
Option A is the strongest distractor because local zones genuinely are the low latency answer, and if latency were the only requirement, it would be correct.

1646
02:19:52,256 --> 02:19:54,340
The second requirement decides it.

1647
02:19:55,278 --> 02:20:02,052
The data must remain physically inside the factory site, and a local zone is an AWS facility somewhere else.

1648
02:20:02,350 --> 02:20:05,856
Only outpost puts AWS hardware in your own building.

1649
02:20:06,096 --> 02:20:13,316
When two requirements appear, test every option against both because the distractor usually satisfies one of them beautifully.

1650
02:20:14,896 --> 02:20:21,316
To recap, region is geography, availability zone is isolation, edge location is caching.

1651
02:20:22,222 --> 02:20:29,604
Multi-availability zone gives high availability while multi-region gives disaster recovery, global reach or data sovereignty.

1652
02:20:30,606 --> 02:20:35,780
Choose a region by compliance, then latency, then service availability, then price.

1653
02:20:36,526 --> 02:20:44,900
Outpost means your own building while local zones means a nearby city. And edge caching does not make the origin more available.

1654
02:20:46,318 --> 02:20:50,084
Next module the ways to deploy and operate on AWS.

1655
02:20:51,536 --> 02:20:59,300
This module covers how you actually interact with AWS and how you describe infrastructure so it can be built repeatedly.

1656
02:21:00,078 --> 02:21:05,412
It is short on services and heavy on distinctions, which is exactly how the exam treats it.

1657
02:21:07,118 --> 02:21:12,612
Start with something that makes this module simple. There is only one AWS API.

1658
02:21:13,070 --> 02:21:20,100
The management console calls it, the command line interface calls it, the software development kits call it,

1659
02:21:20,750 --> 02:21:22,180
CloudFormation calls it.

1660
02:21:23,182 --> 02:21:30,180
There are four ways of making the same request, which is why Cloud Trail can record every action regardless of how it was performed.

1661
02:21:31,310 --> 02:21:33,572
The question is never what AWS can do.

1662
02:21:34,382 --> 02:21:36,516
It is which interface suits the task.

1663
02:21:38,126 --> 02:21:42,404
Four interfaces, all producing the same API calls.

1664
02:21:43,150 --> 02:21:49,252
The management console suits exploring, learning, one-off tasks, and visual review.

1665
02:21:49,870 --> 02:21:54,084
The command line interface suits scripting and repeatable operations from a terminal.

1666
02:21:55,086 --> 02:21:59,940
The software development kits let you call AWS from inside your own application code.

1667
02:22:00,736 --> 02:22:06,564
And infrastructure as code describes a whole environment so it can be rebuilt identically.

1668
02:22:07,086 --> 02:22:11,460
CloudTrail records all four the same way because underneath they are identical.

1669
02:22:12,750 --> 02:22:18,404
The console and the command line interface do the same things and the exam separates them on repeatability.

1670
02:22:19,150 --> 02:22:21,572
The console is a person doing something once.

1671
02:22:22,414 --> 02:22:25,508
Point and click, nothing to learn, good for investigating.

1672
02:22:26,478 --> 02:22:30,372
But it is hard to repeat identically and leaves no record of the steps.

1673
02:22:31,342 --> 02:22:35,236
The command line interface is a script doing something repeatedly.

1674
02:22:35,726 --> 02:22:42,724
Commands can be saved, rerun, used in automation, and reviewed because they are just text.

1675
02:22:43,566 --> 02:22:45,700
One off means console.

1676
02:22:46,382 --> 02:22:49,056
Repeatable means CLI.

1677
02:22:50,862 --> 02:22:56,644
A software development kit is a library for a programming language that wraps the AWS API.

1678
02:22:56,896 --> 02:23:02,884
There are SDKs for Python, Java, JavaScript, .NET, Go, and others.

1679
02:23:03,216 --> 02:23:09,060
You use one when your application itself needs to call AWS rather than a person or a script.

1680
02:23:09,838 --> 02:23:18,212
Credentials still apply, and code running on AWS should use an IAM role rather than embedded keys, exactly as covered in module 6.

1681
02:23:19,456 --> 02:23:22,576
You are not expected to write any code on this exam.

1682
02:23:23,918 --> 02:23:32,164
Now the second half of the module, infrastructure as code, means describing what you want in a file rather than clicking to create it.

1683
02:23:32,656 --> 02:23:35,396
That sounds like a small change and is not.

1684
02:23:36,302 --> 02:23:42,336
Once an environment is a document, you can review it before it exists and keep it in version control.

1685
02:23:43,342 --> 02:23:48,516
You can rebuild it identically elsewhere and delete the whole thing cleanly.

1686
02:23:49,422 --> 02:23:55,652
Every benefit the exam attributes to infrastructure as code follows from that one shift.

1687
02:23:57,294 --> 02:24:00,676
CloudFormation is the infrastructure as code service.

1688
02:24:01,056 --> 02:24:07,108
You write a template in JSON or YAML describing the resources you want.

1689
02:24:07,598 --> 02:24:13,028
CloudFormation creates them as a stack in the right order, working out dependencies itself.

1690
02:24:13,838 --> 02:24:20,964
Deleting the stack removes everything it created, which is what makes temporary test environments cheap and safe.

1691
02:24:22,638 --> 02:24:26,884
Drift detection reports resources that have been changed outside the template.

1692
02:24:27,502 --> 02:24:32,580
And the service itself is free, so you pay only for the resources it creates.

1693
02:24:34,318 --> 02:24:36,516
The exam name specific benefits.

1694
02:24:36,736 --> 02:24:38,468
So learn these phrasings.

1695
02:24:39,214 --> 02:24:43,812
Repeatability, because the same template produces the same environment every time.

1696
02:24:44,430 --> 02:24:49,188
Version control, because infrastructure changes get reviewed like application changes.

1697
02:24:49,936 --> 02:24:53,668
Consistency across environments, so development matches production.

1698
02:24:54,656 --> 02:24:59,780
Faster recovery because rebuilding means running a template rather than remembering what you did.

1699
02:25:00,096 --> 02:25:05,316
And removing human error from provisioning, which is often the exact phrase in the correct answer.

1700
02:25:06,990 --> 02:25:11,376
The cloud development kit is often misunderstood as an alternative to cloud formation.

1701
02:25:11,630 --> 02:25:12,420
It is not.

1702
02:25:14,030 --> 02:25:20,176
With cloud formation, you write a declarative template describing the end state. No programming knowledge is needed.

1703
02:25:20,718 --> 02:25:27,428
With the CDK, you write TypeScript, Python, or Java using loops and conditions instead of repetition.

1704
02:25:29,326 --> 02:25:36,196
But the CDK then synthesizes a CloudFormation template, and CloudFormation does the actual provisioning.

1705
02:25:37,424 --> 02:25:40,612
Elastic Beanstop takes the opposite approach.

1706
02:25:40,864 --> 02:25:50,784
You provide application code and AWS provisions and runs the environment for you. It creates EC2 instances, a load balancer, autoscaling, and health monitoring.

1707
02:25:50,944 --> 02:25:56,260
It supports Java, .NET, PHP, Node, Python, Ruby, Go and Docker.

1708
02:25:57,102 --> 02:26:03,104
Importantly, you keep full access to the underlying resources and can adjust them if you need to.

1709
02:26:03,470 --> 02:26:08,772
The service is free and you pay for the EC2 instances and other resources it creates.

1710
02:26:10,350 --> 02:26:16,868
This is the pair the exam returns to most in this module. With CloudFormation, you describe the infrastructure.

1711
02:26:17,424 --> 02:26:23,844
Any AWS resource in any arrangement with you deciding the architecture and the input is a template.

1712
02:26:24,430 --> 02:26:27,204
With Elastic Beanstalk, you provide the application.

1713
02:26:28,078 --> 02:26:34,852
AWS decides the architecture, gives you a standard web application environment, and the input is your code.

1714
02:26:35,278 --> 02:26:38,724
Define any infrastructure as code means cloud formation.

1715
02:26:39,790 --> 02:26:44,164
Upload an application and let AWS run it means Beanstalk.

1716
02:26:45,742 --> 02:26:48,356
Three deployment models and the exam names.

1717
02:26:48,718 --> 02:26:49,380
All three.

1718
02:26:49,904 --> 02:26:54,116
Cloud means everything runs in the cloud, either built there or fully migrated.

1719
02:26:54,702 --> 02:26:59,140
Hybrid means some workloads in the cloud and some on-premises connected together.

1720
02:27:00,046 --> 02:27:05,572
On-premises means everything stays in your own data center, which is sometimes called private cloud.

1721
02:27:06,382 --> 02:27:13,664
Hybrid is the one that appears most because regulation and legacy systems keep some workloads at home for years.

1722
02:27:14,926 --> 02:27:22,756
Four services connect a hybrid environment and it helps to see them together before the networking module covers them properly.

1723
02:27:23,150 --> 02:27:29,220
Site-to-site VPN connects your network to a VPC over the Internet encrypted.

1724
02:27:29,902 --> 02:27:34,596
Direct Connect provides a dedicated private link with consistent bandwidth.

1725
02:27:35,246 --> 02:27:44,260
Outpost puts AWS hardware in your own data center, and storage gateway gives on-premises systems access to AWS storage.

1726
02:27:45,198 --> 02:27:47,780
Quick and cheap over the internet means VPM.

1727
02:27:48,910 --> 02:27:51,524
Consistent and private means direct connect.

1728
02:27:53,198 --> 02:27:54,116
Work scenario.

1729
02:27:54,143 --> 02:27:59,812
1. A company creates a test environment by hand in the console before each release.

1730
02:28:00,398 --> 02:28:04,548
Engineers report the test environment frequently differs from production in small ways.

1731
02:28:05,518 --> 02:28:09,924
A recent outage was traced to a security group rule present in one but not the other.

1732
02:28:10,830 --> 02:28:16,804
The company wants test environments that match production exactly and can be destroyed afterwards.

1733
02:28:17,326 --> 02:28:18,596
Pause and decide.

1734
02:28:24,814 --> 02:28:33,060
The answer is A. Option C is the interesting distractor because scripting the CLI is a genuine improvement over clicking in the console.

1735
02:28:33,344 --> 02:28:41,444
What it does not fix is that a person still runs the commands and can still skip one and the documentation drifts from reality over time.

1736
02:28:42,446 --> 02:28:45,284
Cloud formation removes the person from the loop entirely.

1737
02:28:45,614 --> 02:28:48,804
And option D is the governance and compliance split again.

1738
02:28:49,934 --> 02:28:52,132
Config would tell you the environments differ.

1739
02:28:52,590 --> 02:28:54,564
The company wants them not to differ.

1740
02:28:56,206 --> 02:28:57,156
Verb scenario.

1741
02:28:57,228 --> 02:29:06,020
2. A small team has written a Python web application. They want it running on AWS with a load balancer and automatic scaling.

1742
02:29:06,638 --> 02:29:11,364
Nobody has infrastructure experience and they do not want to design an architecture.

1743
02:29:13,166 --> 02:29:16,964
They do want the ability to adjust the instance type later if needed.

1744
02:29:17,838 --> 02:29:19,652
Which service should they use?

1745
02:29:20,302 --> 02:29:21,572
Pause and decide.

1746
02:29:27,790 --> 02:29:29,424
The answer is B.

1747
02:29:29,838 --> 02:29:34,276
Two clauses in the stem eliminate the two strongest alternatives, one each.

1748
02:29:34,624 --> 02:29:45,156
They do not want to design an architecture which rules out cloud formation, and they want to adjust the instance type later, which rules out lambda, since serverless has no instance type to adjust.

1749
02:29:46,062 --> 02:29:51,428
That second clause is easy to skim past and it is doing deliberate work.

1750
02:29:52,973 --> 02:30:00,944
Beanstalk sits precisely between the two, giving you a built environment while leaving the resources visible and adjustable.

1751
02:30:02,606 --> 02:30:03,812
Verb scenario 3.

1752
02:30:04,064 --> 02:30:08,900
An operations engineer must drop 300 EC2 instances across several accounts each evening.

1753
02:30:09,454 --> 02:30:11,172
They must start again each morning.

1754
02:30:11,950 --> 02:30:14,468
The instance identifiers are known and stable.

1755
02:30:14,926 --> 02:30:17,508
The task must run without anyone being present.

1756
02:30:18,126 --> 02:30:20,004
Which approach is most appropriate?

1757
02:30:20,878 --> 02:30:22,212
Cause and decide.

1758
02:30:28,304 --> 02:30:29,744
The answer is B.

1759
02:30:30,064 --> 02:30:32,144
This scenario separates two things.

1760
02:30:32,462 --> 02:30:33,828
People blur together.

1761
02:30:34,144 --> 02:30:37,664
Cloud formation describes what infrastructure should exist.

1762
02:30:38,158 --> 02:30:42,864
The CLI performs operations on infrastructure that already exist.

1763
02:30:43,694 --> 02:30:51,556
Starting and stopping instances is an operation, not a description. So option C is the wrong category rather than the wrong service.

1764
02:30:51,984 --> 02:30:58,980
And the phrase without anyone being present is the automation requirement stated plainly, which eliminates the console immediately.

1765
02:31:00,558 --> 02:31:02,148
Worked scenario 4.

1766
02:31:02,702 --> 02:31:06,180
A manufacturer is moving most workloads to AWS.

1767
02:31:06,734 --> 02:31:12,484
But a regulator requires its quality record system to stay in its own data center for 3 more years.

1768
02:31:13,006 --> 02:31:17,700
That system must exchange data continuously with applications now running in AWS.

1769
02:31:18,542 --> 02:31:23,044
The company wants consistent, predictable network performance between the two.

1770
02:31:23,662 --> 02:31:24,900
Pause and decide.

1771
02:31:31,022 --> 02:31:32,384
The answer is B.

1772
02:31:33,070 --> 02:31:35,556
This question has two halves and option.

1773
02:31:36,206 --> 02:31:39,716
D gets one of them right, which makes it the strongest distractor.

1774
02:31:41,518 --> 02:31:47,236
The deployment model is hybrid in both B and D because workloads run in both places.

1775
02:31:47,504 --> 02:31:49,444
The connection is what separates them.

1776
02:31:50,414 --> 02:31:56,388
A side-to-side VPN runs over the public internet, so its performance varies with internet conditions.

1777
02:31:57,070 --> 02:32:02,308
Direct connect is a dedicated private link, which is what consistent and predictable means.

1778
02:32:02,830 --> 02:32:05,796
Watch for two-part answers where only one part differs.

1779
02:32:07,406 --> 02:32:08,196
To recap,

1780
02:32:10,062 --> 02:32:10,532
1.

1781
02:32:11,182 --> 02:32:13,104
API with four interfaces.

1782
02:32:13,390 --> 02:32:22,948
A person means the console, a script means the CLI, an application means an SDK, cloud formation describes infrastructure while Beanstalk runs your application.

1783
02:32:23,184 --> 02:32:26,628
The CDK generates cloud formation rather than replacing it.

1784
02:32:27,374 --> 02:32:33,860
Repeatable, identical, no human error, and destroy afterwards are all cloud formation phrases.

1785
02:32:34,544 --> 02:32:40,836
And remember that describing infrastructure and operating existing infrastructure are different jobs.

1786
02:32:42,606 --> 02:32:46,660
Next module, compute, which is the largest in the course.

1787
02:32:47,904 --> 02:32:55,876
This is the largest module in the course, and compute was the largest cluster in the bank analysis at 34% of all questions.

1788
02:32:56,224 --> 02:33:04,804
It also has eight worked scenarios rather than four because compute is a domain where the skill being tested is selection rather than knowledge.

1789
02:33:06,094 --> 02:33:09,956
There is one question underneath every compute decision on this exam.

1790
02:33:10,224 --> 02:33:12,420
How much of the stack do you want to manage?

1791
02:33:12,686 --> 02:33:15,556
At one end, you run the operating system yourself.

1792
02:33:15,744 --> 02:33:20,292
At the other, you hand AWS a function and never think about a server again.

1793
02:33:21,134 --> 02:33:28,196
Every service in this module sits somewhere on that line, and the scenario always tells you where the company wants to be.

1794
02:33:29,806 --> 02:33:31,908
Here is the spectrum in one table.

1795
02:33:32,080 --> 02:33:41,540
On EC2, you manage the operating system, patching, scaling, and capacity, while AWS manages hardware and the hypervisor.

1796
02:33:42,240 --> 02:33:45,668
With containers on EC2, you still manage the hosts.

1797
02:33:46,894 --> 02:33:50,276
With Fargate, you manage only the container images.

1798
02:33:51,246 --> 02:33:56,676
With Elastic Beanstalk, you provide application code and with Lambda you provide a function and nothing else.

1799
02:33:56,960 --> 02:34:03,040
Read the middle column and watch it shorten. As it does, operational overhead falls, and so does your control.

1800
02:34:04,622 --> 02:34:11,396
EC2 provides virtual servers where you choose the operating system, the size, and the network placement.

1801
02:34:12,160 --> 02:34:19,680
You are responsible for the guest operating system and everything installed on it, exactly as covered in module life.

1802
02:34:20,270 --> 02:34:24,708
Billing is per second for most Linux instances with a 60 second minimum.

1803
02:34:24,960 --> 02:34:30,596
Stopping an instance stops the compute charge, but attached EBS storage still costs money.

1804
02:34:30,880 --> 02:34:34,020
And note the difference between stopping and terminating.

1805
02:34:37,198 --> 02:34:39,236
Stopping preserves the instance.

1806
02:34:39,886 --> 02:34:41,380
Terminating destroys it.

1807
02:34:43,086 --> 02:34:46,320
Instance families group instances by what they are good at.

1808
02:34:46,638 --> 02:34:50,052
General purpose balances compute, memory, and networking.

1809
02:34:50,958 --> 02:34:54,084
Compute optimized suits processor heavy work.

1810
02:34:54,702 --> 02:34:57,796
Memory optimized suits large and memory data sets.

1811
02:34:58,414 --> 02:35:01,252
Storage optimized suits high local disk throughput.

1812
02:35:02,030 --> 02:35:04,720
An accelerated computing provides GPS.

1813
02:35:04,960 --> 02:35:10,628
You are not expected to know type names or specifications only to match a bottleneck to a family.

1814
02:35:10,926 --> 02:35:19,556
An Amazon machine image is the template an instance launches from and it is regional, so copying it elsewhere is a deliberate step.

1815
02:35:20,782 --> 02:35:26,596
EC2 and Lambda are the two ends of the spectrum and the exam contrasts them constantly.

1816
02:35:26,926 --> 02:35:32,452
EC2 runs continuously until you stop it and supports any runtime or software.

1817
02:35:32,720 --> 02:35:36,356
It bills for the time it runs, whether you use it or not.

1818
02:35:37,134 --> 02:35:43,108
Lambda runs when something triggers it and then stops with a maximum of 15 minutes per invocation.

1819
02:35:43,886 --> 02:35:47,972
There are no servers to patch and billing is per request and duration.

1820
02:35:48,320 --> 02:35:51,760
Continuous or longer than 15 minutes means EC2.

1821
02:35:52,334 --> 02:35:54,628
Event driven and short means lambda.

1822
02:35:56,238 --> 02:36:00,560
Lambda's constraints are what decide questions, so learn them precisely.

1823
02:36:01,518 --> 02:36:06,756
15 minutes is the hard maximum for a single invocation, and longer work needs something else.

1824
02:36:07,086 --> 02:36:11,556
It scales automatically from zero to many concurrent executions with no configuration.

1825
02:36:12,494 --> 02:36:17,668
You pay nothing when it is not running, which suits spiky or infrequent workloads.

1826
02:36:17,998 --> 02:36:26,436
It is triggered by S3 uploads, API, gateway requests, event bridge schedules, queue messages, and many other sources.

1827
02:36:26,640 --> 02:36:30,948
And it is stateless, so anything that must persist goes elsewhere.

1828
02:36:32,238 --> 02:36:35,364
Three container services with three distinct jobs.

1829
02:36:35,790 --> 02:36:42,148
ECS is AWS's own container orchestrator, simpler and deeply integrated with AWS.

1830
02:36:42,510 --> 02:36:47,524
EKS is Managed Kubernetes and you choose it when Kubernetes specifically is required.

1831
02:36:48,430 --> 02:36:54,880
ECR is the registry where container images are stored and it orchestrates nothing at all.

1832
02:36:55,182 --> 02:37:06,948
Both ECS and EKS can run on EC2 instances you manage or on Fargate. Watch for ECR as a distractor in questions about running containers. It only stores images.

1833
02:37:08,686 --> 02:37:13,732
ECS and EKS both run containers and the separator is simple.

1834
02:37:14,190 --> 02:37:26,160
ECS is AWS's own orchestrator, simpler to operate, tightly integrated, and needing no Kubernetes knowledge. EKS is managed Kubernetes, giving you the standard portable Kubernetes.

1835
02:37:26,320 --> 02:37:29,348
API show existing tooling and skills transfer.

1836
02:37:29,966 --> 02:37:39,204
The exam is unusually direct here. If the scenario says Kubernetes or mentions existing Kubernetes skills or workloads, choose EKS.

1837
02:37:39,694 --> 02:37:41,892
Otherwise, ECS is the default.

1838
02:37:43,406 --> 02:37:47,568
Fargate and the EC2 launch type are two ways to run the same containers.

1839
02:37:47,982 --> 02:37:51,108
With Fargate, there are no instances to provision or patch.

1840
02:37:51,470 --> 02:37:57,380
You pay for the resources each task requests, and AWS handles capacity entirely.

1841
02:37:57,728 --> 02:38:02,084
With the EC2 launch type, you provision and patch the instances yourself.

1842
02:38:02,990 --> 02:38:08,932
You pay for them whether busy or idle and keep control over instance type and placement.

1843
02:38:09,422 --> 02:38:11,556
No servers to manage means far gate.

1844
02:38:12,048 --> 02:38:17,408
Needing host control or having capacity you already pay for means EC2.

1845
02:38:18,958 --> 02:38:23,876
Two specialized answers that appear when their exact signal is present.

1846
02:38:24,368 --> 02:38:32,288
AWS Batch runs large numbers of batch computing jobs, provisioning compute automatically and queuing the work.

1847
02:38:32,528 --> 02:38:38,884
Its signal is thousands of jobs overnight processing or scientific and financial computation.

1848
02:38:39,630 --> 02:38:50,820
Amazon LightSail is a simplified virtual private server with a predictable monthly price, bundling compute, storage, and networking with pre-configured blueprints such as WordPress.

1849
02:38:51,470 --> 02:38:57,220
Its signal is a simple application, a small team, and a wish to avoid AWS complexity.

1850
02:38:58,670 --> 02:38:59,328
EC2.

1851
02:38:59,726 --> 02:39:05,568
Autoscaling adds and removes instances to match demand within a minimum and maximum use set.

1852
02:39:05,968 --> 02:39:12,356
It also replaces unhealthy instances automatically, which makes it a reliability feature as much as a cost one.

1853
02:39:13,088 --> 02:39:20,368
Dynamic scaling responds to a metric. Scheduled scaling follows a known pattern and predictive scaling forecast demand.

1854
02:39:20,558 --> 02:39:25,604
It works with elastic load balancing so new instances receive traffic automatically.

1855
02:39:25,968 --> 02:39:31,204
And remember the rule from module 1: auto scaling changes the count, never the size.

1856
02:39:32,782 --> 02:39:37,380
Elastic load balancing has four types and two of them carry almost all the questions.

1857
02:39:37,648 --> 02:39:45,092
The application load balancer works at layer 7 with HTTP and HTTPS routing on path or hostname.

1858
02:39:45,934 --> 02:39:53,412
The network load balancer works at layer 4 with TCP and UDP for extreme performance and static IP addresses.

1859
02:39:54,510 --> 02:39:58,660
The gateway load balancer sits in front of third-party virtual appliances.

1860
02:39:59,470 --> 02:40:06,052
And the classic load balancer is the previous generation, still supported inside a VPC, but never the right answer for a new design.

1861
02:40:07,726 --> 02:40:11,844
The application and network load balancers are the pair you must separate.

1862
02:40:12,430 --> 02:40:18,212
The application load balancer understands the request and handles HTTP and HTTPS.

1863
02:40:18,528 --> 02:40:24,324
It routes on URL path, host name or header and integrates with WAF.

1864
02:40:25,168 --> 02:40:27,620
It is the default for web applications.

1865
02:40:28,590 --> 02:40:34,308
The network load balancer moves packets fast and handles TCP, UDP and TLS.

1866
02:40:34,528 --> 02:40:40,612
It provides a static IP address per availability zone. Routing on request content means ALB.

1867
02:40:40,928 --> 02:40:44,164
Static IP or extreme performance means NLB.

1868
02:40:45,838 --> 02:40:48,388
Eight phrasings covering most compute questions.

1869
02:40:48,814 --> 02:40:51,888
Run code on an event with no servers means Lambda.

1870
02:40:52,142 --> 02:40:55,076
Full control of the operating system means EC2.

1871
02:40:55,534 --> 02:40:58,660
Run containers without managing servers means Fargate.

1872
02:40:58,798 --> 02:41:00,848
Kubernetes means EKS.

1873
02:41:01,248 --> 02:41:04,132
Thousands of jobs overnight means batch.

1874
02:41:04,974 --> 02:41:08,292
A simple workload at a predictable monthly price means light sale.

1875
02:41:08,846 --> 02:41:12,004
Handling traffic increases automatically means autoscaling.

1876
02:41:12,814 --> 02:41:17,348
And deploying an application without managing infrastructure means elastic beanstalk.

1877
02:41:18,990 --> 02:41:20,068
Worked scenario.

1878
02:41:20,750 --> 02:41:21,220
1.

1879
02:41:21,902 --> 02:41:27,108
A company needs to generate a thumbnail whenever an image is uploaded to an S3 bucket.

1880
02:41:27,488 --> 02:41:32,868
Uploads arrive unpredictably, sometimes hundreds in a minute, and sometimes none for hours.

1881
02:41:33,422 --> 02:41:35,620
Each thumbnail takes about four seconds.

1882
02:41:36,558 --> 02:41:39,076
The company does not want to manage any servers.

1883
02:41:40,208 --> 02:41:41,648
Pause and decide.

1884
02:41:47,470 --> 02:41:48,496
The answer is B.

1885
02:41:48,736 --> 02:41:50,564
Three signals point the same way.

1886
02:41:50,990 --> 02:41:53,092
Triggered by an upload is event driven.

1887
02:41:53,550 --> 02:41:57,668
Unpredictable volume with quiet periods means you want to pay nothing when idle.

1888
02:41:58,096 --> 02:42:02,404
And no servers to manage rules out anything with an operating system.

1889
02:42:03,216 --> 02:42:09,060
The 4 second duration is there to confirm the work, fits inside Lambda's 15 minute limit.

1890
02:42:09,536 --> 02:42:15,812
Whenever you see an S3 upload triggering processing, Lambda is almost always the intended answer.

1891
02:42:17,550 --> 02:42:19,536
Work scenario 2.

1892
02:42:19,776 --> 02:42:30,692
A research group runs a simulation taking roughly 40 minutes per run. Each night it needs about 2000 of these runs completed with results needed by the following morning.

1893
02:42:31,216 --> 02:42:35,044
The runs are independent and can be retried if one fails.

1894
02:42:37,294 --> 02:42:39,524
Which service is most appropriate?

1895
02:42:40,046 --> 02:42:41,696
Clause and decide.

1896
02:42:47,598 --> 02:42:48,816
The answer is B.

1897
02:42:49,296 --> 02:42:51,716
Compare this deliberately with scenario 1.

1898
02:42:51,936 --> 02:42:56,772
Both describe work that must happen automatically, and the answers are different because of one number.

1899
02:42:56,976 --> 02:43:03,172
4 seconds fits inside lambda, 40 minutes does not, and 15 minutes is a hard limit rather than a guideline.

1900
02:43:03,856 --> 02:43:10,884
The other signature is the volume and independence of the jobs, which is precisely what batch exists for.

1901
02:43:11,246 --> 02:43:16,580
Whenever a duration appears in a compute question, check it against the lambda limit first.

1902
02:43:18,336 --> 02:43:20,004
Work scenario 3.

1903
02:43:20,576 --> 02:43:23,556
A company runs containerized microservices.

1904
02:43:24,016 --> 02:43:33,732
Its platform team has years of Kubernetes experience, maintains a large library of Kubernetes manifests, and wants to keep using its existing tooling.

1905
02:43:34,336 --> 02:43:37,988
It does not want to operate the Kubernetes control plane itself.

1906
02:43:38,574 --> 02:43:41,060
Which service meets these requirements?

1907
02:43:42,254 --> 02:43:43,524
Pause and decide.

1908
02:43:49,536 --> 02:43:51,076
The answer is B.

1909
02:43:51,456 --> 02:43:55,460
Two clauses eliminate the two credible alternatives, one each.

1910
02:43:56,497 --> 02:44:02,756
Existing Kubernetes manifests and tooling rules out ECS, because those manifests do not run on it.

1911
02:44:03,790 --> 02:44:09,092
And not wanting to operate the control plane rules out self-managed Kubernetes on EC2.

1912
02:44:10,016 --> 02:44:13,216
This is a common structure in compute questions.

1913
02:44:13,486 --> 02:44:16,656
Each requirement is placed to kill exactly one option.

1914
02:44:16,942 --> 02:44:21,892
So when you find a clause that seems oddly specific, look for which option it removes.

1915
02:44:23,438 --> 02:44:24,804
Work scenario 4.

1916
02:44:25,296 --> 02:44:28,836
A team already runs containers on Amazon ECS.

1917
02:44:30,414 --> 02:44:33,444
Using EC2 instances it manages.

1918
02:44:34,016 --> 02:44:40,228
Patching those instances and forecasting how many are needed has become the team's largest operational burden.

1919
02:44:40,816 --> 02:44:44,772
The workload is not changing and the team wants to keep using ECS.

1920
02:44:45,710 --> 02:44:46,576
What should it do?

1921
02:44:46,896 --> 02:44:48,580
Pause and decide.

1922
02:44:54,416 --> 02:44:55,780
The answer is B.

1923
02:44:56,238 --> 02:45:02,532
The scenario names two burdens, patching and capacity forecasting, and option B only removes one of them.

1924
02:45:03,056 --> 02:45:05,060
That is a pattern worth watching for.

1925
02:45:05,806 --> 02:45:11,524
An option that solves half the stated problem is a very effective distractor because it feels productive.

1926
02:45:12,398 --> 02:45:16,036
Fargate removes both because there are no instances at all.

1927
02:45:16,416 --> 02:45:25,316
And notice how the phrase wants to keep using ECS protects the answer from option A, which would otherwise look like a reasonable modernization.

1928
02:45:28,896 --> 02:45:29,136
A

1929
02:45:29,136 --> 02:45:31,460
charity wants to host a WordPress site.

1930
02:45:32,014 --> 02:45:40,576
It has one part time technical volunteer, needs a predictable monthly bill for its budget, and does not want to configure networking, load balancing,

1931
02:45:40,736 --> 02:45:41,412
or scaling.

1932
02:45:42,254 --> 02:45:43,812
Traffic is modest and steady.

1933
02:45:44,174 --> 02:45:45,668
Which service best fits?

1934
02:45:46,158 --> 02:45:47,172
Pause and decide.

1935
02:45:53,390 --> 02:45:54,596
The answer is B.

1936
02:45:55,214 --> 02:45:59,492
The deciding phase is predictable monthly bill, and it is worth knowing why.

1937
02:46:01,646 --> 02:46:06,276
Almost every AWS service bills by consumption, which makes the total vary.

1938
02:46:07,157 --> 02:46:14,116
Lightsail bundles compute, storage, and data transfer into a fixed monthly price, and that bundling is its defining feature.

1939
02:46:15,054 --> 02:46:23,280
Option C is the closest miss because Beanstalk also removes configuration work, but it provisions resources whose costs vary.

1940
02:46:23,440 --> 02:46:27,204
So it fails the budget requirement rather than the simplicity.

1941
02:46:30,094 --> 02:46:30,640
1.

1942
02:46:32,334 --> 02:46:33,440
Worked scenario.

1943
02:46:33,600 --> 02:46:39,140
6. An online retailer's web tier runs on EC2 instances behind a load balancer.

1944
02:46:39,440 --> 02:46:42,960
Traffic triples every weekday lunchtime and falls away overnight.

1945
02:46:43,120 --> 02:46:46,980
The company currently runs enough instances for the lunchtime peak all day.

1946
02:46:47,918 --> 02:46:51,780
It wants to reduce cost without risking availability at peak.

1947
02:46:52,654 --> 02:46:53,700
Pause and decide.

1948
02:46:59,822 --> 02:47:01,360
The answer is B.

1949
02:47:01,760 --> 02:47:06,308
Option B repeats the trap from module 1 and it is worth meeting twice.

1950
02:47:06,480 --> 02:47:12,004
Reserved instances genuinely reduce the price per hour, so the option looks financially literate.

1951
02:47:12,880 --> 02:47:21,028
But the company is running peak capacity 24 hours a day, and buying that at a discount still pays for capacity nobody uses.

1952
02:47:21,520 --> 02:47:26,500
Remove the waste first, then consider commitment pricing for whatever baseline remains.

1953
02:47:30,830 --> 02:47:31,360
Option

1954
02:47:32,910 --> 02:47:35,588
A is the size versus count distinction again.

1955
02:47:37,006 --> 02:47:38,308
Worked scenario.

1956
02:47:39,310 --> 02:47:39,840
7.

1957
02:47:41,294 --> 02:47:45,892
A company runs a multiplayer game server communicating over UDP.

1958
02:47:46,638 --> 02:47:51,140
It must distribute traffic across many instances with extremely low latency.

1959
02:47:51,950 --> 02:47:56,708
It must also present a fixed IP address that players' clients are configured to reach.

1960
02:47:57,646 --> 02:48:05,476
It also runs a separate web portal that routes requests to different services based on the URL path, which load balancers should it use.

1961
02:48:05,920 --> 02:48:08,420
Choose to, pause, and decide.

1962
02:48:14,606 --> 02:48:16,080
The answers are A and C.

1963
02:48:16,558 --> 02:48:19,492
Each half of this scenario names its layer explicitly.

1964
02:48:20,206 --> 02:48:26,628
UDP, extreme low latency, and a static IP address are three separate network load balancer signatures.

1965
02:48:27,566 --> 02:48:30,372
Routing on URL path is content-based routing.

1966
02:48:31,342 --> 02:48:36,548
That requires the load balancer to understand HTTP, so it is the application load balancer.

1967
02:48:37,006 --> 02:48:38,724
Option D is worth noting.

1968
02:48:39,822 --> 02:48:47,076
The classic load balancer would technically work for the portal, but it is the previous generation and the exam expects the current recommendation.

1969
02:48:48,782 --> 02:48:49,828
Work scenario.

1970
02:48:50,318 --> 02:48:57,057
8. A financial firm must run a legacy application, requiring a specific, unsupported operating system version.

1971
02:48:57,600 --> 02:49:02,916
It needs full administrative access to configure kernel parameters and must run continuously.

1972
02:49:03,598 --> 02:49:06,276
Which AWS compute option is suitable?

1973
02:49:06,830 --> 02:49:12,777
A Lambda, B Fargate, C EC2, B.

1974
02:49:13,774 --> 02:49:15,076
Elastic Beanstalk.

1975
02:49:15,694 --> 02:49:16,740
Pause and decide.

1976
02:49:22,734 --> 02:49:26,404
The answer is C, and this scenario closes the module deliberately.

1977
02:49:26,926 --> 02:49:28,324
Heavier the module in domain.

1978
02:49:29,518 --> 02:49:34,948
3 pushes you towards managed services because the exam usually rewards less operational overhead.

1979
02:49:35,438 --> 02:49:36,976
This one goes the other way.

1980
02:49:37,550 --> 02:49:46,148
When a requirement names kernel parameters or full administrative access, managed services are eliminated by definition.

1981
02:49:47,278 --> 02:49:50,724
Taking that access away is exactly what makes them managed.

1982
02:49:51,854 --> 02:49:55,856
Sometimes EC2 is right, and this is what that looks like.

1983
02:49:57,230 --> 02:50:03,684
To recap, ask how much of the stack the company wants to manage because the scenario always tells you.

1984
02:50:11,438 --> 02:50:16,964
Kubernetes named means EKS otherwise ECS, and ECR only stores images.

1985
02:50:17,838 --> 02:50:22,656
Fargate removes the hosts while autoscaling changes the count and never the size.

1986
02:50:23,086 --> 02:50:28,804
URL path routing means ALB, while UDP or a static IP means NLB.

1987
02:50:29,518 --> 02:50:30,176
OneNote.

1988
02:50:30,830 --> 02:50:34,500
The purchase options are taught in module 18 rather than here.

1989
02:50:35,246 --> 02:50:36,932
Next module storage.

1990
02:50:38,702 --> 02:50:42,180
Storage questions are usually decided by two things.

1991
02:50:42,830 --> 02:50:47,748
Which shape of storage the workload needs and how often the data will be read.

1992
02:50:48,398 --> 02:50:52,740
Get those two right and most of this module answers itself.

1993
02:50:54,414 --> 02:50:57,860
There are three shapes of storage and they are not interchangeable.

1994
02:50:58,336 --> 02:51:03,012
Object storage holds whole items retrieved by name over HTTP.

1995
02:51:03,616 --> 02:51:06,820
Block storage looks like a disk attached to one machine.

1996
02:51:07,406 --> 02:51:12,176
File storage looks like a shared folder that many machines can mount.

1997
02:51:12,718 --> 02:51:21,124
An application written for one cannot usually use another without being rewritten, which is why the exam leans on this distinction so heavily.

1998
02:51:22,862 --> 02:51:24,507
Three shapes and three answers.

1999
02:51:25,267 --> 02:51:30,747
Object storage means items fetched by name over HTTP which is Amazon S3.

2000
02:51:31,278 --> 02:51:36,736
Block storage means a disk attached to one machine, which is EBS or an instance store.

2001
02:51:37,136 --> 02:51:43,076
File storage means a shared folder that many machines mount, which is EFS or FSX.

2002
02:51:43,886 --> 02:51:46,948
Here is the elimination rule that earns marks.

2003
02:51:47,296 --> 02:51:55,876
If an application must mount a file system without being modified, S3 is out, however attractive its price looks.

2004
02:51:57,358 --> 02:52:00,336
In S3, objects live in buckets.

2005
02:52:00,656 --> 02:52:08,452
Bucket names are globally unique, but the bucket itself lives in one region, which is the detail from module 10 worth repeating.

2006
02:52:09,518 --> 02:52:15,076
It offers 11 9s of durability by storing data redundantly across at least 3.

2007
02:52:16,654 --> 02:52:18,180
Availability zones.

2008
02:52:19,054 --> 02:52:23,376
Storage is effectively unlimited, and a single object can be up to 5TB.

2009
02:52:24,334 --> 02:52:29,540
Versioning keeps previous copies and object lock makes objects immutable for compliance.

2010
02:52:30,702 --> 02:52:33,936
But it is not a file system and cannot be mounted.

2011
02:52:35,214 --> 02:52:37,732
Two numbers that get merged and should not be.

2012
02:52:38,222 --> 02:52:45,764
Durability asks whether the data will still exist, and for S3, that is 119s across almost every class.

2013
02:52:46,734 --> 02:52:52,772
Availability asks whether you can reach it right now, and that is where the classes genuinely differ.

2014
02:52:53,646 --> 02:52:58,336
This matters because one zone classes do not have worse durability in normal operation.

2015
02:52:58,496 --> 02:53:01,156
They store the data in a single availability zone.

2016
02:53:01,518 --> 02:53:06,500
If that zone is unavailable, you cannot reach your data, and if it is destroyed, the data is gone.

2017
02:53:07,950 --> 02:53:11,460
7 classes ordered by how often you read the data.

2018
02:53:12,016 --> 02:53:13,924
Standard for frequent access.

2019
02:53:15,982 --> 02:53:16,896
Intelligent.

2020
02:53:17,136 --> 02:53:23,524
Tiering when the access pattern is unknown or changing because AWS moves objects between tiers for you.

2021
02:53:23,856 --> 02:53:29,056
Standard infrequent access for data still needed in milliseconds but read rarely.

2022
02:53:29,582 --> 02:53:30,372
One zone.

2023
02:53:55,856 --> 02:53:56,256
Both

2024
02:53:56,256 --> 02:54:00,836
of these move data automatically, and the exam separates them on who decides.

2025
02:54:03,182 --> 02:54:03,904
Intelligent.

2026
02:54:04,526 --> 02:54:12,900
Tiering observes actual access and moves objects for you, charging a small monitoring fee per object but no retrieval fees between its tiers.

2027
02:54:13,806 --> 02:54:22,948
A life cycle policy transitions objects after a number of days that you specify with no monitoring charge, but you must know the pattern in advance.

2028
02:54:23,424 --> 02:54:26,916
Unknown or unpredictable access means intelligent.

2029
02:54:28,014 --> 02:54:28,704
Tiering.

2030
02:54:29,774 --> 02:54:32,644
A known age-based rule means a life cycle policy.

2031
02:54:34,254 --> 02:54:37,504
An EBS volume attaches to one EC2 instance.

2032
02:54:37,664 --> 02:54:47,268
It must sit in the same availability zone as that instance, which is a hard constraint. It persists independently of the instance, so terminating the instance need not destroy the volume.

2033
02:54:48,142 --> 02:54:54,756
SSD types suit databases and boot volumes, while HDD types suit large sequential throughput.

2034
02:54:55,104 --> 02:54:59,588
Volumes can be encrypted with KMS and snapshots inherit that encryption.

2035
02:55:00,078 --> 02:55:04,452
And remember that a stopped instance still builds for its attached storage.

2036
02:55:05,998 --> 02:55:11,104
EBS and instance store are both block storage and the difference is survival.

2037
02:55:11,424 --> 02:55:19,364
EBS is network attached, possessed independently of the instance, can be detached and reattached, and can be snapshotted to S3.

2038
02:55:20,384 --> 02:55:24,100
Instance store is physically attached to the host, so it is very fast.

2039
02:55:25,104 --> 02:55:30,276
But the data is lost when the instance stops and it cannot be detached or snapshotted.

2040
02:55:30,638 --> 02:55:33,028
Anything you care about goes on EBS.

2041
02:55:34,144 --> 02:55:37,508
Instance store is for caches, buffers, and scratch data.

2042
02:55:38,862 --> 02:55:43,744
A snapshot is a point-in-time backup of a volume stored in Amazon S3.

2043
02:55:44,144 --> 02:55:51,364
Snapshots are incremental, so only the blocks changed since the last snapshot are stored, which keeps them cheap.

2044
02:55:52,046 --> 02:55:57,796
They can be copied to another region, and that is how EBS data crosses a region boundary.

2045
02:55:58,286 --> 02:56:03,344
A new volume created from a snapshot can be placed in a different availability zone.

2046
02:56:03,694 --> 02:56:08,324
That is the standard way to move a volume since volumes themselves cannot move.

2047
02:56:09,902 --> 02:56:14,340
Both provide shared file storage and the protocol decides between them.

2048
02:56:14,926 --> 02:56:24,836
EFS is mounted by many Linux instances at once using NFS, grows and shrinks automatically, and spans multiple availability zones.

2049
02:56:25,550 --> 02:56:27,197
FSX comes in variants.

2050
02:56:28,302 --> 02:56:35,492
FSX for Windows File Server speaks SMB and supports native Windows features including Active Directory.

2051
02:56:35,854 --> 02:56:39,424
FSX for Lustre serves high performance computing.

2052
02:56:39,598 --> 02:56:45,988
Linux and NFS means EFS. Windows and SMB means FSX for Windows File Server.

2053
02:56:47,630 --> 02:56:55,984
Storage gateway gives on-premises systems access to Adzebur storage using protocols they already speak, which is what makes it a hybrid

2054
02:56:56,224 --> 02:56:56,548
service.

2055
02:56:57,582 --> 02:57:01,508
File gateway presents S3 as an NFS or SMB share.

2056
02:57:02,350 --> 02:57:07,716
Volume gateway presents block storage over ISCCSI backed by S3.

2057
02:57:08,462 --> 02:57:14,212
And Tape Gateway replaces a physical tape library backed by S3 and the Glacier classes.

2058
02:57:14,704 --> 02:57:19,140
The signal is always an existing on-premises system that cannot be changed.

2059
02:57:19,744 --> 02:57:23,876
Tape gateway in particular is the giveaway for replacing tape backups.

2060
02:57:25,486 --> 02:57:33,264
AWS backup centralizes backup across EBS, RDS, Dynamo DB, EFS, FSX and more

2061
02:57:33,424 --> 02:57:34,564
from a single place.

2062
02:57:35,438 --> 02:57:39,652
Backup plans define schedule and retention and can apply across an organization.

2063
02:57:40,590 --> 02:57:46,468
AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery continuously replicates servers into AWS so you can recover quickly.

2064
02:57:47,374 --> 02:57:54,628
It is the answer when a scenario names a recovery time objective for an estate. Keep the two apart. A backup is a copy you restore from.

2065
02:57:55,406 --> 02:57:57,988
Disaster recovery is a standby you fail over to.

2066
02:57:59,374 --> 02:58:03,520
One naming warning because the word Glacier now means two things.

2067
02:58:03,760 --> 02:58:09,860
The S3 Glacier storage classes are fully available and are the recommended way to archive data.

2068
02:58:10,574 --> 02:58:19,844
But Amazon Glacier, the original standalone vault-based service, stopped accepting new customers on the 15th of December 2025.

2069
02:58:20,334 --> 02:58:24,964
Existing data stays accessible indefinitely and no migration is required.

2070
02:58:25,680 --> 02:58:28,196
The exam means the storage classes.

2071
02:58:29,070 --> 02:58:36,132
If a question talks about vaults and archives rather than buckets and objects, it is describing the old service.

2072
02:58:37,678 --> 02:58:40,036
8 phrasings covering most storage questions.

2073
02:58:40,320 --> 02:58:44,080
Many Linux instances sharing files means EFS.

2074
02:58:44,240 --> 02:58:52,452
Windows and SMB means FSX for Windows file server. A single instance needing a persistent disk means EBS.

2075
02:58:52,814 --> 02:58:56,480
Any amount of data over HTTP means S3.

2076
02:58:57,358 --> 02:59:02,436
Archive with 12-hour retrieval acceptable means Glacier Deep Archive.

2077
02:59:02,894 --> 02:59:06,244
An unpredictable access pattern means intelligent.

2078
02:59:07,342 --> 02:59:08,004
Tiering.

2079
02:59:08,974 --> 02:59:11,780
Replacing a tape library means tape gateway.

2080
02:59:12,654 --> 02:59:17,668
And a central backup policy across services means AWS backup.

2081
02:59:18,990 --> 02:59:20,196
Work scenario

2082
02:59:20,942 --> 02:59:21,444
1.

2083
02:59:22,958 --> 02:59:27,492
A company stores legal case files in S3 and must keep them for 10 years.

2084
02:59:27,918 --> 02:59:32,068
They are read a handful of times in the first 90 days and then almost never.

2085
02:59:32,718 --> 02:59:36,836
But a request can arrive at any time and must be satisfied within a day.

2086
02:59:37,742 --> 02:59:41,764
The company wants the lowest storage cost that meets this.

2087
02:59:43,406 --> 02:59:44,548
Pause and decide.

2088
02:59:50,720 --> 02:59:52,132
The answer is B.

2089
02:59:52,590 --> 02:59:57,508
The deciding detail is the retrieval window and it is deliberately generous.

2090
02:59:58,000 --> 03:00:01,700
Must be satisfied within a day is much slower than milliseconds.

2091
03:00:02,510 --> 03:00:07,440
Options A, C and D all make you pay for speed.

2092
03:00:07,694 --> 03:00:08,676
You do not need.

2093
03:00:14,640 --> 03:00:22,884
Always check the stated retrieval window against the class because the exam sets it precisely to make one class correct and the neighboring one wasteful.

2094
03:00:24,398 --> 03:00:25,200
Work scenario.

2095
03:00:25,360 --> 03:00:31,780
2. A company runs a content management application on 12 EC2 instances across three availability zones.

2096
03:00:32,160 --> 03:00:38,660
All 12 must read and write the same set of uploaded documents and the application expects a mounted file system path.

2097
03:00:39,438 --> 03:00:43,460
The instances run Linux and the application cannot be modified.

2098
03:00:44,334 --> 03:00:45,680
Pause and decide.

2099
03:00:51,520 --> 03:00:53,280
The answer is C.

2100
03:00:53,680 --> 03:00:57,220
Two clauses work together to eliminate the strongest distractor.

2101
03:00:59,200 --> 03:01:04,260
The application expects a mounted file system path and it cannot be modified.

2102
03:01:04,640 --> 03:01:07,748
Either one alone might leave S3 arguable.

2103
03:01:08,400 --> 03:01:13,760
Together they close it because using S3 would require changing the application.

2104
03:01:14,318 --> 03:01:20,100
This is the elimination rule from the opening of the module and the exam plans it exactly this way.

2105
03:01:20,974 --> 03:01:23,876
Option B fails on both counts since

2106
03:01:25,550 --> 03:01:28,356
EBS is single instance and single zone.

2107
03:01:29,997 --> 03:01:31,197
Work Scenario 3

2108
03:01:32,238 --> 03:01:36,388
A data science team writes large temporary files while a job runs.

2109
03:01:36,814 --> 03:01:43,556
The files are regenerated on every run, are never needed afterwards, and the team wants the fastest possible disk throughput.

2110
03:01:44,974 --> 03:01:48,420
Cost matters and they are willing to lose the data at any time.

2111
03:01:49,390 --> 03:01:51,040
Which storage should they use?

2112
03:01:51,374 --> 03:01:52,484
Pause and decide.

2113
03:01:58,702 --> 03:02:00,068
The answer is B.

2114
03:02:02,542 --> 03:02:06,820
Instance store is usually the wrong answer because most data matters.

2115
03:02:07,214 --> 03:02:10,852
This scenario is the exception and it says so explicitly.

2116
03:02:11,502 --> 03:02:17,252
Regenerated on every run, never needed afterwards, and willing to lose the data at any time.

2117
03:02:18,222 --> 03:02:22,468
Those three phases exist to make ephemeral storage acceptable.

2118
03:02:22,800 --> 03:02:30,916
Once it is acceptable, instance store wins on both speed and cost because it is physically attached and included in the instance price.

2119
03:02:32,302 --> 03:02:33,924
Worked scenario 4.

2120
03:02:34,894 --> 03:02:38,640
A company has a large archive of research data in S3.

2121
03:02:39,040 --> 03:02:47,684
Some datasets are downloaded weekly, others untouched in two years, and analysts occasionally revive an old dataset without warning.

2122
03:02:48,654 --> 03:02:56,612
The company wants to reduce cost but cannot predict which data will be needed and does not want retrieval delays or fees.

2123
03:03:00,590 --> 03:03:01,924
Pause and decide.

2124
03:03:08,137 --> 03:03:11,040
The answer is C. Compare this with scenario one.

2125
03:03:11,438 --> 03:03:15,812
Deliberately, because both are about archive data and the answers are opposite.

2126
03:03:17,070 --> 03:03:21,840
There, the access pattern was known and the retrieval window was generous.

2127
03:03:22,126 --> 03:03:24,676
So a lifecycle rule to deep archive one.

2128
03:03:25,582 --> 03:03:30,880
Here the pattern is explicitly unpredictable and both delays and retrieval fees are ruled out.

2129
03:03:31,182 --> 03:03:34,756
Three constraints and only intelligent. Tiering satisfies all three.

2130
03:03:35,502 --> 03:03:38,532
Predictability is the hinge between these two questions.

2131
03:03:39,950 --> 03:03:41,028
Worked scenario.

2132
03:03:43,182 --> 03:03:43,760
5.

2133
03:03:44,640 --> 03:03:57,520
A company still backs up on-premises servers to physical tape using backup software that writes to a tape library. It wants to stop handling tapes and store backups in AWS instead.

2134
03:03:57,760 --> 03:04:02,148
It does not want to replace or reconfigure its existing backup software.

2135
03:04:02,560 --> 03:04:03,908
Pause and decide.

2136
03:04:10,000 --> 03:04:11,300
The answer is B.

2137
03:04:11,600 --> 03:04:19,908
Option C is the interesting one because Glacier Deep Archive is genuinely where this data should end up, and Tape Gateway will put it there.

2138
03:04:20,160 --> 03:04:22,468
What C misses is the constraint.

2139
03:04:23,022 --> 03:04:30,628
The backup software writes to a tape library and it cannot write to S3 without being reconfigured, which the company ruled out.

2140
03:04:31,182 --> 03:04:34,628
Every form of storage gateway exists for that same reason.

2141
03:04:35,406 --> 03:04:42,916
An existing system speaks a protocol, and you need AWS storage behind that protocol without changing the system.

2142
03:04:44,627 --> 03:04:45,987
Work scenario 6.

2143
03:04:46,638 --> 03:04:52,324
An EC2 instance in one availability zone has an EBS volume holding application data.

2144
03:04:52,640 --> 03:05:00,452
The company is redesigning for high availability and needs an identical volume attached to a new instance in a different availability zone.

2145
03:05:01,326 --> 03:05:04,996
The data must be copied across with minimal downtime.

2146
03:05:05,646 --> 03:05:06,960
How should it do this?

2147
03:05:07,246 --> 03:05:08,388
Pause and decide.

2148
03:05:14,542 --> 03:05:15,812
The answer is B.

2149
03:05:16,334 --> 03:05:21,796
Option C is worth naming explicitly because it is a fabricated feature that sounds entirely plausible.

2150
03:05:22,638 --> 03:05:29,440
Multi-availability zone is a real AWS term, but it belongs to RDS, not to EBS volumes.

2151
03:05:30,062 --> 03:05:36,260
The exam does use invented options built from real vocabulary borrowed from a neighboring service.

2152
03:05:36,640 --> 03:05:43,108
If an option describes a feature you have never encountered, be suspicious rather than assuming the date.

2153
03:05:44,526 --> 03:05:53,124
To recap, decide object, block or file first and remember that a mountable file system requirement eliminates S3.

2154
03:05:53,966 --> 03:05:56,260
Two facts pick an S3 class.

2155
03:05:56,910 --> 03:06:00,196
How often the data is read and how fast it must come back.

2156
03:06:01,070 --> 03:06:07,524
Unpredictable access means intelligent, tiering, while a known age rule means a lifecycle policy.

2157
03:06:08,238 --> 03:06:10,852
EBS persists and is zone-bound.

2158
03:06:11,120 --> 03:06:16,868
Instance store is fast and disappears, and snapshots are how data crosses zones and regions.

2159
03:06:17,360 --> 03:06:19,876
NFS and Linux means EFS.

2160
03:06:20,240 --> 03:06:23,492
SMB and Windows means FSX.

2161
03:06:24,654 --> 03:06:26,596
Next module, databases.

2162
03:06:28,174 --> 03:06:36,036
AWS deliberately offers many databases rather than one on the principle that each workload has a shape that suits it.

2163
03:06:37,486 --> 03:06:39,760
The exam tests that principle directly.

2164
03:06:40,078 --> 03:06:43,428
It gives you a workload and asks which database fits.

2165
03:06:43,520 --> 03:06:47,428
So the skill is matching a shape rather than knowing any one service deeply.

2166
03:06:49,134 --> 03:06:53,028
Ask two questions in order and most of this module resolves.

2167
03:06:54,606 --> 03:06:56,624
First, is the data relational?

2168
03:06:57,262 --> 03:07:04,772
If a question mentions SQL joins, transactions, or an existing relational schema, you are in the RDS family.

2169
03:07:06,254 --> 03:07:08,708
If not, ask what shape the data has.

2170
03:07:09,584 --> 03:07:14,304
Key value lookups, documents, graphs, time-ordered analytics.

2171
03:07:14,766 --> 03:07:19,140
Each shape has one AWS answer and the exam expects you to name it.

2172
03:07:20,462 --> 03:07:23,300
7 services, each suiting a shape.

2173
03:07:23,694 --> 03:07:27,140
RDS is relational with six engines managed for you.

2174
03:07:27,744 --> 03:07:34,148
Aurora is relational to MySQL and Postgres compatible with higher performance.

2175
03:07:34,624 --> 03:07:40,964
Dynamo DB is key value and document serverless with single-digit millisecond responses.

2176
03:07:41,838 --> 03:07:50,144
Redshift is analytics over a data warehouse, columnar, and petabyte scale. ElasticAche is an in-memory cache in front of something slower.

2177
03:07:50,304 --> 03:07:57,732
Neptune is for graphs, where relationships between things are the point. And DocumentDB serves MongoDB compatible workloads.

2178
03:07:59,214 --> 03:08:03,904
This is the shared responsibility question from module 5 applied to databases.

2179
03:08:04,110 --> 03:08:08,516
With RDS, AWS patches the operating system and the engine.

2180
03:08:08,814 --> 03:08:15,300
It runs automated backups with point-in-time recovery and makes multi-availability zone failover a checkbox.

2181
03:08:16,046 --> 03:08:20,100
With a database on EC2, you do all of that yourself.

2182
03:08:20,304 --> 03:08:24,580
But you get full control, including unsupported engines and versions.

2183
03:08:25,024 --> 03:08:27,204
RDS is the default answer.

2184
03:08:27,664 --> 03:08:32,676
Choose EC2 only when the scenario names something RDS cannot do.

2185
03:08:34,286 --> 03:08:36,900
RDS supports six engines.

2186
03:08:37,344 --> 03:08:44,064
My SQL, Postgres, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server, and BB2.

2187
03:08:45,104 --> 03:08:51,620
The engine is the same one an application already uses, so existing applications usually work unchanged.

2188
03:08:52,224 --> 03:08:57,252
That is why RDS is the standard answer for lift and shift database migrations.

2189
03:08:58,126 --> 03:09:05,636
It provides automated backups with point-in-time recovery plus manual snapshots you keep as long as you like.

2190
03:09:06,254 --> 03:09:08,548
Storage can be encrypted with KMS.

2191
03:09:09,344 --> 03:09:11,972
And you have no operating system access.

2192
03:09:12,064 --> 03:09:15,204
So a scenario needing that eliminates RDS.

2193
03:09:16,784 --> 03:09:24,100
Aurora is part of the RDS family rather than a separate service, and the separator is engine compatibility.

2194
03:09:24,686 --> 03:09:32,420
Aurora is MySQL and Postgres compatible only and offers higher throughput than the standard engines.

2195
03:09:33,134 --> 03:09:38,884
It stores six copies of your data across three availability zones and has a serverless option.

2196
03:09:39,344 --> 03:09:47,364
Standard RDS also covers Oracle, SQL Server, DB2, and MariaDB and is the only option for those.

2197
03:09:47,982 --> 03:09:52,064
So Oracle or SQL Server means standard RDS, never Aurora.

2198
03:09:53,806 --> 03:09:58,532
This is the pair the module exists for and the exam tests it constantly.

2199
03:09:58,784 --> 03:10:08,868
A multi-availability zone deployment keeps a standby copy in another zone using synchronous replication and fails over automatically if the primary fails.

2200
03:10:09,678 --> 03:10:13,264
It exists for availability and the standby is not there to serve reads.

2201
03:10:13,664 --> 03:10:22,340
Read replicas are additional readable copies using asynchronous replication with no automatic failover, and they can live in another region.

2202
03:10:24,846 --> 03:10:27,344
Surviving failure means multi-AZ.

2203
03:10:28,366 --> 03:10:32,132
Read queries overwhelming the database means read replicas.

2204
03:10:33,646 --> 03:10:41,124
Dynamo DB is a key value and document database that is fully serverless. So there is nothing to provision or patch.

2205
03:10:41,504 --> 03:10:46,020
Its defining claim is single digit millisecond performance at any scale.

2206
03:10:46,544 --> 03:10:52,612
On demand capacity charges per request, while provision capacity is cheaper for steady predictable load.

2207
03:10:53,486 --> 03:11:00,944
Global tables replicate across regions and Dynamo DB accelerator adds an in-memory cache for microsecond reads.

2208
03:11:01,358 --> 03:11:06,884
What it does not do is joins or SQL, so complex relational queries are the wrong fit.

2209
03:11:08,398 --> 03:11:14,372
DynamoDB and Redshift Co. occurred 22 times in the bank analysis, so separate them carefully.

2210
03:11:14,798 --> 03:11:23,364
DynamoDB handles many small fast operations, looking up one item by its key constantly to serve an application in real time.

2211
03:11:24,334 --> 03:11:33,060
Redshift handles few large complex queries, aggregating millions of rows to produce a report for analysts. The separator is who is waiting.

2212
03:11:33,486 --> 03:11:36,196
If an application is waiting, it is DynamoDB.

2213
03:11:36,544 --> 03:11:39,140
If an analyst is waiting, it is Redshift.

2214
03:11:40,526 --> 03:11:47,012
Redshift is a columnar data warehouse built for analytical queries over very large datasets.

2215
03:11:47,424 --> 03:11:54,916
Columnar storage is why it is fast for this work, because a query reading three columns does not read the other 97.

2216
03:11:55,726 --> 03:12:01,700
It uses SQL, so analysts and existing business intelligence tools work with it directly.

2217
03:12:03,854 --> 03:12:11,140
Redshift serverless removes cluster management and Redshift Spectrum Queries data sitting in Amazon S3.

2218
03:12:11,918 --> 03:12:17,060
But it is not for serving an application, so avoid it for high volume single row lookups.

2219
03:12:18,798 --> 03:12:26,788
Elastic Cache is an in-memory cache that sits in front of a database and serves repeated reads in microseconds.

2220
03:12:29,454 --> 03:12:35,204
Its classic use is relieving a database whose read load is dominated by the same few queries.

2221
03:12:36,142 --> 03:12:45,924
The engines are Valkyrie, Redis open source, and memcaged, with Valky support added in October 2024 after the Redis license changed.

2222
03:12:46,464 --> 03:12:50,084
It is also used for session storage, leaderboards, and rate limiting.

2223
03:12:51,024 --> 03:12:53,220
But a cache is not a database.

2224
03:12:53,504 --> 03:12:57,156
So anything that must persist belongs somewhere durable.

2225
03:12:58,817 --> 03:13:02,948
Two purpose built answers that appear when their exact signal is present.

2226
03:13:03,406 --> 03:13:08,612
Amazon Neptune is a graph database where the relationships between items are what you query.

2227
03:13:09,006 --> 03:13:15,524
Recommendation engines, fraud detection rings, social networks, and knowledge graphs are the named use cases.

2228
03:13:16,142 --> 03:13:24,784
Amazon Document DB is a document database with MongoDB compatibility and its signal is an existing MongoDB application the company does not want to rewrite.

2229
03:13:24,944 --> 03:13:29,412
Both are managed, so AWS handles patching, backups, and availability.

2230
03:13:30,990 --> 03:13:33,476
One availability warning for this module.

2231
03:13:37,390 --> 03:13:49,700
Amazon QLDB, the ledger database, reached end of support on the 31st of July 2025. AWS's migration path was to Aurora Postgres.

2232
03:13:50,606 --> 03:14:00,036
Older question banks still offer QLDB as the answer for an immutable, cryptographically verifiable ledger, and that service no longer exists.

2233
03:14:01,262 --> 03:14:07,780
Amazon Key Spaces and Amazon MemoryDB are not on the in scope list, so recognize them without studying them.

2234
03:14:08,558 --> 03:14:10,980
Everything else in this module is current.

2235
03:14:12,270 --> 03:14:15,024
Eight phrasings covering most database questions.

2236
03:14:15,264 --> 03:14:19,184
Migrating an Oracle application unchanged means RDS.

2237
03:14:19,566 --> 03:14:23,716
Key value lookups at single digit milliseconds means Dynamo DB.

2238
03:14:24,464 --> 03:14:28,292
Business intelligence over historical data means redshift.

2239
03:14:28,814 --> 03:14:31,204
A recommendation engine means Neptune.

2240
03:14:31,886 --> 03:14:34,628
An existing MongoDB workload means Document DB.

2241
03:14:34,864 --> 03:14:38,224
Repeated reads overloading a database means Elasticash.

2242
03:14:38,384 --> 03:14:41,700
Surviving an availability zone failure means multi-AZ.

2243
03:14:42,958 --> 03:14:46,784
And reporting queries slowing the application means read replicas.

2244
03:14:48,494 --> 03:14:49,956
Worked scenario 1.

2245
03:14:50,414 --> 03:14:56,384
A company runs a critical order processing system on Amazon RDS for Postgres.

2246
03:14:57,294 --> 03:15:03,332
An availability zone disruption made the database unreachable for two hours halting orders.

2247
03:15:04,304 --> 03:15:12,580
The company wants the database to keep serving requests if a single zone becomes unavailable, with failover happening automatically.

2248
03:15:13,582 --> 03:15:14,980
What should it configure?

2249
03:15:15,822 --> 03:15:16,900
Pause and decide.

2250
03:15:23,072 --> 03:15:24,420
The answer is B.

2251
03:15:24,672 --> 03:15:33,220
Option A is genuinely tempting because a read replica does put a copy in another availability zone, which sounds like it addresses zone failure.

2252
03:15:33,966 --> 03:15:34,916
Two things kill it.

2253
03:15:35,374 --> 03:15:38,628
The replication is asynchronous, so the replica may lag.

2254
03:15:39,470 --> 03:15:45,316
And promoting it is a manual action, whereas the requirement says failover must happen automatically.

2255
03:15:45,632 --> 03:15:53,380
That single adverb decides the question. Read the requirement for automatic against manual whenever both options place a copy elsewhere.

2256
03:15:55,054 --> 03:15:56,164
Worked scenario.

2257
03:15:57,152 --> 03:16:06,916
2. The same order processing database is now highly available, but the finance team has begun running large reporting queries against it during the working day.

2258
03:16:07,822 --> 03:16:12,132
Order processing has become noticeably slower whenever those reports run.

2259
03:16:12,942 --> 03:16:15,872
The reports can tolerate data a few seconds out of date.

2260
03:16:16,112 --> 03:16:17,312
What should the company do?

2261
03:16:17,614 --> 03:16:18,628
Pause and decide.

2262
03:16:24,782 --> 03:16:29,508
The answer is B, and this is deliberately the same database as scenario 1.

2263
03:16:30,030 --> 03:16:32,740
Same system, different problem, different answer.

2264
03:16:33,390 --> 03:16:36,452
There the problem was surviving failure, so multi-AZ.

2265
03:16:37,390 --> 03:16:42,372
Here the problem is red load computing with writes, so read replicas.

2266
03:16:43,182 --> 03:16:47,140
Notice the sentence about tolerating data a few seconds out of date.

2267
03:16:47,534 --> 03:16:50,436
That exists to make asynchronous replication acceptable.

2268
03:16:52,014 --> 03:16:55,952
And its presence is a strong hint that read replicas are intended.

2269
03:16:57,262 --> 03:16:58,564
Verb scenario 3.

2270
03:16:58,958 --> 03:17:07,172
A retailer wants to suggest products by finding customers who bought similar combinations of items, then following those connections, several steps outward.

2271
03:17:07,630 --> 03:17:11,268
The queries are about how items and customers connect to one another.

2272
03:17:12,206 --> 03:17:17,156
Existing relational queries doing this require many joins and have become too slow.

2273
03:17:17,518 --> 03:17:19,712
Which database is designed for this?

2274
03:17:20,078 --> 03:17:21,188
Pause and decide.

2275
03:17:27,312 --> 03:17:29,232
The answer is C.

2276
03:17:30,894 --> 03:17:33,604
Three signals point at a graph database.

2277
03:17:34,112 --> 03:17:36,484
The queries are about how things connect.

2278
03:17:36,752 --> 03:17:39,492
They follow connections several steps outward.

2279
03:17:39,952 --> 03:17:47,908
And the relational version needs many joins and is too slow, which is precisely the problem graph databases exist to solve.

2280
03:17:48,526 --> 03:17:54,148
The phrase recommendation engine alone is usually enough to reach for Neptune.

2281
03:17:54,606 --> 03:18:00,004
But here the scenario also describes the traversal pattern, which is the more honest reason.

2282
03:18:01,678 --> 03:18:03,332
Worked scenario 4.

2283
03:18:04,302 --> 03:18:08,196
A news website stores articles in Amazon RDS.

2284
03:18:08,974 --> 03:18:17,636
90% of database reads are requests for the same 20 popular articles, repeated thousands of times an hour, and the database is struggling.

2285
03:18:18,222 --> 03:18:19,908
The articles change rarely.

2286
03:18:20,814 --> 03:18:24,100
What is the most appropriate way to reduce database load?

2287
03:18:25,070 --> 03:18:26,340
Pause and decide.

2288
03:18:32,430 --> 03:18:33,792
The answer is B.

2289
03:18:34,352 --> 03:18:41,380
Compare this with scenario 2 because both describe read load overwhelming a database and the answers differ.

2290
03:18:41,998 --> 03:18:47,396
There, the reads were large varied reporting queries, which a replica handles well.

2291
03:18:48,078 --> 03:18:54,020
Here the reads are the same, 20 items requested thousands of times an hour, and the items rarely change.

2292
03:18:54,702 --> 03:18:56,196
That is the cache signature.

2293
03:18:56,750 --> 03:19:00,388
Repetition and low volatility together mean elastic hash.

2294
03:19:01,422 --> 03:19:03,844
Varied queries mean read replicas.

2295
03:19:05,518 --> 03:19:06,916
Work scenario 5.

2296
03:19:07,598 --> 03:19:11,812
A gaming company needs to store player profiles and session state.

2297
03:19:12,192 --> 03:19:19,524
It expects unpredictable growth from thousands to millions of players and needs consistent single digit millisecond reads.

2298
03:19:20,014 --> 03:19:23,044
It does not want to manage servers or plan capacity.

2299
03:19:24,014 --> 03:19:28,676
Each profile is retrieved by player identifier with no complex joints.

2300
03:19:29,806 --> 03:19:31,556
Which database should it use?

2301
03:19:32,398 --> 03:19:33,700
Pause and decide.

2302
03:19:39,822 --> 03:19:47,364
The answer is C, and this scenario stacks four dynamo DB signals so you can practice recognizing them together.

2303
03:19:48,462 --> 03:19:50,756
Unpredictable growth to millions.

2304
03:19:52,494 --> 03:19:55,556
Consistent single-digit millisecond reads.

2305
03:19:56,238 --> 03:19:59,300
No servers to manage and no capacity planning.

2306
03:20:00,302 --> 03:20:03,684
And retrieval by a single identifier with no joints.

2307
03:20:04,622 --> 03:20:10,340
Option B is the closest miss because Aurora serverless does scale automatically.

2308
03:20:11,118 --> 03:20:12,932
But it is still a relational engine.

2309
03:20:13,390 --> 03:20:17,604
And the last sentence tells you the relational features would go unused.

2310
03:20:19,246 --> 03:20:22,596
To recap, ask relational first, then shape.

2311
03:20:23,278 --> 03:20:25,572
Multi-availability zone is availability.

2312
03:20:25,998 --> 03:20:30,596
While red replicas are performance, an automatic failover is the deciding word between them.

2313
03:20:31,758 --> 03:20:34,437
Repeated identical reads means elastic cache,

2314
03:20:35,438 --> 03:20:38,500
while varied heavy reads means red replicas.

2315
03:20:39,342 --> 03:20:44,880
Serving an application means DynamoDB while answering a business question means red shift.

2316
03:20:45,040 --> 03:20:52,932
And Aurora is MySQL or Postgres compatible only. So Oracle and SQL server means standard RDS.

2317
03:20:53,280 --> 03:20:56,036
Next module, networking and content delivery.

2318
03:20:57,550 --> 03:21:01,156
Networking accounted for 19% of the bank analysis.

2319
03:21:01,838 --> 03:21:09,188
It has eight worked scenarios here because the questions turn on precise distinctions rather than on knowing what a service does.

2320
03:21:09,774 --> 03:21:14,148
Two services can both connect you to AWS and only one fits the requirement.

2321
03:21:15,822 --> 03:21:17,636
This module answers three questions.

2322
03:21:17,920 --> 03:21:25,892
Where does traffic enter your environment, which is gateways and subnets? What decides whether traffic is allowed, which is security groups and network?

2323
03:21:27,342 --> 03:21:28,228
SELs.

2324
03:21:29,230 --> 03:21:35,332
And how does traffic reach users who are far away, which is CloudFront, Route 53, and Global Accelerator?

2325
03:21:36,622 --> 03:21:40,708
Almost every question here belongs to exactly one of those three.

2326
03:21:42,446 --> 03:21:48,804
AVPC is a logically isolated section of the AWS cloud where you control the network layout.

2327
03:21:49,280 --> 03:21:58,180
AVPC belongs to one region and each subnet within it belongs to one availability zone, which is a detail that decides questions.

2328
03:21:59,840 --> 03:22:03,236
Root tables decide where traffic from a subnet is sent.

2329
03:22:04,078 --> 03:22:15,364
The VPC itself costs nothing, though some components inside it, such as NAT gateways, do. And nothing inside a VPC is reachable from the internet unless you deliberately arrange it.

2330
03:22:16,974 --> 03:22:20,484
This distinction is simpler than people expect.

2331
03:22:20,880 --> 03:22:28,804
A subnet is public if its root table sends internet-bound traffic to an internet gateway and private if it does not.

2332
03:22:29,280 --> 03:22:31,076
That is the entire difference.

2333
03:22:31,680 --> 03:22:37,220
Public subnets hold load balancers, NAT gateways and bastion hosts.

2334
03:22:38,080 --> 03:22:45,828
Private subnets hold application servers and databases, which reach the internet outbound only through a NAT gateway.

2335
03:22:46,222 --> 03:22:49,060
Nothing else about the two kinds of subnet differs.

2336
03:22:50,542 --> 03:23:02,640
An Internet gateway allows two-way traffic between a VPC and the Internet. A NAT gateway lets private resources reach the internet outbound while blocking anything inbound.

2337
03:23:02,800 --> 03:23:06,724
It sits in a public subnet with the private subnet routing to it.

2338
03:23:07,182 --> 03:23:11,972
NAT gateways are charged per hour and per gigabyte processed.

2339
03:23:12,430 --> 03:23:14,404
So they turn up in cost questions too.

2340
03:23:15,214 --> 03:23:22,720
And note that a NAT gateway is not a security control. It permits outbound traffic rather than filtering it.

2341
03:23:24,046 --> 03:23:37,540
The VPC classic and worth holding precisely. Security groups attached to an instance's network interface are stateful, so return traffic is allowed automatically and have allow rules only.

2342
03:23:38,830 --> 03:23:42,080
Network ACS attached to a subnet and are stateless.

2343
03:23:42,240 --> 03:23:44,800
So return traffic needs its own rule.

2344
03:23:45,326 --> 03:23:50,884
They have both allow and deny rules evaluated in numbered order, two separators.

2345
03:23:51,120 --> 03:23:53,604
The word instance or subnet in the question.

2346
03:23:55,246 --> 03:23:59,396
And any requirement to block a specific address which only a network

2347
03:24:00,270 --> 03:24:01,348
ACL can do.

2348
03:24:03,182 --> 03:24:10,532
A VPC endpoint lets resources inside a VPC reach an AWS service without traversing the Internet.

2349
03:24:11,246 --> 03:24:16,772
Gateway endpoints serve Amazon S3 and DynamoDB and carry no additional charge.

2350
03:24:17,646 --> 03:24:23,844
Interface endpoints powered by AWS PrivateLink serve most other services and are charged.

2351
03:24:24,686 --> 03:24:31,776
Because traffic stays on the AWS network, endpoints satisfy requirements about data not crossing the public internet.

2352
03:24:32,176 --> 03:24:38,692
They can also remove the need for a NAD gateway when the only outbound traffic is to AWS services.

2353
03:24:40,334 --> 03:24:44,896
Route 53 does three jobs, and the exam names all three almost verbatim.

2354
03:24:45,358 --> 03:24:49,412
Domain registration, DNS, routing, and health checking.

2355
03:24:50,958 --> 03:24:57,156
Health checks let it route traffic away from endpoints that have stopped responding, which is how it contributes to availability.

2356
03:24:58,030 --> 03:25:06,340
It is a global service rather than a regional one, and it routes to endpoints outside AWS just as well as to AWS resources.

2357
03:25:06,736 --> 03:25:11,364
Remember that it resolves names to addresses and does not carry your application traffic.

2358
03:25:12,718 --> 03:25:15,204
5 routing policies worth recognizing.

2359
03:25:15,566 --> 03:25:17,892
Simple sends everyone to one destination.

2360
03:25:18,382 --> 03:25:25,188
Weighted splits traffic by proportion, which is useful for testing a new version on a small share of users.

2361
03:25:26,382 --> 03:25:30,628
Latency sends users to whichever region responds fastest for them.

2362
03:25:31,566 --> 03:25:38,084
Failover sends traffic to a primary and switches to a standby when health checks fail.

2363
03:25:38,542 --> 03:25:44,164
And geolocation routes based on where the user is, often for compliance or language reasons.

2364
03:25:45,870 --> 03:25:52,644
CloudFront caches content at edge locations so users fetch it from somewhere nearby instead of from the origin.

2365
03:25:56,142 --> 03:26:03,044
That origin can be S3, an application load balancer or a server outside AWS entirely.

2366
03:26:03,790 --> 03:26:09,508
It reduces latency and also reduces load on the origin because cache requests never reach it.

2367
03:26:10,350 --> 03:26:13,956
That makes it a cost answer as well as a performance one.

2368
03:26:14,446 --> 03:26:18,564
WAF and Shield standard both operate at the cloud front edge.

2369
03:26:18,926 --> 03:26:22,724
And signed URLs restrict access to private content.

2370
03:26:24,302 --> 03:26:29,156
These two both use the AWS edge network and are genuinely confused.

2371
03:26:29,776 --> 03:26:37,220
CloudFront caches content, storing copies at edge locations, and works with HTTP and HTTPS.

2372
03:26:38,030 --> 03:26:40,068
Global Accelerator caches nothing.

2373
03:26:40,814 --> 03:26:46,628
It improves the network path, taking traffic onto the AWS backbone at the nearest edge.

2374
03:26:47,406 --> 03:26:50,500
It works with any TCP or UDP traffic.

2375
03:26:51,022 --> 03:26:54,788
It also provides static IP addresses that never change.

2376
03:26:57,247 --> 03:26:59,616
Cacheable content means cloud front.

2377
03:26:59,936 --> 03:27:05,604
Non-HTTP traffic or a static IP requirement means global accelerator.

2378
03:27:07,406 --> 03:27:11,076
Two ways to connect your own network to AWS.

2379
03:27:13,390 --> 03:27:21,776
Direct Connect is a dedicated physical link that bypasses the internet, giving consistent predictable bandwidth and latency,

2380
03:27:21,966 --> 03:27:24,896
but it takes weeks to provision and costs more.

2381
03:27:25,536 --> 03:27:33,316
A side-to-side VPN runs encrypted over your existing internet connection, can be set up in hours and costs little,

2382
03:27:34,062 --> 03:27:36,804
but its performance varies with internet conditions.

2383
03:27:37,358 --> 03:27:40,580
Consistent and predictable means direct connect.

2384
03:27:41,710 --> 03:27:43,780
Quick and cheap means VPN.

2385
03:27:44,750 --> 03:27:48,324
Watch for stated timescales because they often decide it.

2386
03:27:50,030 --> 03:27:52,644
Two services sharing a word and nothing else.

2387
03:27:53,136 --> 03:27:59,556
Transit Gateway is a network hub connecting many VPCs and on premises networks through a single point.

2388
03:27:59,776 --> 03:28:04,548
It replaces a tangle of individual pairing connections as the number of VPCs grows.

2389
03:28:05,358 --> 03:28:05,856
A P

2390
03:28:08,448 --> 03:28:11,780
A gateway creates, publishes, and manages.

2391
03:28:13,518 --> 03:28:15,620
A P is for applications to call.

2392
03:28:16,238 --> 03:28:21,348
It handles throttling, authorization, and versioning and commonly sits in front of Lambda.

2393
03:28:22,638 --> 03:28:25,348
Many VPCs means transit gateway.

2394
03:28:26,094 --> 03:28:29,488
Publishing an API means API gateway.

2395
03:28:30,798 --> 03:28:33,476
Eight phrasings covering most networking questions.

2396
03:28:33,998 --> 03:28:36,608
Instances needing patches but not reachable.

2397
03:28:36,928 --> 03:28:39,108
Inbound means a NAT gateway.

2398
03:28:39,630 --> 03:28:42,180
Blocking one specific address means a network.

2399
03:28:42,608 --> 03:28:43,428
ACL.

2400
03:28:43,968 --> 03:28:47,108
Restricting access to an instance means a security group.

2401
03:28:47,726 --> 03:28:52,900
Traffic to S3 avoiding the internet means a VPC gateway endpoint.

2402
03:28:53,646 --> 03:28:57,444
Slow static content for worldwide users means cloud front.

2403
03:28:58,446 --> 03:29:03,780
Non-HTTP traffic with static addresses means global accelerator.

2404
03:29:04,686 --> 03:29:08,676
Consistent bandwidth to your data center means direct connect.

2405
03:29:09,134 --> 03:29:13,220
And connecting 40 VPCs means transit gateway.

2406
03:29:14,957 --> 03:29:16,900
Worked scenario 1.

2407
03:29:17,888 --> 03:29:23,044
A company runs application servers that must download operating system patches from the internet.

2408
03:29:24,238 --> 03:29:28,900
Security policy states that nothing on the internet may open a connection to those servers.

2409
03:29:29,168 --> 03:29:33,380
The servers sit in a private subnet with no internet access at all.

2410
03:29:34,062 --> 03:29:35,332
What should the company add?

2411
03:29:35,854 --> 03:29:36,996
Pause and decide.

2412
03:29:43,118 --> 03:29:44,288
The answer is B.

2413
03:29:44,528 --> 03:29:47,140
This scenario has a keen structure worth noticing.

2414
03:29:47,728 --> 03:29:53,808
Three of the four options make the servers reachable from the internet in some way, and the policy forbids exactly that.

2415
03:29:53,968 --> 03:29:59,620
Only one option provides outbound access without inbound, which is the definition of a NAT gateway.

2416
03:30:00,288 --> 03:30:03,648
When a requirement pays outbound, yes with inbound.

2417
03:30:03,888 --> 03:30:07,268
No. The answer is a NAT gateway every time.

2418
03:30:08,814 --> 03:30:10,340
Worked scenario 2.

2419
03:30:10,766 --> 03:30:17,732
A security team has identified a single IP address repeatedly probing a company's public web servers.

2420
03:30:18,222 --> 03:30:23,844
They want to block all traffic from that address before it reaches any instance in the web subnet.

2421
03:30:24,688 --> 03:30:31,728
The web servers already have security groups permitting HTTP and HTTPS from anywhere.

2422
03:30:31,918 --> 03:30:33,124
How should they block it?

2423
03:30:33,582 --> 03:30:34,788
Pause and decide.

2424
03:30:40,750 --> 03:30:41,828
The answer is B.

2425
03:30:42,288 --> 03:30:45,220
This question tests one fact and nothing else.

2426
03:30:45,568 --> 03:30:50,500
Security groups have no deny rules, so blocking a specific address is impossible with them.

2427
03:30:51,008 --> 03:30:56,100
Options A and D both describe capabilities that do not exist.

2428
03:30:57,006 --> 03:31:02,980
That is a distractor style worth recognizing because both sound reasonable if you have not learned the constraint.

2429
03:31:03,182 --> 03:31:07,908
And option C is the overcorrection, blocking everyone rather than one address.

2430
03:31:09,582 --> 03:31:10,788
Worked scenario.

2431
03:31:11,246 --> 03:31:20,068
3. A media company serves large video files and images from Amazon S3 to viewers across Europe, Asia, and South America.

2432
03:31:20,368 --> 03:31:26,564
Viewers far from the buckets region report slow starts and the S3 data transfer bill is rising.

2433
03:31:27,278 --> 03:31:31,524
The content is identical for every viewer and changes infrequently.

2434
03:31:31,808 --> 03:31:33,540
What should the company use?

2435
03:31:34,350 --> 03:31:35,524
Pause and decide.

2436
03:31:41,486 --> 03:31:43,488
The answer is A.

2437
03:31:44,334 --> 03:31:48,164
Two details make this cloud front rather than global accelerator.

2438
03:31:48,782 --> 03:31:54,340
The content is identical for every viewer and changes infrequently, which makes it cacheable.

2439
03:31:54,958 --> 03:32:00,388
And the transfer bill is rising, which caching fixes because cached requests never reach the origin.

2440
03:32:01,326 --> 03:32:07,716
Global accelerator would improve the path, but every request would still hit S3, so the bill would not fall.

2441
03:32:08,270 --> 03:32:10,564
Option D is a good check of understanding.

2442
03:32:11,214 --> 03:32:15,972
Latency routing needs somewhere to choose between, and there is only one bucket.

2443
03:32:17,358 --> 03:32:18,500
Worked scenario.

2444
03:32:25,358 --> 03:32:28,516
A gaming company runs servers in three regions.

2445
03:32:29,390 --> 03:32:37,840
Players connect over UDP and the company must publish two fixed IP addresses that never change because players' clients have them hard

2446
03:32:38,000 --> 03:32:38,480
coded.

2447
03:32:38,640 --> 03:32:43,760
It wants players routed to the healthiest, nearest region automatically.

2448
03:32:44,400 --> 03:32:46,800
Which service meets these requirements?

2449
03:32:47,182 --> 03:32:48,400
Pause and decide.

2450
03:32:54,222 --> 03:32:55,492
The answer is B.

2451
03:32:56,000 --> 03:32:58,084
Compare this directly with scenario.

2452
03:32:58,670 --> 03:33:02,756
3. Because they use the same pair of services and go opposite ways.

2453
03:33:03,280 --> 03:33:07,428
There the content was cacheable, HTTP, so CloudFront.

2454
03:33:07,760 --> 03:33:13,668
Here, the traffic is UDP and cannot be cached, and the company needs fixed IP addresses.

2455
03:33:14,606 --> 03:33:18,180
Either of those facts alone eliminates CloudFront.

2456
03:33:20,526 --> 03:33:23,140
This is the cleanest way to hold the pair.

2457
03:33:23,886 --> 03:33:29,956
CloudFront caches HTTP content. Global accelerator improves the path for anything.

2458
03:33:31,246 --> 03:33:32,708
Worked scenario 5.

2459
03:33:33,006 --> 03:33:39,588
A bank runs applications in a VPC that write large volumes of data to Amazon S3.

2460
03:33:39,920 --> 03:33:43,920
Compliance requires that this traffic never traverse the public internet.

2461
03:33:44,398 --> 03:33:51,200
The applications currently reach S3 through an AT gateway and the NAT charges are significant.

2462
03:33:51,694 --> 03:33:55,012
The bank wants to satisfy compliance and reduce cost.

2463
03:33:55,694 --> 03:33:56,900
Pause and decide.

2464
03:34:03,054 --> 03:34:07,108
The answer is B and both stated requirements point at it.

2465
03:34:07,662 --> 03:34:12,000
Compliance is satisfied because endpoint traffic stays on the AWS network.

2466
03:34:12,302 --> 03:34:18,692
Cost falls because gateway endpoints for S3 carry no charge, replacing NAT gateway processing fees.

2467
03:34:19,534 --> 03:34:21,636
Option A is the classic confusion.

2468
03:34:22,480 --> 03:34:25,988
Direct Connect joins your own data center to AWS.

2469
03:34:26,606 --> 03:34:32,836
In this scenario, both ends are already inside AWS, so there is no external network involved at all.

2470
03:34:34,542 --> 03:34:35,620
Worked scenario.

2471
03:34:37,614 --> 03:34:38,160
6.

2472
03:34:39,278 --> 03:34:43,364
A manufacturer needs to connect its factory network to AWS.

2473
03:34:43,726 --> 03:34:51,748
Data must move continuously with predictable latency for a control application and there is budget for dedicated infrastructure.

2474
03:34:52,270 --> 03:34:57,732
It also wants a lower cost connection in place within a week to begin testing before the main link is ready.

2475
03:34:58,350 --> 03:35:00,964
Choose to pause and decide.

2476
03:35:07,054 --> 03:35:09,028
The answers are A and B.

2477
03:35:09,454 --> 03:35:14,084
The scenario contains two different timescales and each one selects a service.

2478
03:35:17,166 --> 03:35:21,860
Predictable latency with budget for dedicated infrastructure points at Direct Connect.

2479
03:35:22,766 --> 03:35:28,160
In place within a week, points at a VPN because Direct Connect takes weeks to provision.

2480
03:35:28,622 --> 03:35:31,748
This pairing is common in real deployments too.

2481
03:35:32,302 --> 03:35:40,420
A VPN is set up first for immediate connectivity and often kept afterwards as a backup path for the Direct Connect link.

2482
03:35:42,062 --> 03:35:43,108
Worked scenario.

2483
03:35:44,080 --> 03:35:49,120
7. A company has grown to 40 VPCs across several teams.

2484
03:35:49,582 --> 03:35:54,308
They increasingly need to communicate with one another and with the corporate data center.

2485
03:35:55,278 --> 03:36:02,480
The network team is maintaining a large and growing number of individual peering connections and wants a single place to manage this.

2486
03:36:02,766 --> 03:36:04,612
Which service should it adopt?

2487
03:36:06,158 --> 03:36:07,268
Pause and decide.

2488
03:36:13,422 --> 03:36:14,532
The answer is B.

2489
03:36:15,118 --> 03:36:22,180
The deciding phrase is a large and growing number of individual peering connections because that describes a mesh and meshes grow badly.

2490
03:36:22,734 --> 03:36:26,180
40 VPCs fully paired would need hundreds of connections.

2491
03:36:27,566 --> 03:36:32,228
Transit Gateway replaces the mesh with a hub, so each VPC connects once.

2492
03:36:33,198 --> 03:36:37,156
Option C is here as the name collision from earlier in the module.

2493
03:36:37,552 --> 03:36:43,012
API, Gateway, and Transit Gateway share a word and do entirely unrelated jobs.

2494
03:36:44,558 --> 03:36:45,604
Worked scenario.

2495
03:36:46,126 --> 03:36:49,796
8. A company is releasing a new version of its website.

2496
03:36:50,318 --> 03:36:57,156
It wants 10% of visitors sent to it and 90% kept on the current version so it can compare error rates.

2497
03:36:57,646 --> 03:37:01,284
Both versions run behind their own load balancers in the same region.

2498
03:37:02,094 --> 03:37:05,572
Which Route 53 routing policy should it use?

2499
03:37:06,192 --> 03:37:07,748
Pause and decide.

2500
03:37:13,934 --> 03:37:15,012
The answer is C.

2501
03:37:15,632 --> 03:37:20,644
A stated percentage split is always weighted routing, and that is the whole question.

2502
03:37:21,072 --> 03:37:25,220
But notice the final sentence that both versions run in the same region.

2503
03:37:25,632 --> 03:37:28,676
That is placed there to eliminate latency-based routing.

2504
03:37:29,646 --> 03:37:34,852
With both endpoints in one region, there is no latency difference for it to act on.

2505
03:37:35,438 --> 03:37:41,072
Scenarios often add a clause whose only job is to close off one specific wrong answer.

2506
03:37:42,382 --> 03:37:53,312
To recap, a subnet is public or private purely because of its root table. Outbound, yes, with inbound no means a NAT gateway, while blocking one address means a network.

2507
03:37:54,574 --> 03:37:58,500
Traffic that must not cross the public internet means a VPC endpoint.

2508
03:37:59,182 --> 03:38:05,700
CloudFront caches, HTTP content, while global accelerator improves the path for any protocol.

2509
03:38:06,272 --> 03:38:12,484
Predictable means direct connect, quick and cheap means VPN, and the two are often used together.

2510
03:38:12,974 --> 03:38:15,332
Next module, monitoring and management.

2511
03:38:17,038 --> 03:38:21,956
This module contains the most frequently co-occurring group in the entire bank analysis.

2512
03:38:22,478 --> 03:38:27,684
Cloud Trail and CloudWatch appear together 23 times and config joins them constantly.

2513
03:38:28,366 --> 03:38:29,924
They are not similar services.

2514
03:38:30,798 --> 03:38:37,872
They are three answers to three different questions, and the exam relies on you separating them.

2515
03:38:39,152 --> 03:38:42,532
Hold these three questions and this module resolves.

2516
03:38:42,832 --> 03:38:44,032
How is it behaving?

2517
03:38:44,192 --> 03:38:45,476
Which is CloudWatch?

2518
03:38:46,192 --> 03:38:48,292
Who did it, which is Cloud Trail?

2519
03:38:48,592 --> 03:38:52,228
What was the configuration and has it changed? Which is config?

2520
03:38:52,672 --> 03:38:56,964
Every question in this area is one of those three wearing different clothes.

2521
03:38:57,792 --> 03:39:02,212
Your first move is to work out which one the scenario is actually asking.

2522
03:39:03,790 --> 03:39:09,028
Here is the separator in one table with the kind of evidence each service actually produces.

2523
03:39:09,486 --> 03:39:14,084
CloudWatch answers, how is it performing and its evidence is a measurement.

2524
03:39:14,638 --> 03:39:17,892
CPU at 90% or a failed status check.

2525
03:39:18,638 --> 03:39:24,580
CloudRail answers who did that and when, and its evidence names and identity and an AP icon.

2526
03:39:25,454 --> 03:39:31,268
Config answers, what was it set to, and its evidence is a configuration state over a period of time.

2527
03:39:32,206 --> 03:39:35,072
A metric, an actor, or a setting.

2528
03:39:36,272 --> 03:39:38,884
CloudWatch has four parts worth knowing.

2529
03:39:39,312 --> 03:39:45,476
Metrics are numeric measurements over time, and most AWS services publish them automatically.

2530
03:39:46,032 --> 03:39:51,172
Logs collects application and system log files you send to it and can query them.

2531
03:39:51,792 --> 03:39:55,872
Alarms watch a metric and act when it crosses a threshold.

2532
03:39:56,352 --> 03:40:02,832
Dashboards combine metrics from several services, regions and accounts, and one detail the exam likes.

2533
03:40:03,152 --> 03:40:08,804
EC2 does not report memory or disk usage by default because those live inside the operating system.

2534
03:40:09,072 --> 03:40:11,012
They require the CloudWatch agent.

2535
03:40:12,558 --> 03:40:14,020
An alarm has three states.

2536
03:40:14,272 --> 03:40:23,072
Okay, ALR and insufficient data. Its actions can notify through Amazon SNS, trigger auto scaling, or stop and reboot an instance.

2537
03:40:23,312 --> 03:40:28,676
Alarms are how monitoring turns into automation because detection on its own changes nothing.

2538
03:40:29,646 --> 03:40:36,836
You can create billing alarms on estimated charges, although AWS budgets is the better answer for cost questions.

2539
03:40:37,358 --> 03:40:40,932
And note the pairing that appears constantly on this exam.

2540
03:40:41,392 --> 03:40:47,044
Notify someone when a metric crosses a value means a CloudWatch alarm plus SN.

2541
03:40:48,878 --> 03:40:54,340
CloudRail records AP, i calls across AWS, whoever made them and however.

2542
03:40:54,766 --> 03:41:03,332
Console clicks CLI commands SDK calls and cloud formation all become API calls. So all of them are recorded.

2543
03:41:05,518 --> 03:41:10,532
The last 90 days of management events are viewable free in the console as event history.

2544
03:41:11,214 --> 03:41:16,548
Creating a trail delivers events to S3 for indefinite retention and can cover all regions.

2545
03:41:16,974 --> 03:41:22,660
And log file integrity validation proves the log has not been altered, which is what auditors ask for.

2546
03:41:24,398 --> 03:41:28,196
The most tested pair on this exam, so hold it firmly.

2547
03:41:29,454 --> 03:41:36,708
Cloud Trail records, API calls, and the identity behind them answering which user changed a security group.

2548
03:41:37,614 --> 03:41:39,744
Its subject is an action by someone.

2549
03:41:40,206 --> 03:41:45,924
CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and alarms answering whether an instance failed a status check.

2550
03:41:46,798 --> 03:41:49,316
Its subject is a measurement over time.

2551
03:41:49,710 --> 03:41:54,244
If a question names a user or asks who, it is CloudTrail.

2552
03:41:54,704 --> 03:42:00,388
If it asks how much, how fast, or whether something failed, it is CloudWatch.

2553
03:42:02,126 --> 03:42:05,572
Config and CloudTrail both keep history, which is why they compete.

2554
03:42:07,598 --> 03:42:15,236
Config keeps the history of what a resource looked like. So it answers whether a bucket was ever public and it evaluates rules for compliance.

2555
03:42:16,304 --> 03:42:19,504
Cloud Trail keeps the history of who did something.

2556
03:42:20,110 --> 03:42:24,036
They are complementary and a real investigation usually uses both.

2557
03:42:24,430 --> 03:42:26,756
Config answers what the configuration was.

2558
03:42:27,182 --> 03:42:29,252
Cloud Trail answers who changed it.

2559
03:42:29,806 --> 03:42:32,708
Any mention of compliance rules points at config.

2560
03:42:34,382 --> 03:42:39,904
Trusted advisor continuously checks your account against AWS best practices.

2561
03:42:40,144 --> 03:42:48,836
There are six categories cost optimization, performance, security, fault tolerance, service limits, and operational excellence.

2562
03:42:49,646 --> 03:42:57,956
Be aware that most study material says five because operational excellence was only added in October 2023.

2563
03:42:59,214 --> 03:43:04,388
The original five are unchanged, so a question listing those five is still correct.

2564
03:43:05,262 --> 03:43:10,436
Every account gets service limit checks free while the full set requires a paid support plan.

2565
03:43:12,142 --> 03:43:15,812
Both services suggest improvements and the exam makes you choose.

2566
03:43:16,064 --> 03:43:25,476
Trusted Advisor runs broad best practice checks across six categories for the whole account, finding idle resources, open ports, and quotas you are approaching.

2567
03:43:26,062 --> 03:43:28,324
Compute Optimizer does one thing deeply.

2568
03:43:29,344 --> 03:43:39,172
It analyzes actual utilization metrics for EC2 instances, auto-scaling groups, EBS volumes, and lambda functions and recommends a specific different size.

2569
03:43:39,824 --> 03:43:43,428
Is this resource the right size? Means compute optimizer.

2570
03:43:43,984 --> 03:43:46,596
Anything broader means trusted advisor.

2571
03:43:48,334 --> 03:43:51,748
Systems manager is a collection of tools for operating a fleet.

2572
03:43:52,462 --> 03:43:57,344
Patch manager applies operating system patches across many instances on a schedule.

2573
03:43:57,678 --> 03:44:05,092
Session manager gives you a shell on an instance with no SSH keys, no bastion host, and no open inbound ports.

2574
03:44:05,824 --> 03:44:09,184
That is why it appears so often in security scenarios.

2575
03:44:09,504 --> 03:44:14,788
Parameter store holds configuration values and secrets encrypted with KMS.

2576
03:44:15,424 --> 03:44:22,596
Run command executes a command across many instances at once and it works on servers outside AWS 2.

2577
03:44:24,302 --> 03:44:30,020
The AWS Health dashboard answers whether a problem is AWS's or yours.

2578
03:44:30,544 --> 03:44:35,268
Service Health shows the general status of AWS services publicly.

2579
03:44:35,918 --> 03:44:43,716
Your account health shows events affecting your specific resources, including scheduled maintenance, instance retirements,

2580
03:44:43,918 --> 03:44:46,276
and issues touching things you actually own.

2581
03:44:47,584 --> 03:44:48,516
The health.

2582
03:44:49,742 --> 03:44:53,584
AP I gives programmatic access for your own tooling.

2583
03:44:54,126 --> 03:44:59,424
And the important boundary is that it reports on AWS's health, not your applications.

2584
03:44:59,824 --> 03:45:02,500
Your application is CloudWatch's job.

2585
03:45:03,950 --> 03:45:07,504
Three smaller services with unmistakable signals.

2586
03:45:07,918 --> 03:45:13,284
X-ray traces a request through a distributed application to find where the time is going.

2587
03:45:13,998 --> 03:45:18,756
Its signal is microservices where nobody knows which component is low.

2588
03:45:19,118 --> 03:45:24,004
Service quotas shows your quotas and lets you request increases from one place.

2589
03:45:24,942 --> 03:45:33,028
And license manager tracks software license usage against your agreements, which links back to the licensing discussion in module 1.

2590
03:45:33,774 --> 03:45:37,092
None appears often, but each is obvious when it does.

2591
03:45:38,798 --> 03:45:41,840
Eight phrasings covering nearly every question in this area.

2592
03:45:42,190 --> 03:45:45,520
Which user deleted the resource means cloud trail.

2593
03:45:45,742 --> 03:45:50,080
Alert me when CPU exceeds a threshold means a cloud watch alarm with SNS.

2594
03:45:50,320 --> 03:45:53,828
Was this resource ever configured incorrectly? Means config.

2595
03:45:54,640 --> 03:45:59,108
Recommendations to cut cost and improve security means trusted advisor.

2596
03:45:59,520 --> 03:46:02,820
Is this instance the right size means compute optimizer?

2597
03:46:03,246 --> 03:46:04,640
Connecting without opening.

2598
03:46:04,960 --> 03:46:07,428
SSH means session manager.

2599
03:46:07,920 --> 03:46:14,077
Is AWS having an incident means the health dashboard? And which microservice is slow means X-Ray.

2600
03:46:15,977 --> 03:46:16,964
Worked scenario.

2601
03:46:17,934 --> 03:46:18,404
1.

2602
03:46:19,278 --> 03:46:23,332
An S3 bucket holding customer records was found to be publicly readable.

2603
03:46:23,886 --> 03:46:34,884
The security team needs three things which I AM identity changed the bucket policy, whether the bucket had ever been public before and whether anyone was alerted.

2604
03:46:35,694 --> 03:46:38,532
The company runs CloudRail, config, and cloud watch.

2605
03:46:38,720 --> 03:46:41,732
Which service answers the first two questions in order?

2606
03:46:42,190 --> 03:46:43,364
Pause and decide.

2607
03:46:49,454 --> 03:46:57,348
The answer is B. This scenario puts all three services in one question, so you have to separate them properly rather than by elimination.

2608
03:46:57,600 --> 03:47:02,148
Which identity made the call is an actor question? So cloud trail.

2609
03:47:03,840 --> 03:47:09,092
Whether the bucket had ever been public before is a state over time question. So config.

2610
03:47:10,030 --> 03:47:21,380
Option D is the near miss and it is a common error because CloudWatch feels like the general monitoring answer, but it holds no record of what a resource's settings used to be.

2611
03:47:24,686 --> 03:47:33,604
An operations team wants to be notified when any production EC2 instance sustains CPU above 80% for 15 minutes.

2612
03:47:34,478 --> 03:47:37,412
An engineer should investigate before users are affected.

2613
03:47:37,998 --> 03:47:40,708
The notification should reach an on-call email group.

2614
03:47:41,550 --> 03:47:43,332
Which combination meets this?

2615
03:47:44,320 --> 03:47:45,920
Cause and decide.

2616
03:47:51,077 --> 03:47:52,857
The answer is B.

2617
03:47:53,454 --> 03:48:00,740
The giveaway is the shape of the requirement rather than the service names, a numeric value, a threshold, and a duration.

2618
03:48:01,550 --> 03:48:05,924
That is the definition of a metric alarm and metrics live in CloudWatch.

2619
03:48:07,310 --> 03:48:10,000
The second half of the pairing matters too.

2620
03:48:10,542 --> 03:48:14,212
CloudWatch detects and SNS delivers the notification.

2621
03:48:14,560 --> 03:48:20,772
Neither does the other's job, which is why this combination appears together so often on this exam.

2622
03:48:22,542 --> 03:48:23,652
Worked scenario.

2623
03:48:27,950 --> 03:48:32,900
A company must patch operating systems on 200 EC2 instances every month.

2624
03:48:33,280 --> 03:48:43,812
It also wants engineers to open a shell on any instance for troubleshooting. But policy forbids opening inbound SSH ports and forbids distributing SSH keys.

2625
03:48:44,560 --> 03:48:48,388
The company wants one service covering both needs.

2626
03:48:49,134 --> 03:48:50,244
Pause and decide.

2627
03:48:56,366 --> 03:48:57,540
The answer is A.

2628
03:48:58,080 --> 03:49:04,772
Systems Manager is a collection of tools rather than a single feature, which is why one service answers both halves.

2629
03:49:05,680 --> 03:49:09,924
Patch manager covers the patching and session manager covers the shell access.

2630
03:49:10,766 --> 03:49:19,332
Option D is worth noting because a Bastion host was the traditional answer to this problem for years and older material still recommends it.

2631
03:49:20,142 --> 03:49:26,980
The scenario forbids exactly what a bastion requires, and session manager exists precisely to replace that pattern.

2632
03:49:28,622 --> 03:49:39,204
What scenario 4. A company's application suddenly slows down. Engineers confirm their own instances are healthy with normal CPU and no errors in application logs.

2633
03:49:39,440 --> 03:49:42,852
They suspect an issue with an AWS service in their region.

2634
03:49:43,598 --> 03:49:50,000
They want to know whether AWS has a problem affecting their resources and whether maintenance is scheduled.

2635
03:49:50,510 --> 03:49:51,588
Where should they look?

2636
03:49:52,206 --> 03:49:53,252
Pause and decide.

2637
03:49:59,630 --> 03:50:00,800
The answer is B.

2638
03:50:01,360 --> 03:50:05,668
The STEM eliminates the obvious answers before you reach the options.

2639
03:50:06,080 --> 03:50:10,820
Their instances are healthy, CPU is normal, and there are no application errors.

2640
03:50:11,280 --> 03:50:17,040
That rules out every service that watches their own resources, which is most of this module.

2641
03:50:17,550 --> 03:50:25,924
What remains is a question about AWS's side of the shared responsibility model and the health dashboard is where that lives.

2642
03:50:26,240 --> 03:50:31,556
Note the account view specifically because it shows events touching resources you own.

2643
03:50:33,070 --> 03:50:36,900
What scenario? 5. A finance team asks for two things.

2644
03:50:37,280 --> 03:50:44,900
First, a list of EC2 instances at consistently low utilization with a recommendation for a smaller instance type.

2645
03:50:45,646 --> 03:50:55,460
Second, a broader review flagging idle load balancers, unassociated elastic IP addresses, and security groups with unrestricted ports.

2646
03:50:56,814 --> 03:50:58,660
Which two services should they use?

2647
03:50:59,182 --> 03:51:00,580
Pause and decide.

2648
03:51:06,798 --> 03:51:08,292
The answers are A and B.

2649
03:51:08,846 --> 03:51:13,828
Two requirements, one service each, and the separator is depth against breadth.

2650
03:51:14,990 --> 03:51:22,276
Recommending a specific smaller instance type from measured utilization is Compute Optimizer, which does that one thing well.

2651
03:51:23,200 --> 03:51:30,480
A broad sweep across load balancers, elastic IP addresses, and security groups is trusted advisor.

2652
03:51:30,720 --> 03:51:41,572
Option D is worth noticing because CloudWatch supplies the utilization metrics that compute optimizer analysis. Supplying data and making a recommendation are different jobs.

2653
03:51:42,958 --> 03:51:48,880
To recap, how is it behaving? Is CloudWatch? Who did it is CloudRail? What was it set to?

2654
03:51:49,040 --> 03:51:49,860
Is config.

2655
03:51:50,254 --> 03:51:55,492
A named user always means CloudRail and a threshold on a number always means CloudWatch.

2656
03:51:56,238 --> 03:52:02,880
An alarm plus SNS is the standard notification pairing where one detects and the other delivers.

2657
03:52:03,886 --> 03:52:11,396
Session manager gives shell access with no open ports and no keys, replacing the bastion host.

2658
03:52:11,854 --> 03:52:17,380
And the health dashboard reports AWS's health while CloudWatch reports yours.

2659
03:52:18,542 --> 03:52:24,320
Next module closes domain 3 with integration, analytics, and AI.

2660
03:52:26,126 --> 03:52:30,180
This module closes domain 3 and covers three families at once.

2661
03:52:30,670 --> 03:52:36,740
Services that connect components together, services that analyze data, and the machine learning lineup.

2662
03:52:37,678 --> 03:52:39,908
The last of those is pure recognition.

2663
03:52:40,430 --> 03:52:45,156
You are given a task and asked to name the service, and the tasks barely vary.

2664
03:52:46,862 --> 03:52:49,220
Three families, three different skills.

2665
03:52:49,600 --> 03:52:57,120
Integration questions are about decoupling, meaning letting components work independently. So one failing does not take the others with it.

2666
03:52:57,280 --> 03:53:01,380
Analytics questions are usually decided by where the data already sits.

2667
03:53:01,680 --> 03:53:04,228
And the AI questions are pure vocabulary.

2668
03:53:04,846 --> 03:53:06,788
Given a task, name the service.

2669
03:53:07,246 --> 03:53:12,100
Knowing that each family is tested differently makes the module much easier.

2670
03:53:13,742 --> 03:53:16,228
These three appear together constantly.

2671
03:53:16,782 --> 03:53:24,036
SNS pushes one message to many subscribers. So use it when several things all need the same notification.

2672
03:53:24,590 --> 03:53:30,116
SQS holds messages in a queue that consumers pull from, each processed once.

2673
03:53:30,574 --> 03:53:34,560
Use it when work must be buffered and done exactly once.

2674
03:53:35,118 --> 03:53:43,600
Event bridge routes events to different targets based on rules about the events content. Broadcast means S and S.

2675
03:53:44,462 --> 03:53:46,560
Buffer work means SQS.

2676
03:53:47,007 --> 03:53:49,412
Root by content means event bridge.

2677
03:53:51,216 --> 03:53:55,108
SNS and SQS are the classic decoupling pair.

2678
03:53:55,630 --> 03:54:00,388
SNS is publish and subscribe, so a message goes to every subscriber.

2679
03:54:00,736 --> 03:54:07,524
It is pushed immediately to endpoints such as email, SMS, Lambda, or even an SQS queue.

2680
03:54:07,856 --> 03:54:09,156
Nothing waits around.

2681
03:54:09,806 --> 03:54:19,332
SQS is a queue of work where each message is processed by one consumer that pulls when ready and messages persist until processed.

2682
03:54:19,982 --> 03:54:22,656
Notify several places means SNS.

2683
03:54:22,926 --> 03:54:25,924
Handwork to a worker means SQS.

2684
03:54:27,374 --> 03:54:33,540
Event bridge is an event bus that routes events to targets based on rules about the event's content.

2685
03:54:34,126 --> 03:54:42,148
AWS services publish events to it, including ECPU state changes, which is how it detects things happening in your account.

2686
03:54:42,656 --> 03:54:48,676
It can also run on a schedule, making it the standard way to trigger a lambda function periodically.

2687
03:54:50,158 --> 03:54:55,940
Targets include Lambda, SNS, SQS, and step functions.

2688
03:54:56,576 --> 03:55:02,180
And it receives events from software as a service application outside AWS as well.

2689
03:55:03,822 --> 03:55:12,356
Step functions coordinates multiple services into a defined workflow with steps, branches, retrees, and error handling.

2690
03:55:12,942 --> 03:55:17,892
The workflow is visual so you can see which step failed and where a run stopped.

2691
03:55:18,926 --> 03:55:22,816
The signal is a multi-step process with order or conditions.

2692
03:55:23,438 --> 03:55:28,516
It commonly orchestrates several lambda functions that would otherwise call one another directly.

2693
03:55:29,102 --> 03:55:35,972
But a single trigger firing one function is not orchestration and reaching for step functions there would be over-engineering.

2694
03:55:37,710 --> 03:55:38,736
Now, analytics.

2695
03:55:39,056 --> 03:55:40,336
And here is the shortcut.

2696
03:55:40,750 --> 03:55:46,884
These questions are usually decided by where the data already sits rather than by what you want to do with it.

2697
03:55:48,782 --> 03:55:53,124
Data already in S3 that you want to query occasionally points one way.

2698
03:55:54,094 --> 03:55:57,572
Data you load into a warehouse for constant heavy analysis.

2699
03:55:57,856 --> 03:55:58,756
Points another.

2700
03:55:59,278 --> 03:56:02,468
Data arriving continuously in real time points to a third.

2701
03:56:03,150 --> 03:56:07,076
Read the scenario for the data's current location and state first.

2702
03:56:08,558 --> 03:56:10,176
7 Analytics Services.

2703
03:56:10,654 --> 03:56:15,536
Athena queries data already in S3 using SQL without loading it anywhere.

2704
03:56:15,776 --> 03:56:19,776
Redshift is a warehouse you load data into for sustained heavy analytics.

2705
03:56:20,096 --> 03:56:24,964
Kinesis handles data arriving continuously that must be processed in real time.

2706
03:56:25,742 --> 03:56:30,948
Glue does extract, transform, and load, plus a catalog of what your data is.

2707
03:56:31,278 --> 03:56:34,756
EMR runs big data frameworks like Spark and Hadoop.

2708
03:56:35,136 --> 03:56:38,180
Open Search Service searches and analysis logs.

2709
03:56:38,862 --> 03:56:43,332
And QuickSight builds dashboards and business intelligence visualizations.

2710
03:56:44,974 --> 03:56:50,980
Athena and Redshift both answer analytical questions with SQL and the separator is whether you move the data.

2711
03:56:51,296 --> 03:56:52,996
Athena is serverless.

2712
03:56:53,456 --> 03:56:59,780
SQL directly against S3 with nothing to provision or load, charging per query by data scanned.

2713
03:57:00,366 --> 03:57:03,012
That suits occasional or ad hoc questions.

2714
03:57:04,014 --> 03:57:12,004
Redshift stores the data in an optimized warehouse you load and keep running, which suits constant heavy analytics.

2715
03:57:12,686 --> 03:57:16,836
Occasional queries on data already in S3 means Athena.

2716
03:57:17,646 --> 03:57:20,068
A team querying all day means Redshift.

2717
03:57:21,326 --> 03:57:27,300
Kinesis handles data arriving continuously, such as click streams, telemetry, and application logs.

2718
03:57:28,334 --> 03:57:32,452
The giveaway phases are real-time, streaming, and as the data arrives.

2719
03:57:33,294 --> 03:57:36,068
Data streams gives you the stream to process yourself.

2720
03:57:36,878 --> 03:57:45,412
Data fire hose delivers it to a destination for you, commonly landing data in S3, Redshift or OpenSearch with no code.

2721
03:57:45,934 --> 03:57:47,652
And be careful of the opposite.

2722
03:57:48,718 --> 03:57:55,524
Batch processing at the end of the day is not streaming and points at glue, EMR, or batch instead.

2723
03:57:57,198 --> 03:57:59,480
One naming warning for this module.

2724
03:57:59,982 --> 03:58:05,844
The exam guide writes it as Amazon QuickSight and describes business intelligence dashboards.

2725
03:58:06,884 --> 03:58:13,668
The product itself was renamed Amazon Quick Suite in October 2025, Then shortened again to Amazon Quick.

2726
03:58:14,446 --> 03:58:17,092
The capability has not changed, only the label.

2727
03:58:18,062 --> 03:58:23,236
So answer with QuickSight on the exam and expect to see Quick when you open the console.

2728
03:58:24,206 --> 03:58:27,344
Module 22 covers this alongside the other renames.

2729
03:58:29,262 --> 03:58:34,564
Now the machine learning family, and it is worth being blunt about how it is tested.

2730
03:58:36,046 --> 03:58:37,796
This is a vocabulary test.

2731
03:58:38,606 --> 03:58:43,204
A scenario names a task and you name the service that does that task.

2732
03:58:43,822 --> 03:58:46,304
There is almost no reasoning involved.

2733
03:58:46,862 --> 03:58:50,404
That makes these the fastest marks on the exam

2734
03:58:51,246 --> 03:58:55,492
if you learn the table and frustrating losses if you do not.

2735
03:58:57,166 --> 03:59:05,412
Eight rows and the exam quotes them almost word for word. Identifying objects, faces, or text in images and video is recognition.

2736
03:59:06,062 --> 03:59:09,988
Understanding text, including sentiment and entities, is comprehend.

2737
03:59:10,862 --> 03:59:12,868
Text into speech is poly.

2738
03:59:15,022 --> 03:59:17,060
Speech into text is transcribe.

2739
03:59:17,838 --> 03:59:20,836
Translating between languages is translate.

2740
03:59:22,064 --> 03:59:27,460
Extracting text and data from scanned documents and forms is extract.

2741
03:59:29,038 --> 03:59:31,584
Building a conversational chatbot is Lex.

2742
03:59:32,750 --> 03:59:37,060
And building, training and deploying your own modules is SageMaker.

2743
03:59:37,742 --> 03:59:38,224
AI.

2744
03:59:59,264 --> 04:00:06,180
Comprehend works out what text means, analyzing sentiment, entities and key phrases from text you already have.

2745
04:00:06,638 --> 04:00:08,964
A scanned document means text tract.

2746
04:00:09,344 --> 04:00:12,292
Understanding the resulting text means comprehend.

2747
04:00:12,864 --> 04:00:15,716
Real solutions often use both in sequence.

2748
04:00:17,358 --> 04:00:20,804
Three services for when the pre-built ones are not enough.

2749
04:00:21,358 --> 04:00:22,148
SageMaker.

2750
04:00:23,118 --> 04:00:27,620
AI is for building, training, and deploying your own machine learning models.

2751
04:00:28,144 --> 04:00:30,852
Its signal is a custom model or a problem.

2752
04:00:31,342 --> 04:00:33,252
The pre-built services do not cover.

2753
04:00:34,030 --> 04:00:38,439
Bedrock provides access to foundation models for generative AI applications.

2754
04:00:38,510 --> 04:00:45,264
And Amazon Q is a generative AI assistant that answers questions about your data and systems.

2755
04:00:45,504 --> 04:00:52,740
Note that Amazon Q business closed to new customers on the 31st of July this year, folding into Amazon Quick.

2756
04:00:54,414 --> 04:00:57,124
4 business applications with clear signals.

2757
04:00:57,678 --> 04:01:03,652
Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center for voice and chat customer service, priced pay as you go.

2758
04:01:04,144 --> 04:01:07,972
Simple email service sends bulk and transactional email at scale.

2759
04:01:08,704 --> 04:01:12,932
Workspaces provides virtual desktops users connect to from anywhere.

2760
04:01:13,838 --> 04:01:19,396
And App Stream 2.0 streams a single application rather than a whole desktop.

2761
04:01:20,430 --> 04:01:23,332
And remember the name collision from module 21.

2762
04:01:24,144 --> 04:01:29,700
Amazon Connect is a contact center while AWS Direct Connect is a network link.

2763
04:01:31,374 --> 04:01:34,052
Eight phrasings covering all three families.

2764
04:01:34,384 --> 04:01:38,884
Decoupling so a slow worker does not block means SQS.

2765
04:01:39,424 --> 04:01:43,492
Notifying several systems of the same event means SNS.

2766
04:01:43,904 --> 04:01:47,620
Running a Lambda function on a schedule means event bridge.

2767
04:01:48,064 --> 04:01:52,068
Coordinating a multi-step process means step functions.

2768
04:01:53,006 --> 04:01:56,580
Querying existing S3 data occasionally means Athena.

2769
04:01:57,184 --> 04:02:00,708
Processing clickstream data as it arrives means kinesis.

2770
04:02:01,294 --> 04:02:04,420
Analyzing reviews for sentiment means comprehend.

2771
04:02:04,704 --> 04:02:07,844
And reading scanned invoices means text track.

2772
04:02:11,984 --> 04:02:18,820
An online shop's checkout writes an order, then calls an inventory service, a payment service, and an email service in turn.

2773
04:02:19,470 --> 04:02:24,292
When the email service is slow, checkouts time out and customers abandon baskets.

2774
04:02:25,070 --> 04:02:30,944
The company wants checkout to complete immediately and the downstream work to happen reliably afterwards.

2775
04:02:31,246 --> 04:02:33,028
Which service should it introduce?

2776
04:02:33,550 --> 04:02:34,944
Pause and decide.

2777
04:02:40,718 --> 04:02:42,314
The answer is B.

2778
04:02:42,832 --> 04:02:46,372
The two requirements together describe a cube precisely.

2779
04:02:51,822 --> 04:02:55,620
Complete immediately means the checkout must not wait.

2780
04:02:55,982 --> 04:02:59,940
And happen reliably afterwards means the work must not be lost.

2781
04:03:00,622 --> 04:03:04,740
That is buffering plus persistence, which is what SQS provides.

2782
04:03:05,646 --> 04:03:15,780
Option A is worth care because SNS does decouple in one sense. But it pushes messages out rather than holding them. So a slow or unavailable subscriber is still a problem.

2783
04:03:17,454 --> 04:03:18,532
Bot scenario.

2784
04:03:19,342 --> 04:03:19,748
2.

2785
04:03:20,430 --> 04:03:25,380
A company wants three things whenever a new file lands in an S3 bucket.

2786
04:03:25,966 --> 04:03:32,804
An email to its operations team, a message written to a logging system, and a lambda function invoked.

2787
04:03:33,678 --> 04:03:41,028
Every one of the three must receive every event, and the file processing application should not know about any of these consumers.

2788
04:03:42,286 --> 04:03:43,492
Which service fits?

2789
04:03:43,886 --> 04:03:44,932
Pause and decide.

2790
04:03:51,072 --> 04:03:52,420
The answer is B.

2791
04:03:52,592 --> 04:03:54,564
Compare this with scenario 1.

2792
04:03:54,830 --> 04:03:59,428
Deliberately, because they are the same family and the answers differ on one word.

2793
04:04:00,174 --> 04:04:04,164
There, the work had to be done once by a worker. So a queue.

2794
04:04:05,038 --> 04:04:12,196
Here, all three consumers must receive every event which is fan out, and that is exactly what a publish and subscribe topic does.

2795
04:04:15,118 --> 04:04:18,532
The final sentence adds the other SNS property.

2796
04:04:18,912 --> 04:04:23,876
Publishers never know who is subscribed, so consumers can be added without changing the application.

2797
04:04:25,484 --> 04:04:26,628
Worked scenario.

2798
04:04:27,118 --> 04:04:35,312
3. An analyst needs to answer occasional questions about three years of application log files that already sit in Amazon S3

2799
04:04:35,472 --> 04:04:40,484
in compressed text format. The questions arise a few times a month and are different each time.

2800
04:04:41,422 --> 04:04:46,020
The company does not want to run infrastructure or load the data anywhere.

2801
04:04:46,862 --> 04:04:48,132
Pause and decide.

2802
04:04:54,350 --> 04:04:55,300
The answer is

2803
04:04:56,014 --> 04:04:56,272
B.

2804
04:04:57,006 --> 04:04:59,684
Three phrases in the stem point the same way.

2805
04:05:00,366 --> 04:05:02,512
The data already sits in S3.

2806
04:05:02,926 --> 04:05:05,540
The questions are occasional and different each time.

2807
04:05:06,126 --> 04:05:08,676
And no infrastructure and no loading.

2808
04:05:10,394 --> 04:05:16,974
Athena matches all three because it queries S3 in place, charges only per query and provisions nothing.

2809
04:05:18,254 --> 04:05:26,774
Redshift would work, but you would pay for a running warehouse to answer a handful of questions a month. That is the wrong shape of cost for the workload.

2810
04:05:28,686 --> 04:05:35,748
Work scenario 4 An insurer receives thousands of scanned claim forms as PDF files.

2811
04:05:36,398 --> 04:05:41,412
It needs to pull values out of name fields on each form into a structured record.

2812
04:05:41,872 --> 04:05:46,212
Then it must work out whether the free text description indicates an urgent case.

2813
04:05:47,022 --> 04:05:50,432
It wants managed services rather than building its own models.

2814
04:05:50,592 --> 04:05:52,484
Choose to pause and decide.

2815
04:05:58,350 --> 04:06:02,340
The answers are A and B, and this is the text trap, then.

2816
04:06:03,150 --> 04:06:05,636
Comprehend pairing applied exactly.

2817
04:06:07,502 --> 04:06:11,396
Get the text out of the document, then work out what it means.

2818
04:06:12,078 --> 04:06:18,660
Option C is the trap for anyone who reasons that a scan is an image and recognition handles images.

2819
04:06:19,566 --> 04:06:24,036
Recognition identifies objects and faces, not form fields.

2820
04:06:25,646 --> 04:06:28,900
And option D phase on a stated constraint.

2821
04:06:29,550 --> 04:06:33,636
Since the company wants managed services rather than building models itself.

2822
04:06:38,192 --> 04:06:42,752
A retailer wants to analyze clickstream data as visitors browse.

2823
04:06:42,992 --> 04:06:48,388
It updates a live dashboard showing which products are being viewed in the last few minutes.

2824
04:06:48,752 --> 04:06:53,316
The data arrives continuously and must not be processed in nightly batches.

2825
04:06:54,190 --> 04:06:57,060
Which service should it use to ingest this data?

2826
04:06:57,774 --> 04:06:58,948
Pause and decide.

2827
04:07:05,166 --> 04:07:06,800
The answer is B.

2828
04:07:07,600 --> 04:07:12,452
This scenario stacks four signals for streaming, so they are easy to recognize as a set.

2829
04:07:14,126 --> 04:07:15,812
Data arriving continuously.

2830
04:07:17,870 --> 04:07:24,196
As visitors browse the last few minutes and explicitly not nightly batches.

2831
04:07:25,040 --> 04:07:32,400
Clickstream analysis is kinesis' headline use case and the phrase appears in AWS's own descriptions.

2832
04:07:32,640 --> 04:07:37,680
Notice too that the final sentence rules out options A and D by naming batches.

2833
04:07:37,840 --> 04:07:41,668
That is the scenario eliminating options for you if you read carefully.

2834
04:07:43,374 --> 04:07:44,516
Warp scenario.

2835
04:07:45,294 --> 04:07:54,532
6. A company records customer service calls and wants to know without a person listening which calls contained and angry customer.

2836
04:07:55,278 --> 04:08:01,476
It has thousands of hours of audio and no machine learning specialist and wants managed services only.

2837
04:08:02,414 --> 04:08:05,360
Which two services should it use in order?

2838
04:08:06,638 --> 04:08:07,940
Pause and decide.

2839
04:08:13,806 --> 04:08:15,972
The answers are A and B.

2840
04:08:16,560 --> 04:08:24,068
Audio in and Meaning Out always takes two steps because no single AWS service does both.

2841
04:08:24,718 --> 04:08:27,588
Transcribe converts speech to text.

2842
04:08:27,840 --> 04:08:31,268
Then comprehend analysis that text for sentiment.

2843
04:08:32,526 --> 04:08:33,040
Option

2844
04:08:33,840 --> 04:08:35,908
C is the direction trap.

2845
04:08:36,846 --> 04:08:42,160
Poly and transcribe are opposites, and picking the wrong one is a common slip under time pressure.

2846
04:08:42,542 --> 04:08:44,452
Poly turns text into speech.

2847
04:08:45,134 --> 04:08:47,364
Transcribe turns speech into text.

2848
04:08:49,326 --> 04:08:52,548
The company already has audio, so it needs transcribe.

2849
04:08:54,190 --> 04:09:03,204
To recap, and this closes domain 3. Broadcast is SNS, queuing work is SQS, routing by content is EventBridge.

2850
04:09:03,886 --> 04:09:07,332
Several ordered steps with error handling is step functions.

2851
04:09:08,974 --> 04:09:15,360
Athena queries S3 in place while Redshift is a warehouse you load and kinesis is data still arriving.

2852
04:09:16,334 --> 04:09:21,034
Textract gets text out of documents while Comprehend works out what text means.

2853
04:09:21,600 --> 04:09:26,212
And poly makes speech from text while transcribe makes text from speech.

2854
04:09:26,766 --> 04:09:28,772
Learn the AI table verbatim.

2855
04:09:30,254 --> 04:09:34,054
Next module opens domain 4 with pricing modules.

2856
04:09:36,014 --> 04:09:43,780
This module opens domain 4, the smallest domain at 12%, but one where the questions are unusually answerable.

2857
04:09:44,400 --> 04:09:51,300
On demand and reserved co occurred 35 times in the bank analysis, more than any other pair in the whole set.

2858
04:09:52,142 --> 04:09:56,356
Purchase options are the single most tested thing in this domain.

2859
04:09:57,646 --> 04:10:01,476
Every discount AWS offers on compute comes from one of two things.

2860
04:10:01,838 --> 04:10:11,268
Either you commit to using something for 1 or 3 years and AWS discounts it because your usage is predictable, or you accept that your workload can be interrupted

2861
04:10:11,982 --> 04:10:14,724
and AWS sells you spare capacity cheaply.

2862
04:10:15,662 --> 04:10:17,060
Commitment or interruption.

2863
04:10:17,998 --> 04:10:23,108
If you know which of those the workload can offer, the purchase option follows immediately.

2864
04:10:24,334 --> 04:10:26,880
Four options and each row is a clade.

2865
04:10:27,360 --> 04:10:32,708
On demand gives up nothing and gets full flexibility at the highest price.

2866
04:10:35,566 --> 04:10:43,600
Savings plans commit to a level of spend for 1 or 3 years and get up to 72% off flexibly across services.

2867
04:10:44,014 --> 04:10:58,240
Reserved instances commit to usage of particular instance attributes for the same terms and discount. And spot instances accept interruption in exchange for up to 90% of spare capacity.

2868
04:10:59,790 --> 04:11:03,140
Read the middle column because that is what you are actually paying with.

2869
04:11:04,814 --> 04:11:08,160
This is the most co-occurring pair in the whole bank analysis.

2870
04:11:08,366 --> 04:11:10,372
So learn the separator precisely.

2871
04:11:10,894 --> 04:11:13,092
On demand has no commitment.

2872
04:11:13,998 --> 04:11:21,412
You pay per second or hour and stop whenever you like, which suits unpredictable or short-lived workloads.

2873
04:11:21,806 --> 04:11:23,652
And workloads you cannot size in.

2874
04:11:24,590 --> 04:11:31,876
Reserved instances commit you for one or three years in exchange for a large discount which suits workloads running continuously.

2875
04:11:32,974 --> 04:11:35,396
The catch is that you pay whether you use it or not.

2876
04:11:35,950 --> 04:11:38,660
Steady and predictable means reserved.

2877
04:11:39,886 --> 04:11:42,788
Unpredictable or temporary means on demand.

2878
04:11:44,526 --> 04:11:49,348
Reserved instances come in one or three year terms with three years discounting more.

2879
04:11:49,966 --> 04:11:56,720
Payment options are all upfront, which saves the most, then partial upfront, then no upfront.

2880
04:11:57,262 --> 04:12:01,668
Standard reservations discount most, but lock the instance attributes.

2881
04:12:02,638 --> 04:12:10,084
Convertible reservations discount less, but let you change instance family, operating system, or tenancy during the term.

2882
04:12:11,406 --> 04:12:20,292
And usefully, in an organization, an unused reservation in one account can apply to another account's matching usage.

2883
04:12:20,910 --> 04:12:22,916
Thanks to consolidated billing.

2884
04:12:24,686 --> 04:12:30,724
With a savings plan, you commit to a dollar amount per hour for one or three years rather than to specific instances.

2885
04:12:33,326 --> 04:12:41,412
Compute savings plans are the most flexible, applying across instance families and regions and covering Fargate and Lambda as well as EC2.

2886
04:12:42,158 --> 04:12:42,800
EC2.

2887
04:12:43,246 --> 04:12:49,412
Instance savings plans discount more, but commit you to an instance family in one region.

2888
04:12:50,542 --> 04:12:57,156
Usage above your commitment is simply built at on-demand rates, so nothing breaks if you exceed it.

2889
04:12:58,830 --> 04:13:02,692
And note that reserved instances never cover Fargate or Lambda.

2890
04:13:04,430 --> 04:13:10,244
Both mechanisms commit you and both discount similarly, so the exam needs another separator.

2891
04:13:11,502 --> 04:13:20,836
Reserved instances are tied to instance attributes such as family, size, region, and platform and can be resold in the reserved instance marketplace.

2892
04:13:21,760 --> 04:13:29,892
Savings plans commit to hourly spend and apply automatically to matching usage with nothing to modify when workloads change.

2893
04:13:30,542 --> 04:13:32,580
The clean tiebreaker is coverage.

2894
04:13:33,774 --> 04:13:39,940
If a scenario mentions Fargate or Lambda alongside EC2, only a compute savings plan covers all three.

2895
04:13:41,742 --> 04:13:47,140
Spot instances offer up to 90% off by using spare AWS capacity.

2896
04:13:47,680 --> 04:13:53,760
The condition is that AWS can reclaim the instance with a 2-minute warning when it needs the capacity back.

2897
04:13:54,862 --> 04:13:59,460
That makes spot suitable for fault-tolerant, stateless, restartable work.

2898
04:14:00,334 --> 04:14:06,692
Batch processing, data analysis, continuous integration builds and rendering are the name use cases.

2899
04:14:08,080 --> 04:14:14,884
It is never suitable for anything that must run to completion uninterrupted or that holds important state in memory.

2900
04:14:16,590 --> 04:14:17,920
Three tenancy options.

2901
04:14:18,318 --> 04:14:25,156
Shared tenancy is the default where your instances share physical hardware with other customers isolated by the hypervisor.

2902
04:14:25,966 --> 04:14:31,428
Dedicated instances run on hardware dedicated to your account but give you no visibility into it.

2903
04:14:32,206 --> 04:14:37,508
Dedicated hosts give you the physical server itself with visibility of sockets and cores.

2904
04:14:38,080 --> 04:14:44,900
That last point is the exam's favorite because socket based or core based licenses you already own require that visibility.

2905
04:14:45,614 --> 04:14:50,276
Both dedicated options are chosen for compliance or licensing, not performance.

2906
04:14:51,950 --> 04:14:53,324
Here is the decision table.

2907
04:14:53,838 --> 04:14:58,404
Steady and running for years means reserved instances or savings plans.

2908
04:14:58,990 --> 04:15:04,132
Steady, but possibly moving to Fargate or Lambda means compute savings plans.

2909
04:15:04,654 --> 04:15:06,864
Interruptible and restartable means spot.

2910
04:15:07,264 --> 04:15:10,788
Short term, unpredictable, or brand new means on demand.

2911
04:15:11,758 --> 04:15:20,704
Bound by a socket-based license means dedicated hosts and needing guaranteed capacity in one availability zone without a discount

2912
04:15:20,944 --> 04:15:24,708
means on demand capacity reservations.

2913
04:15:26,094 --> 04:15:28,580
Read the workload shape rather than its size.

2914
04:15:30,318 --> 04:15:36,260
Purchase options cover the compute line of the bill, but the exam also tests what else costs money.

2915
04:15:36,864 --> 04:15:42,340
Data transfer is the classic surprise because the rules are asymmetric and not obvious.

2916
04:15:43,118 --> 04:15:49,412
It is worth learning the four rules properly because they turn up in cost questions across several domains, not just this one.

2917
04:15:50,830 --> 04:15:53,828
Four rules and they are asymmetric by design.

2918
04:15:54,144 --> 04:15:59,012
Data transfer into AWS from the internet is free, so uploading costs nothing.

2919
04:15:59,694 --> 04:16:04,676
Data transfer out to the internet is charged, and that is where most surprise costs come from.

2920
04:16:05,454 --> 04:16:08,932
Transfer between availability zones in the same region is charged.

2921
04:16:09,838 --> 04:16:14,084
Transfer between regions is charged and costs more than between zones.

2922
04:16:14,384 --> 04:16:19,492
And transfer within a single availability zone using private addresses is free.

2923
04:16:21,166 --> 04:16:23,300
In is free, out costs.

2924
04:16:24,078 --> 04:16:25,476
Check the direction first.

2925
04:16:27,182 --> 04:16:29,636
Here is a domain for change worth knowing.

2926
04:16:29,998 --> 04:16:36,292
The exam teaches a free tier with three parts always free, 12 months free, and short-term trials.

2927
04:16:36,686 --> 04:16:42,436
On the 15th of July 2025, AWS replaced that for new accounts with a credit model.

2928
04:16:43,118 --> 04:16:49,828
New accounts now choose a free or paid plan and receive $100 of credit plus up to 100 more.

2929
04:16:50,144 --> 04:16:52,292
The free plan runs for 6 months.

2930
04:16:53,486 --> 04:16:56,772
Accounts created before that date keep the old model.

2931
04:16:57,422 --> 04:17:00,064
Answer with the 3-part model on the exam.

2932
04:17:01,344 --> 04:17:03,744
Some services cost nothing themselves.

2933
04:17:12,064 --> 04:17:17,824
But you always pay for what they create, so Beanstalk is free while its EC2 instances are not.

2934
04:17:18,094 --> 04:17:21,380
That distinction is the most common distractor shape in this domain.

2935
04:17:22,126 --> 04:17:26,980
Also remember that a stopped instance still builds for its EBS volume.

2936
04:17:27,424 --> 04:17:30,212
N80 gateways, unattached,

2937
04:17:31,854 --> 04:17:37,540
elastic IP addresses and idle load balancers all cost money while doing nothing.

2938
04:17:39,214 --> 04:17:39,584
Web

2939
04:17:43,314 --> 04:17:45,874
application on 20 EC2 instances.

2940
04:17:46,554 --> 04:17:50,774
It has run continuously for 4 years and is expected to continue indefinitely.

2941
04:17:51,630 --> 04:17:54,692
The instance type has not changed and is not expected to.

2942
04:17:55,104 --> 04:17:57,572
The company wants the lowest possible cost.

2943
04:17:58,224 --> 04:18:00,644
Which purchase option should it choose?

2944
04:18:01,024 --> 04:18:02,404
Pause and decide.

2945
04:18:08,654 --> 04:18:09,984
The answer is C.

2946
04:18:10,624 --> 04:18:21,604
Every clause in the stem argues for maximum commitment, four years of history, an indefinite future, and an instance type that has not changed and is not expected to.

2947
04:18:22,894 --> 04:18:28,068
Option D is the instructive miss because convertible reservations sound safer.

2948
04:18:29,038 --> 04:18:31,076
But flexibility is not flea.

2949
04:18:32,064 --> 04:18:37,732
You give up discount to buy the right to change, and this company has told you it will not need to change.

2950
04:18:38,222 --> 04:18:40,964
Do not pay for flexibility, the scenario rules out.

2951
04:18:42,542 --> 04:18:44,144
Worked scenario 2.

2952
04:18:44,398 --> 04:18:46,596
A media company renders video overnight.

2953
04:18:46,944 --> 04:18:53,344
Each render job takes about 20 minutes, jobs are independent, and any failed job can simply be run again.

2954
04:18:53,678 --> 04:18:55,684
Total volume varies night to night.

2955
04:18:56,718 --> 04:19:01,380
The company wants the lowest compute cost and has no deadline beyond the following morning.

2956
04:19:02,384 --> 04:19:03,812
Pause and decide.

2957
04:19:09,646 --> 04:19:11,280
The answer is B.

2958
04:19:11,694 --> 04:19:16,100
Spot needs three things to be safe, and the scenario states all three explicitly.

2959
04:19:16,400 --> 04:19:19,652
The jobs are independent, so losing one does not affect others.

2960
04:19:19,840 --> 04:19:23,364
A failed job can simply be run again, so interruption is recoverable.

2961
04:19:24,320 --> 04:19:29,028
And there is no deadline beyond the following morning, so delay is tolerable.

2962
04:19:29,440 --> 04:19:33,764
When you see those three together, spot is almost always intended.

2963
04:19:34,254 --> 04:19:39,940
Compare this with scenario 1, where customer facing and continuous made spot impossible.

2964
04:19:41,710 --> 04:19:42,820
Work scenario 3.

2965
04:19:43,200 --> 04:19:47,940
A company runs a steady workload on EC2 and wants a large discount for 3 years.

2966
04:19:49,920 --> 04:19:56,580
Over that period, it expects to move parts of the workload to Fargate and to rewrite some components and lambda functions.

2967
04:19:57,358 --> 04:20:00,804
It wants its commitment to keep applying as the architecture changes.

2968
04:20:01,646 --> 04:20:02,852
Pause and decide.

2969
04:20:09,070 --> 04:20:10,212
The answer is C.

2970
04:20:10,990 --> 04:20:15,556
Naming Fargate and Lambda alongside EC2 eliminates three options at once.

2971
04:20:16,302 --> 04:20:23,140
Reserved instances of either kind cover only EC2 and EC2 instance, savings plans are limited to one region.

2972
04:20:23,822 --> 04:20:26,852
Only a compute savings plan spans all three services.

2973
04:20:27,758 --> 04:20:37,028
Option B is the near miss because convertible sounds like the flexible choice. Its flexibility is about instance attributes, not about which service you use.

2974
04:20:38,638 --> 04:20:40,516
Worked scenario 4.

2975
04:20:40,880 --> 04:20:43,972
A finance team is reviewing an unexpectedly large bill.

2976
04:20:44,400 --> 04:20:48,708
Investigation shows charges for a large volume of data leaving the region.

2977
04:20:49,120 --> 04:20:55,844
It also shows idle elastic IP addresses and a N80 gateway processing significant traffic.

2978
04:20:56,654 --> 04:21:01,700
They also see charges for EBS volumes attached to instances stopped for months.

2979
04:21:02,160 --> 04:21:04,676
Which statement about these charges is correct?

2980
04:21:05,454 --> 04:21:07,076
Pause and decide.

2981
04:21:13,198 --> 04:21:14,244
The answer is C.

2982
04:21:14,638 --> 04:21:20,000
This question is built differently from the others because it tests pricing facts rather than service selection.

2983
04:21:20,240 --> 04:21:22,720
Three of the four options state a rule backwards.

2984
04:21:22,960 --> 04:21:25,892
Transfer in is free and out is charged, not the reverse.

2985
04:21:26,720 --> 04:21:32,772
An elastic IP address attached to a running instance is free while an idle one is charged.

2986
04:21:33,678 --> 04:21:36,644
And a stopped instance still bins for storage.

2987
04:21:37,520 --> 04:21:43,844
Each of those is a small fact stated the wrong way around, which is why direction matters so much here.

2988
04:21:45,550 --> 04:21:46,596
Worked scenario.

2989
04:21:48,622 --> 04:21:49,120
5.

2990
04:21:50,382 --> 04:21:56,800
A company must run a legacy database whose license is sold per physical CPU socket.

2991
04:21:57,294 --> 04:22:05,348
The agreement requires the company to demonstrate exactly how many sockets the software runs on and the company already owns the licenses.

2992
04:22:06,254 --> 04:22:09,828
It wants to use them on AWS rather than buying new ones.

2993
04:22:10,286 --> 04:22:11,524
Pause and decide.

2994
04:22:17,550 --> 04:22:18,480
The answer is C.

2995
04:22:18,734 --> 04:22:25,220
Options B and C both give you dedicated hardware. So the words dedicated and isolated will not separate them.

2996
04:22:27,758 --> 04:22:29,476
The deciding word is visibility.

2997
04:22:30,414 --> 04:22:38,084
The company must demonstrate exactly how many sockets the software runs on, and only dedicated hosts expose socket and core counts.

2998
04:22:38,990 --> 04:22:43,120
Dedicated instances isolate the hardware without showing you what is in it.

2999
04:22:43,374 --> 04:22:50,084
Whenever a question mentions bring your own license with socket or core terms, the answer is dedicated hosts.

3000
04:22:51,694 --> 04:22:56,900
To recap, discounts come from commitment or from accepting interruption and nothing else.

3001
04:22:57,550 --> 04:23:04,420
Steady and unchanging means reserved, while steady but moving to Farget or Lambda means compute savings plans.

3002
04:23:05,710 --> 04:23:12,452
Spot needs three things stated in the scenario interruptible, restartable, and no tight deadline.

3003
04:23:13,040 --> 04:23:18,468
Socket based licenses mean dedicated hosts because only they show you the sockets.

3004
04:23:18,720 --> 04:23:22,852
And data in is free while data out costs, so check direction before answering.

3005
04:23:27,054 --> 04:23:30,064
Next module the cost management tools.

3006
04:23:31,310 --> 04:23:33,860
The previous module covered what things cost.

3007
04:23:34,704 --> 04:23:38,948
This one covers the tools for planning, watching, and understanding that spend.

3008
04:23:39,790 --> 04:23:46,660
The services are few and their boundaries are clean, which makes this one of the most reliably answerable modules in the course.

3009
04:23:48,366 --> 04:23:54,224
Every tool in this module answers a question about money at a different point in time.

3010
04:23:54,574 --> 04:23:57,252
Before you spend anything, you estimate.

3011
04:23:57,934 --> 04:24:01,668
While you are spending, you set limits and watch for surprises.

3012
04:24:02,062 --> 04:24:04,868
After you have spent, you analyze what happened.

3013
04:24:05,504 --> 04:24:06,340
Work out

3014
04:24:07,086 --> 04:24:13,476
which of those three the scenario is describing, and the tool follows almost automatically.

3015
04:24:15,118 --> 04:24:16,864
5 tools map to time.

3016
04:24:17,264 --> 04:24:20,704
Before you build, the pricing calculator estimates cost.

3017
04:24:21,358 --> 04:24:28,772
During, AWS budget sets a threshold and alerts you before you exceed it, and cost anomaly detection spots unusual.

3018
04:24:29,326 --> 04:24:30,596
Spend automatically.

3019
04:24:31,086 --> 04:24:35,524
After, cost explorer visualizes and analyses what you actually spent.

3020
04:24:36,384 --> 04:24:42,384
The cost and usage report provides the most detailed line item data for your own analysis.

3021
04:24:42,624 --> 04:24:46,692
Estimating something unbuilt is always the pricing calculator.

3022
04:24:48,142 --> 04:24:53,028
This is the pair the module turns on and it appeared 16 times in the bank analysis.

3023
04:24:53,424 --> 04:24:55,012
Budgets looks forward.

3024
04:24:55,184 --> 04:25:02,692
You set a threshold and receive an alert on either actual or forecast spend and it acts before you overspend.

3025
04:25:03,566 --> 04:25:05,572
Cost explorer looks backward.

3026
04:25:06,478 --> 04:25:14,948
You visualize and analyze historical cost and usage, filtering and grouping by service, tag, region or account.

3027
04:25:15,502 --> 04:25:18,180
Alert me when we exceed means budgets.

3028
04:25:20,174 --> 04:25:23,460
Show me what we spent and where means cost explorer.

3029
04:25:25,264 --> 04:25:32,836
With AWS budgets, you set a threshold for cost or usage and are notified when actual or forecast spend crosses it.

3030
04:25:33,344 --> 04:25:39,108
Budgets can be scoped to a service, a tag, a linked account, or the whole organization.

3031
04:25:40,064 --> 04:25:45,860
Forecast-based alerts are the real value because they warn you before the month ends rather than after.

3032
04:25:47,342 --> 04:25:58,500
There are also budgets tracking reserved instance and savings plans utilization, and budget actions can automatically apply a policy or stop resources when a threshold is breached.

3033
04:25:59,790 --> 04:26:06,340
Cost Explorer visualizes historical cost and usage with graphs and tables you can filter and group.

3034
04:26:06,958 --> 04:26:14,884
Grouping by linked account is how a company reports spend per department when each department has its own AWS account.

3035
04:26:15,104 --> 04:26:17,824
And that exact scenario appears often.

3036
04:26:18,384 --> 04:26:23,556
It also groups by service, region, instance type, and cost allocation tag.

3037
04:26:23,984 --> 04:26:26,980
It provides write sizing and purchase recommendations.

3038
04:26:27,264 --> 04:26:33,476
And it does forecast, but that forecast comes from past usage rather than from a plan for something new.

3039
04:26:34,734 --> 04:26:37,264
Both concern what you already spent.

3040
04:26:37,424 --> 04:26:41,124
And the separator is whether you want an answer or the data.

3041
04:26:41,742 --> 04:26:48,100
Cost Explorer is a tool you look at with graphs and tables in the console that answer questions directly.

3042
04:26:49,006 --> 04:26:51,556
It needs no setup beyond enabling it.

3043
04:26:52,462 --> 04:26:55,684
The cost and usage report is a file due process.

3044
04:26:56,544 --> 04:27:05,828
It is the most granular billing data available, delivered to S3 on a schedule and queried with Athena, Redshift, or a business intelligence tool.

3045
04:27:06,144 --> 04:27:08,804
Console analysis means cost explorer.

3046
04:27:10,094 --> 04:27:12,388
Feeding your own systems means the report.

3047
04:27:13,966 --> 04:27:18,288
The pricing calculator estimates the cost of an architecture before you build it.

3048
04:27:18,448 --> 04:27:23,428
You discard the services and quantities, and it produces a monthly estimate you can share.

3049
04:27:23,918 --> 04:27:29,408
Critically, it never sees your account, so it has no knowledge of what you actually run or spend.

3050
04:27:29,966 --> 04:27:33,604
That makes it useful for business cases and comparing architectures.

3051
04:27:33,902 --> 04:27:36,004
And remember the boundary from module 3.

3052
04:27:36,654 --> 04:27:39,588
It cannot compare against your current on-premises costs,

3053
04:27:40,430 --> 04:27:42,884
which is what migration evaluator does.

3054
04:27:44,174 --> 04:27:50,884
Tags are key value labels on resources and cost allocation tags make those labels appear in billing data.

3055
04:27:51,598 --> 04:27:59,076
That is how a company attributes spend to a project, team, or environment when everything sits in one account.

3056
04:27:59,598 --> 04:28:09,060
They must be activated in the billing console before they appear in reports, and activation is not retrospective, so data from before activation is not tagged.

3057
04:28:09,838 --> 04:28:11,236
Here is the useful pairing.

3058
04:28:12,142 --> 04:28:14,372
Separate accounts per department means

3059
04:28:15,534 --> 04:28:17,124
group by linked account.

3060
04:28:18,190 --> 04:28:21,380
One shared account means cost allocation tags.

3061
04:28:23,022 --> 04:28:28,868
Consolidated billing gives one bin for all accounts in an organization paid by the management account.

3062
04:28:29,488 --> 04:28:37,156
Usage is aggregated across those accounts, so volume discount tiers are reached sooner than any single account would reach them alone.

3063
04:28:38,190 --> 04:28:42,948
Reserved instance and savings plans benefits can apply across accounts too.

3064
04:28:43,328 --> 04:28:48,356
Each account's individual costs remain visible, so nothing is hidden by combining them.

3065
04:28:49,194 --> 04:28:51,300
And organizations itself cost nothing.

3066
04:28:51,566 --> 04:28:53,924
So the saving comes purely from aggregation.

3067
04:28:55,630 --> 04:29:03,748
Cost anomaly detection uses machine learning to learn your normal spending pattern, then alerts you when spend deviates from it.

3068
04:29:05,358 --> 04:29:12,100
The key distinction is that it detects unusual spend rather than spend above a threshold and that decides questions.

3069
04:29:13,070 --> 04:29:18,276
It catches a service suddenly costing more or a resource someone left running by mistake.

3070
04:29:18,608 --> 04:29:25,988
But a department that steadily overspends every month is not anomalous, so anomaly detection stays silent while budgets would fire.

3071
04:29:26,368 --> 04:29:29,892
It is free and alerts go by email or SNS.

3072
04:29:31,470 --> 04:29:35,236
Seven phrasings covering nearly every cost management question.

3073
04:29:35,502 --> 04:29:39,012
Estimating before building means the pricing calculator.

3074
04:29:39,248 --> 04:29:42,528
Alert before exceeding an allocation means budgets.

3075
04:29:42,768 --> 04:29:46,404
Showing last quarter spent by department means cost explorer.

3076
04:29:46,848 --> 04:29:50,564
Detecting an unexpected spike means anomaly detection.

3077
04:29:51,534 --> 04:29:56,452
Loading detailed line items into your own tools means the cost and usage report.

3078
04:29:57,550 --> 04:30:01,732
Attributing spend within one account means cost allocation tags.

3079
04:30:02,606 --> 04:30:07,648
And one bill with better volume discounts means consolidated billing.

3080
04:30:09,038 --> 04:30:09,924
Work scenario.

3081
04:30:10,208 --> 04:30:20,292
1. A finance director wants a monthly report of AWS spend broken down by department. Each department has its own AWS account under one organization with consolidated billing.

3082
04:30:21,070 --> 04:30:28,580
Separately, the director wants an email whenever any department's forecast monthly spend is on track to exceed its allocation.

3083
04:30:30,368 --> 04:30:32,676
Choose to pause and decide.

3084
04:30:38,542 --> 04:30:42,884
The answers are A and B, two requirements and one tool each.

3085
04:30:46,030 --> 04:30:50,692
The backward-looking departmental breakdown is cost explorer grouping by linked account.

3086
04:30:51,886 --> 04:30:54,532
The forward-looking threshold alert is budgets.

3087
04:30:55,694 --> 04:31:01,220
Option E is the sharpest distractor because anomaly detection does send alerts about spend.

3088
04:31:02,126 --> 04:31:08,068
But it alerts on spend that is unusual relative to your pattern, not on crossing a planned allocation.

3089
04:31:08,448 --> 04:31:12,644
A department that steadily overspends every month would never trigger it.

3090
04:31:14,384 --> 04:31:16,044
Work Scenario 2.

3091
04:31:17,198 --> 04:31:21,764
A company is planning to migrate a three-tier application to AWS.

3092
04:31:22,670 --> 04:31:27,364
Before committing, the team needs a monthly cost estimate for the proposed design.

3093
04:31:28,078 --> 04:31:34,372
It covers EC2 instances, an RDS database, and S3 storage for a board proposal.

3094
04:31:35,342 --> 04:31:37,732
Nothing has been deployed to AWS yet.

3095
04:31:44,784 --> 04:31:46,304
The answer is C.

3096
04:31:46,944 --> 04:31:51,748
The final sentence does all the work, and it is worth noticing how efficiently.

3097
04:31:56,782 --> 04:31:59,364
Nothing has been deployed to AWS yet.

3098
04:31:59,824 --> 04:32:07,012
Every other tool in this module reads your actual account usage. So, with no usage, there is nothing for them to read.

3099
04:32:07,726 --> 04:32:12,964
The pricing calculator is the only one that works from a description rather than from data.

3100
04:32:13,390 --> 04:32:20,740
Whenever a scenario says before building, planning, or proposed, check whether the account has any usage at all.

3101
04:32:22,254 --> 04:32:23,236
Worked scenario.

3102
04:32:24,462 --> 04:32:30,308
3. A company runs everything in a single AWS account shared by four project teams.

3103
04:32:30,958 --> 04:32:37,956
Finance wants to know how much each project costs, but every resource sits in the same account, so there is nothing to group by.

3104
04:32:38,464 --> 04:32:41,316
The company is willing to change how resources are labeled.

3105
04:32:41,984 --> 04:32:43,664
What should it implement?

3106
04:32:44,142 --> 04:32:45,316
Pause and decide.

3107
04:32:51,024 --> 04:32:52,452
The answer is B.

3108
04:32:53,134 --> 04:32:56,864
The last sentence is an invitation and spotting those is a useful habit.

3109
04:32:57,104 --> 04:33:06,180
Willing to change how resources are labeled points directly at tagging and it is placed there to make option B correct rather than option A. Option D deserves a note.

3110
04:33:06,894 --> 04:33:12,100
The cost and usage report is more detailed than anything else, but detail is not the problem here.

3111
04:33:12,464 --> 04:33:17,348
Without tags, the line items still contain nothing that identifies a project.

3112
04:33:18,958 --> 04:33:20,644
Worked scenario 4.

3113
04:33:21,806 --> 04:33:27,012
A company's AWS bill is normally stable at around $30,000 a month.

3114
04:33:27,598 --> 04:33:36,484
In one week, spending on a single service tripled with no corresponding change in the business and nobody noticed until the invoice arrived.

3115
04:33:37,390 --> 04:33:43,492
The company wants to be told automatically next time without having to predict what it will be.

3116
04:33:44,174 --> 04:33:45,092
Pause and decide.

3117
04:33:51,264 --> 04:33:52,784
The answer is B.

3118
04:33:53,344 --> 04:33:57,764
Option A is genuinely close, and separating them is the point.

3119
04:33:58,254 --> 04:34:02,404
A budget threshold requires someone to decide in advance what number matters.

3120
04:34:02,926 --> 04:34:07,588
The scenario explicitly says the company does not want to predict what will happen.

3121
04:34:09,424 --> 04:34:12,132
A threshold is a prediction by definition.

3122
04:34:12,782 --> 04:34:18,052
Anomaly detection learns the pattern instead and flags what does not fit.

3123
04:34:18,382 --> 04:34:23,812
Note also that one service tripling might not push the monthly total past a threshold at all.

3124
04:34:25,494 --> 04:34:26,154
Worked Scenario

3125
04:34:29,358 --> 04:34:36,580
A holding company owns 12 businesses, each with its own AWS account, each paying its own bill separately.

3126
04:34:37,422 --> 04:34:42,340
Finance wants a single invoice and has been told the group might also pay less overall.

3127
04:34:42,894 --> 04:34:45,824
The businesses must still see their individual costs.

3128
04:34:46,478 --> 04:34:50,084
What should the company do and why might costs fall?

3129
04:34:50,926 --> 04:34:52,068
Pause and decide.

3130
04:34:57,934 --> 04:34:59,504
The answer is B.

3131
04:35:00,238 --> 04:35:08,612
Three requirements and consolidated billing satisfies all of them without moving a single workload. One invoice from the management account.

3132
04:35:10,190 --> 04:35:12,740
Individual costs still visible per account.

3133
04:35:13,614 --> 04:35:19,504
An aggregated usage reaching volume discount years sooner, which is why the total falls.

3134
04:35:20,078 --> 04:35:27,652
Option A is the overcorrection, solving the billing problem by destroying the account isolation that keeps the businesses separate.

3135
04:35:28,304 --> 04:35:32,740
Consolidated billing exists precisely, so you do not have to make that trade.

3136
04:35:34,350 --> 04:35:37,344
To recap, sort the question by time.

3137
04:35:37,646 --> 04:35:43,076
Before means the pricing calculator, during means budgets, after means cost explorer.

3138
04:35:43,726 --> 04:35:47,556
Nothing deployed yet eliminates every tool except the pricing calculator.

3139
04:35:48,174 --> 04:35:54,308
Budgets alerts on a threshold you chose, while anomaly detection alerts on what does not fit the pattern.

3140
04:35:54,894 --> 04:36:01,860
Separate accounts means grouping by linked account, while one shared account means cost allocation tags.

3141
04:36:02,224 --> 04:36:04,676
And remember tags are not retrospective.

3142
04:36:05,504 --> 04:36:11,556
Next module support plans, partners and technical resources, which closes domain 4.

3143
04:36:13,294 --> 04:36:24,864
This module closes domain 4 and the teaching content. Support plans are among the most predictable questions on the exam because a scenario states a requirement and exactly one tier provides it.

3144
04:36:25,024 --> 04:36:29,264
The reasoning has not changed in years, even though the plan names are about to.

3145
04:36:30,574 --> 04:36:33,904
Almost every support plan question turns on one of two things.

3146
04:36:34,144 --> 04:36:41,060
How quickly AWS responds when something is broken, and whether you are assigned a named technical contact.

3147
04:36:41,504 --> 04:36:44,356
Everything else, including price, is secondary.

3148
04:36:45,230 --> 04:36:50,916
If you can place a scenario against those two questions, you can answer support plan questions in seconds.

3149
04:36:52,814 --> 04:36:54,820
Five tiers including the free one.

3150
04:36:55,374 --> 04:37:04,196
Basic is free and gives documentation, repost, the health dashboard and code, trusted advisor checks, but no technical support cases at all.

3151
04:37:04,846 --> 04:37:06,692
Developer adds business hours,

3152
04:37:07,406 --> 04:37:09,316
email support for one contact.

3153
04:37:10,190 --> 04:37:16,704
Business adds 24-7 phone, email, and chat, plus the full trusted advisor checks it.

3154
04:37:17,102 --> 04:37:21,700
Enterprise on ramp adds faster response and a pool of technical account managers.

3155
04:37:22,144 --> 04:37:27,108
And Enterprise adds the fastest response and a designated technical account manager.

3156
04:37:28,846 --> 04:37:30,884
These are the numbers that decide questions.

3157
04:37:31,182 --> 04:37:34,304
General guidance is 24 hours on every paid plan.

3158
04:37:34,670 --> 04:37:36,164
System impaired is 12 hours.

3159
04:37:36,590 --> 04:37:41,668
Production impaired is 4 hours and production down is 1 hour, both available from business supports.

3160
04:37:42,478 --> 04:37:46,820
And business critical system down is 15 minutes, available only on enterprise.

3161
04:37:47,662 --> 04:37:51,172
Enterprise on ramp offers 30 minutes for that same severity.

3162
04:37:51,534 --> 04:37:56,452
Notice that developer does not cover production severities at all, which is often the deciding fact.

3163
04:37:57,838 --> 04:38:04,100
Business and enterprise co-occurred 17 times in the analysis. So this is the pair to hold firmly.

3164
04:38:04,654 --> 04:38:12,704
Business gives 24-7 phone, email and chat, 1 hour response for production down, and the full trusted advisor checks

3165
04:38:12,944 --> 04:38:13,264
it.

3166
04:38:13,838 --> 04:38:22,544
Enterprise adds a designated technical account manager, 15-minute response for business critical systems down, trusted advisor priority,

3167
04:38:22,798 --> 04:38:25,732
well-architected reviews, and concierge support.

3168
04:38:26,478 --> 04:38:29,316
The single tierest signal is the technical account manager.

3169
04:38:30,382 --> 04:38:32,324
Business has never included one.

3170
04:38:33,966 --> 04:38:38,212
The technical account manager is the most reliable giveaway in this domain.

3171
04:38:38,464 --> 04:38:50,500
It is a named AWS employee who knows your environment and acts as your primary technical contact. They provide proactive guidance, architectural review, and help planning events such as a launch.

3172
04:38:51,374 --> 04:38:54,916
A designated TAM appears only at Enterprise.

3173
04:38:55,982 --> 04:39:00,388
Enterprise on ramp gives access to a pool rather than a named individual.

3174
04:39:01,230 --> 04:39:05,732
And basic developer and business include no TAM in any form.

3175
04:39:07,438 --> 04:39:11,856
Support plans reach into other services, which produces some good distractors.

3176
04:39:12,206 --> 04:39:21,124
Trusted Advisor gives code checks on every account, but the full set requires business or above, and trusted advisor priority requires enterprise.

3177
04:39:22,016 --> 04:39:30,096
The shield response team requires business or enterprise even if you already pay $3,000 a month for Shield Advanced,

3178
04:39:30,350 --> 04:39:31,844
which catches people out.

3179
04:39:32,816 --> 04:39:41,540
Well, architecture reviews with an AWS expert come with enterprise and the support API requires business or above.

3180
04:39:43,214 --> 04:39:45,456
Now the change that affects this module most.

3181
04:39:46,030 --> 04:39:56,292
On the 2nd of December 2025, AWS announced that developer, business and enterprise on ramp all retire on the 1st of January 2027.

3182
04:39:57,198 --> 04:40:04,484
Business support plus replaces developer and business from $29 a month with 30-minute critical response.

3183
04:40:05,614 --> 04:40:10,096
Enterprise support continues with its minimum reduced from $15,000 to $5,000.

3184
04:40:10,816 --> 04:40:15,428
And a new top tier unified operations starts at $50,000.

3185
04:40:16,398 --> 04:40:19,332
On the exam, answer with the old four names.

3186
04:40:20,750 --> 04:40:23,936
The RWS Partner Network has two kinds of member.

3187
04:40:24,526 --> 04:40:30,404
Consulting partners are firms that design, build and manage AWS environments for customers.

3188
04:40:31,246 --> 04:40:37,188
Technology partners are independent software vendors whose products run on or integrate with AWS.

3189
04:40:38,126 --> 04:40:43,652
Partners are certified by AWS and hold competencies in areas such as security or migration.

3190
04:40:44,176 --> 04:40:49,764
Separately, AWS Professional Services is AWS's own consulting organization.

3191
04:40:50,176 --> 04:40:56,676
So a third party doing the work means a consulting partner while AWS doing it means professional services.

3192
04:40:58,286 --> 04:41:06,564
AWS Marketplace is a digital catalog of third-party software that runs on AWS sold by independent vendors.

3193
04:41:06,976 --> 04:41:12,324
Software can be deployed in a few clicks, often as a pre-configured Amazon machine image.

3194
04:41:12,816 --> 04:41:18,148
Charges appear on your AWS bill, so there is no separate procurement process per vendor.

3195
04:41:18,976 --> 04:41:29,296
The classic exam scenario is needing an EC2 instance with a third-party firewall or security tool already installed and keep it separate from service catalog,

3196
04:41:29,454 --> 04:41:31,972
which lists your own approved internal products.

3197
04:41:33,710 --> 04:41:37,572
Several free resources are named directly in the exam guide.

3198
04:41:38,190 --> 04:41:44,336
AWS Repost is the community question and answer service, which replace the old AWS forums.

3199
04:41:45,006 --> 04:41:49,892
The AWS Knowledge Center holds answers to frequently asked technical questions.

3200
04:41:50,830 --> 04:41:55,748
AWS Prescriptive Guidance publishes patterns, strategies, and migration playbooks.

3201
04:41:56,016 --> 04:42:00,388
White papers and documentation are free to everyone, including basic accounts.

3202
04:42:00,656 --> 04:42:06,276
And AWS Trust and Safety is where you report abuse originating from AWS resources.

3203
04:42:07,950 --> 04:42:11,332
Seventh raisings covering nearly every question here.

3204
04:42:12,078 --> 04:42:15,492
A designated technical account manager means enterprise.

3205
04:42:17,038 --> 04:42:18,496
Business critical system.

3206
04:42:19,150 --> 04:42:22,340
Down with 15-minute response means enterprise.

3207
04:42:24,016 --> 04:42:28,900
24-7 phone support with no name contact means business.

3208
04:42:29,742 --> 04:42:33,508
Business hours email only for non-production means developer.

3209
04:42:34,318 --> 04:42:38,628
Documentation and community answers at no cost means basic.

3210
04:42:39,886 --> 04:42:43,364
A third-party firewall pre-installed means marketplace.

3211
04:42:44,270 --> 04:42:48,772
And a partner firm to run a migration means an APN consulting partner.

3212
04:42:50,542 --> 04:42:51,460
Work scenario.

3213
04:42:52,176 --> 04:42:56,644
1. A company runs its main revenue generating platform on AWS.

3214
04:42:56,976 --> 04:43:03,940
It requires a 15 minute response if that platform goes down and a named AWS contact who understands its architecture.

3215
04:43:04,462 --> 04:43:08,260
It also wants an annual well architected review by an AWS expert.

3216
04:43:09,134 --> 04:43:11,204
Cost is not the primary concern.

3217
04:43:11,694 --> 04:43:12,836
Pause and decide.

3218
04:43:18,926 --> 04:43:20,640
The answer is D.

3219
04:43:21,134 --> 04:43:28,320
Enterprise on ramp is the interesting distractor because it satisfies weaker versions of two requirements.

3220
04:43:28,480 --> 04:43:34,948
It gives 30 minutes rather than 15 and a pool of technical account managers rather than a named one.

3221
04:43:35,758 --> 04:43:39,120
Both are close and neither is what was asked for.

3222
04:43:39,662 --> 04:43:44,880
This is a distractor that would be correct if the numbers in the STEM were slightly softer.

3223
04:43:45,422 --> 04:43:48,036
Read the stated figure, not the general shape.

3224
04:43:49,710 --> 04:43:51,040
Worked scenario 2.

3225
04:43:51,280 --> 04:43:56,480
A startup runs only development and test workloads on AWS with no production systems.

3226
04:43:56,720 --> 04:44:02,596
It wants to open technical support cases and receive guidance during working hours at the lowest possible cost.

3227
04:44:03,886 --> 04:44:05,988
Nobody needs to be reachable overnight.

3228
04:44:06,702 --> 04:44:08,132
Which plan should it choose?

3229
04:44:08,686 --> 04:44:10,244
Pause and decide.

3230
04:44:16,142 --> 04:44:17,092
The answer is

3231
04:44:17,600 --> 04:44:17,920
B.

3232
04:44:18,240 --> 04:44:22,820
Option A is a genuine trap and it catches people who optimize on cost alone.

3233
04:44:26,382 --> 04:44:37,600
Basic is free and it is the cheapest, but it includes no technical support cases whatsoever. You get documentation, white papers, repost, and the health dashboard, and that is on.

3234
04:44:38,254 --> 04:44:45,220
The moment a scenario says the company wants to open support cases, BASIC is eliminated regardless of budget.

3235
04:44:45,998 --> 04:44:48,036
And option C fails the other way.

3236
04:44:48,430 --> 04:44:52,000
Adding overnight coverage the startup has explicitly ruled out.

3237
04:44:53,454 --> 04:44:54,564
Worked scenario.

3238
04:44:54,958 --> 04:45:00,420
3. A company already subscribes to Shield Advanced for its public web application.

3239
04:45:01,102 --> 04:45:08,996
During a large denial of service attack, it tries to contact the AWS Shield response team and finds it cannot open the case.

3240
04:45:09,710 --> 04:45:11,876
The company is on developer support.

3241
04:45:13,486 --> 04:45:15,520
Why can it not reach the team?

3242
04:45:16,960 --> 04:45:17,920
And decide.

3243
04:45:23,982 --> 04:45:26,000
The answer is A.

3244
04:45:26,862 --> 04:45:30,756
This question exists because it catches a very reasonable assumption.

3245
04:45:31,280 --> 04:45:39,716
The company has paid $3,000 a month for Shield Advanced and would expect that to include everything Shield Advanced offers.

3246
04:45:40,622 --> 04:45:45,700
But reaching the Shield response team depends on a business or enterprise support plan as well.

3247
04:45:46,320 --> 04:45:52,804
Option D is the sophisticated distractor because it states something true about developer support that is not the reason.

3248
04:45:53,198 --> 04:45:56,836
Watch for options that are factually correct but answer a different question.

3249
04:45:58,446 --> 04:45:59,840
Worked scenario 4.

3250
04:46:00,080 --> 04:46:08,612
A company wants to deploy a commercial network firewall appliance onto an EC2 instance with the software already installed and licensed.

3251
04:46:09,440 --> 04:46:11,780
It does not want to install it itself.

3252
04:46:12,590 --> 04:46:18,884
Separately, it wants an external firm with proven AWS expertise to run its upcoming migration.

3253
04:46:19,822 --> 04:46:22,404
Choose to pause and decide.

3254
04:46:28,480 --> 04:46:30,880
The answers are A and C.

3255
04:46:31,360 --> 04:46:36,100
Option B is the closest miss, and the separator is a single word.

3256
04:46:36,480 --> 04:46:45,924
The company wants an external firm and AWS Professional Services is AWS's own consulting organization rather than an external one.

3257
04:46:46,670 --> 04:46:49,028
Both would be capable of running the migration.

3258
04:46:49,200 --> 04:46:52,324
So the scenario has to tell you which it wants, and it does.

3259
04:46:53,710 --> 04:46:57,714
Option B is the marketplace and service catalog distinction from module nine.

3260
04:46:58,494 --> 04:47:02,148
Third party commercial software means marketplace.

3261
04:47:03,822 --> 04:47:06,660
To recap, and this closes domain 4.

3262
04:47:07,342 --> 04:47:14,116
A designated technical account manager means enterprise, and it is the single clearest signal in this domain.

3263
04:47:15,150 --> 04:47:17,360
15 minutes means enterprise.

3264
04:47:17,678 --> 04:47:19,940
30 minutes means enterprise.

3265
04:47:20,430 --> 04:47:23,364
On ramp, one hour means business.

3266
04:47:24,238 --> 04:47:27,840
Basic includes no technical support cases at all.

3267
04:47:28,398 --> 04:47:34,724
Shield Advanced still needs a business or enterprise plan to reach the Shield response team.

3268
04:47:35,022 --> 04:47:40,228
And Marketplace sells third party software while service catalog lists your own.

3269
04:47:40,814 --> 04:47:42,980
That completes the teaching modules.

3270
04:47:43,438 --> 04:47:47,300
Next come the three cross cutting modules and the practice exams.

3271
04:47:48,974 --> 04:47:54,436
This module contains almost no new information, and that is exactly the point.

3272
04:47:54,894 --> 04:47:57,124
Everything here you have already met.

3273
04:47:57,646 --> 04:48:05,828
What this module does is put the services that appear together as options side by side and give you one sentence that separates each pair.

3274
04:48:06,656 --> 04:48:11,460
It converts understanding into recognition, which is what you need under time pressure.

3275
04:48:12,942 --> 04:48:16,836
A word on how this list was chosen because it is not guesswork.

3276
04:48:17,710 --> 04:48:24,356
900 AP practice questions were analyzed for which services co-occur inside the same question.

3277
04:48:24,974 --> 04:48:31,492
The pairs in this module are the ones that genuinely turn up together as competing options ranked by how often.

3278
04:48:32,430 --> 04:48:38,980
That is a different list from the one you would write by intuition and it is a more useful one.

3279
04:48:40,750 --> 04:48:46,084
Pair 1 and the single most frequently co-occurring pair in the whole analysis.

3280
04:48:48,014 --> 04:48:49,988
Cloud Trail records who did what.

3281
04:48:50,884 --> 04:48:53,184
Which user updated that security group?

3282
04:48:53,584 --> 04:48:55,364
Which identity deleted that bucket?

3283
04:48:56,558 --> 04:48:58,864
It is an audit trail of API calls.

3284
04:48:59,598 --> 04:49:02,020
CloudWatch records how things are behaving.

3285
04:49:02,958 --> 04:49:07,332
CPU metrics, log data, alarms, status checks.

3286
04:49:07,726 --> 04:49:11,972
The separator is whether the question is asking about an actor or a measurement.

3287
04:49:12,336 --> 04:49:17,284
If a user or identity is named in the STEM, it is loud trail almost every time.

3288
04:49:18,894 --> 04:49:21,696
Pair 2, Shield and WA.

3289
04:49:23,150 --> 04:49:29,220
AF both protect web applications and both attach to similar places, which is why they compete.

3290
04:49:30,158 --> 04:49:31,780
Shield deals with volume.

3291
04:49:32,206 --> 04:49:41,892
It absorbs distributed denial of service attacks at the network and transport layers, and Shield standard is free and automatic for every AWS customer.

3292
04:49:42,830 --> 04:49:45,188
WAF deals with content.

3293
04:49:45,536 --> 04:49:52,964
It reads each HTTP request and applies rules about SQL injection, cross-site scripting, source address or country.

3294
04:49:53,678 --> 04:49:54,896
Flooding means Shield.

3295
04:49:55,502 --> 04:49:58,148
Inspecting the request means WAF.

3296
04:49:59,854 --> 04:50:05,856
Pair 3, Guard Duty and Inspector are both detection services and they are constantly swapped.

3297
04:50:06,254 --> 04:50:07,972
Guard duty watches behavior.

3298
04:50:08,206 --> 04:50:11,812
It reads logs and network flow to find unusual.

3299
04:50:12,302 --> 04:50:17,892
API calls, crypto mining or credentials being used from somewhere improbable.

3300
04:50:19,086 --> 04:50:20,964
It finds an attack in progress.

3301
04:50:21,902 --> 04:50:28,324
Inspector scans for weaknesses, unpatched software vulnerabilities on EC2 instances,

3302
04:50:29,134 --> 04:50:34,244
in container images and in lambda functions, plus unintended network exposure.

3303
04:50:34,958 --> 04:50:36,996
It finds what could be exploited.

3304
04:50:37,614 --> 04:50:39,972
Active attack means guard duty.

3305
04:50:40,558 --> 04:50:42,852
Latent weakness means inspector.

3306
04:50:44,622 --> 04:50:46,852
Pair 4 completes the detection trio.

3307
04:50:47,470 --> 04:50:49,572
Machi looks inside your data.

3308
04:50:50,016 --> 04:50:58,948
It uses machine learning and pattern matching over Amazon S3 to find personally identifiable information and other sensitive content and classify it.

3309
04:50:59,726 --> 04:51:04,612
Guard duty never looks inside your data, only at behavior around it.

3310
04:51:05,326 --> 04:51:06,724
So the separator is easy.

3311
04:51:07,118 --> 04:51:13,700
If the word sensitive data, personally identifiable information, or S3 content appear, it is Makey.

3312
04:51:14,158 --> 04:51:17,732
Makey is the only one of the three that reads your actual content.

3313
04:51:19,150 --> 04:51:21,956
Pair 5 is subtle and it appears often.

3314
04:51:22,336 --> 04:51:30,468
Both config and cloud trail keep history. Config keeps the history of what a resource looked like and can evaluate rules against it.

3315
04:51:30,896 --> 04:51:32,576
Was this bucket ever public?

3316
04:51:32,878 --> 04:51:37,124
Is this volume encrypted? Does this instance have the required tags?

3317
04:51:37,806 --> 04:51:40,548
Cloud Trail keeps the history of who did something.

3318
04:51:41,070 --> 04:51:44,176
The two are complementary and questions exploit that.

3319
04:51:44,496 --> 04:51:47,056
Config answers what the configuration was.

3320
04:51:47,376 --> 04:51:49,444
Cloud Trail answers who changed it.

3321
04:51:49,776 --> 04:51:52,736
Any mention of compliance rules points at config.

3322
04:51:54,542 --> 04:51:55,344
Pair 6.

3323
04:51:55,662 --> 04:52:01,412
Trusted Advisor and Config both tell you something is wrong, and the difference is whose standard is being applied.

3324
04:52:02,286 --> 04:52:12,836
Trusted Advisor applies AWS's best practices across cost optimization, performance, security, fault tolerance, and service limits, and you did not write those checks.

3325
04:52:13,806 --> 04:52:20,548
Config applies rules that you enable or author. So you decide what compliance means for your organization.

3326
04:52:21,184 --> 04:52:24,548
Best practice recommendation means trusted advisor.

3327
04:52:26,222 --> 04:52:28,644
Compliance with our policy means config.

3328
04:52:30,446 --> 04:52:32,624
Pair 7, the VPC classic.

3329
04:52:32,846 --> 04:52:41,156
Security groups set on an instance's network interface are stateful, so return traffic is allowed automatically and only have allow rules.

3330
04:52:42,350 --> 04:52:43,024
Network.

3331
04:52:43,342 --> 04:52:48,228
ACL sit on a subnet and are stateless, so return traffic needs its own rule.

3332
04:52:49,134 --> 04:52:53,184
They have both allow and deny rules evaluated in numbered order.

3333
04:52:53,518 --> 04:52:54,468
Two separators.

3334
04:52:55,118 --> 04:53:03,104
The word instance or subnet in the question and the requirement to block a specific address, which only a network ACL can do

3335
04:53:03,278 --> 04:53:05,028
because security groups have no deny.

3336
04:53:06,766 --> 04:53:11,716
Pair 8 is a pure naming trap and it showed up 15 times in the analysis.

3337
04:53:12,430 --> 04:53:22,788
AWS Direct Connect is a dedicated private physical connection between your premises and AWS used for consistent bandwidth and hybrid networking.

3338
04:53:23,758 --> 04:53:31,824
Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center for voice and chat customer service. They share one word and absolutely nothing else.

3339
04:53:32,064 --> 04:53:33,796
Direct Connect is a cable.

3340
04:53:34,224 --> 04:53:38,724
Amazon Connect is a call center. Read the whole name every time.

3341
04:53:40,462 --> 04:53:41,220
Pair 9.

3342
04:53:41,924 --> 04:53:46,464
Plain IAM manages identity inside a single AWS account.

3343
04:53:46,784 --> 04:53:49,316
Users, groups, roles and policies.

3344
04:53:49,710 --> 04:53:58,756
IAM Identity Center gives people single sign on across many accounts and connects an existing corporate identity provider or directly.

3345
04:53:59,664 --> 04:54:05,732
It used to be called AWS Single Sign On and older material still uses that name.

3346
04:54:06,606 --> 04:54:08,644
The separator is the number of accounts.

3347
04:54:10,222 --> 04:54:12,304
One account means IAM.

3348
04:54:13,486 --> 04:54:19,876
Many accounts or an external directory means IAM identity center.

3349
04:54:21,614 --> 04:54:22,404
Pair 10.

3350
04:54:27,438 --> 04:54:31,300
Budgets looks forward and cost explorer looks backward.

3351
04:54:33,646 --> 04:54:40,324
Budgets is where you set a threshold and receive an alert when forecast or actual spend crosses it.

3352
04:54:41,614 --> 04:54:46,304
Cost explorer is where you visualize and analyze historical cost and usage.

3353
04:54:46,510 --> 04:54:50,276
Filtering and grouping by service, tag or linked account.

3354
04:54:50,798 --> 04:54:56,676
So a question about reviewing spend by department across several accounts is cost explorer.

3355
04:54:57,102 --> 04:55:00,544
A question about being warned before overspending is budgets.

3356
04:55:00,878 --> 04:55:02,660
Alert me means budgets.

3357
04:55:03,054 --> 04:55:05,424
Show me means cost explorer.

3358
04:55:06,638 --> 04:55:09,156
Then more pairs at one line each.

3359
04:55:09,584 --> 04:55:13,860
Relational means RDS. Key value means dynamo DB.

3360
04:55:14,190 --> 04:55:18,404
Operational lookups means dynamo DB. Analytics means red shift.

3361
04:55:19,584 --> 04:55:27,620
Aurora is an RDS engine for my SQL and Postgres. So Oracle or SQL server means plain RDS.

3362
04:55:28,494 --> 04:55:30,864
Linux and NFS means EFS.

3363
04:55:31,118 --> 04:55:33,444
Windows and SMB means FSX.

3364
04:55:33,824 --> 04:55:45,092
A disk on one instance is EBS. Objects at any scale is S3. AWS's own orchestrator is ECS. Kubernetes is EKS. No servers means Fargate.

3365
04:55:45,742 --> 04:55:46,944
Region is geography.

3366
04:55:47,184 --> 04:55:50,372
Availability zone is isolated data centers.

3367
04:55:50,894 --> 04:55:53,028
Edge location is content delivery.

3368
04:55:53,550 --> 04:55:58,276
And cloud formation defines any infrastructure while Beanstalk just runs your app.

3369
04:55:59,950 --> 04:56:01,028
Worked scenario

3370
04:56:02,702 --> 04:56:03,172
1.

3371
04:56:03,886 --> 04:56:06,788
A security team is investigating an incident.

3372
04:56:07,246 --> 04:56:15,492
Overnight, an Amazon S3 bucket that had been private became publicly readable, and by morning, it was private again.

3373
04:56:16,174 --> 04:56:17,668
The team needs two things.

3374
04:56:18,318 --> 04:56:25,924
Which I AM identity made the change and whether the bucket has ever been publicly readable at any point in the last year.

3375
04:56:26,734 --> 04:56:28,864
Which two services should they use?

3376
04:56:29,134 --> 04:56:30,404
Pause and decide.

3377
04:56:37,166 --> 04:56:39,012
The answers are A and C.

3378
04:56:39,662 --> 04:56:44,832
This scenario deliberately requires you to split the two most confused pairs at once.

3379
04:56:45,166 --> 04:56:49,028
Which identity made the change is a WHO question. So Cloud Trail.

3380
04:56:50,606 --> 04:56:55,072
Has it ever been public over the last year? Is a configuration state over time question.

3381
04:56:55,246 --> 04:56:56,196
So config.

3382
04:56:57,710 --> 04:56:58,852
Look at D though.

3383
04:56:59,758 --> 04:57:08,132
Mckay. Is genuinely the sensible next step in a real investigation and it is wrong here purely because the question did not ask about the data.

3384
04:57:09,710 --> 04:57:16,228
That pattern, a correct real-world action that answers a question nobody asked, is the most common distractor design on this exam.

3385
04:57:17,902 --> 04:57:19,140
What scenario?

3386
04:57:19,630 --> 04:57:28,432
2. A public web application behind an application load balancer is being hit from thousands of addresses. There are two separate problems.

3387
04:57:28,782 --> 04:57:36,548
A flood of meaningless traffic saturating the connection and a smaller set of requests attempting SQL injection against the login form.

3388
04:57:37,390 --> 04:57:39,524
Which combination addresses both?

3389
04:57:40,206 --> 04:57:41,636
Pause and decide.

3390
04:57:48,270 --> 04:57:49,632
The answer is B.

3391
04:57:50,030 --> 04:57:55,940
This scenario plants two problems at two different layers so that neither service alone can cover it.

3392
04:57:56,462 --> 04:58:01,232
Volume saturating the connection is a layer 3 and 4 problem, which is Shield.

3393
04:58:02,030 --> 04:58:07,172
SQL injection in a request is a layer 7 content problem, which is WAF.

3394
04:58:08,046 --> 04:58:09,988
Option D is worth dwelling on.

3395
04:58:10,862 --> 04:58:14,308
Guard duty would notice both of these and tell you about them.

3396
04:58:14,926 --> 04:58:20,672
But detecting and protecting are different jobs, and the question asks how to address the problems.

3397
04:58:21,166 --> 04:58:28,068
That distinction between detection services and protection services separates several questions on this exam.

3398
04:58:29,806 --> 04:58:31,460
Worked scenario 3.

3399
04:58:31,918 --> 04:58:37,252
A company runs Windows servers on premises that share files using the SMB protocol.

3400
04:58:37,646 --> 04:58:45,348
It is migrating to AWS and wants a fully managed file system. The existing Windows applications can mount without modification.

3401
04:58:46,062 --> 04:58:53,860
A second team runs Linux application servers that need a shared file system mounted by many instances at once.

3402
04:58:54,352 --> 04:58:57,220
Which two services should the company use?

3403
04:58:59,374 --> 04:59:00,708
Pause and decide.

3404
04:59:07,374 --> 04:59:08,804
The answers are C and D.

3405
04:59:09,232 --> 04:59:12,900
The protocol names are doing all the work here, which is typical.

3406
04:59:13,232 --> 04:59:16,772
SMB and Windows means FSX for Windows File Server.

3407
04:59:17,232 --> 04:59:24,900
Many Linux instances sharing a file system means EFS. Notice how the phrase without modification quietly eliminates S3.

3408
04:59:25,774 --> 04:59:29,924
Object storage is excellent, but it is not a mountable file system.

3409
04:59:30,254 --> 04:59:33,604
So applications written against a file share would have to be rewritten.

3410
04:59:33,934 --> 04:59:38,660
Watch for phrases like without modification and without changing the application.

3411
04:59:40,014 --> 04:59:43,792
They almost always exist to eliminate one specific option.

3412
04:59:45,102 --> 04:59:45,988
Worked scenario.

3413
04:59:46,798 --> 04:59:52,192
4. A finance director wants a monthly report of AWS spend broken down by department.

3414
04:59:52,430 --> 04:59:58,532
Each department has its own AWS account and all accounts sit under one organization with consolidated billing.

3415
04:59:59,054 --> 05:00:06,724
Separately, the director wants an email whenever any single department's forecast monthly spend exceeds its allocation.

3416
05:00:08,014 --> 05:00:10,500
Which two services meet these requirements?

3417
05:00:11,278 --> 05:00:12,452
Pause and decide.

3418
05:00:18,862 --> 05:00:23,236
The answers are A and B, and this is the pair 10 separator applied directly.

3419
05:00:27,246 --> 05:00:36,516
The backward-looking departmental breakdown is cost explorer, which can group by linked account. The forward-looking threshold alert is budgets.

3420
05:00:36,752 --> 05:00:40,752
Look closely at Edo because it is the best distractor here.

3421
05:00:41,358 --> 05:00:46,116
Cost anomaly detection does send alerts about spend, so it feels right.

3422
05:00:46,894 --> 05:00:52,676
But it alerts on unusual spikes relative to your normal pattern, not on crossing a planned allocation.

3423
05:00:53,582 --> 05:00:59,972
A department that steadily overspends every month is not anomalous, and anomaly detection would stay silent.

3424
05:01:02,752 --> 05:01:14,244
5. A retailer needs two things from its data. Its website must look up a customer's basket by customer identifier thousands of times a second in single digit milliseconds.

3425
05:01:14,830 --> 05:01:21,476
Its analysts separately need to aggregate four years of completed orders to produce quarterly revenue reports.

3426
05:01:21,792 --> 05:01:23,780
Which two services should it use?

3427
05:01:25,230 --> 05:01:26,244
Pause and decide.

3428
05:01:32,942 --> 05:01:34,884
The answers are B and C.

3429
05:01:35,374 --> 05:01:41,412
This is the DynamoDB and Redshift separator applied directly, and the test is who is waiting.

3430
05:01:42,254 --> 05:01:46,276
A website waiting thousands of times a second means DynamoDB.

3431
05:01:46,926 --> 05:01:51,940
Analyst waiting for a quarterly report over four years of data means redshift.

3432
05:01:52,558 --> 05:01:54,340
Option D is worth a moment.

3433
05:01:55,344 --> 05:02:04,068
ElastiCache helps when the same few items are read repeatedly, but every customer's basket is different, so there is almost nothing to cache.

3434
05:02:05,518 --> 05:02:06,628
Worked scenario.

3435
05:02:07,310 --> 05:02:07,872
6.

3436
05:02:08,654 --> 05:02:16,868
A platform team runs containerized services and maintains a large library of existing Kubernetes manifests it wants to keep using.

3437
05:02:17,358 --> 05:02:22,468
It has decided it no longer wants to patch or size any EC2 instances.

3438
05:02:23,072 --> 05:02:26,052
It also needs somewhere to store its container images.

3439
05:02:26,992 --> 05:02:31,204
Which two services should it use for orchestration and compute?

3440
05:02:31,632 --> 05:02:33,060
Pause and decide.

3441
05:02:39,566 --> 05:02:41,220
The answers are B and C.

3442
05:02:41,966 --> 05:02:45,252
This scenario asks you to make two separations at once.

3443
05:02:45,838 --> 05:02:55,972
Kubernetes manifests decide EKS over ECS. No longer patching or sizing instances decides Fargate over the EC2 launch type.

3444
05:02:57,870 --> 05:03:03,332
Option D is the interesting one because ECR genuinely is needed here for the images.

3445
05:03:04,272 --> 05:03:11,108
It is wrong only because the question asks specifically about orchestration and compute, and ECR does neither.

3446
05:03:11,566 --> 05:03:16,100
Read what the question asks for, not what the company needs overall.

3447
05:03:17,712 --> 05:03:18,884
Worked scenario.

3448
05:03:19,232 --> 05:03:25,828
7. A company already runs its web application across three availability zones behind a load balancer.

3449
05:03:26,592 --> 05:03:30,500
Availability has been excellent and nothing has failed in a year.

3450
05:03:30,990 --> 05:03:38,564
Users in Asia and South America still report pages taking several seconds to load while users near the region are unaffected.

3451
05:03:40,014 --> 05:03:41,312
What should the company do?

3452
05:03:41,552 --> 05:03:42,852
Pause and decide.

3453
05:03:49,472 --> 05:03:50,724
The answer is B.

3454
05:03:51,152 --> 05:03:54,628
One sentence decides this, and it is easy to skim past.

3455
05:03:55,392 --> 05:03:57,924
Users near the region are unaffected.

3456
05:03:58,272 --> 05:04:06,116
That single fact eliminates every explanation involving the application itself because a slow application would be slow for everyone.

3457
05:04:06,958 --> 05:04:11,108
What is left is distance, and distance is what edge locations exist to solve.

3458
05:04:12,334 --> 05:04:15,652
Availability zones give you high availability inside a region.

3459
05:04:16,462 --> 05:04:20,004
They do nothing whatsoever about how far away a user is.

3460
05:04:21,390 --> 05:04:22,372
Worked scenario.

3461
05:04:26,830 --> 05:04:27,232
8.

3462
05:04:28,718 --> 05:04:32,292
An insurance company is opening a customer service center.

3463
05:04:32,622 --> 05:04:40,228
It needs a pay-as-you-go system for handling inbound customer calls and chats, which remote agents will use from home.

3464
05:04:40,974 --> 05:04:48,912
The same company separately needs a dedicated private network link between its head office and AWS

3465
05:04:49,232 --> 05:04:52,196
with consistent bandwidth for a claim system.

3466
05:04:52,718 --> 05:04:55,364
Which two services meet these requirements?

3467
05:04:55,918 --> 05:04:57,124
Pause and decide.

3468
05:05:03,728 --> 05:05:09,828
The answers are A and B, and this scenario exists purely to make the name collision explicit.

3469
05:05:10,128 --> 05:05:14,148
Both correct answers contain the word connect and they have nothing else in common.

3470
05:05:14,528 --> 05:05:17,956
Amazon Connect is a contact center for talking to customers.

3471
05:05:18,382 --> 05:05:22,468
AWS Direct Connect is a cable between your building and AWS.

3472
05:05:23,406 --> 05:05:28,612
A company can genuinely need both at once, which is exactly what this question describes.

3473
05:05:29,088 --> 05:05:31,396
Read the whole service name every time.

3474
05:05:33,166 --> 05:05:34,564
How to use this module.

3475
05:05:35,086 --> 05:05:41,008
Rewatch it last in the two days before your exam because it is built for recall rather than for learning.

3476
05:05:41,248 --> 05:05:48,452
Learn the separator sentence rather than the service description because the separator is what you can actually apply in 90 seconds.

3477
05:05:49,198 --> 05:05:54,468
When two options both look light, name the pair first and then apply its separator.

3478
05:05:55,086 --> 05:05:59,236
Remember that detection services tell you and protection services stop it.

3479
05:05:59,598 --> 05:06:06,372
And watch for that most common distractor of all, the correct real-world action that answers a question nobody asked.

3480
05:06:07,888 --> 05:06:16,548
This module exists because certification content lags the AWS catalog, and on this exam, the gap has become unusually wide.

3481
05:06:17,326 --> 05:06:25,220
Several answers that are still marked correct describe services that are close to new customers renamed or scheduled for shutdown.

3482
05:06:25,774 --> 05:06:34,564
Nobody else will tell you this, and you will hit every one of these contradictions the moment you open current AWS documentation.

3483
05:06:36,206 --> 05:06:40,196
Before anything else, here is the rule that governs this whole module.

3484
05:06:40,622 --> 05:06:43,844
In the exam, answer from the documented catalog.

3485
05:06:44,686 --> 05:06:51,876
The exam guide and the question bank define what is correct on the day, and arguing with a scoring engine is not a strategy.

3486
05:06:52,750 --> 05:06:57,252
Outside the exam, know the reality because you will be working with the current console.

3487
05:06:57,806 --> 05:06:59,172
Both halves matter.

3488
05:06:59,694 --> 05:07:04,608
The mark and the truth are different things here, and the gap is temporary.

3489
05:07:05,966 --> 05:07:08,292
A little context on why the gap opened.

3490
05:07:08,848 --> 05:07:16,208
CLF-C02 launched in September 2023. So its question pool predates most of these changes.

3491
05:07:16,448 --> 05:07:23,748
Since then, AWS has run two large portfolio calls, one in July 2024 and another in late 2025.

3492
05:07:24,590 --> 05:07:30,468
The exam guides in scope service list gets refreshed more often than the question pool gets rewritten.

3493
05:07:30,798 --> 05:07:33,888
So you can find questions in circulation testing services.

3494
05:07:34,190 --> 05:07:36,164
The current guide does not list at all.

3495
05:07:36,528 --> 05:07:39,012
Where those two disagree, trust the guide.

3496
05:07:40,750 --> 05:07:46,596
Start with the largest single change because it affects more questions than anything else here.

3497
05:07:47,886 --> 05:07:54,048
The RWS Snow family, the physical devices you ship data on, is being wound down completely.

3498
05:07:54,382 --> 05:07:58,084
This matters because Snow devices are a classic exam answer.

3499
05:07:58,862 --> 05:08:03,396
Large data set, poor network, tight deadline, ship a snowball.

3500
05:08:04,462 --> 05:08:07,396
That reasoning appears throughout older question banks.

3501
05:08:08,910 --> 05:08:10,048
Here is the timeline.

3502
05:08:10,382 --> 05:08:14,148
Snowmobile, the shipping container, retired in March 2024.

3503
05:08:14,794 --> 05:08:19,524
Snowcone discontinued in November 2024 with support ending a year later.

3504
05:08:20,014 --> 05:08:22,692
The older snowball models went at the same time.

3505
05:08:23,662 --> 05:08:27,524
Snowball Edge closed to new customers on the 7th of November

3506
05:08:28,558 --> 05:08:29,668
2025

3507
05:08:30,446 --> 05:08:38,436
and support for all snowball devices ends in commercial regions on the 31st of December 2026.

3508
05:08:39,374 --> 05:08:48,528
Critically, the Snow family no longer appears on the InScope service list for this exam at all, while older question banks still test

3509
05:08:48,688 --> 05:08:49,348
it heavily.

3510
05:08:50,926 --> 05:08:55,460
So what replaced it for managed online transfer AWS DataSync?

3511
05:08:55,968 --> 05:09:01,348
For a physical drop-off without shipping your own device, the AWS Data Transfer Terminal.

3512
05:09:01,998 --> 05:09:04,548
For a dedicated private link, direct connect.

3513
05:09:05,328 --> 05:09:10,468
And where the real need was edge compute rather than transfer AWS outposts.

3514
05:09:11,406 --> 05:09:16,608
Note that data sync itself is not on the in scope list for this exam, which is a good illustration of the problem.

3515
05:09:16,848 --> 05:09:22,500
The old answer is examinable and unavailable, while the current answer is available and not examinable.

3516
05:09:24,046 --> 05:09:26,980
Now, the change that does the most damage to domain 4.

3517
05:09:27,758 --> 05:09:34,852
Preparation. On the 2nd of December 2025, AWS announced a complete restructure of its support portfolio.

3518
05:09:35,694 --> 05:09:43,492
The four paid tiers every study guide features are being replaced by three new ones. The old names disappear at the start of 2027.

3519
05:09:45,070 --> 05:09:46,212
Here is the mapping.

3520
05:09:46,638 --> 05:09:49,252
Basic support is unchanged and still free.

3521
05:09:49,774 --> 05:09:55,332
Developer and business support are both replaced by a single new tier, Business Support Plus.

3522
05:09:56,430 --> 05:10:00,836
It starts at $29 a month with 30-minute critical response.

3523
05:10:01,486 --> 05:10:07,824
Enterprise OnRamp is being automatically upgraded to Enterprise Support through 2026.

3524
05:10:08,302 --> 05:10:14,784
Enterprise support survives with its minimum price cut from $15,000 a month to $5,000.

3525
05:10:15,438 --> 05:10:19,748
And there is a new top tier called Unified Operations from $50,000 a month.

3526
05:10:20,110 --> 05:10:22,692
On the exam, answer with the old four names.

3527
05:10:24,398 --> 05:10:29,956
Fortunately, the reasoning behind support questions has not changed, only the labels.

3528
05:10:30,304 --> 05:10:37,604
Basic is free and gives documentation, repost, the health, dashboard, and core trusted advisor checks.

3529
05:10:38,670 --> 05:10:43,588
A technical account manager first appears at Enterprise and that is the classic giveaway.

3530
05:10:44,494 --> 05:10:50,944
Business is the first tier with 24-7 phone and chat and the full trusted advisor check set.

3531
05:10:51,310 --> 05:10:55,344
Enterprise gives 15-minute response for business critical systems.

3532
05:10:55,664 --> 05:11:02,116
Down where Enterprise OnRamp gives 30. Do not write business support plus on the exam.

3533
05:11:02,574 --> 05:11:04,324
That name is not in the pool.

3534
05:11:05,838 --> 05:11:07,184
And here is the rest of it.

3535
05:11:07,278 --> 05:11:14,148
QuickSight was renamed to Amazon Quick Suite in October 2025, then shortened again to Amazon Quick.

3536
05:11:14,704 --> 05:11:19,428
The exam guide currently writes it as Amazon QuickSight, 2 words.

3537
05:11:21,678 --> 05:11:24,100
SageMaker is now SageMaker.

3538
05:11:24,704 --> 05:11:26,532
AI in the guide.

3539
05:11:27,342 --> 05:11:31,812
Amazon Q business closed to new customers at the end of July this year.

3540
05:11:32,526 --> 05:11:41,156
Code Catlist closed in November 2025 and is not on the in-scope list, yet older banks use it as the correct CICD answer.

3541
05:11:41,998 --> 05:11:52,356
Code commit, Cloud9, Forecast, S3 Select, Cloud Search, SimpleDB, and Data Pipeline all close to new customers on the 25th of July 2024.

3542
05:11:53,870 --> 05:12:01,412
There is a second category worth knowing services that older banks test, but the current guide explicitly puts out of scope.

3543
05:12:02,062 --> 05:12:11,172
AWS Cloud Shell is a good example because it appears as a correct answer in older material and is now on the out of scope list.

3544
05:12:11,822 --> 05:12:15,344
So are Amazon personalize fraud detector and web docs.

3545
05:12:15,664 --> 05:12:25,604
So our billing conductor, data exchange, key spaces and memoryDB, Code Deploy and Network Firewall are out, while code build and code pipeline are in.

3546
05:12:27,470 --> 05:12:30,020
Being able to recognize these costs, nothing.

3547
05:12:30,464 --> 05:12:32,196
Studying them costs time.

3548
05:12:33,934 --> 05:12:37,060
These four went further than closing to new customers.

3549
05:12:37,390 --> 05:12:38,308
They ended.

3550
05:12:39,024 --> 05:12:45,508
Amazon QLDB, the ledger database, reached end of support on the 31st of July 2025.

3551
05:12:46,064 --> 05:12:48,612
Aurora Postgres was the migration path.

3552
05:12:49,518 --> 05:12:55,332
The standalone Amazon Glacier Ward Service closed to new customers in December 2025.

3553
05:12:55,584 --> 05:13:00,164
The S3 Glacier Storage classes are unaffected and fully available.

3554
05:13:01,582 --> 05:13:06,852
AWS WAF Classic was fully retired in September 2025.

3555
05:13:08,014 --> 05:13:12,292
And DMS Fleet Advisor ended in May this year.

3556
05:13:13,582 --> 05:13:18,980
This second category is subtler and easier to miss because the names did not change.

3557
05:13:19,424 --> 05:13:28,100
Root user MFA is described everywhere as recommended and has been enforced on all account types since June 2025.

3558
05:13:30,126 --> 05:13:33,316
Trusted Advisors 5 check categories became 6.

3559
05:13:34,254 --> 05:13:39,684
The Free Tiers 3-part model became a credit model for accounts created after July 2025.

3560
05:13:40,110 --> 05:13:44,164
Organizations gained resource control policies and declarative policies.

3561
05:13:45,198 --> 05:13:46,980
Elasticage gained Valky.

3562
05:13:47,886 --> 05:13:55,364
And the classic load balancer was retired only in EC2 Classic, remaining supported inside a VPC.

3563
05:13:57,006 --> 05:13:58,756
Work scenario 1.

3564
05:13:59,182 --> 05:14:02,660
And this one is about exam behavior rather than architecture.

3565
05:14:04,206 --> 05:14:05,348
You are sitting the exam.

3566
05:14:06,158 --> 05:14:15,812
A question describes 80 terabytes of archive data, a slow unreliable internet link, and a one month deadline to get it into Amazon S3.

3567
05:14:16,686 --> 05:14:25,024
The options include AWS Snowball Edge, AWS Direct Connect, AWS Datasync, and uploading over the public internet.

3568
05:14:26,478 --> 05:14:30,304
You know Snowball closed to new customers last November.

3569
05:14:30,544 --> 05:14:31,824
What should you select?

3570
05:14:32,270 --> 05:14:33,380
Pause and decide.

3571
05:14:39,982 --> 05:14:42,244
Select a snowball edge.

3572
05:14:42,734 --> 05:14:46,756
This will feel wrong, and that feeling is the point of the module.

3573
05:14:47,406 --> 05:14:52,900
The scoring key was written when snowball was the offline transfer answer and it has not been rewritten.

3574
05:14:54,382 --> 05:14:57,424
Option C is what you would actually do today.

3575
05:14:57,966 --> 05:15:03,652
It is deliberately in this list because it claps anyone who has read recent documentation.

3576
05:15:03,950 --> 05:15:05,444
Note the additional clue.

3577
05:15:05,744 --> 05:15:15,012
Data sync is not on the in-scope service list, which is usually a strong hint that it is not the expected answer. Answer the question that was written.

3578
05:15:16,430 --> 05:15:17,252
Work scenario.

3579
05:15:17,966 --> 05:15:22,372
2. A question asks which support plan a company should choose.

3580
05:15:22,990 --> 05:15:31,524
It runs business critical production workloads, wants a designated technical account manager, and needs a 15-minute response when production is down.

3581
05:15:32,462 --> 05:15:38,372
The options are basic, developer, business, and enterprise support, which meets the requirements.

3582
05:15:38,926 --> 05:15:40,260
Pause and decide.

3583
05:15:46,958 --> 05:15:50,212
The answer is D enterprise support.

3584
05:15:50,702 --> 05:15:52,804
This scenario is here to reassure you.

3585
05:15:54,542 --> 05:16:00,704
The support plan restructure is dramatic, but it barely touches the reasoning the exam actually tests.

3586
05:16:01,230 --> 05:16:10,468
Designated technical account manager and 15-minute business critical response have always meant enterprise and they still do in the new lineup.

3587
05:16:11,022 --> 05:16:16,228
What changed is that developer, business, and enterprise on ramp are being folded away.

3588
05:16:17,198 --> 05:16:20,068
The mapping from requirement to tier is stable.

3589
05:16:20,910 --> 05:16:23,556
Learn the mapping, not the price list.

3590
05:16:25,070 --> 05:16:26,308
Worked scenario.

3591
05:16:26,784 --> 05:16:30,372
3. You are revising with an older practice bank.

3592
05:16:32,174 --> 05:16:41,264
A question asks which service models visualizes and automates an application release workflow and gives Amazon Code Catalyst as the marked answer.

3593
05:16:41,838 --> 05:16:48,676
You check the current exam guide and find code catalyst is not listed in scope, while code pipeline and code build both are.

3594
05:16:49,104 --> 05:16:50,948
What is the right conclusion to draw?

3595
05:16:51,438 --> 05:16:52,548
Pause and decide.

3596
05:16:59,264 --> 05:17:00,864
The answer is A.

3597
05:17:01,230 --> 05:17:05,892
This is the general procedure for the whole module, so it is worth stating plainly.

3598
05:17:06,224 --> 05:17:14,244
When a question bank and the official in scope list disagree, the in scope list wins because it is the more recently updated document.

3599
05:17:15,182 --> 05:17:22,148
Model, visualize, and automate a release workflow is code pipeline's own product description and code pipeline is in scope.

3600
05:17:22,464 --> 05:17:26,628
Note the contrast with scenario 1 where I told you to pick snowball.

3601
05:17:27,278 --> 05:17:33,316
The difference is that there the alternative was also out of scope, so the old answer was still the best available.

3602
05:17:33,934 --> 05:17:37,220
Here there is an in-scope replacement sitting right there.

3603
05:17:39,024 --> 05:17:40,708
Worked scenario 4.

3604
05:17:41,614 --> 05:17:44,900
You created an AWS account last month to practice.

3605
05:17:46,510 --> 05:17:53,956
At sign up, you chose a plan, received $100 in credits, and earned another 100 by completing onboarding tasks.

3606
05:17:54,304 --> 05:17:56,580
There was no mention of 12 months of anything.

3607
05:17:57,454 --> 05:18:05,824
A practice question then asks you to describe the AWS free tier and offers an option naming always free, 12 months free, and short

3608
05:18:05,984 --> 05:18:06,692
term trials.

3609
05:18:07,104 --> 05:18:08,452
What should you select?

3610
05:18:09,582 --> 05:18:10,820
Pause and decide.

3611
05:18:17,326 --> 05:18:22,308
The answer is A, and this scenario is the entire module compressed into one question.

3612
05:18:22,958 --> 05:18:30,628
Here you have first hand evidence from your own AWS account that contradicts the exam, which is the most convincing kind of evidence there is,

3613
05:18:31,310 --> 05:18:33,380
and it is still the wrong basis for answering.

3614
05:18:34,350 --> 05:18:40,720
The exam tests a documented catalog with a publication date, not the console in front of you.

3615
05:18:41,550 --> 05:18:43,520
Option C deserves a word too.

3616
05:18:43,758 --> 05:18:49,092
There is no penalty for guessing and no credit for objecting, so never leave a question blank.

3617
05:18:50,766 --> 05:18:55,040
To use this module, in the exam, answer from the documented catalog.

3618
05:18:55,342 --> 05:19:00,960
Where an in-scope replacement exists, prefer it as with code pipeline over code catalyst.

3619
05:19:01,486 --> 05:19:06,180
Where no in-scope replacement exists, the old answer stands, as with Snowball.

3620
05:19:06,638 --> 05:19:09,444
Read the in-scope and out-of-scope lists yourself.

3621
05:19:09,998 --> 05:19:14,756
They take 10 minutes and they're the most current thing AWS publishes about this exam.

3622
05:19:14,990 --> 05:19:20,868
And remember that some changes are removals while others are behavior moving under an unchanged name.

3623
05:19:21,134 --> 05:19:23,300
The second kind is harder to spot.

3624
05:19:23,630 --> 05:19:24,320
One last

3625
05:19:24,320 --> 05:19:30,884
thing. Every fact in this module has a date attached and AWS keeps changing the catalog.

3626
05:19:32,654 --> 05:19:33,860
Check before you sit.

3627
05:19:35,310 --> 05:19:39,396
There is no AWS product content in this module at all.

3628
05:19:39,680 --> 05:19:44,612
It is about how AWS writes questions and how to read them under time pressure.

3629
05:19:45,422 --> 05:19:50,756
A real share of wrong answers come from misreading the question rather than not knowing the material.

3630
05:19:51,278 --> 05:19:54,000
That share is recoverable in 20 minutes.

3631
05:19:55,310 --> 05:19:56,516
Here is the premise.

3632
05:19:57,326 --> 05:20:04,260
On the real exam, you will meet questions where you can define every single service in the option list and you will still get it wrong.

3633
05:20:05,230 --> 05:20:10,500
Not because you did not know enough, but because the question contained a word you skimmed past.

3634
05:20:11,438 --> 05:20:16,676
Those marks are the cheapest ones available to you because you already did the studying.

3635
05:20:18,414 --> 05:20:24,196
AWS capitalizes qualifier words deliberately and each one points somewhere specific.

3636
05:20:24,686 --> 05:20:32,420
Most cost effective means the cheapest option that still meets every stated requirement, which is not the same as simply cheapest.

3637
05:20:33,360 --> 05:20:39,652
Least operational overhead means the most managed service. So serverless beats managed beats self-run.

3638
05:20:40,302 --> 05:20:44,228
Most secure means tightest permissions, even if it is more work.

3639
05:20:44,846 --> 05:20:49,316
Fasties to implement means least setup now, even if it costs more later.

3640
05:20:50,254 --> 05:20:56,068
And design to means the service whose primary purpose this is, not one that could be bent into doing it.

3641
05:20:57,582 --> 05:20:59,396
Here is a reading order that works.

3642
05:20:59,680 --> 05:21:08,068
Read the last line first because the actual question lives at the end and the scenario makes more sense once you know what is being asked.

3643
05:21:08,400 --> 05:21:11,268
Find the qualifier and note what it eliminates.

3644
05:21:12,046 --> 05:21:17,988
Read the scenario hunting for constraints because each constraint kills options.

3645
05:21:18,830 --> 05:21:22,212
Then predict the answer before you read the options at all.

3646
05:21:24,046 --> 05:21:26,916
That habit is the most valuable one in this module.

3647
05:21:27,758 --> 05:21:32,240
If you read four plausible options, first, one of them will anchor you.

3648
05:21:32,782 --> 05:21:35,652
Finally, eliminate rather than select.

3649
05:21:37,166 --> 05:21:40,644
AWS reuses a small set of distractor shapes.

3650
05:21:41,120 --> 05:21:47,520
The right answer to a different question is the commonest, a perfectly sensible action that nobody asked for.

3651
05:21:47,760 --> 05:21:53,924
The heavier version works but takes more effort than needed and loses whenever the qualifier is less overhead.

3652
05:21:54,830 --> 05:21:58,308
The adjacent service sits in the same category with the wrong job.

3653
05:21:58,880 --> 05:22:07,652
The absolute contains words like all, always, never, unlimited, or eliminates entirely and is nearly always wrong.

3654
05:22:08,526 --> 05:22:13,252
And the name collision, which needs no explanation beyond reading the whole name.

3655
05:22:14,510 --> 05:22:20,100
5 more patterns, all of which appeared in worked scenarios earlier in this course.

3656
05:22:21,198 --> 05:22:27,620
Mitigate instead of eliminate, where three options manage a flaw better and only one removes it.

3657
05:22:28,176 --> 05:22:33,348
The half solution, where the scenario names two problems and the option fixes one.

3658
05:22:34,350 --> 05:22:39,236
The invented feature, built from real vocabulary borrowed from a neighboring service.

3659
05:22:40,142 --> 05:22:41,892
Correct but off question.

3660
05:22:42,256 --> 05:22:45,092
A true statement answering something nobody asked.

3661
05:22:46,286 --> 05:22:50,336
And the near-miss number, right in shape but wrong in figure.

3662
05:22:51,630 --> 05:22:55,492
Stems are built from four kinds of clause and each behaves differently.

3663
05:22:56,174 --> 05:23:06,436
A goal signaled by wants to or needs to picks the answer. A constraint signaled by must or cannot or without eliminates options before anything else is weighed.

3664
05:23:07,118 --> 05:23:19,620
A preference signaled by would prefer or ideally breaks a tie but never beats a constraint. And an invitation signaled by willing to or able to permits an approach the question wants you to choose.

3665
05:23:20,334 --> 05:23:25,136
Confusing a goal with a constraint is the commonest reading error on this exam.

3666
05:23:26,446 --> 05:23:27,332
Two formats.

3667
05:23:27,776 --> 05:23:31,460
Multiple choice has one correct answer and three distractors.

3668
05:23:32,736 --> 05:23:40,004
Multiple response has two or more correct out of five or more options, and the stem always tells you how many to select.

3669
05:23:40,974 --> 05:23:45,508
There is no partial credit on multiple response, so two right and one wrong scores.

3670
05:23:46,115 --> 05:23:46,295
Zero.

3671
05:23:47,470 --> 05:23:49,572
Here is the useful structural insight.

3672
05:23:50,016 --> 05:23:55,876
Choose two questions almost always contain two distinct requirements with one service answering each.

3673
05:23:57,326 --> 05:24:03,844
If you find yourself picking two services that do the same thing, reread the scenario for the second requirement you missed.

3674
05:24:05,358 --> 05:24:14,180
On timing, 90 minutes for 65 questions is about 83 seconds each, and most prepared candidates finish with time to spare.

3675
05:24:14,656 --> 05:24:18,896
Flag anything taking more than 90 seconds and move on at once.

3676
05:24:19,136 --> 05:24:23,588
Come back with fresh eyes because later questions often jog something loose.

3677
05:24:24,462 --> 05:24:30,256
Always put an answer down before moving on, even on flagged questions, because there is no penalty for guessing.

3678
05:24:30,336 --> 05:24:35,492
And do not change an answer on a third look unless you can name the specific thing you misread the first time.

3679
05:24:35,950 --> 05:24:38,404
Remember too that 15 questions are unscored.

3680
05:24:39,214 --> 05:24:41,732
The one that defeats you may not count.

3681
05:24:43,342 --> 05:24:47,460
When you genuinely do not know, there is a reasonable order of guessing.

3682
05:24:48,334 --> 05:24:49,956
Eliminate absolutes first.

3683
05:24:50,510 --> 05:24:54,596
Anything promising all, always, never or unlimited.

3684
05:24:55,470 --> 05:25:01,188
If the qualifier says least operational overhead, eliminate anything that means managing servers.

3685
05:25:02,094 --> 05:25:07,012
Prefer a purpose built service over a general one that could be configured to do the job.

3686
05:25:07,456 --> 05:25:14,852
Prefer the AWS managed option over building it yourself because this exam rewards managed with great consistency.

3687
05:25:15,296 --> 05:25:20,388
And if a service is not on the in scope list, it is very unlikely to be the answer.

3688
05:25:21,646 --> 05:25:26,500
Work scenario 1. And this one is about the qualifier rather than the services.

3689
05:25:26,816 --> 05:25:29,476
A company needs a nightly image resizing job.

3690
05:25:29,696 --> 05:25:35,140
It takes about 4 minutes, runs once a day, and the company does not want to manage infrastructure.

3691
05:25:36,046 --> 05:25:41,924
The question ends with the phrase which solution will meet these requirements with the least operational overhead?

3692
05:25:42,336 --> 05:25:44,676
Which option does the qualifier point at?

3693
05:25:45,390 --> 05:25:46,756
Pause and decide.

3694
05:25:53,422 --> 05:26:00,144
The answer is C. Notice that you could rank these four options without knowing anything about the workload purely from the qualifier.

3695
05:26:00,304 --> 05:26:05,744
Least operational overhead orders them serverless, then managed, then self-run.

3696
05:26:06,144 --> 05:26:11,264
Lambda, then autoscaling, then EC2, then EKS.

3697
05:26:12,142 --> 05:26:20,036
The 4 minute runtime is the only technical detail that matters, and it only matters because it confirms the job fits inside Lambda's 15 minute limit.

3698
05:26:20,464 --> 05:26:22,980
Also worth noticing what the question did not say.

3699
05:26:23,824 --> 05:26:27,172
Cost is never mentioned, so cheapness is not a tiebreaker here.

3700
05:26:28,686 --> 05:26:30,116
Work scenario 2.

3701
05:26:30,624 --> 05:26:42,308
Same nightly image job, but the question now ends with most cost effectively. The company adds that it already runs a fleet of EC2 instances sitting at low utilization overnight.

3702
05:26:42,544 --> 05:26:47,684
The job is stateless and can be restarted if interrupted. Does the answer change?

3703
05:26:48,784 --> 05:26:50,084
Pause and decide.

3704
05:26:56,398 --> 05:27:03,264
The answer is B and this pair is the whole point of the module. The scenario is nearly identical to the previous one.

3705
05:27:03,502 --> 05:27:10,372
One qualifier changed and the correct answer moved. Under least overhead, lambda wins because there is nothing to manage.

3706
05:27:11,022 --> 05:27:18,404
Under most cost effective, using capacity you already pay for wins because idle overnight capacity has no marginal cost.

3707
05:27:18,944 --> 05:27:24,708
Option C is the classic overreach. Re-architecting a production fleet to solve a nightly batch job.

3708
05:27:25,184 --> 05:27:28,484
Find the qualifier before you read the options every single time.

3709
05:27:30,055 --> 05:27:31,235
Worked Scenario 3

3710
05:27:31,235 --> 05:27:34,276
which is about a feeling you will have on exam day.

3711
05:27:35,374 --> 05:27:39,264
A question reads A company wants to improve its security posture.

3712
05:27:39,566 --> 05:27:40,964
Which service should it use?

3713
05:27:41,486 --> 05:27:47,908
The options are guard duty, inspector, security hub, and Macy, and the scenario gives no other detail.

3714
05:27:48,718 --> 05:27:51,344
What should you do with a question like this?

3715
05:27:51,726 --> 05:27:52,868
Pause and decide.

3716
05:27:59,534 --> 05:28:03,172
The answer is B and the reason matters more than the mark.

3717
05:28:03,904 --> 05:28:08,132
AWS does not publish questions with four equally correct answers.

3718
05:28:08,398 --> 05:28:11,076
They are reviewed specifically to prevent that.

3719
05:28:11,584 --> 05:28:16,900
So when every option looks right, the correct interpretation is not that the question is unfair.

3720
05:28:17,838 --> 05:28:19,396
It is that you have skimmed a word.

3721
05:28:19,758 --> 05:28:28,772
That feeling of all four being plausible is information. Treat it as a signal to reread the stem, and the distinguishing detail will usually be sitting in the second sentence.

3722
05:28:35,264 --> 05:28:36,384
Consider this STEM.

3723
05:28:36,944 --> 05:28:41,344
A company wants to reduce the time it takes to provision test environments.

3724
05:28:41,584 --> 05:28:45,604
Regulation requires that all test data remains within the United Kingdom.

3725
05:28:46,478 --> 05:28:49,636
The team would prefer a solution its engineers already know.

3726
05:28:50,606 --> 05:28:53,956
And the company is willing to adopt new tooling if necessary.

3727
05:28:55,022 --> 05:28:58,052
Which clause will eliminate the most answer options?

3728
05:28:59,022 --> 05:29:00,260
Pause and decide.

3729
05:29:06,944 --> 05:29:08,304
The answer is B.

3730
05:29:08,895 --> 05:29:11,940
Work a stem in this order and questions get noticeably faster.

3731
05:29:12,304 --> 05:29:14,980
Constraints first because they remove options outright.

3732
05:29:15,406 --> 05:29:17,604
Then the goal which selects among what survives.

3733
05:29:18,670 --> 05:29:21,540
Then preferences, only if two options still remain.

3734
05:29:22,382 --> 05:29:24,900
Notice what clauses C and D do together here.

3735
05:29:25,744 --> 05:29:33,504
The team would prefer familiar tooling, but the company is willing to adopt new tooling which cancels that preference almost entirely.

3736
05:29:34,035 --> 05:29:38,244
Stems sometimes contain a clause whose only job is to neutralize another one.

3737
05:29:39,854 --> 05:29:42,372
Some practicalities for the evening before.

3738
05:29:43,278 --> 05:29:47,104
Rewatch the confusion pairs module because it is built for that moment.

3739
05:29:47,264 --> 05:29:51,396
Reread the in-scope and out-of-scope lists, which takes 10 minutes.

3740
05:29:52,366 --> 05:29:59,076
Do not take a fresh practice exam the night before because a poor score costs you sleep and teaches you nothing you can act on.

3741
05:29:59,504 --> 05:30:04,624
If you are testing online, run the system test in advance and clear your desk completely.

3742
05:30:05,486 --> 05:30:10,852
And remember the scoring is compensatory, so one weak domain cannot fail you on its own.

3743
05:30:11,470 --> 05:30:17,188
Confidence here comes from having a procedure for hard questions, not from having met every service.

3744
05:30:18,704 --> 05:30:21,252
This is the first of three practice exams.

3745
05:30:21,584 --> 05:30:25,444
20 questions with the domains mixed exactly as they will be on the day.

3746
05:30:25,824 --> 05:30:31,716
Each question pauses for 9 seconds before the walkthrough, so you can take this without touching the pause button.

3747
05:30:32,464 --> 05:30:36,100
Commit to an answer out loud before each walkthrough begins.

3748
05:30:37,806 --> 05:30:39,428
Four things before you start.

3749
05:30:39,760 --> 05:30:49,600
Answer every question, including ones you are unsure of, because guessing is free and this is practice for that habit. Write your answers down since you cannot score by domain from memory.

3750
05:30:49,806 --> 05:30:54,020
Each walkthrough names its domain so you can total per domain afterwards.

3751
05:30:54,542 --> 05:31:00,580
And do not pause to look things up because a score inflated by notes tells you nothing you can act on.

3752
05:31:01,998 --> 05:31:02,788
Question 1.

3753
05:31:03,342 --> 05:31:09,028
A company needs to validate uploaded CSV files as soon as they arrive in an S3 bucket.

3754
05:31:09,520 --> 05:31:16,516
Validation takes about 90 seconds per file and files arrive in unpredictable bursts, sometimes none for hours.

3755
05:31:16,880 --> 05:31:20,612
The company does not want to manage servers or pay for idle capacity.

3756
05:31:21,440 --> 05:31:23,396
Which service should it use?

3757
05:31:33,838 --> 05:31:37,444
The answer is B, lambda, and this is domain 3.

3758
05:31:37,742 --> 05:31:41,252
Four signals lineup triggered by an S3 upload.

3759
05:31:42,350 --> 05:31:45,156
Unpredictable bursts with idle periods,

3760
05:31:45,806 --> 05:31:52,068
no servers to manage, and 90 seconds, which sits comfortably inside the 15 minute limit.

3761
05:31:52,686 --> 05:31:57,572
Whenever a duration appears in a compute question, check it against that limit first.

3762
05:31:59,182 --> 05:32:00,132
Question 2.

3763
05:32:00,878 --> 05:32:06,916
A hospital's appointment booking system sees demand rise sharply every winter and fall away in summer.

3764
05:32:07,630 --> 05:32:12,960
On premises, it bought servers for the winter peak, which sat mostly idle the rest of the

3765
05:32:12,960 --> 05:32:21,476
year. After migrating, the number of instances now grows and shrinks automatically as demand changes.

3766
05:32:32,078 --> 05:32:35,396
The answer is B, elasticity, and this is domain one.

3767
05:32:35,760 --> 05:32:39,460
The pair to separate is always agility and elasticity.

3768
05:32:39,760 --> 05:32:42,180
Agility measures time to do something new.

3769
05:32:42,830 --> 05:32:45,732
Elasticity measures capacity following demand.

3770
05:32:46,318 --> 05:32:52,740
Here, the demand curve is unchanged. Winter is still winter, and only the capacity now moves with it.

3771
05:32:54,030 --> 05:32:54,756
Question 3.

3772
05:32:55,246 --> 05:32:59,044
A company runs an image processing function on AWS Lambda.

3773
05:32:59,534 --> 05:33:08,065
A security audit asks who patches the operating system underneath the function and who is responsible for vulnerabilities in the function's own

3774
05:33:08,320 --> 05:33:08,960
code.

3775
05:33:10,606 --> 05:33:12,100
Which statement is correct?

3776
05:33:22,734 --> 05:33:25,120
The answer is A, and this is domain

3777
05:33:25,120 --> 05:33:29,732
2. The line moves by service and lambda sits at the far end of it.

3778
05:33:30,254 --> 05:33:34,340
There is no operating system you can reach, so AWS patches it.

3779
05:33:35,022 --> 05:33:40,580
What never moves on any service is your data, your permissions, and your own code.

3780
05:33:41,774 --> 05:33:42,820
Question 4.

3781
05:33:43,120 --> 05:33:48,800
A company runs a steady production workload on EC2 and wants a substantial discount over 3 years.

3782
05:33:49,040 --> 05:33:54,532
During that period, it plans to migrate some components to Fargate and rewrite others as Lambda functions.

3783
05:33:54,720 --> 05:33:57,956
It wants the discount to keep applying as those migrations happen.

3784
05:34:08,526 --> 05:34:11,120
The answer is B, and this is domain 4.

3785
05:34:11,630 --> 05:34:16,708
Naming Fargate and Lambda alongside EC2 eliminates everything else in one move.

3786
05:34:19,246 --> 05:34:28,000
Reserved instances cover only EC2, and so do EC2 instance savings plans. Only a compute savings plan spans all three services.

3787
05:34:29,294 --> 05:34:33,680
Question 5. A company stores rendered video thumbnails in Amazon S3.

3788
05:34:34,062 --> 05:34:44,292
They are accessed infrequently but must be available in milliseconds when requested. If they were ever lost, the company could regenerate everyone from the source video within an hour.

3789
05:34:45,134 --> 05:34:49,028
It wants the lowest storage cost consistent with those facts.

3790
05:34:59,758 --> 05:35:02,660
The answer is C, and this is domain 3.

3791
05:35:03,566 --> 05:35:06,180
Two facts pick an S3 class.

3792
05:35:07,600 --> 05:35:12,996
Infrequent access with millisecond retrieval narrows it to the infrequent access tiers.

3793
05:35:13,806 --> 05:35:21,680
Then the regeneration clause decides between them because losing an availability zone would cause this company an hour of rendering

3794
05:35:21,920 --> 05:35:23,760
rather than the data itself.

3795
05:35:25,070 --> 05:35:26,160
Question 6.

3796
05:35:26,400 --> 05:35:33,124
A company's finance director needs to change the AWS account's registered email address and downgrade its support plan.

3797
05:35:34,862 --> 05:35:40,900
An administrator with the administrator access policy attached attempts both and fines. Neither is possible.

3798
05:35:42,190 --> 05:35:44,964
Why can the administrator not perform these tasks?

3799
05:35:55,694 --> 05:35:58,564
The answer is B, and this is domain 2.

3800
05:35:58,990 --> 05:36:02,372
Rather than memorizing the root task list, learn its shape.

3801
05:36:03,040 --> 05:36:08,708
These are account level actions changing the account itself rather than anything inside it.

3802
05:36:10,062 --> 05:36:16,612
Anything that changes the account, its identity, its plan, or its existence needs the root user.

3803
05:36:18,318 --> 05:36:19,140
Question 7.

3804
05:36:19,680 --> 05:36:24,320
A company is redesigning a workload to reduce the energy it consumes.

3805
05:36:24,480 --> 05:36:32,868
It plans to move to more efficient instance types, shut down non-production environments overnight, and choose regions with lower carbon intensity.

3806
05:36:33,760 --> 05:36:38,308
Which pillar of the well-architected framework does this represent.

3807
05:36:48,622 --> 05:36:49,840
The answer is C.

3808
05:36:50,000 --> 05:36:52,560
Sustainability and this is domain one.

3809
05:36:53,102 --> 05:36:54,948
Notice why option A is tempting.

3810
05:36:55,374 --> 05:36:59,920
Every action listed also reduces cost, so a cost answer would feel defensible.

3811
05:37:00,174 --> 05:37:03,812
But the goal sentence says energy consumed, not money spent.

3812
05:37:04,654 --> 05:37:11,120
The goal picks the pillar, and sustainability is the sixth pillar that older material omits.

3813
05:37:12,985 --> 05:37:21,188
Question 8 An application on EC2 instances in a private subnet writes large volumes of data to Amazon S3.

3814
05:37:22,160 --> 05:37:30,436
Compliance requires the traffic never to traverse the public internet, and the finance team has queried the NAT gateway processing charges.

3815
05:37:31,120 --> 05:37:33,188
What should the company implement?

3816
05:37:43,840 --> 05:37:45,188
The answer is A.

3817
05:37:45,440 --> 05:37:46,948
And this is domain three.

3818
05:37:47,680 --> 05:37:53,028
Two separate concerns: compliance and cost, and one answer resolves both.

3819
05:37:53,774 --> 05:37:58,372
Gateway endpoints for S3 and Dynamo DB carry no additional charge.

3820
05:37:59,502 --> 05:38:03,120
That is why they settle the finance objection at the same time as the compliance.

3821
05:38:03,360 --> 05:38:04,000
One.

3822
05:38:05,390 --> 05:38:06,212
Question 9.

3823
05:38:06,702 --> 05:38:15,360
A company pushes container images to Amazon ECR. Security wants to know continuously whether any image contains software with known published vulnerabilities.

3824
05:38:15,520 --> 05:38:22,340
It also wants alerting when a new vulnerability affects a stored image. Nothing should need installing into the images.

3825
05:38:32,960 --> 05:38:36,800
The answer is B, and this is domain two.

3826
05:38:37,280 --> 05:38:44,160
Behavior is guard duty, data content is messy, and vulnerabilities are inspector.

3827
05:38:44,720 --> 05:38:54,800
The rescanning detail confirms it because inspector continuously re-evaluates stored images as new vulnerabilities are published rather than only scanning at push

3828
05:38:55,040 --> 05:38:55,360
time.

3829
05:38:56,782 --> 05:38:57,668
Question 10.

3830
05:38:58,190 --> 05:39:06,468
A company must migrate a Microsoft SQL server database to a managed AWS database service.

3831
05:39:07,790 --> 05:39:12,868
The application cannot be modified and requires SQL Server specifically.

3832
05:39:13,998 --> 05:39:20,548
The company also wants automated backups and the option of automatic failover to another availability zone.

3833
05:39:30,830 --> 05:39:33,284
The answer is B, and this is domain play.

3834
05:39:33,806 --> 05:39:38,596
Aurora appears in almost every relational question as a distractor because it sounds like the premium answer.

3835
05:39:39,278 --> 05:39:41,892
It is compatible with MySQL and Postgres only.

3836
05:39:42,638 --> 05:39:48,804
The moment a scenario names SQL server Oracle MariaDB or DB2, the answer is standard RDS.

3837
05:39:50,574 --> 05:39:51,428
Question 11.

3838
05:39:51,790 --> 05:39:56,388
A company runs several hundred virtual machines on VMware in its cone data center.

3839
05:39:56,768 --> 05:40:04,548
It wants to move the whole estate to AWS quickly without rebuying hardware and without changing how its operations team works day to day.

3840
05:40:04,928 --> 05:40:07,908
No applications will be modified or re-architected.

3841
05:40:18,368 --> 05:40:19,888
The answer is C.

3842
05:40:20,334 --> 05:40:22,212
Relocate, and this is domain one.

3843
05:40:22,848 --> 05:40:28,676
Relocate is the strategy most study material omits, which makes it a reliable exam question.

3844
05:40:29,198 --> 05:40:32,196
The separator from re-host is scale and operations.

3845
05:40:33,888 --> 05:40:37,284
Re-host moves servers onto EC2 individually.

3846
05:40:38,158 --> 05:40:42,852
Relocate moves an entire virtualization estate with the operating model intact.

3847
05:40:44,302 --> 05:40:45,168
Question 12.

3848
05:40:45,518 --> 05:40:50,820
A company's public website suffered an outage when its TLS certificate expired unnoticed.

3849
05:40:51,470 --> 05:40:56,580
The certificate was bought from a third party and installed manually on an application load balancer.

3850
05:40:57,134 --> 05:41:01,188
The company wants renewal to happen without anyone remembering to act.

3851
05:41:11,470 --> 05:41:14,116
The answer is C, and this is domain 2.

3852
05:41:16,558 --> 05:41:21,380
Three of the four options store something more securely, and storage was never the problem.

3853
05:41:22,638 --> 05:41:30,180
The certificate expired. Only certificate manager removes the human from the renewal, which is what the final clause demands.

3854
05:41:31,408 --> 05:41:32,676
Question 13.

3855
05:41:33,038 --> 05:41:43,300
An operations team receives CloudWatch alarms for CPU and network on its EC2 fleet, but it has no visibility of memory usage or free disk space inside the instances.

3856
05:41:44,878 --> 05:41:47,408
It wants those metrics in the same dashboards.

3857
05:41:47,726 --> 05:41:50,660
The instances run a standard Amazon Linux image.

3858
05:42:01,358 --> 05:42:04,260
The answer is B, and this is domain 3.

3859
05:42:04,622 --> 05:42:08,048
This follows directly from the shared responsibility model.

3860
05:42:08,368 --> 05:42:22,116
AWS can see what the hypervisor sees, which is CPU and network. Memory and free disk space exist inside the guest operating system, which is yours. So something you install has to report them.

3861
05:42:23,758 --> 05:42:24,644
Question 14.

3862
05:42:24,848 --> 05:42:28,768
A company runs a business critical trading platform on AWS.

3863
05:42:28,928 --> 05:42:37,892
It needs a 15-minute response if that platform goes down and a named AWS contact who understands its architecture and can advise proactively.

3864
05:42:38,208 --> 05:42:39,620
Budget is available.

3865
05:42:50,126 --> 05:42:52,964
The answer is D, and this is domain 4.

3866
05:42:53,262 --> 05:42:56,036
Both stated requirements are enterprise signals.

3867
05:42:56,750 --> 05:43:01,476
15 minutes for business critical down and a designated technical account manager.

3868
05:43:02,286 --> 05:43:03,300
Enterprise on

3869
05:43:04,142 --> 05:43:09,380
Ramp is deliberately closed, offering 30 minutes and a pool rather than a named individual.

3870
05:43:10,126 --> 05:43:13,028
Read the stated figure, not the general shape.

3871
05:43:14,254 --> 05:43:15,328
Question 15.

3872
05:43:15,726 --> 05:43:28,292
A company beginning its cloud program has no standard way to build environments, no landing zone, and no agreed approach to modernizing its applications. Its engineers each set things up differently.

3873
05:43:29,070 --> 05:43:32,068
Which CAF perspective addresses this?

3874
05:43:42,576 --> 05:43:46,656
The answer is a platform, and this is domain one.

3875
05:43:47,214 --> 05:43:50,660
The tell is that everything missing is technical infrastructure,

3876
05:43:52,110 --> 05:43:56,068
a landing zone, build standards, a modernization approach.

3877
05:43:56,976 --> 05:44:06,020
Contrast that with people, which would be engineers lacking the skills, and governance, which would be nobody controlling cost or risk.

3878
05:44:07,470 --> 05:44:08,516
Question 16.

3879
05:44:08,816 --> 05:44:11,620
An insurance claim is processed in seven steps.

3880
05:44:12,016 --> 05:44:15,684
Some run in parallel and one branches depending on the claim value.

3881
05:44:16,256 --> 05:44:21,060
Any step that fails must be retried three times before the claim goes for manual review.

3882
05:44:21,710 --> 05:44:25,796
The company wants to see where a claim stopped when something goes wrong.

3883
05:44:36,558 --> 05:44:39,268
The answer is C, and this is domain 3.

3884
05:44:39,694 --> 05:44:42,020
Four signals all point at orchestration.

3885
05:44:42,286 --> 05:44:43,780
Steps in a defined order.

3886
05:44:44,270 --> 05:44:45,508
Parallel execution,

3887
05:44:46,318 --> 05:44:50,244
conditional branching, and retry behavior with a fallback.

3888
05:44:51,086 --> 05:44:58,404
Add the requirement to see where a run stopped, and step functions is the only service that offers a visual workflow.

3889
05:44:59,982 --> 05:45:01,056
Question 17.

3890
05:45:01,230 --> 05:45:04,736
A company is expanding from one AWS account to about 40.

3891
05:45:04,910 --> 05:45:14,372
It wants a secure multi-account environment established quickly with standard controls from the outset. It also wants centralized logging and a repeatable way to request new accounts.

3892
05:45:15,214 --> 05:45:17,956
It has no existing design and little time.

3893
05:45:28,885 --> 05:45:36,964
The answer is B And this is domain 2. Organizations is not wrong in the sense of being incapable, and that is what makes it a good distractor.

3894
05:45:37,230 --> 05:45:41,220
What rules it out is the combination of quickly and no existing design.

3895
05:45:42,030 --> 05:45:44,976
Control tower is the opinionated setup.

3896
05:45:45,205 --> 05:45:49,465
organizations is the raw capability underneath it.

3897
05:45:49,902 --> 05:45:50,756
Question 18.

3898
05:45:51,214 --> 05:45:57,220
A company runs its entire platform in a single AWS region across three availability zones.

3899
05:45:57,536 --> 05:46:05,988
Its regulator now requires it to demonstrate it can continue operating if that entire region became unavailable for an extended period.

3900
05:46:06,478 --> 05:46:09,828
Availability within the region has never been a problem.

3901
05:46:20,462 --> 05:46:23,236
The answer is B, and this is domain 3.

3902
05:46:23,918 --> 05:46:25,348
The distinction is precise.

3903
05:46:26,574 --> 05:46:32,196
Multiple availability zones protect against losing a data center, and this company already has that.

3904
05:46:33,166 --> 05:46:38,020
Losing an entire region is a different failure, and only a second region protects against it.

3905
05:46:38,896 --> 05:46:46,096
The final sentence confirms that availability inside the region is not the problem.

3906
05:46:47,776 --> 05:46:48,996
Question 19.

3907
05:46:49,296 --> 05:46:53,056
A finance team is comparing on-premises costs with AWS.

3908
05:46:53,216 --> 05:47:01,476
It lists the data center lease, the three yearly server refresh, its security operations team, and the electricity bill for cooling.

3909
05:47:03,936 --> 05:47:07,140
Which of these disappear entirely after migrating?

3910
05:47:08,256 --> 05:47:09,156
Choose two.

3911
05:47:23,470 --> 05:47:27,716
The word doing the work is eliminated, not reduced, not shifted.

3912
05:47:27,982 --> 05:47:31,268
Only the facilities and the hardware purchase disappear completely.

3913
05:47:32,302 --> 05:47:41,700
Security, training, and licensing all continue in some form, which is why they appear so often as distractors in this question shape.

3914
05:47:43,278 --> 05:47:44,228
Question 20.

3915
05:47:45,646 --> 05:47:50,916
A contractor needs temporary access to read objects in one Amazon S3 bucket for six weeks.

3916
05:47:51,406 --> 05:47:58,340
A junior administrator proposes attaching the administrator access policy because it is quicker than writing something specific.

3917
05:47:59,374 --> 05:48:01,092
The security team objects.

3918
05:48:01,710 --> 05:48:03,300
What should be done instead?

3919
05:48:14,094 --> 05:48:16,772
The answer is B, and this is domain 2.

3920
05:48:17,294 --> 05:48:20,740
This exam never rewards convenience over least privilege.

3921
05:48:21,968 --> 05:48:28,228
Options A and D are the same mistake wearing different clothes, one direct and one through a group.

3922
05:48:30,368 --> 05:48:32,996
And option C is worth stating plainly.

3923
05:48:33,870 --> 05:48:37,988
Rude credentials are never shared with anyone for any duration.

3924
05:48:39,598 --> 05:48:40,900
Here is the scoring guide.

3925
05:48:41,328 --> 05:48:44,900
Domain 1 was questions 2, 7, 11, 15, and 19.

3926
05:48:45,248 --> 05:48:49,476
Domain 2 was 3, 6, 9, 12, 17, and 20.

3927
05:48:50,542 --> 05:48:55,012
Domain 3 was 1, 5, 8, 10, 13, 16, and 18.

3928
05:48:55,598 --> 05:48:57,412
Domain 4 was 4 and 14.

3929
05:48:58,318 --> 05:49:01,636
16 or more out of 20 is a comfortable position.

3930
05:49:02,126 --> 05:49:05,700
Below 12, work your weakest domain before taking exam.

3931
05:49:06,542 --> 05:49:06,928
2.

3932
05:49:08,334 --> 05:49:09,476
What to do next?

3933
05:49:09,888 --> 05:49:12,100
Score by domain rather than by total.

3934
05:49:12,368 --> 05:49:18,148
Because 2 misses in one domain matters more than 5 spread evenly. Rewatch only your weakest domain.

3935
05:49:18,766 --> 05:49:23,428
Do not take exam 2 immediately because the gap is what makes the second score meaningful.

3936
05:49:24,238 --> 05:49:30,020
And review any question you guessed correctly, since a lucky guess is a gap the score cannot show you.

3937
05:49:34,062 --> 05:49:38,692
Exam 2 covers different topics entirely rather than retesting this paper.

3938
05:49:40,046 --> 05:49:41,252
Practice exam 2.

3939
05:49:42,128 --> 05:49:45,764
20 questions, none of them repeating a topic from exam.

3940
05:49:47,648 --> 05:49:52,068
1. 3 of them are deliberate near twins of questions you have already seen.

3941
05:49:52,448 --> 05:49:56,228
Same situation, one detail changed, different answer.

3942
05:49:57,102 --> 05:50:03,108
Those three are the most useful questions in this paper because they test whether you read or whether you remembered.

3943
05:50:04,718 --> 05:50:07,268
Same rules as before with one addition.

3944
05:50:08,526 --> 05:50:13,252
Only take this paper if you have already worked your weakest domain from exam 1.

3945
05:50:13,998 --> 05:50:17,892
Otherwise, you are measuring the same gap twice and learning nothing.

3946
05:50:18,574 --> 05:50:20,964
Write your answers down and score by domain.

3947
05:50:21,966 --> 05:50:28,068
And resist recognizing a question shape because three of these look familiar and end differently.

3948
05:50:29,486 --> 05:50:33,488
Keep your exam one score to hand for the comparison at the end.

3949
05:50:34,862 --> 05:50:35,588
Question 1.

3950
05:50:36,526 --> 05:50:40,288
A company runs a database on Amazon RDS for Postgres.

3951
05:50:42,478 --> 05:50:49,156
A security audit asks who applies patches to the database engine and who patches the underlying operating system.

3952
05:50:51,022 --> 05:50:54,820
It also asks who manages database user accounts.

3953
05:50:55,790 --> 05:50:57,764
Which statement is correct?

3954
05:51:08,078 --> 05:51:10,448
The answer is A and this is domain 2.

3955
05:51:10,670 --> 05:51:12,996
This is the first of the three paired questions.

3956
05:51:13,518 --> 05:51:19,140
Exam 1 asked the same thing about Lambda, and the answer was also that AWS patches.

3957
05:51:19,950 --> 05:51:21,252
But the reason differs.

3958
05:51:22,158 --> 05:51:25,648
With Lambda, there is no operating system you can reach at all.

3959
05:51:26,128 --> 05:51:31,172
With RDS, there is one and AWS patches it as part of the managed service.

3960
05:51:31,648 --> 05:51:35,620
Either way, database users and access control stay yours.

3961
05:51:37,358 --> 05:51:38,052
Question 2.

3962
05:51:39,182 --> 05:51:45,316
A company runs a document management application on 15 Linux EC2 instances spread across three.

3963
05:51:46,702 --> 05:51:47,940
Availability zones.

3964
05:51:48,208 --> 05:51:54,052
Every instance must read and write the same set of files and the application expects a mounted file system path.

3965
05:51:54,958 --> 05:51:58,340
The source code is not available and cannot be changed.

3966
05:52:08,878 --> 05:52:12,004
The answer is C and this is domain 3.

3967
05:52:12,398 --> 05:52:14,928
Two clauses work together to eliminate S3.

3968
05:52:15,406 --> 05:52:19,748
The application expects a mounted file system path and the code cannot be changed.

3969
05:52:20,558 --> 05:52:24,964
Either alone might leave S3 arguable and together they close it.

3970
05:52:25,806 --> 05:52:32,388
Then Linux and many instances across zones separates EFS from FSX.

3971
05:52:33,806 --> 05:52:34,596
Question 3.

3972
05:52:34,848 --> 05:52:42,052
A retailer used to take 11 weeks to launch a new regional storefront. Most of it spent procuring and installing servers.

3973
05:52:43,630 --> 05:52:46,820
Two of the last four storefronts closed within a year of opening.

3974
05:52:47,694 --> 05:52:54,564
On AWS, the same launch now takes 9 days, and the retailer trials new regions far more readily.

3975
05:53:05,166 --> 05:53:09,028
The answer is B Agility and this is domain one.

3976
05:53:09,966 --> 05:53:11,968
This is the second paired question.

3977
05:53:13,518 --> 05:53:19,460
Exam 1 described a hospital whose capacity moved with the seasons, and that was elasticity.

3978
05:53:19,888 --> 05:53:22,288
This retailer's capacity never changes.

3979
05:53:22,510 --> 05:53:26,660
What changed is the time to launch from 11 weeks to 9 days.

3980
05:53:27,488 --> 05:53:29,252
Agility measures time.

3981
05:53:29,648 --> 05:53:31,876
Elasticity measures capacity.

3982
05:53:33,582 --> 05:53:36,388
Leading which one moved is the whole task.

3983
05:53:38,094 --> 05:53:38,916
Question 4.

3984
05:53:39,502 --> 05:53:44,484
A mobile game needs to store player inventories retrieved by player identifier.

3985
05:53:44,768 --> 05:53:53,924
The developers expect between 10,000 and 5 million players, cannot predict the growth curve and need consistent single-digit millisecond reads.

3986
05:53:54,702 --> 05:54:00,484
There are no joins, and the team does not want to size or manage database servers.

3987
05:54:11,150 --> 05:54:13,732
The answer is C, and this is domain 3.

3988
05:54:14,766 --> 05:54:19,908
Four signals appear together, and recognizing them as a set is faster than reasoning through each.

3989
05:54:28,206 --> 05:54:30,805
And retrieval by a single identifier with no joins.

3990
05:54:31,886 --> 05:54:34,948
That combination is DynamoDB's signature.

3991
05:54:36,238 --> 05:54:37,124
Question 5.

3992
05:54:37,774 --> 05:54:42,500
An application on an EC2 instance needs to write objects to an S3 bucket.

3993
05:54:42,862 --> 05:54:49,668
A developer created an IAM user, generated access keys, and stored them in a file on the instance.

3994
05:54:50,190 --> 05:54:52,708
A security review asks for a better approach.

3995
05:54:53,422 --> 05:54:55,168
What should the company do?

3996
05:55:05,582 --> 05:55:08,324
The answer is C, and this is domain 2.

3997
05:55:08,928 --> 05:55:12,580
This is the mitigate versus eliminate pattern from module 23.

3998
05:55:13,358 --> 05:55:18,660
Three options make the long-term credential safer, and one removes the need for it entirely.

3999
05:55:19,470 --> 05:55:30,756
Option D is the cleverest of the three because secrets manager is genuinely the right home for a secret, but the instance still needs credentials to reach it. So the problem has only moved.

4000
05:55:32,110 --> 05:55:33,088
Question 6.

4001
05:55:33,422 --> 05:55:36,848
A research institute processes genome sequences overnight.

4002
05:55:37,102 --> 05:55:44,644
Each job runs for roughly two hours. The jobs are independent, and any job interrupted part way through can simply be stacked again.

4003
05:55:45,008 --> 05:55:49,668
Results are needed by 9 the next morning, and cost is the primary concern.

4004
05:55:59,918 --> 05:56:02,916
The answer is B, and this is domain 4.

4005
05:56:03,630 --> 05:56:09,968
Spot requires 3 things to be safe, and this scenario states each one deliberately. The jobs are independent.

4006
05:56:10,208 --> 05:56:17,124
An interrupted job can be started again, and results are not needed until 9 the next morning, so delay is tolerable.

4007
05:56:18,062 --> 05:56:24,932
Note that the 2-hour runtime is irrelevant to Spot, though it would have ruled out Lambda.

4008
05:56:26,542 --> 05:56:27,460
Question 7.

4009
05:56:27,888 --> 05:56:33,348
A company runs its platform in a single region across 3 availability zones.

4010
05:56:34,126 --> 05:56:41,892
Availability has been excellent with no outage in 2 years, and users in Europe and North America are satisfied.

4011
05:56:42,350 --> 05:56:47,488
Users in Southeast Asia report pages taking 4 to 6 seconds to load.

4012
05:56:47,982 --> 05:56:52,740
The content is largely static and identical for every user.

4013
05:57:03,278 --> 05:57:05,732
The answer is C, and this is domain 3.

4014
05:57:06,158 --> 05:57:09,380
This is the third pair question and the most instructive.

4015
05:57:10,670 --> 05:57:14,244
Exam 1 described the same single region across three zones.

4016
05:57:14,606 --> 05:57:19,492
But its regulator asked about surviving the loss of that region, so the answer was a second region.

4017
05:57:20,430 --> 05:57:23,620
Here nothing has failed, and users elsewhere are happy.

4018
05:57:24,046 --> 05:57:26,564
That makes it distance, not availability.

4019
05:57:26,990 --> 05:57:29,248
And distance means edge locations.

4020
05:57:30,862 --> 05:57:31,808
Question 8.

4021
05:57:32,128 --> 05:57:40,516
A team ships one large release each month, has no automated rollback, and diagnoses incidents by reading logs on individual servers.

4022
05:57:40,848 --> 05:57:48,868
It proposes to deploy small changes frequently, script its rollback, centralize logging, and hold a review after every incident.

4023
05:57:49,518 --> 05:57:52,420
No change to the application architecture is planned.

4024
05:58:02,862 --> 05:58:05,924
The answer is B, and this is domain one.

4025
05:58:06,768 --> 05:58:12,516
The separator between operational excellence and reliability is where the fix lives.

4026
05:58:13,294 --> 05:58:17,796
Reliability changes the architecture so the system survives failure.

4027
05:58:18,702 --> 05:58:21,764
Operational excellence changes how the team works.

4028
05:58:22,608 --> 05:58:29,988
Every item on this list is a process change, and the final sentence removes any doubt by saying the architecture is untouched.

4029
05:58:31,598 --> 05:58:32,448
Question 9.

4030
05:58:32,768 --> 05:58:40,608
A company security team is alerted that an EC2 instance is making outbound connections to a known botnet address.

4031
05:58:40,848 --> 05:58:45,636
An IAM access key is also being used from a country with no offices.

4032
05:58:46,446 --> 05:58:52,708
The team wants a service that would surface both automatically with nothing installed on the instances.

4033
05:59:03,502 --> 05:59:05,892
The answer is B, and this is domain 2.

4034
05:59:06,158 --> 05:59:11,076
Both symptoms are behaviors rather than weaknesses, which removes inspector immediately.

4035
05:59:11,248 --> 05:59:20,772
Neither concerns data content, which removes Mickey. And the phrase nothing installed on the instances is doing deliberate work because guard duty is agentless by design.

4036
05:59:22,222 --> 05:59:23,076
Question 10.

4037
05:59:24,174 --> 05:59:30,564
An Amazon S3 buckets policy was changed at some point overnight, briefly allowing public access.

4038
05:59:31,168 --> 05:59:37,252
The company needs to establish which IAAM identity made the change and at what time.

4039
05:59:37,488 --> 05:59:41,220
It has Cloud Trail, CloudWatch, and Config all enabled.

4040
05:59:51,854 --> 05:59:54,692
The answer is C. And this is domain 3.

4041
05:59:55,278 --> 05:59:58,884
Option B is the closest miss and worth dwelling on.

4042
05:59:59,598 --> 06:00:06,212
Config genuinely holds the history, and it would tell you the bucket was public between two timestamps.

4043
06:00:06,702 --> 06:00:09,892
What it cannot tell you is who did it.

4044
06:00:10,830 --> 06:00:13,764
Config answers what the configuration was.

4045
06:00:15,150 --> 06:00:17,348
CloudRail answers who changed it.

4046
06:00:18,798 --> 06:00:19,728
Question 11.

4047
06:00:20,046 --> 06:00:24,612
A company runs an aging self-hosted customer relationship management system.

4048
06:00:25,038 --> 06:00:33,764
Rather than move it, the company will cancel it entirely and subscribe to a commercial CRM product delivered as software as a service.

4049
06:00:34,606 --> 06:00:40,260
No code will be migrated and the company will no longer run the software itself.

4050
06:00:50,958 --> 06:00:52,048
The answer is C.

4051
06:00:52,208 --> 06:00:54,404
Repurchase, and this is domain one.

4052
06:00:54,768 --> 06:00:59,300
Refactor and repurchase both abandon the system you had, so they get swapped.

4053
06:01:02,702 --> 06:01:05,124
The separator is ownership afterwards.

4054
06:01:05,934 --> 06:01:08,580
Refactor means you rewrite it and still own it.

4055
06:01:10,126 --> 06:01:13,988
Repurchase means you stop owning software at all and rent someone else's.

4056
06:01:15,694 --> 06:01:16,804
Question 12.

4057
06:01:17,168 --> 06:01:24,356
A bank must store the keys protecting its most sensitive records in hardware. No other AWS customer shares.

4058
06:01:24,928 --> 06:01:32,420
Its regulator requires the bank to demonstrate that AWS personnel cannot access the key material under any circumstances.

4059
06:01:33,390 --> 06:01:35,620
Cost and operational effort are secondary.

4060
06:01:46,350 --> 06:01:48,900
The answer is B, and this is domain 2.

4061
06:01:49,262 --> 06:01:55,908
KMS is normally the right answer for keys, and choosing CloudHSM without a stated reason would be wrong.

4062
06:01:56,462 --> 06:01:58,724
Here, the reason is stated twice over.

4063
06:01:59,470 --> 06:02:06,180
Hardware, no other customer shares, and AWS personnel unable to access the key material.

4064
06:02:07,022 --> 06:02:12,036
Those two clauses exist precisely to make Cloud HSM correct.

4065
06:02:13,518 --> 06:02:14,468
Question 13.

4066
06:02:16,590 --> 06:02:24,580
An order service writes an order, then calls a stock service, a payment service, and a notification service one after another.

4067
06:02:25,198 --> 06:02:30,148
When the notification service is slow, order submissions time out and customers give

4068
06:02:30,208 --> 06:02:37,988
up. The company wants order submission to return immediately with downstream work guaranteed to happen afterwards.

4069
06:02:48,270 --> 06:02:51,076
The answer is B, and this is domain 3.

4070
06:02:52,174 --> 06:02:55,364
The two requirements together describe a queue precisely.

4071
06:02:56,238 --> 06:03:02,724
Return immediately means the caller must not wait, and guaranteed afterwards means the work must not be lost.

4072
06:03:03,502 --> 06:03:06,308
Buffering plus persistence is SQS.

4073
06:03:07,246 --> 06:03:12,260
Compare it with question 16 in exam 1, where ordering and retree is made it.

4074
06:03:12,608 --> 06:03:14,276
Step functions instead.

4075
06:03:15,758 --> 06:03:16,836
Question 14.

4076
06:03:17,088 --> 06:03:26,308
A finance director wants a report showing last quarter's AWS spend broken down by six departments, each with its own AWS account under one organization.

4077
06:03:26,958 --> 06:03:34,768
Separately, the director wants an email as soon as any department's forecast spend indicates it will exceed its allocation this month.

4078
06:03:34,990 --> 06:03:35,716
Choose two.

4079
06:03:46,318 --> 06:03:49,732
The answers are A and B, and this is domain four.

4080
06:03:50,094 --> 06:03:52,004
Sort by time and it resolves.

4081
06:03:52,448 --> 06:03:57,668
Last quarter spend is backward looking, which is cost explorer grouping by linked account.

4082
06:03:58,190 --> 06:04:03,012
A forecast crossing an allocation is forward looking, which is budgets.

4083
06:04:03,950 --> 06:04:12,388
Option D is the sharp distractor because it also emails about spend, but only when spend is unusual rather than when it crosses a plan.

4084
06:04:13,966 --> 06:04:14,848
Question 15.

4085
06:04:15,118 --> 06:04:26,116
A migration has stored. The infrastructure is built and working, but the operations team has never run a cloud environment, is anxious about losing its expertise, and has no training program.

4086
06:04:26,766 --> 06:04:29,668
Which CAF perspective does this belong to?

4087
06:04:40,206 --> 06:04:41,568
The answer is B.

4088
06:04:42,048 --> 06:04:44,164
People and this is domain one.

4089
06:04:45,166 --> 06:04:53,028
Option A is deliberate bait because the stem literally says operations team and there is an operations perspective.

4090
06:04:53,488 --> 06:04:55,844
But read what the gap actually is.

4091
06:04:56,334 --> 06:04:59,488
Never run a cloud environment is a skills gap.

4092
06:05:00,046 --> 06:05:03,812
Anxious about losing expertise is a change management gap.

4093
06:05:04,238 --> 06:05:06,372
No training program is a training gap.

4094
06:05:06,702 --> 06:05:08,208
All three are people.

4095
06:05:09,902 --> 06:05:10,785
Question 16.

4096
06:05:11,408 --> 06:05:15,088
A company with 90 AWS accounts has a rule.

4097
06:05:15,342 --> 06:05:23,492
No account outside its research organizational unit may create resources in regions beyond the European Union, whoever attempts it.

4098
06:05:24,206 --> 06:05:31,268
Several accounts have local administrators with full IAM permissions, and the restriction must not be removable by them.

4099
06:05:41,902 --> 06:05:46,500
The answer is B, and this is domain 2. Two clauses work together.

4100
06:05:47,008 --> 06:05:50,788
Whoever attempts it and not removable by local administrators.

4101
06:05:51,246 --> 06:05:59,008
Anything built inside IAAM can be undone by someone with IAAM permissions, which eliminates options A and D.

4102
06:05:59,758 --> 06:06:01,700
An SCP sits above.

4103
06:06:02,286 --> 06:06:07,716
IAM entirely and binds even the root user of a member account.

4104
06:06:08,974 --> 06:06:09,988
Question 17.

4105
06:06:10,542 --> 06:06:16,868
A company runs a multiplayer game server communicating over UDP and requiring extremely low latency.

4106
06:06:17,358 --> 06:06:22,768
Its clients are configured with fixed IP addresses that cannot be changed without a client update.

4107
06:06:22,958 --> 06:06:26,372
It needs to distribute connections across many backend instances.

4108
06:06:36,814 --> 06:06:39,492
The answer is B, and this is domain 3.

4109
06:06:40,174 --> 06:06:46,244
Three signatures appear together UDP, which the application load balancer cannot carry at all,

4110
06:06:47,022 --> 06:06:48,516
extreme low latency,

4111
06:06:49,614 --> 06:06:54,244
and fixed IP addresses, which only the network load balancer provides.

4112
06:06:54,830 --> 06:06:57,984
Any one of the three would be enough on its own.

4113
06:06:59,022 --> 06:07:00,036
Question 18.

4114
06:07:00,366 --> 06:07:07,460
A finance manager asks why AWS can offer lower prices than the company achieves in its own data center.

4115
06:07:08,064 --> 06:07:11,812
AWS also has to buy hardware, power, and networking.

4116
06:07:12,334 --> 06:07:18,532
An architect explains that AWS combines the usage of very large numbers of customers when it purchases.

4117
06:07:29,038 --> 06:07:32,260
The answer is B, and this is domain one.

4118
06:07:33,934 --> 06:07:39,396
The architect's sentence is the definition of economies of scale almost word for word.

4119
06:07:39,982 --> 06:07:43,012
Aggregation across customers is the mechanism.

4120
06:07:43,598 --> 06:07:50,564
Watch for the distractors that usually accompany it, which are price matching against competitors and the free tier.

4121
06:07:51,438 --> 06:07:53,764
Neither is economies of scale.

4122
06:07:55,246 --> 06:07:56,132
Question 19.

4123
06:07:56,784 --> 06:08:02,404
An engineering firm of 600 staff already runs Microsoft Active Directory on premises.

4124
06:08:04,014 --> 06:08:08,004
It is opening 18 AWS accounts, one per project.

4125
06:08:08,718 --> 06:08:14,404
Staff should sign in to whichever accounts they are entitled to using existing corporate credentials.

4126
06:08:15,246 --> 06:08:19,812
The identity team refuses to maintain separate credentials in each account.

4127
06:08:30,382 --> 06:08:33,156
The answer is C, and this is domain two.

4128
06:08:33,744 --> 06:08:38,180
Three separate signals point the same way, 18 accounts rather than one.

4129
06:08:39,406 --> 06:08:41,060
An existing corporate directory.

4130
06:08:42,670 --> 06:08:45,508
And an explicit refusal to duplicate credentials.

4131
06:08:45,966 --> 06:08:48,100
Option B is the population trap.

4132
06:08:48,974 --> 06:08:56,036
Cognito serves your application's customers, and these 600 people are staff signing in to AWS itself.

4133
06:08:57,584 --> 06:08:58,692
Question 20.

4134
06:08:59,024 --> 06:09:04,196
A company must run a legacy application requiring an unsupported operating system version.

4135
06:09:04,384 --> 06:09:09,764
It needs administrative access to tuned kernel parameters and must run continuously.

4136
06:09:10,224 --> 06:09:12,676
Which AWS compute option is suitable.

4137
06:09:22,990 --> 06:09:25,732
The answer is C and this is domain 3.

4138
06:09:26,350 --> 06:09:30,468
Most of this exam rewards choosing the more managed service.

4139
06:09:30,704 --> 06:09:33,504
So this question deliberately goes the other way.

4140
06:09:34,030 --> 06:09:46,212
Naming kernel parameters and an unsupported operating system eliminates managed services by definition because removing that access is exactly what makes a service managed.

4141
06:09:47,918 --> 06:09:49,316
Here is the scoring guide.

4142
06:09:50,944 --> 06:09:56,004
Domain 1 was questions 3, 8, 11, 15, and 18.

4143
06:09:56,544 --> 06:10:01,028
Domain 2 was 1, 5, 9, 12, 16, and 19.

4144
06:10:01,584 --> 06:10:06,180
Domain 3 was 2, 4, 7, 10, 13, 17, and 20.

4145
06:10:07,184 --> 06:10:09,220
Domain 4 was 6 and 14.

4146
06:10:10,190 --> 06:10:16,612
And pay particular attention to questions 1, 3 and 7, which were the paired near-quins of exam.

4147
06:10:17,294 --> 06:10:17,764
1.

4148
06:10:18,446 --> 06:10:22,916
Missing those specifically means you recognize the shape rather than reading the detail.

4149
06:10:24,590 --> 06:10:25,732
What to do next?

4150
06:10:27,534 --> 06:10:34,756
Compare domain scores across both papers rather than the totals, because a domain weak in both is your real gap.

4151
06:10:35,406 --> 06:10:41,572
A domain that improved was probably a knowledge gap, while one that did not is more likely a reading habit.

4152
06:10:42,350 --> 06:10:44,068
Leave several days before exam.

4153
06:10:44,366 --> 06:10:55,716
3 because recall after a gap is what predicts a pass. Use that gap for module 21, the confusion pairs. And if both papers scored above 16, spend the time on module 22 instead.

4154
06:10:57,038 --> 06:10:58,500
The last practice exam.

4155
06:10:59,022 --> 06:11:02,372
20 questions on topics neither of the first two papers touched.

4156
06:11:02,862 --> 06:11:08,420
A poor score here is a genuine gap rather than a repeat of something you already know you missed.

4157
06:11:09,422 --> 06:11:14,372
The scoring guide at the end reads the trend across all three papers rather than just this one.

4158
06:11:15,534 --> 06:11:17,316
Four things before you start.

4159
06:11:17,710 --> 06:11:19,652
Take this several days after exam.

4160
06:11:19,920 --> 06:11:23,684
2. Because recall after a gap is what predicts a pass.

4161
06:11:23,920 --> 06:11:27,620
Score by domain again and keep both previous scores to hand.

4162
06:11:27,920 --> 06:11:32,644
Every topic here is new, so weak answers are real gaps rather than repeats.

4163
06:11:33,550 --> 06:11:36,004
And do not take this the night before your exam.

4164
06:11:36,240 --> 06:11:41,668
A poor score at that point costs you sleep and teaches you nothing you can add on in time.

4165
06:11:43,278 --> 06:11:43,972
Question 1.

4166
06:11:44,494 --> 06:11:48,292
An analyst needs to answer occasional ad hoc questions.

4167
06:11:49,102 --> 06:11:54,884
About two years of application log files already sitting in Amazon S3 as compressed text.

4168
06:11:55,630 --> 06:11:59,428
The questions arise perhaps twice a month and are different each time.

4169
06:12:00,430 --> 06:12:05,156
The company does not want to run infrastructure or load the data into anything.

4170
06:12:15,726 --> 06:12:18,640
The answer is B, and this is domain 3.

4171
06:12:18,894 --> 06:12:20,800
Three phrases point the same way.

4172
06:12:21,040 --> 06:12:25,956
The data already sits in S3. The questions are occasional and different each time.

4173
06:12:26,734 --> 06:12:28,900
And no infrastructure and no loading.

4174
06:12:29,806 --> 06:12:34,084
Athena query is in place, provisions nothing, and charges per query.

4175
06:12:34,990 --> 06:12:41,252
Redshift is a warehouse you load and keep running, which is the wrong shape of cost for twice a month.

4176
06:12:42,542 --> 06:12:43,300
Question 2.

4177
06:12:43,726 --> 06:12:52,740
A company runs an application on Amazon EC2 instances. An auditor asks who applies security patches to the guest operating system on those instances.

4178
06:12:52,960 --> 06:12:56,516
It also asks who destroys failed disk drives in the data center.

4179
06:12:56,960 --> 06:12:58,404
Which statement is correct?

4180
06:13:09,070 --> 06:13:11,680
The answer is A, and this is domain 2.

4181
06:13:11,920 --> 06:13:21,120
This question completes a set running across all three papers. Exam 1 asked about Lambda, exam 2 about RDS, and this one about EC2.

4182
06:13:21,390 --> 06:13:25,444
In all three, the patching answer moves as the service becomes less managed.

4183
06:13:26,094 --> 06:13:31,204
And in all three, physical security and hardware handling stayed with AWS.

4184
06:13:31,440 --> 06:13:34,404
Some of the line moves, some of it never does.

4185
06:13:35,950 --> 06:13:36,708
Question 3.

4186
06:13:37,774 --> 06:13:44,484
A payments company is designing a service that must continue processing correctly if an entire availability zone becomes unavailable.

4187
06:13:45,998 --> 06:13:49,348
It must return to full capacity without an engineer intervening.

4188
06:13:49,870 --> 06:13:51,556
It is deploying across three

4189
06:13:52,334 --> 06:13:57,920
availability zones with health checks and automatic replacement of failed instances.

4190
06:14:08,302 --> 06:14:12,068
The answer is D. Reliability, and this is domain one.

4191
06:14:13,838 --> 06:14:17,796
Two phrases decided almost word for word from the definition.

4192
06:14:18,382 --> 06:14:24,164
Continue processing correctly and return to full capacity without an engineer intervening.

4193
06:14:26,414 --> 06:14:26,960
Option

4194
06:14:28,366 --> 06:14:29,924
A is worth noticing.

4195
06:14:30,990 --> 06:14:35,940
The word payments make security feel relevant and it would be in real life.

4196
06:14:36,430 --> 06:14:40,452
But the exam grades the stated requirement, not the industry.

4197
06:14:42,094 --> 06:14:43,012
Question 4.

4198
06:14:43,280 --> 06:14:49,508
A security team has identified a single IP address repeatedly scanning a company's public web servers.

4199
06:14:49,840 --> 06:14:55,044
It wants to block all traffic from that address before it reaches any instance in the web subnet.

4200
06:14:55,758 --> 06:15:03,012
The instances already have security groups permitting HTTP and HTTPS from anywhere.

4201
06:15:13,582 --> 06:15:16,240
The answer is B, and this is domainly.

4202
06:15:17,038 --> 06:15:18,692
One fact decides this entirely.

4203
06:15:19,438 --> 06:15:25,092
Security groups have allowed rules only, so blocking a specific address is impossible with them.

4204
06:15:26,510 --> 06:15:27,040
Options.

4205
06:15:28,686 --> 06:15:34,756
A and D both describe capabilities that do not exist, which is a distractor style worth recognizing.

4206
06:15:35,150 --> 06:15:40,400
And option C is the overcorrection, blocking everyone to stop one address.

4207
06:15:41,870 --> 06:15:42,788
Question 5.

4208
06:15:43,150 --> 06:15:53,828
A healthcare provider holds several million objects across many Amazon S3 buckets and does not know which contain patient records with personally identifiable information.

4209
06:15:54,320 --> 06:15:58,468
A regulator has asked it to identify and classify that data.

4210
06:15:59,440 --> 06:16:03,652
It wants a managed service rather than building its own scanning.

4211
06:16:14,094 --> 06:16:16,900
The answer is C, and this is domain 2.

4212
06:16:17,520 --> 06:16:25,764
Personally identifiable information plus S3 is the MECI signature, and MECI is the only one of the detection services that opens your data.

4213
06:16:26,606 --> 06:16:28,640
Option D is the instructive miss.

4214
06:16:29,710 --> 06:16:38,788
Security Hub is a genuinely useful service, but aggregation layers depend on the layers beneath them. So they are rarely the answer to a discovery question.

4215
06:16:40,302 --> 06:16:41,440
Question 6.

4216
06:16:41,840 --> 06:16:48,644
A company has run the same fleet of 12 VC2 instances continuously for 5 years to serve its code product.

4217
06:16:49,040 --> 06:16:53,604
The instance type has not changed and will not change for at least 3 more years.

4218
06:16:53,920 --> 06:16:58,180
It wants the largest possible discount and has no need for flexibility.

4219
06:17:08,910 --> 06:17:12,772
The answer is C, and this is domain 4.

4220
06:17:13,646 --> 06:17:16,612
Every clause argues for maximum commitment.

4221
06:17:17,806 --> 06:17:22,852
5 years of history, 3 more expected, and no need for flexibility.

4222
06:17:24,014 --> 06:17:28,452
Option B is the instructive miss because convertible sounds safer.

4223
06:17:28,974 --> 06:17:31,200
Flexibility is not free.

4224
06:17:31,726 --> 06:17:37,796
You give up discount to buy the right to change, and this company has told you it will not change.

4225
06:17:39,200 --> 06:17:40,240
Question 7.

4226
06:17:40,480 --> 06:17:45,120
A logistics company receives thousands of scanned delivery notes as PDF files.

4227
06:17:45,280 --> 06:17:53,700
It needs to extract values from name fields into a structured record, then determine whether the free text driver comments indicate a complaint.

4228
06:17:54,318 --> 06:17:58,564
It has no machine learning specialists and wants managed services only.

4229
06:17:59,886 --> 06:18:00,612
Choose two.

4230
06:18:11,150 --> 06:18:13,892
The answers are B and C and this is domain 3.

4231
06:18:14,318 --> 06:18:20,804
Getting text out of a document and understanding the text are two different jobs, and no single service does both.

4232
06:18:22,926 --> 06:18:26,308
Text tract, extracts, then comprehend interprets.

4233
06:18:27,310 --> 06:18:31,408
Option A is the trap for anyone reasoning that a scan is an image.

4234
06:18:31,502 --> 06:18:33,252
So recognition handles it.

4235
06:18:33,888 --> 06:18:38,116
Recognition finds objects and faces, not form fields.

4236
06:18:39,918 --> 06:18:40,928
Question 8.

4237
06:18:41,168 --> 06:18:43,248
A cloud program is underway.

4238
06:18:43,568 --> 06:18:51,556
Finance has discovered teams can provision resources without approval and there is no way to attribute spend to business units.

4239
06:18:51,808 --> 06:18:56,580
The chief risk officer cannot show regulatory obligations are tracked.

4240
06:18:57,168 --> 06:19:01,316
Which CAF perspective covers these concerns?

4241
06:19:12,048 --> 06:19:16,132
The answer is C, governance, and this is domain one.

4242
06:19:16,928 --> 06:19:21,700
Two concerns from two different executives and both land in the same perspective.

4243
06:19:22,608 --> 06:19:26,276
Uncontrolled spend with no attribution is cost management.

4244
06:19:26,848 --> 06:19:30,692
Tracking regulatory obligations is risk and compliance management.

4245
06:19:31,502 --> 06:19:36,900
Option B is the pattern matching trap because a chief risk officer sounds like security.

4246
06:19:37,248 --> 06:19:40,068
Read what is being asked for, not who is asking.

4247
06:19:41,646 --> 06:19:42,468
Question 9.

4248
06:19:42,862 --> 06:19:50,948
An external auditor wants evidence that the data centers hosting a company's workloads meet ISO 27001.

4249
06:19:51,328 --> 06:19:55,364
It also wants AWS's most recent SOC2 report.

4250
06:19:55,918 --> 06:19:58,852
The company cannot inspect any AWS facility.

4251
06:20:09,568 --> 06:20:12,484
The answer is A, and this is domain 2.

4252
06:20:12,928 --> 06:20:19,728
Whenever an auditor asks about AWS's half of the shared responsibility model, you cannot inspect it yourself.

4253
06:20:19,968 --> 06:20:23,588
So you evidence it with AWS's own audit reports.

4254
06:20:24,142 --> 06:20:25,808
Those live in artifact.

4255
06:20:26,368 --> 06:20:32,548
Options B and D both look at your half of the model, which is why neither can help.

4256
06:20:34,094 --> 06:20:34,820
Question 10.

4257
06:20:35,246 --> 06:20:40,772
An EC2 instance in one availability zone has an EBS volume holding application data.

4258
06:20:42,446 --> 06:20:49,604
The company is redesigning for high availability and needs an identical volume attached to a new instance in a different availability zone.

4259
06:20:50,126 --> 06:20:52,324
The data must be copied across.

4260
06:21:02,688 --> 06:21:06,288
The answer is B and this is domain 3.

4261
06:21:06,528 --> 06:21:11,620
Option C is worth naming because it is a fabricated feature built from real vocabulary.

4262
06:21:13,102 --> 06:21:19,620
Multi-availability zone is a genuine AWS term that belongs to RDS, not to EBS volumes.

4263
06:21:20,238 --> 06:21:27,172
If an option describes a feature you have never encountered, suspect invention before, assuming you missed something.

4264
06:21:28,846 --> 06:21:29,732
Question 11.

4265
06:21:31,054 --> 06:21:36,420
During a discovery exercise before a migration, a company finds an internal reporting tool.

4266
06:21:37,070 --> 06:21:42,468
It has no logins for 16 months, no owner, and no entry in the service catalog.

4267
06:21:43,182 --> 06:21:45,348
The team that built it left two years ago.

4268
06:21:45,774 --> 06:21:47,408
What should be done with it?

4269
06:21:57,742 --> 06:21:58,848
The answer is C.

4270
06:21:59,168 --> 06:22:01,284
Retire. And this is domain one.

4271
06:22:01,728 --> 06:22:03,648
Retire is the strategy people.

4272
06:22:04,654 --> 06:22:12,484
Forget is the strategy at all. Discovery exercises routinely find that around a tenth of an estate runs with nobody using it.

4273
06:22:13,006 --> 06:22:16,068
Switching those off is the cheapest possible migration outcome.

4274
06:22:16,974 --> 06:22:23,652
Retain is the distractor, and the separator is that retaining requires a stated reason to keep something.

4275
06:22:25,294 --> 06:22:26,208
Question 12.

4276
06:22:26,542 --> 06:22:31,012
A company runs a public web application behind an application load balancer.

4277
06:22:31,408 --> 06:22:39,428
Its logs show repeated attempts to inject SQL statements through the search form and a smaller number of cross site scripting attempts.

4278
06:22:39,968 --> 06:22:43,332
It wants these requests blocked before they reach the application.

4279
06:22:53,934 --> 06:22:57,028
The answer is B, and this is domain 2.

4280
06:22:57,518 --> 06:22:58,720
The verb decides it.

4281
06:22:58,880 --> 06:23:02,660
Blocked is a protection verb which removes guard duty immediately.

4282
06:23:03,040 --> 06:23:06,320
Then the layer decides between the protection services.

4283
06:23:06,640 --> 06:23:13,892
SQL injection and cross-site scripting live inside the request content, which only WAF can read.

4284
06:23:14,638 --> 06:23:18,372
Shield handles volume and a network.

4285
06:23:19,680 --> 06:23:22,820
ACL only sees addresses and ports.

4286
06:23:24,558 --> 06:23:25,476
Question 13.

4287
06:23:25,760 --> 06:23:30,320
A company has grown to 55 VPCs across many teams.

4288
06:23:30,574 --> 06:23:35,140
They increasingly need to communicate with each other and with the corporate data center.

4289
06:23:35,440 --> 06:23:42,020
The network team maintains a steadily growing number of individual peering connections and wants one place to manage this.

4290
06:23:52,622 --> 06:23:55,236
The answer is B and this is domain 3.

4291
06:23:55,600 --> 06:24:01,636
The deciding phase is a steadily growing number of individual peering connections because that describes a mesh.

4292
06:24:03,150 --> 06:24:08,080
55 VPCs fully peered would need well over a thousand connections.

4293
06:24:08,814 --> 06:24:13,668
Transit gateway replaces the mesh with a hub, So each VPC connects once.

4294
06:24:14,510 --> 06:24:16,836
Option C is the name collision again.

4295
06:24:18,160 --> 06:24:19,300
Question 14.

4296
06:24:19,694 --> 06:24:25,920
A company reviewing a large AWS bill finds significant charges for data transferred to users on the internet.

4297
06:24:26,320 --> 06:24:29,860
There are further charges for data replicated between two regions.

4298
06:24:30,160 --> 06:24:35,492
A manager suggests reducing the volume of data uploaded into AWS would help.

4299
06:24:35,920 --> 06:24:37,348
Is the manager correct?

4300
06:24:47,950 --> 06:24:50,564
The answer is B, and this is domain 4.

4301
06:24:50,960 --> 06:24:56,164
Three options state a transfer rule backwards, which is the standard shape for pricing questions.

4302
06:24:56,654 --> 06:24:58,500
But look carefully at option D.

4303
06:24:58,894 --> 06:25:03,140
It correctly says the manager is wrong, then justifies it with a false claim.

4304
06:25:04,110 --> 06:25:09,124
Transfer between regions is charged and the company is paying for exactly that.

4305
06:25:09,742 --> 06:25:14,468
A right verdict, supported by a wrong reason, is still a wrong answer.

4306
06:25:15,758 --> 06:25:16,800
Question 15.

4307
06:25:17,040 --> 06:25:27,748
A company preparing to leave its data center makes a large hardware purchase every three years and pays a fixed annual lease. It must also decide capacity 18 months in advance.

4308
06:25:28,000 --> 06:25:33,796
After migrating, it expects to pay monthly for what it actually consumes with no advance purchase.

4309
06:25:44,526 --> 06:25:47,428
The answer is B, and this is domain 1.

4310
06:25:48,078 --> 06:25:50,884
The whole scenario describes payments and nothing else.

4311
06:25:51,598 --> 06:25:55,588
A large advance purchase becomes monthly charges that follow consumption.

4312
06:25:56,078 --> 06:26:01,764
Option C is the tempting one because deciding capacity 18 months ahead sounds slow.

4313
06:26:02,318 --> 06:26:08,388
But the scenario never says anything got faster, only that the money changed shape.

4314
06:26:09,600 --> 06:26:10,852
Question 16.

4315
06:26:11,280 --> 06:26:17,444
An organization runs 60 AWS accounts, each with guard duty, inspector, and messi enabled.

4316
06:26:17,760 --> 06:26:22,916
The security team reviews findings by signing in to each account in turn, which takes most of a week.

4317
06:26:23,120 --> 06:26:28,132
It wants one view of all findings and to measure the estate against the CIS benchmark.

4318
06:26:38,510 --> 06:26:41,892
The answer is B, and this is domain 2.

4319
06:26:42,318 --> 06:26:47,748
Compare this with question 5 in this same paper where Security Hub was the wrong answer.

4320
06:26:48,224 --> 06:26:52,804
There, nothing was producing findings, so aggregation had nothing to aggregate.

4321
06:26:53,582 --> 06:27:00,772
Here, three detection services already run across 60 accounts, and the only problem is seeing them together.

4322
06:27:01,294 --> 06:27:03,780
That is exactly when aggregation is right.

4323
06:27:05,454 --> 06:27:06,436
Question 17.

4324
06:27:08,014 --> 06:27:16,068
A platform team runs containerized services using a large library of existing Kubernetes manifests it intends to keep.

4325
06:27:17,038 --> 06:27:21,412
It will no longer patch, size, or manage any EC2 instances.

4326
06:27:21,998 --> 06:27:25,828
It wants AWS to run both the control plane and the compute.

4327
06:27:29,326 --> 06:27:30,064
Choose two.

4328
06:27:40,878 --> 06:27:43,972
The answer is B and C and this is domain three.

4329
06:27:44,624 --> 06:27:47,812
Two separations, each decided by one clause.

4330
06:27:48,224 --> 06:27:56,388
Kubernetes manifest decide EKS over ECS. No longer patching or sizing instances decides Fargate over EC2.

4331
06:27:57,454 --> 06:28:00,100
Option E is needed in real life for the images.

4332
06:28:00,304 --> 06:28:04,484
And it is wrong here because the question asks about orchestration and compute.

4333
06:28:06,094 --> 06:28:07,024
Question 18.

4334
06:28:07,278 --> 06:28:13,156
A company wants a structured review of its main workload against AWS best practices.

4335
06:28:13,504 --> 06:28:21,060
It will answer a set of questions about the workload, receive a prioritized improvement plan, and repeat the exercise each quarter.

4336
06:28:21,870 --> 06:28:26,180
It does not want a service that simply watches the account continuously.

4337
06:28:36,846 --> 06:28:39,620
The answer is B, and this is domain one.

4338
06:28:41,422 --> 06:28:46,948
The final sentence exists purely to eliminate trusted advisor, which is the strongest distractor here.

4339
06:28:47,598 --> 06:28:51,108
Both services tell you what to improve, and the separator is how.

4340
06:28:52,526 --> 06:28:56,164
The well-architected tool is a review you sit down and complete.

4341
06:28:57,102 --> 06:29:01,348
Trusted advisor watches the account continuously without being asked.

4342
06:29:02,862 --> 06:29:03,908
Question 19.

4343
06:29:04,224 --> 06:29:07,264
A security team is asked two questions by an auditor.

4344
06:29:07,504 --> 06:29:12,612
Are any S3 buckets or IAM roles shared with an account outside the organization?

4345
06:29:12,944 --> 06:29:17,828
And which IAAM roles and access keys have not been used in the last 90 days?

4346
06:29:18,144 --> 06:29:20,836
The team wants a single service that answers both.

4347
06:29:31,534 --> 06:29:32,644
The answer is B.

4348
06:29:33,006 --> 06:29:34,276
And this is domain 2.

4349
06:29:35,022 --> 06:29:38,244
The auditor's two questions map precisely onto the two things.

4350
06:29:39,022 --> 06:29:40,388
Access analyzer does.

4351
06:29:41,198 --> 06:29:44,292
External access findings and unused access findings.

4352
06:29:44,750 --> 06:29:46,340
Option C deserves a note.

4353
06:29:47,246 --> 06:29:53,604
Cloud Trail does hold the evidence, but you would have to analyze 90 days of logs to extract it.

4354
06:29:54,254 --> 06:29:56,644
Prefer the service designed for the question.

4355
06:29:58,286 --> 06:29:59,428
Question 20.

4356
06:29:59,950 --> 06:30:03,172
A company is releasing a new version of its website.

4357
06:30:03,438 --> 06:30:10,212
It wants 5% of visitors sent to it and 95% kept on the current version to compare error rates.

4358
06:30:10,464 --> 06:30:14,628
Both versions run behind their own load balancers in the same region.

4359
06:30:25,216 --> 06:30:27,844
The answer is B, and this is domain 3.

4360
06:30:28,016 --> 06:30:34,916
A stated percentage split is always weighted routing, but notice the final sentence that both versions run in the same region.

4361
06:30:35,616 --> 06:30:43,684
That clause exists only to eliminate latency based routing because with both endpoints in one region there is no latency difference to act on.

4362
06:30:44,096 --> 06:30:48,612
Scenarios often add a clause whose sole job is closing off one wrong answer.

4363
06:30:50,126 --> 06:30:52,096
Here is the final scoring guide.

4364
06:30:52,526 --> 06:30:57,348
Domain 1 was questions 3, 8, 11, 15, and 18.

4365
06:30:57,776 --> 06:31:01,284
Domain 2 was 2, 5, 9, 12, 16, and 19.

4366
06:31:01,742 --> 06:31:05,776
Domain 3 was 1, 4, 7, 10, 13, 17, and 20.

4367
06:31:06,286 --> 06:31:08,100
Domain 4 was 6 and 14.

4368
06:31:09,136 --> 06:31:17,348
Across all three papers, the domain split is 15, 18, 21, and 6, which lands within a point of the real exams waiting.

4369
06:31:19,022 --> 06:31:21,220
How to read all three papers together.

4370
06:31:21,902 --> 06:31:25,700
Improving across them means your gaps where knowledge and revision worked.

4371
06:31:26,254 --> 06:31:30,308
Flat across them usually means a reading habit rather than a knowledge gap.

4372
06:31:30,606 --> 06:31:32,356
So rewatch module 23.

4373
06:31:33,296 --> 06:31:42,948
One domain weak in all three is the only result that should change your plan and you should work that domain and nothing else. 16 or more on each paper is a comfortable position.

4374
06:31:43,216 --> 06:31:47,812
And misses spread evenly usually mean pace rather than knowledge.

4375
06:31:49,518 --> 06:31:50,820
That is the whole course.

4376
06:31:51,726 --> 06:31:55,684
26 modules, and if one idea survives, it should be this one.

4377
06:31:56,496 --> 06:32:01,604
You are not trying to know everything about AWS, and nobody sitting this exam does.

4378
06:32:02,336 --> 06:32:07,332
You are trying to separate two options that both look right using one word in the question.

4379
06:32:08,270 --> 06:32:11,616
Rewatch module 21 in the days before you sit,

4380
06:32:12,302 --> 06:32:17,156
reread the in scope list, pass the procedure you have practiced, and good luck.

